heiro:6000001 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Norwegian Medical Products Agency on 15D"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000030 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.dmp.no/globalassets/documents/offentlig-finansiering-og-pris/dokumentasjon-til-metodevurdering/submission-guideline-july-2026.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Norwegian Medical Products Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "15D Accepted Where EQ-5D Data Are Lacking, Mapped to EQ-5D" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Norwegian Medical Products Agency requires health-related quality of life in health economic analyses to be measured with a generic preference-based instrument, preferably the EQ-5D-5L. Its submission guidelines name the 15D among the other generic preference-based instruments that can be used where patient-reported EQ-5D data are lacking. Values from such an instrument must then be mapped to EQ-5D values using validated methods and compared with published quality of life data for the relevant patient group." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Norwegian Medical Products Agency, Submission guidelines for single technology assessment of medicinal products, valid from 1 January 2018, updated 6 July 2026, sections 11.1 and 11.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "15d" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:15d .

heiro:6000002 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Resources and Services Administration (HRSA) on 340B Drug Pricing Program"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000047 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hrsa.gov/opa"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Resources and Services Administration (HRSA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Ceiling Prices for Covered Entities Defined in Statute" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HRSA states that manufacturers participating in Medicaid agree to provide outpatient drugs to covered entities at significantly reduced prices. Covered entities are defined in statute and include federally qualified health centres, Ryan White HIV/AIDS grant recipients, several types of hospital and specialised clinics; each must register, recertify every year and comply with programme requirements. A revised rebate pilot, announced in August 2026, allows manufacturers whose plans HRSA approves to provide the ceiling price through a rebate instead of an upfront discount for drugs selected for Medicare price negotiation, from 1 January 2027." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HRSA Office of Pharmacy Affairs, 340B Drug Pricing Program and 340B Eligibility web pages (Public Health Service Act section 340B(a)(4), 42 U.S.C. 256b), last reviewed August 2026; HRSA, Notice Regarding 340B Rebate Model Pilot Program, 91 FR 48883, 3 August 2026, section VIII" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "340b-program" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:340b-program .

heiro:6000003 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Abbreviated New Drug Application"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/types-applications/abbreviated-new-drug-application-anda"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Generic Approval Based on Bioequivalence to the Reference Drug" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA states that generic applications are termed abbreviated because they are generally not required to include preclinical or clinical data to establish safety and effectiveness. The applicant must instead show that its product performs in the same manner as the brand-name drug it references, the reference listed drug, delivering the same amount of active ingredient into the bloodstream in the same amount of time. FDA notes that the Hatch-Waxman Amendments of 1984 established bioequivalence as the basis for approving generic copies and created 180-day generic drug exclusivity." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Abbreviated New Drug Application (ANDA), content current as of 20 May 2026; Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act section 505(j) (21 U.S.C. 355(j)); 21 CFR 314.3 (definition of reference listed drug) and 21 CFR Part 320" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "abbreviated-new-drug-application" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:abbreviated-new-drug-application .

heiro:6000004 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Abbreviated New Drug Application"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/human-regulatory-overview/marketing-authorisation/generic-hybrid-medicines/generic-hybrid-applications"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Generic Medicines Relying on an Authorised Reference Medicine" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA states that a generic applicant is not required to provide the results of pre-clinical tests and clinical trials if it can show that the product is a generic of a reference medicinal product authorised in the EU for at least 8 years. The generic must have the same qualitative and quantitative composition in active substance and the same pharmaceutical form as the reference product, with bioequivalence shown by bioavailability studies. An application can be submitted once the 8 years of data exclusivity have expired, but the generic can be placed on the market only after 10 or 11 years." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EMA, Generic and hybrid applications: questions and answers, questions 2.1 (Rev. January 2021) and 2.5, on generic applications under Article 10(1) of Directive 2001/83/EC and Article 6 of Regulation (EC) No 726/2004" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "abbreviated-new-drug-application" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:abbreviated-new-drug-application .

heiro:6000005 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Absenteeism"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Productivity Costs of Absence Excluded From the Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE sets the reference-case perspective on costs as that of the NHS and personal social services, on the grounds that it does not set the NHS budget, and states that productivity costs should not be included. Costs of time lost from work through illness are therefore left out of the base-case cost-effectiveness estimate. They can be presented separately, as additional information for the committee, where they may be a critical component of a technology's value." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.9 and 4.4.23, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absenteeism" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absenteeism .

heiro:6000006 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Absenteeism"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Absence From Paid Work Valued With the Friction Cost Method" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Productivity losses belong in the Dutch reference case, which takes a societal perspective, and paid and unpaid work count equally. For paid work the guideline separates absence from work (absenteeism) from reduced productivity while at work (presenteeism), noting that both may be important, and names the iMTA Productivity Cost Questionnaire as a frequently used instrument. Valuation must use the friction cost method, which assumes that staff absent for an extended period can be replaced, so losses arise mainly during the friction period an employer needs to replace them." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), sections 2.2 and 3.2.1.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absenteeism" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absenteeism .

heiro:6000007 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Absenteeism"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Incapacity to Work Valued at Friction Costs of 80% of Wage Costs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG treats productivity losses from incapacity to work, occupational invalidity and premature death as indirect costs and considers them primarily on the cost side. When the societal perspective is taken, losses from paid labour should as a rule be valued with the friction cost approach, using average labour costs per working day, with friction costs assumed to be 80% of wage costs. The friction period follows the average time needed to fill a position in Germany, and the human capital approach can be examined in sensitivity analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, sections 4.7.1 and 4.7.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absenteeism" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absenteeism .

heiro:6000008 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of TLV on Absenteeism"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000028 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.tlv.se/download/18.467926b615d084471ac3230c/1510316374332/TLVAR_2017_1.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "TLV" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Production Losses Valued With the Human Capital Method" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TLV's general guidelines ask for health economic analyses from a societal perspective, covering the costs of treatment and illness whoever bears them. Production losses are classed as indirect costs and are to be estimated with the human capital method. Each cost calculation should be run twice, once including these indirect costs and once excluding them." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Tandvårds- och läkemedelsförmånsverket (TLV), general guidelines on economic evaluations (TLVAR 2003:2), reprinted as amended by TLVAR 2017:1 (in force 15 February 2017), points 2 and 6" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absenteeism" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absenteeism .

heiro:6000009 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Absolute Risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng197/chapter/Recommendations#discussing-numerical-information"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Absolute Risk and Natural Frequencies in Risk Discussions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's shared decision making guideline tells healthcare professionals to use absolute risk rather than relative risk when discussing options with people. Risks should be given as natural frequencies such as 10 in 100 rather than percentages, with the same denominator when comparing risks and over a defined period of time where relevant. The committee found absolute risks much clearer than relative risks, judged relative risks presented alone to be misleading and said relative risk should only supplement absolute risk." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG197: Shared decision making, recommendations 1.4.7 to 1.4.10 and rationale for recommendations 1.4.1 to 1.4.11, published 17 June 2021" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absolute-risk" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absolute-risk .

heiro:6000010 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Absolute Risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/68555290"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Baseline Risk Modelled Separately From Relative Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NICE Decision Support Unit treats the absolute natural history under the comparator as a separate component of a cost-effectiveness model, to which relative treatment effects from trials are then applied. Baseline response should be as specific as possible to the population of interest, which may favour recent trials, cohort studies or registers, and the choice of sources should be justified. The unit prefers a baseline model built independently of the relative effects model and does not recommend using the unweighted mean of trial baseline arms." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 5: Evidence Synthesis in the Baseline Natural History Model, August 2011, last updated April 2012, Executive Summary and sections 1, 2.1 and 2.2.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absolute-risk" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absolute-risk .

heiro:6000011 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Absolute Risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/169803/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Absolute Frequencies for Treatment and Control Groups in DTC Promotion" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance for industry recommends that direct-to-consumer promotion present efficacy and risk probabilities as absolute frequencies, such as 57 out of 100, or as percentages. Where a study has a control group, the corresponding figure for that group should also be given. If a relative figure such as a 50% reduction in risk is used, the absolute probabilities should appear prominently alongside it, because consumers understand relative figures less easily and may misjudge a drug's benefit or risk." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA Guidance for Industry: Presenting Quantitative Efficacy and Risk Information in Direct-to-Consumer (DTC) Promotional Labeling and Advertisements, Revision 1, December 2023, sections III.A and III.B" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absolute-risk" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absolute-risk .

heiro:6000012 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Absolute Risk Reduction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng197/chapter/Recommendations#discussing-numerical-information"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Changes in Risk Expressed in Absolute Terms" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's shared decision making guideline asks healthcare professionals to describe a change in risk in absolute terms, for example that the risk of an event increases from 1 in 1,000 to 2 in 1,000, rather than saying that the risk doubles. Comparisons should use natural frequencies with the same denominator, such as 7 in 100 against 20 in 100. The committee found absolute risks much clearer and said relative risk should only be introduced as a supplement to absolute risks." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG197: Shared decision making, recommendations 1.4.7 to 1.4.10 and rationale for recommendations 1.4.1 to 1.4.11, published 17 June 2021" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absolute-risk-reduction" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absolute-risk-reduction .

heiro:6000013 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Absolute Shortfall"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Severity Modifier Using Absolute and Proportional QALY Shortfall" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE assesses severity in technology appraisals using both absolute and proportional QALY shortfall, and applies the QALY weight implied by whichever shows greater severity. An absolute shortfall of 12 to 18 QALYs or a proportional shortfall of 0.85 to 0.95 gives a weight of 1.2, and at least 18 QALYs or at least 0.95 gives 1.7; a result on a cut-off takes the higher level. Shortfall is discounted at the reference-case rate. Since August 2026, shortfall should be calculated with EQ-5D-5L utilities and population norms, with the cut-offs unchanged." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.2.12 to 6.2.18 and Table 6.1, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51), section 1.12 and appendix C question 21, published 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absolute-shortfall" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absolute-shortfall .

heiro:6000014 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Absolute Shortfall"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://sheffield.ac.uk/nice-dsu/tsds/severity-shortfall-tsd"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "TSD 23 Method for Calculating Absolute Shortfall" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NICE Decision Support Unit defines absolute shortfall as the QALYs expected for the age- and sex-matched general population minus those expected under current NHS care, counted from the point at which the technology is considered. General-population life expectancy should come from ONS life tables for England, weighted by the sex mix of the patient group, with EQ-5D-5L population norms following NICE's adoption of that value set in August 2026. The 3.5% reference-case discount rate applies, and carer quality of life is excluded." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 23: A guide to calculating severity shortfall for NICE evaluations, January 2024, updated September 2026, executive summary and sections 2.1, 3.1, 3.4, 3.6, 4.1 and 4.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absolute-shortfall" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absolute-shortfall .

heiro:6000015 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Absolute Shortfall"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2018/05/07/ziektelast-in-de-praktijk/Ziektelast+in+de+praktijk_definitief.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Absolute Shortfall Reported Alongside Proportional Shortfall" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland measures disease burden with proportional shortfall, which sets the reference value for the maximum acceptable cost per QALY. Its disease burden method also reports absolute shortfall, the total quality-adjusted future life years lost to the condition, together with the remaining QALYs with the condition. Absolute shortfall is presented as a proxy for the fair innings view, which gives younger patients more priority, so that conflicts with the proportional measure stay visible and can be weighed as separate arguments in the final advice." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Ziektelast in de praktijk: de theorie en praktijk van het berekenen van ziektelast bij pakketbeoordelingen, 7 May 2018, sections 2.2, 2.3 and 3.4 and annex 1 (Stappenplan ziektelastbepaling)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absolute-shortfall" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absolute-shortfall .

heiro:6000016 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Norwegian Medical Products Agency on Absolute Shortfall"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000030 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.dmp.no/globalassets/documents/offentlig-finansiering-og-pris/dokumentasjon-til-metodevurdering/submission-guideline-july-2026.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Norwegian Medical Products Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Severity Quantified as Undiscounted Absolute Shortfall" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Norwegian Medical Products Agency requires severity in health technology assessments to be quantified using absolute shortfall, the expected QALYs lost with current standard treatment compared with the general population of the same age. Absolute shortfall is required in cost-utility analyses but not in cost-minimisation analyses. The calculation uses undiscounted QALYs, starting from the mean age of the relevant Norwegian patient group, with general-population QALYs based on Norwegian mortality data and age-specific utility values." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Norwegian Medical Products Agency, Submission guidelines for single technology assessment of medicinal products, valid from 1 January 2018, updated 6 July 2026, sections 13.1 and 13.2 and appendix A.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "absolute-shortfall" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:absolute-shortfall .

heiro:6000017 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of US Department of Veterans Affairs on Academic Detailing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000048 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.pbm.va.gov/PBM/AcademicDetailingService/Documents/508/IB10-960ADS-DetailerADImplentation_508Ready.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "US Department of Veterans Affairs" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Academic Detailing Required Across the Veterans Health Administration" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The VA Academic Detailing Service defines academic detailing as a service for clinicians, by clinicians, that provides individualised face-to-face outreach using balanced, evidence-based information to improve Veteran health outcomes. A March 2015 memorandum from the Interim Under Secretary for Health required academic detailing programmes throughout the Veterans Health Administration, staffed at 3.0 to 7.0 full-time equivalents per Veterans Integrated Service Network, although no additional funding was provided. The service selected clinical pharmacy specialists as detailers and recommends that programmes carry out impact, outcome and process evaluations." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Department of Veterans Affairs, Pharmacy Benefits Management Academic Detailing Service, VA Academic Detailing Implementation Guide (IB 10-960), December 2016, Part 2 (Overview of Academic Detailing), Part 3 (Developing an Academic Detailing Program) and Part 7 (Evaluations)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "academic-detailing" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:academic-detailing .

heiro:6000018 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Academic Detailing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng15/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Academic Detailing as an Antimicrobial Stewardship Intervention" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE recommends that commissioners and providers consider education-based programmes for health and social care practitioners, for example academic detailing, clinical education or educational outreach, as one of several antimicrobial stewardship interventions. These sit alongside review of prescribing by stewardship teams, promotion of antimicrobials recommended in local or national guidelines, and IT or decision support systems. In its medicines optimisation guideline, NICE separately recommends considering the principles of the PINCER intervention, which include educational outreach with regular reinforcement of educational messages, to reduce medicines-related patient safety incidents." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG15, Antimicrobial stewardship: systems and processes for effective antimicrobial medicine use, published 18 August 2015, recommendation 1.1.10; NICE guideline NG5, Medicines optimisation: the safe and effective use of medicines to enable the best possible outcomes, published 4 March 2015, recommendation 1.1.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "academic-detailing" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:academic-detailing .

heiro:6000019 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Accelerated Approval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://uscode.house.gov/view.xhtml?req=granuleid:USC-prelim-title21-section356&num=0&edition=prelim"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Accelerated Approval on Surrogate Endpoints with Confirmatory Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act, the FDA may approve a product for a serious or life-threatening condition on an effect on a surrogate endpoint that is reasonably likely to predict clinical benefit. It may require postapproval studies to verify the predicted clinical benefit. Since the December 2022 amendments, the FDA may require these studies to be underway before approval and must set their conditions, such as enrolment targets and milestones, by the approval date. It may withdraw approval through expedited procedures if a sponsor lacks due diligence or a study fails to verify benefit." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act section 506(c), 21 U.S.C. 356(c)(1) to (3), as amended by Pub. L. 117-328, division FF, title III, section 3210 (29 December 2022); United States Code, accessed 29 September 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accelerated-approval" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accelerated-approval .

heiro:6000020 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Accelerated Approval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/human-regulatory-overview/marketing-authorisation/conditional-marketing-authorisation"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Conditional Marketing Authorisation on Less Comprehensive Data" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The CHMP may grant a conditional marketing authorisation on less comprehensive clinical data than normally required for medicines for seriously debilitating or life-threatening diseases, including orphan medicines, or for a public health emergency. All criteria must be met: a positive benefit-risk balance, the likelihood that comprehensive data will follow, an unmet medical need, and a benefit of immediate availability that outweighs the risk of data still being required. The authorisation is valid for one year and can be renewed annually, and the holder must fulfil specific obligations, such as completing studies, within defined timelines." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Conditional marketing authorisation (regulatory guidance page), section Criteria and conditions, accessed 29 September 2026; legal basis Article 14-a of Regulation (EC) No 726/2004 and Regulation (EC) No 507/2006" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accelerated-approval" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accelerated-approval .

heiro:6000021 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of MHRA on Accelerated Approval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000026 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/guidance/conditional-marketing-authorisations-exceptional-circumstances-marketing-authorisations-and-national-scientific-advice#guidance-for-uk-conditional-marketing-authorisation-applications"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "MHRA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "UK Conditional Marketing Authorisation, Renewable Annually" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The MHRA offers a national conditional marketing authorisation scheme for products that fulfil an unmet medical need, where the comprehensive clinical data normally required are incomplete but expected soon. The applicant must show a positive benefit-risk balance and the likelihood of providing comprehensive data after authorisation, and that the benefit of immediate availability outweighs the risk of data still being required. Authorisations are valid for one year, are renewable annually on application, and can convert to a standard authorisation once the specific obligations are fulfilled. Since 1 January 2025 the MHRA grants them UK-wide." ;
    heiro:2000018 "MHRA, Conditional marketing authorisations, exceptional circumstances marketing authorisations and national scientific advice (guidance), sections 1 and 2, last updated 11 March 2025; Human Medicines Regulations 2012, regulation 50I" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accelerated-approval" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accelerated-approval .

heiro:6000022 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Accelerated Approval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2#modelling-methods"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Appraising Surrogate Evidence and Managed Access for up to 5 Years" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE treats final clinical end points as more informative than surrogate outcomes in cost-utility analysis, and expects any surrogate relationship to be supported by evidence and quantified. It recognises three levels of evidence, the strongest being randomised trials showing that a technology's effect on the surrogate corresponds to a commensurate effect on the final outcome. Uncertainty in the relationship must be quantified and included in probabilistic sensitivity analysis. Where a medicine is plausibly cost effective but the evidence is too uncertain, a committee can recommend it with managed access while data are collected for up to 5 years." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.6.5 to 4.6.11 and 6.4.6, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accelerated-approval" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accelerated-approval .

heiro:6000023 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Accelerated Failure Time Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD14-Survival-analysis.updated-March-2013.v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Accelerated Failure Time Models Report an Acceleration Factor" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NICE Decision Support Unit classifies the log-logistic and log normal distributions as accelerated failure time models and notes that the Weibull can be parameterised either as a proportional hazards or as an accelerated failure time model, whereas the Gompertz can only be a proportional hazards model. In an accelerated failure time model the treatment effect is an acceleration factor that acts multiplicatively on the time scale. Log-logistic and log normal models do not produce a single hazard ratio, so the proportional hazards assumption does not hold for them, although treatment group can still be included as a covariate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 14: Survival analysis for economic evaluations alongside clinical trials, extrapolation with patient-level data (Latimer), June 2011, last updated March 2013, sections 2.2, 2.3, 2.4 and 2.9" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accelerated-failure-time" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accelerated-failure-time .

heiro:6000024 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of UN Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights on Acceptability of Healthcare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000034 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/g00/439/34/pdf/g0043934.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "UN Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Acceptability as an Element of the Right to Health" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Committee treats acceptability as one of four interrelated elements of the right to health, alongside availability, accessibility and quality. All health facilities, goods and services must be respectful of medical ethics and culturally appropriate, sensitive to gender and life-cycle requirements, and designed to respect confidentiality and improve the health status of those concerned. States must ensure that privatisation of the health sector does not threaten acceptability, and that health services are culturally appropriate, with staff trained to respond to the needs of vulnerable or marginalised groups." ;
    heiro:2000018 "UN Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, General Comment No. 14 (2000): The right to the highest attainable standard of health (article 12), E/C.12/2000/4, 11 August 2000, paragraphs 12(c), 35 and 37" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "acceptability" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:acceptability .

heiro:6000025 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Acceptability of Healthcare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240094765"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Acceptability Coverage as a Domain of Effective Coverage" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's handbook for assessing barriers to effective coverage defines acceptability coverage as the proportion of the population for whom services are acceptable, a domain of the Tanahashi framework that follows availability and accessibility and precedes contact. It adopts the right to health definition and adds that acceptability is shaped by perceptions, expectations, personal beliefs and past experiences with providers. Listed barriers include discriminatory attitudes of health personnel, perceptions of low quality, gender norms, low trust, weak confidentiality and poor fit with cultural beliefs. Effective coverage depends on the acceptability domain first being achieved." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Handbook for conducting assessments of barriers to effective coverage with health services: in support of equity-oriented reforms towards universal health coverage (2024), ISBN 978-92-4-009476-5, Module 1 (Tanahashi framework for effective coverage) and Table 1 (evidence synthesis framework), pages 7 to 12" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "acceptability" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:acceptability .

heiro:6000026 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of UK Medical Research Council on Acceptability of Healthcare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000049 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8482308/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "UK Medical Research Council" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Acceptability as a Question for Complex Intervention Research" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The MRC framework for developing and evaluating complex interventions, commissioned jointly with the National Institute for Health Research, shifts the focus of research from whether an intervention is effective to whether and how it will be acceptable, implementable, cost effective, scalable and transferable across contexts. Feasibility studies should assess predefined progression criteria, which can cover acceptability, adherence and the likelihood of cost effectiveness. Engaging potential intervention users to inform refinements can improve the feasibility and acceptability of an intervention." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Skivington K, et al. A new framework for developing and evaluating complex interventions: update of Medical Research Council guidance. BMJ 2021;374:n2061, published 30 September 2021, sections on research perspectives, intervention refinement and the feasibility phase" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "acceptability" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:acceptability .

heiro:6000027 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Access Barrier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564021"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Direct Payments and Other Barriers to Accessing Health Services" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The World Health Report 2010 identifies reliance on direct payments, including user fees, as by far the greatest obstacle to universal coverage, since people who must pay on the spot are deterred from using services. WHO states that removing these charges helps poorer people obtain care but does not guarantee it. Transport costs and lost income can be even more prohibitive than service charges, and services that are unavailable or far away cannot be used even when free. The report also cites cultural and language barriers, such as settings where women are prevented from travelling alone." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, The World Health Report: Health Systems Financing: the Path to Universal Coverage (2010), Message from the Director-General (page vi); Executive summary, Removing financial risks and barriers to access and Other barriers to accessing health services (pages xiv to xvi); Chapter 3, Other barriers to access (pages 51 to 52)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-barrier" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-barrier .

heiro:6000028 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO and World Bank on Access Barrier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005,
        heiro:5000007 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240117808"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO and World Bank" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Financial Barriers and Forgone Care Missed by Hardship Measures" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The 2025 global monitoring report defines financial protection as the absence of both financial barriers to needed care and financial hardship from out-of-pocket spending. It states that the SDG financial hardship indicator does not capture people kept from care by financial barriers, so the population without financial protection is underestimated. Forgone care is defined as a recognised need left unmet because of barriers at any point in the care pathway. Across 38 low- and middle-income countries, a median of 58.6% of women reported problems accessing health care in 2014 to 2023, down from 67.8%." ;
    heiro:2000018 "WHO and World Bank, Tracking Universal Health Coverage: 2025 Global Monitoring Report (5 December 2025), Glossary of key terms (pages x to xi); Introduction, Fig. 0.1 Financial hardship and financial barriers to accessing health care (page 6); Chapter 2, section 2.3 Inequalities in forgone care and unmet need hamper UHC progress (pages 30 to 31)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-barrier" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-barrier .

heiro:6000029 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development on Access Barrier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000009 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/health-at-a-glance-2025_8f9e3f98-en/full-report/unmet-needs-for-healthcare_c994e8b1.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Unmet Need for Medical Care Due to Cost, Distance or Waiting Times" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Health at a Glance 2025 reports that on average across 28 OECD countries, 3.4% of the population had unmet medical care needs due to cost, distance or waiting times in 2024, up from 2.7% in 2019. Waiting times were the main reason in most countries, cost was an important barrier and distance was cited less often. People in the lowest income quintile were 2.5 times more likely than those in the highest to report unmet needs. The report states that policies need to secure an adequate supply and distribution of health workers and services and address financial barriers to care." ;
    heiro:2000018 "OECD, Health at a Glance 2025: OECD Indicators (13 November 2025), Chapter 5 Access and coverage, Unmet needs for healthcare, including Figures 5.4 and 5.5 and Definition and comparability" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-barrier" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-barrier .

heiro:6000030 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Access Negotiation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/what-nice-does/patient-access-schemes-and-pricing-agreements"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Simple and Complex Patient Access Schemes Reviewed for NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that a company unable to set a cost-effective NHS list price can offer a simple confidential discount through a patient access scheme, the preferred route because it needs less monitoring. Complex schemes, such as outcomes-based agreements, dose caps, rebates or upfront free stock, are non-confidential and are reviewed with an NHS expert panel over at least 12 weeks. Simple discount reviews take at least 4 weeks, or 10 working days on the fast track where requirements are met. NICE advises NHS England, which makes the final decision." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE. Patient access schemes and pricing agreements (web page), sections 'What are patient access schemes?', 'How we assess patient access schemes' and 'The proposal review process', accessed 29 September 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-negotiation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-negotiation .

heiro:6000031 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Access Negotiation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/long-read/nhs-commercial-framework-for-new-medicines/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Simple Discounts First, Commercial Access Agreements at Its Discretion" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS England treats a simple confidential discount through a patient access scheme as the fastest and preferred route, and considers complex arrangements only once simple discounts are shown to be unsuitable. Commercial access agreements are confidential and at NHS England's discretion, using formats such as budget caps, price-volume agreements, outcomes-based rebates and indication-specific pricing. They are normally reserved for value propositions at or below the lower end of the standard NICE threshold range. Where net budget impact exceeds £40 million in any of the first 3 years, NHS England can negotiate to manage affordability." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England. NHS commercial framework for new medicines, paragraphs 15 to 17, 83, 93, 115 to 118 (including table 1) and 134, published 29 January 2025, last updated 31 January 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-negotiation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-negotiation .

heiro:6000032 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Access Negotiation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/files/document/fact-sheet-negotiated-prices-initial-price-applicability-year-2026.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Medicare Negotiated Prices for 10 Part D Drugs From 1 January 2026" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS states that the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022 gave Medicare, for the first time, the ability to negotiate directly the prices of certain high-expenditure, single-source drugs without generic or biosimilar competition. In the first cycle CMS selected 10 Part D drugs, whose negotiated prices, termed maximum fair prices in statute, take effect from 1 January 2026. Each negotiation involved an initial CMS offer, a manufacturer counteroffer and three meetings; five prices were agreed through the meetings and five by acceptance of a written final offer." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. Fact Sheet: Medicare Drug Price Negotiation Program: Negotiated Prices for Initial Price Applicability Year 2026, August 2024, page 1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-negotiation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-negotiation .

heiro:6000033 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Federal Joint Committee (G-BA) on Access Negotiation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000035 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.g-ba.de/english/benefitassessment/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Federal Joint Committee (G-BA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "AMNOG Price Negotiation Follows the G-BA Benefit Assessment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The G-BA states that under AMNOG, in force since 1 January 2011, companies launching a new medicine must submit a dossier on its additional benefit over the appropriate comparator, and the G-BA resolution forms the basis for pricing. The GKV-Spitzenverband and the company then have six months to negotiate a reimbursement price, set as a rebate on the company's original price. Without agreement, an arbitration board sets the price using European price levels. A medicine without additional benefit joins the reference price system or, if that is not possible, cannot exceed the comparator's annual treatment costs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Federal Joint Committee (G-BA). Benefit Assessment of Medicinal Products: the benefit assessment of medicinal products in accordance with SGB V, section 35a (English web page), sections 'The responsibilities of the G-BA following AMNOG' and 'Price negotiation', accessed 29 September 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-negotiation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-negotiation .

heiro:6000034 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Access Policy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564021"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Three Coverage Dimensions and the Retreat from User Fees" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The World Health Report 2010 frames the move towards universal coverage along three dimensions: who is covered, which services are covered and what proportion of the costs is met from pooled funds. A country can extend coverage to more people, include other services or reduce cost sharing and fees, trading these choices off within limited pooled funds. The report names reliance on direct payments, including user fees, as by far the greatest obstacle to progress. It warns that abolishing fees without planning for extra demand and replacement funding can leave staff unpaid and dispensaries empty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, The World Health Report: Health Systems Financing: the Path to Universal Coverage (2010), Message from the Director-General (page vi); Chapter 1, Making the right choices and Fig. 1.2 (page 12); Chapter 3, The retreat from direct payments (pages 45 to 46)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-policy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-policy .

heiro:6000035 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Department of Health and Social Care on Access Policy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000029 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/supplements-to-the-nhs-constitution-for-england/the-handbook-to-the-nhs-constitution-for-england#right-you-have-the-right-to-access-certain-services-commissioned-by-nhs-bodies-within-maximum-waiting-times-or-for-the-nhs-to-take-all-reasonable-steps-to-offer-you-a-range-of-suitable-alternative-providers-if-this-is-not-possible-the-waiting-times-are-described-in-the-handbook-to-the-nhs-constitution"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Department of Health and Social Care" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Maximum 18 Week Wait Backed by an Alternative Provider Duty" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NHS Constitution for England gives patients a right to start consultant-led treatment within a maximum of 18 weeks from referral for non-urgent conditions. Where this is not possible, the commissioning integrated care board or NHS England must take all reasonable steps to offer suitable alternative providers able to treat the patient sooner. The right ceases to apply where the patient chooses to wait longer, delay is in the patient's best clinical interests, active monitoring is clinically appropriate or treatment is no longer necessary. Maternity services and services that are not consultant-led are outside the right." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Department of Health and Social Care, The Handbook to the NHS Constitution for England (updated 24 January 2025), Rights and pledges covering access to health services: right to access services within maximum waiting times, Exceptions and Source of the right; The NHS Constitution for England (updated 17 August 2023), Access to health services; legal basis in Part 9 of the National Health Service Commissioning Board and Clinical Commissioning Groups (Responsibilities and Standing Rules) Regulations 2012 (SI 2012/2996), as amended" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-policy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-policy .

heiro:6000036 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Access to Care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/universal-health-coverage-(uhc)"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Universal Health Coverage as Access Without Financial Hardship" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO defines universal health coverage as meaning that “all people have access to the full range of quality health services they need, when and where they need them, without financial hardship”. The definition spans the full continuum of services, from health promotion and prevention to treatment, rehabilitation and palliative care. Progress is monitored through a service coverage index and the proportion of the population facing financial hardship in health. WHO also reports that national averages can mask inequalities in access to care along economic, educational and geographic lines." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Universal health coverage (UHC) fact sheet, 5 December 2025, Overview" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-to-care" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-to-care .

heiro:6000037 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of UN Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights on Access to Care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000034 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://docs.un.org/en/E/C.12/2000/4"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "UN Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Right to Health: Availability, Accessibility, Acceptability and Quality" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Committee holds that the right to health contains four interrelated elements: availability, accessibility, acceptability and quality, applied according to the conditions in each State party. Accessibility has four overlapping dimensions: non-discrimination, physical accessibility, economic accessibility (affordability) and information accessibility. Payment for health services must follow the principle of equity, so that poorer households are not disproportionately burdened with health expenses compared with richer households. Acceptability requires services that respect medical ethics and are culturally appropriate, while quality requires scientifically and medically appropriate care." ;
    heiro:2000018 "UN Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, General Comment No. 14 (2000): The right to the highest attainable standard of health (article 12 of the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights), E/C.12/2000/4, 11 August 2000, paragraph 12" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-to-care" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-to-care .

heiro:6000038 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Department of Health and Social Care on Access to Care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000029 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-nhs-constitution-for-england/the-nhs-constitution-for-england#access-to-health-services"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Department of Health and Social Care" ;
    heiro:2000016 "NHS Constitution Rights of Access to NHS Services in England" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Constitution states that access to NHS services is based on clinical need, not an individual’s ability to pay, and that services are free of charge except in limited circumstances sanctioned by Parliament. Patients have the right to access NHS services and not to be refused access on unreasonable grounds. They also have the right to care that is appropriate, meets their needs and reflects their preferences, and the right not to be unlawfully discriminated against in the provision of NHS services. Patients may expect the NHS to assess community health requirements and commission services to meet them." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Department of Health and Social Care, The NHS Constitution for England, updated 17 August 2023, Principles that guide the NHS (principle 2) and Patients and the public: your rights and the NHS pledges to you (Access to health services)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "access-to-care" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:access-to-care .

heiro:6000039 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Geographic Accessibility of Healthcare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-B/part-422/subpart-C/section-422.116"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Maximum Time and Distance Standards for Medicare Advantage Networks" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS requires network-based Medicare Advantage plans to meet maximum time and distance standards, which measure the relationship between the approximate locations of beneficiaries and of network providers and facilities, and which vary by county type. For primary care the base standard is 10 minutes and 5 miles in large metro counties, rising to 70 minutes and 60 miles in counties with extreme access considerations. At least 90% of beneficiaries in large metro and metro counties, and 85% elsewhere, must have at least one provider of each specialty type within the standards." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, 42 CFR 422.116 (Network adequacy), paragraphs (a)(3), (c), (d)(1), (d)(2) table 1 and (d)(4) (eCFR, current text)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accessibility" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accessibility .

heiro:6000040 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of U.S. Department of Health and Human Services on Geographic Accessibility of Healthcare"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000024 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-I/subchapter-A/part-5/appendix-Appendix%20A%20to%20Part%205"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Travel Time of 30 Minutes in Primary Care Shortage Area Designation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The federal criteria for designating primary care health professional shortage areas define a rational service area as a county or group of counties whose population centres lie within 30 minutes' travel time of each other, or an area with limited access to neighbouring resources, measured generally by travel time over 30 minutes. As a guide, 30 minutes equals 20 miles on primary roads, 15 miles in mountainous terrain and 25 miles on interstate highways. Designation also requires a high population to physician ratio and nearby providers that are overutilised, excessively distant or inaccessible." ;
    heiro:2000018 "42 CFR Part 5, Appendix A (Criteria for Designation of Areas Having Shortages of Primary Medical Care Professional(s)), Part I, paragraphs A and B.1; 42 CFR 5.2 (eCFR, current text)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accessibility" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accessibility .

heiro:6000041 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Accountable Care Organization"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/medicare/payment/fee-for-service-providers/shared-savings-program-ssp-acos/about"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Shared Savings for an Assigned Medicare Population" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In the Medicare Shared Savings Program, an accountable care organisation agrees to be held accountable for the quality, cost and experience of care of an assigned fee-for-service population, and must have at least 5,000 assigned beneficiaries. An ACO that meets the minimum savings rate and the quality performance standard shares savings from the first dollar. From 2024 the sharing rate is 40% in BASIC Level A, capped at 10% of the updated benchmark, and 75% in the ENHANCED track, capped at 20%; two-sided ACOs must also share losses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Shared Savings Program: About the Program (web page); 42 CFR 425.100(c), 425.110(a), 425.605(d)(1)(i) and 425.610(d) and (e) (eCFR, current text)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accountable-care-organization" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accountable-care-organization .

heiro:6000042 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Healthcare Accreditation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/06/16/2026-12069/medicare-program-strengthening-oversight-of-accrediting-organizations-aos-and-preventing-ao"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Deemed Status Through CMS-Approved Accrediting Organisations" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under Medicare, a provider or supplier accredited through a CMS-approved accrediting organisation's programme is deemed to meet the Medicare conditions, and for some provider types seeking this deemed status is voluntary. Approved organisations must re-survey every accredited provider, unannounced, no later than 36 months after the prior accreditation, and CMS may run validation surveys of accredited providers. A final rule of June 2026, effective 16 June 2027, bars an accrediting organisation from selling fee-based consulting to a provider it accredits before the initial accreditation survey." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Medicare Program; Strengthening Oversight of Accrediting Organizations (AOs) and Preventing AO Conflicts of Interest, and Related Provisions, final rule with comment period, 91 FR 36370 (16 June 2026), sections II.B and III.F; 42 CFR 488.5(a)(4)(i) and 488.9" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accreditation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accreditation .

heiro:6000043 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Healthcare Accreditation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/jcms/c_2044304/en/healthcare-organisations-accreditation-programme-in-france"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Mandatory Hospital Certification Every Four Years" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS describes its certification of hospitals as the regulatory accreditation process that all French public and private hospitals must undergo, in place since 1996 under the Public Health Code. Peer surveyors appointed and trained by HAS assess each hospital every four years against a standard of 12 objectives in three chapters, using patient, pathway and target tracers, system audits and observations. The sixth cycle began in 2025, and decisions are certified with distinction, certified subject to conditions or not certified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, The certification of hospitals for quality of care (web page, updated 18 November 2025); French Public Health Code, Articles L. 6113-3 et seq." ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accreditation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accreditation .

heiro:6000044 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Classification Accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/statistical-guidance-reporting-results-studies-evaluating-diagnostic-tests-guidance-industry-and-fda"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Paired Accuracy Measures Rather Than Overall Agreement Alone" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The FDA's statistical guidance on diagnostic test studies defines diagnostic accuracy as the extent of agreement between a new test and a reference standard, and recommends describing it with paired measures, such as sensitivity and specificity or positive and negative likelihood ratios, or with ROC analysis, together with confidence intervals. It discourages the stand-alone use of overall agreement, the share of all subjects classified alike, because overall agreement can look good when positive or negative agreement is very low. Its worked examples show that agreement changes with condition prevalence." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Statistical Guidance on Reporting Results from Studies Evaluating Diagnostic Tests: Guidance for Industry and FDA Staff (final, issued 13 March 2007), sections 3 and 4 and section 7 (Appendices)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "accuracy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:accuracy .

heiro:6000045 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Acquired Treatment Resistance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-clinical-evaluation-anticancer-medicinal-products-revision-6_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Characterising Secondary Resistance in Anticancer Drug Development" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA's guideline on anticancer medicines asks development programmes to study secondary resistance to treatment alongside primary resistance. Tumour tissue collected at disease progression is important for characterising secondary resistance mechanisms, with circulating tumour DNA or cells as complementary samples. Non-clinical studies should explore resistance mechanisms, particularly for targeted drugs, where clonal selection or new resistance mechanisms may explain tumour escape, and exploratory trials are advised to recruit patients with secondary as well as primary resistance on prior therapy." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Guideline on the clinical evaluation of anticancer medicinal products (EMA/CHMP/205/95 Rev.6, adopted by CHMP 18 November 2023), sections 5.5, 6.2.1 and 6.2.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "acquired-resistance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:acquired-resistance .

heiro:6000046 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Acquisition Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Prices Paid in the NHS, Including Known Price Reductions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case values technologies at prices that reflect as closely as possible those paid in the NHS, using price reductions when one is known to be available across the NHS, from sources such as patient access schemes, commercial access agreements, NHS Supply Chain, eMIT and the drugs tariff. Where the acquisition price varies substantially, costs should still reflect prices actually paid. The list price may be used when no NHS-wide reduction or nationally agreed price exists, and VAT is excluded from economic evaluations but included in budget impact." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.4.4, 4.4.8 and 4.4.19, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "acquisition-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:acquisition-cost .

heiro:6000047 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Acquisition Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Acquisition Costs Set by Product Type, Administration and Price" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's reference case sets intervention acquisition costs by three factors: whether the product is generic, biosimilar or branded, whether a provider must administer it, and whether its price is known. Self-administered generics are costed at the median wholesale acquisition cost (WAC), with RED BOOK the primary source, and provider-administered drugs at the average sales price, with the mark-up held as a separate input. Branded drugs use an estimated net price in the base case, and the WAC or ASP is used to calculate the discount needed to reach each health benefit price benchmark." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale (current as of 23 October 2025), Model Parameters and Data Inputs: Resource Use and Costs (Intervention acquisition costs)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "acquisition-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:acquisition-cost .

heiro:6000048 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Acquisition Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Public List Price at the Lowest Realistic Cost Pack" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's guidelines state that technology costs should reflect the cost of the technology to the HSE. In general the public list price paid for a drug is used in the reference case, taken for community drug schemes from the Primary Care Reimbursement Service files, with the product, formulation and pack size giving the lowest cost that is realistic in practice. Mandatory manufacturer rebates should be reflected, while further discounts count only if consistently available throughout the HSE and guaranteed for the period specified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), section 2.11.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "acquisition-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:acquisition-cost .

heiro:6000049 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Active Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/the-scope-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Comparators Drawn From Established NHS Practice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's scope identifies all potentially relevant comparators that are established practice in the NHS, which may include branded and generic medicines, biosimilars and unlicensed technologies in established clinical use, while technologies recommended with managed access are not suitable comparators. In selecting the most appropriate comparators, the committee considers established NHS practice, the natural history of the condition without suitable treatment, existing NICE guidance, cost effectiveness and regulatory status. A cost-ineffective practice may remain a comparator if its use is embedded in clinical practice." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 2.2.12, 2.2.13, 2.2.15 and 6.2.2 to 6.2.4, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "active-comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:active-comparator .

heiro:6000050 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Active Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/7be11d76-9a78-426c-8e32-79d30a115a64_en?filename=hta_jca_scoping-process_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Member States Define and Specify Comparators in the PICO Scoping" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's scoping guidance for EU joint clinical assessments defines a comparator as the treatment or treatments against which the technology under assessment should be compared, with each Member State defining comparators for the patient populations it requests. Comparators may be licensed or, depending on national context, unlicensed, and can include any intervention, best supportive care or watchful waiting. Naming standard of care alone is not sufficient: its components must be specified, and Member States should clarify whether background therapy applies in both groups." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment, Guidance on the scoping process, version 1.0 (13 November 2024, adopted 28 November 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282), section 3.1.4 (Comparators) and figure 3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "active-comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:active-comparator .

heiro:6000051 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Active Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Routine Care as the Reference-Case Comparator" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's guidelines prefer routine care as the reference-case comparator, meaning the technology or technologies most widely used in clinical practice in Ireland for the target population, and in practice limit comparators to the recommended standard of care and those in routine use. Comparators must be identified and justified, because the choice critically determines relative cost effectiveness. Unlicensed technologies may be considered if they are part of established clinical practice, and in the absence of an active comparator a comparator of no intervention is appropriate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), section 2.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "active-comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:active-comparator .

heiro:6000052 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Active Control Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/ich-e-10-choice-control-group-clinical-trials-step-5_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Assay Sensitivity and Margin Setting in Active Control Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICH E10, adopted by EMA, describes an active control trial as one in which subjects are randomly assigned to the test treatment or to an active control treatment. A non-inferiority or equivalence trial needs assay sensitivity, deduced from historical evidence that similarly designed trials regularly distinguished effective treatments from less effective or ineffective ones, and from appropriate trial conduct. Without that historical evidence, showing efficacy through non-inferiority should not be attempted, and the margin cannot exceed the smallest effect the active drug would reliably be expected to have against placebo." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, ICH E10 Choice of control group in clinical trials, Step 5 (CPMP/ICH/364/96, legal effective date 1 January 2001), sections 1.3.4, 1.5 and 1.5.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "active-control-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:active-control-trial .

heiro:6000053 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Active Control Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/non-inferiority-clinical-trials"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Non-Inferiority Margins M1 and M2 Based on the Active Control Effect" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The FDA's guidance notes that the effect of the active control cannot be measured in a non-inferiority trial, because there is no concurrent placebo group, so its entire effect (M1) must be estimated from past performance, preferably in placebo-controlled trials. Ruling out a loss of M1 shows only that the test drug has some effect, so the margin is usually a smaller M2, the largest clinically acceptable degree of inferiority, set by clinical judgement. A larger M2 may be justified if the test drug has an important advantage, such as on safety." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Non-Inferiority Clinical Trials to Establish Effectiveness: Guidance for Industry (final, November 2016), sections III.A and III.C" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "active-control-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:active-control-trial .

heiro:6000054 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Activities of Daily Living"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-C/part-441/subpart-K/section-441.505"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ADL and IADL Assistance Under Medicaid Community First Choice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS regulations for the Medicaid Community First Choice option define activities of daily living as basic personal everyday activities such as eating, toileting, grooming, dressing, bathing and transferring, and separately define instrumental activities of daily living, such as meal preparation, managing finances and shopping. A State that elects the option must provide hands-on assistance, supervision or cueing with these activities and help people acquire the skills to accomplish them. Eligibility requires an annual determination that, without these services, the person would otherwise need an institutional level of care, such as a nursing facility." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Code of Federal Regulations, 42 CFR Part 441, Subpart K (Home and Community-Based Attendant Services and Supports State Plan Option (Community First Choice)), sections 441.505, 441.510 and 441.520 (eCFR, up to date as of 1 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "activities-of-daily-living" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:activities-of-daily-living .

heiro:6000055 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Internal Revenue Service (IRS) on Activities of Daily Living"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000051 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.irs.gov/publications/p502"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Internal Revenue Service (IRS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Two of Six ADLs for 90 Days Defines a Chronically Ill Individual" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For the US medical expense deduction and qualified long-term care insurance, IRS guidance treats a person as chronically ill if a licensed health care practitioner has certified, within the previous 12 months, that the person cannot perform at least two activities of daily living without substantial assistance for at least 90 days because of a loss of functional capacity, or needs substantial supervision because of severe cognitive impairment. The six activities are eating, toileting, transferring, bathing, dressing and continence. Qualified long-term care services must be required by a chronically ill individual and provided under a practitioner's plan of care." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Internal Revenue Service, Publication 502 (2025), Medical and Dental Expenses, section Long-Term Care (Qualified Long-Term Care Services; Chronically ill individual)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "activities-of-daily-living" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:activities-of-daily-living .

heiro:6000056 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Australian Government on Activity-Based Funding"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000052 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://federalfinancialrelations.gov.au/sites/federalfinancialrelations.gov.au/files/2026-04/nhra-2026-31-addendum-consolidated.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Australian Government" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Activity Based Funding in the National Health Reform Agreement 2026 to 2031" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The 2026 to 2031 Addendum to Australia's National Health Reform Agreement defines activity based funding as funding public hospital and health services for individual patients through national classifications, cost weights and national efficient prices developed by the Independent Health and Aged Care Pricing Authority (IHACPA). For each ABF service category, the Commonwealth pays the volume of weighted services in National Weighted Activity Units multiplied by the national efficient price and an input contribution rate whose minimum rises from 40.5% in 2026-27 to 42.5% in 2030-31. The national efficient price also gives States a price signal for access, efficiency and quality." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Addendum to the National Health Reform Agreement 2026-2031 (compilation, Federal Financial Relations), clause 15, Schedule A clauses A8, A20 to A22, A36 and A58, and Appendix A (Definitions)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "activity-based-funding" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:activity-based-funding .

heiro:6000057 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Activity Limitation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/classification/icf/icfbeginnersguide.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Activity Limitations as the Person-Level Component of the ICF" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health (ICF), endorsed by all 191 WHO Member States at the World Health Assembly in May 2001, defines activity limitations as difficulties an individual may have in executing activities, an activity being the execution of a task or action. Disability is an umbrella term for impairments, activity limitations and participation restrictions, which sit at the levels of the body, the whole person and the person in a social context. The ICF distinguishes capacity, what a person can do in a standard environment, from performance in the person's usual environment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Towards a Common Language for Functioning, Disability and Health: ICF (WHO/EIP/GPE/CAS/01.3, 2002), sections Introduction and Concepts of functioning and disability; WHO International Classification of Functioning, Disability and Health (ICF) web page (resolution WHA54.21)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "activity-limitation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:activity-limitation .

heiro:6000058 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Actuarial Standards Board on Actuarial Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000036 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.actuarialstandardsboard.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/04/asop005_186.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Actuarial Standards Board" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ASOP No. 5 on Estimating Incurred Health Claims and Unpaid Claims" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Actuarial Standards Board defines the unpaid claims liability as the unpaid portion of incurred claims, including unreported claims and claims reported but not yet paid. Its standard describes the development, or lag, method, which groups past claims by the periods in which they were incurred and paid to build a payment pattern, as appropriate and widely used for short-term benefits. Projection methods may supplement it for the most recent months, and the actuary should consider plan provisions, economic influences, claimant behaviour, seasonality and credibility." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Actuarial Standards Board, Actuarial Standard of Practice No. 5: Incurred Health and Disability Claims, revised edition, Doc. No. 186, March 2017, effective 1 September 2017, sections 2.5, 2.17, 3.2, 3.4.1 and 3.4.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "actuarial-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:actuarial-analysis .

heiro:6000059 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Financial Reporting Council on Actuarial Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000053 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.frc.org.uk/documents/4287/TAS_100_General_Actuarial_Standards_Version_2.0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Financial Reporting Council" ;
    heiro:2000016 "TAS 100 Principles for Technical Actuarial Work in the UK" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Financial Reporting Council's Technical Actuarial Standard 100, version 2.0, applies to technical actuarial work completed on or after 1 July 2023 and must be applied by all members of the Institute and Faculty of Actuaries carrying out such work within its geographic scope. Its mandatory principles require practitioners to allow for material risks, justify material judgements, check data for accuracy and bias, use appropriate and mutually consistent assumptions, and use models that are fit for purpose and tested. Material actuarial information must include a statement confirming compliance." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Financial Reporting Council, Technical Actuarial Standard 100: General Actuarial Standards, version 2.0, March 2023, effective 1 July 2023, sections 1.1 and 1.6 to 1.8 and Principles 1 to 5" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "actuarial-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:actuarial-analysis .

heiro:6000060 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Actuarial Projection"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/data-research/statistics-trends-and-reports/national-health-expenditure-data/projected"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Ten-Year National Health Expenditure Projections Under Current Law" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The CMS Office of the Actuary produces annual projections of US national health expenditure by source of funds, type of service and sponsor, with the latest running from 2025 to 2034. Its methodology rests on accepted econometric and actuarial projection techniques within a current-law framework that assumes no legislative change, with demographic inputs by age and sex taken from the Social Security Trustees' projections. CMS projects average spending growth of 5.4% a year against GDP growth of 4.1%, raising health's share of GDP from 18.0% in 2024 to 20.6% in 2034, and states that uncertainty increases with the projection horizon." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Office of the Actuary, Projections of National Health Expenditures and Health Insurance Enrollment: Methodology and Model Specification (last updated 24 June 2026), Introduction and section 1b; National Health Expenditure Projections 2025-2034: Forecast Summary" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "actuarial-projection" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:actuarial-projection .

heiro:6000061 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Medicare Boards of Trustees on Actuarial Projection"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000054 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/oact/tr/2026"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Medicare Boards of Trustees" ;
    heiro:2000016 "75-Year Actuarial Estimates for the Medicare Trust Funds" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Boards of Trustees of the Medicare trust funds make actuarial estimates for a 75-year period, 2026 to 2100 in the 2026 report, under intermediate assumptions, with low-cost and high-cost alternatives for Hospital Insurance, and show an illustrative alternative alongside the current-law projections. The 2026 report projects that the Hospital Insurance trust fund will be depleted in the second quarter of 2033, after which income would cover 89% of scheduled benefits, and puts the 75-year actuarial deficit at 0.56% of taxable payroll. The Trustees conclude that Medicare faces a substantial financial shortfall that needs further legislation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "2026 Annual Report of the Boards of Trustees of the Federal Hospital Insurance and Federal Supplementary Medical Insurance Trust Funds (June 2026), sections I (Introduction) and II (Overview), including table II.A1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "actuarial-projection" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:actuarial-projection .

heiro:6000062 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Office for Budget Responsibility (OBR) on Actuarial Projection"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000055 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4cb4c74889d85e75ab40e0/E03599617_OBR_FRS_2026_Accessible.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Office for Budget Responsibility (OBR)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "50-Year UK Health Spending Projections in the Fiscal Risks Report" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In its July 2026 Fiscal risks and sustainability report, the OBR projects UK health spending in its baseline scenario to rise from 8% of GDP in 2030-31 to 13% by 2075-76, real growth of 2.5% a year, drawing on the ONS 2024-based population projections. Demographic factors explain 0.4 percentage points of annual real growth, income effects 1.2 points (an income elasticity of 0.8) and other cost pressures 1 point. Without the other cost pressures health spending would reach around 9% of GDP, and healthy life expectancy scenarios give 12% and 15%." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Office for Budget Responsibility, Fiscal risks and sustainability (July 2026), chapter 2 paragraph 2.6 and chapter 3 (Long-term spending projections) paragraph 3.15" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "actuarial-projection" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:actuarial-projection .

heiro:6000063 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of U.S. Department of Health and Human Services on Actuarial Value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000024 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-B/part-156/subpart-B/section-156.140"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Bronze to Platinum Tiers at 60%, 70%, 80% and 90% Actuarial Value" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Federal regulations assign a health plan to the bronze, silver, gold or platinum level of coverage when its actuarial value is 60%, 70%, 80% or 90%, within a de minimis variation. Issuers must calculate actuarial value with the AV Calculator that HHS issues for each benefit year and updates annually. Where a plan design does not fit the calculator, an actuary who is a member of the American Academy of Actuaries must certify the fit or the adjustments. Silver plan variations with cost-sharing reductions must reach 73%, 87% or 94% for the three groups eligible for those reductions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Code of Federal Regulations, title 45, part 156, sections 156.135, 156.140 and 156.420, eCFR text up to date as of 1 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "actuarial-value" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:actuarial-value .

heiro:6000064 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Actuarial Value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/files/document/final-2027-av-calculator-methodology.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "AV Calculator Uses a Standard Population and Tier-Specific Spending" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS builds the 2027 AV Calculator from masked person-level claims data for 2021 to 2023 from issuers of risk adjustment covered plans, projected to plan year 2027. Because spending is affected by plan design through induced demand, it uses four sets of continuance tables based on bronze, silver, gold and platinum designs. CMS describes the calculator as a comparison tool that was not developed for pricing. With wider ranges from the 2025 rule stayed by a federal court, plan year 2027 de minimis ranges stay at plus or minus 2 percentage points for the four tiers." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Center for Consumer Information and Insurance Oversight, Final 2027 Actuarial Value (AV) Calculator Methodology, 25 February 2026, Introduction, Part I (Regulatory Background; Claims Data) and Part II" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "actuarial-value" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:actuarial-value .

heiro:6000065 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Actuarial Standards Board on Actuarial Value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000036 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.actuarialstandardsboard.org/wp-content/uploads/2015/10/asop050_182.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Actuarial Standards Board" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ASOP No. 50 on Determining Actuarial Value Under the ACA" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Actuarial Standards Board defines actuarial value as the proportion of total allowed medical costs for a specified population that a health insurance plan is contractually obliged to pay. Its standard tells actuaries to use the HHS calculator when placing plans in metal levels and, for plan designs the calculator cannot accommodate, to keep data, methods and assumptions consistent with those underlying it. Unreasonable calculator results that regulators still require should be documented. Actuarial values calculated for premium setting fall outside the standard." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Actuarial Standards Board, Actuarial Standard of Practice No. 50: Determining Minimum Value and Actuarial Value under the Affordable Care Act, September 2015, effective 31 January 2016, sections 1.2, 2.1 and 3.1 to 3.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "actuarial-value" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:actuarial-value .

heiro:6000066 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Evidence Adaptation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Documenting and Adjusting Evidence From Outside Ireland" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's 2025 guidelines require any use of data not derived from the Irish population to be documented and its relevance to the Irish healthcare system established, with assumptions stated and uncertainty explored in sensitivity analysis. Transferring clinical data between countries is usually accepted as reasonable if population criteria are clearly described and key parameters tested, but economic data are generally not considered transferable because prices and resource use differ. Where costs from other countries are used, the transfer assumptions must be reported and costs converted to Irish euro equivalents using purchasing power parity indices." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), sections 2.11.3 and 2.18" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adaptation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adaptation .

heiro:6000067 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Evidence Adaptation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Belgian Prices Preferred Over Converted Foreign Costs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "KCE's Belgian guidelines state that resource use should be valued at Belgian prices rather than foreign prices converted to euros, and simple currency conversion of foreign values is not recommended except where the item is minor or no reliable Belgian data exist. Foreign costs should be inflated with the source country's consumer price index and then converted using purchasing power parity indices. Studies may not be excluded from the literature review merely for not being Belgian, and context-specific studies are preferred to transferring results from other countries." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (first edition 8 May 2025, second edition 18 June 2025), sections 5.2, 5.9.3.1 and 5.15, and appendix 8.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adaptation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adaptation .

heiro:6000068 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Adaptive Trial Design"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/adaptive-design-clinical-trials-drugs-and-biologics-guidance-industry"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Prospectively Planned Adaptations With Controlled Error Rates" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's final guidance defines an adaptive design as a clinical trial design allowing prospectively planned modifications to one or more aspects of the design based on accumulating data from trial subjects. Trials intended to provide substantial evidence of effectiveness should adequately control the chance of erroneous conclusions, estimate treatment effects reliably, prespecify the design completely and maintain trial integrity. Adequately prespecified adaptations based on non-comparative data have no or limited effect on the Type I error probability, while conventional end-of-trial estimates that ignore the adaptations tend to overestimate the treatment effect." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Adaptive Designs for Clinical Trials of Drugs and Biologics: Guidance for Industry (final, November 2019; docket FDA-2018-D-3124), sections II.A, III, III.A, III.B and IV" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adaptive-trial-design" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adaptive-trial-design .

heiro:6000069 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Adaptive Trial Design"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/scientific-guidelines/methodological-issues-confirmatory-clinical-trials-planned-adaptive-design"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adaptations to Confirmatory Trials Must Be Planned and Justified" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA's reflection paper, still its current effective version, says design modifications in a phase III trial contradict its confirmatory nature and will rarely be acceptable without further justification. The interim analysis and the anticipated type of modification must be described and justified in the protocol, and adaptations introduced without proper planning make the trial exploratory. An adaptive design must control the pre-specified type I error, give correct estimates and confidence intervals for the treatment effect, and pre-plan methods to assess homogeneity across stages. ICH E20 on adaptive designs remains a Step 2b draft at EMA." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use, Reflection paper on methodological issues in confirmatory clinical trials planned with an adaptive design (CHMP/EWP/2459/02, adopted 18 October 2007), executive summary" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adaptive-trial-design" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adaptive-trial-design .

heiro:6000070 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Added Therapeutic Value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2021-03/doctrine_ct.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ASMR Rates Added Clinical Value From Major to None Against a Comparator" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HAS Transparency Committee doctrine, adopted in February 2023, defines the improvement in actual clinical benefit (ASMR) as the medical value a medicine adds to existing care, rated major (I), important (II), moderate (III), minor (IV) or none (V). As a relative judgement, it needs a comparison with a clinically relevant comparator at the same point in the treatment pathway, and a double-blind randomised head-to-head trial is expected where possible. ASMR V covers progress not shown, such as a non-inferiority design, and uncertain added value. The ASMR frames the price negotiation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Doctrine de la commission de la transparence (CT): principes d'évaluation de la CT relatifs aux médicaments en vue de leur accès au remboursement (adopted by the CT on 15 February 2023), sections 3.1, 3.1.1 and 3.2; HAS, Comprendre l'évaluation des médicaments (updated 1 September 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "added-therapeutic-value" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:added-therapeutic-value .

heiro:6000071 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Federal Joint Committee (G-BA) on Added Therapeutic Value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000035 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.g-ba.de/english/benefitassessment/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Federal Joint Committee (G-BA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "AMNOG Assessment of Additional Benefit Over the Appropriate Comparator" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the AMNOG reform, in force since January 2011, the G-BA assesses within six months of marketing authorisation whether a new medicine has an additional benefit over the appropriate comparator that the G-BA specifies, and the company's dossier must prove that benefit. The G-BA may delegate the assessment to IQWiG, and its resolution states the extent of additional benefit, which forms the basis of the reimbursement price. A medicine without additional benefit enters the reference price system or, if no group fits, may not exceed the comparator's annual treatment costs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Federal Joint Committee (G-BA), Benefit Assessment of Medicinal Products: the benefit assessment of medicinal products in accordance with the German Social Code, Book Five (SGB V), section 35a (English web page, accessed 3 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "added-therapeutic-value" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:added-therapeutic-value .

heiro:6000072 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Added Therapeutic Value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Added Benefit Graded by Certainty and Extent in Six Categories" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In assessing a company dossier under section 35a of the German Social Code Book V, IQWiG first classifies the probability of an effect on each patient-relevant outcome as proof, indication or hint, then grades the extent of each effect as major, considerable, minor or non-quantifiable. The overall conclusion uses six categories: major, considerable, minor or non-quantifiable added benefit, no added benefit proven, or less benefit than the appropriate comparator therapy. Confidence interval thresholds for extent depend on the outcome, so smaller effects qualify when the outcome is more serious, such as all-cause mortality." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 3.1.4 and 3.3.3 and Appendix A" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "added-therapeutic-value" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:added-therapeutic-value .

heiro:6000073 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Added Therapeutic Value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/7be11d76-9a78-426c-8e32-79d30a115a64_en?filename=hta_jca_scoping-process_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Joint Clinical Assessments Leave the Added Value Verdict to Member States" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the EU HTA Regulation, joint clinical assessment reports must not contain any value judgement or conclusions on the overall clinical added value of a technology. They are limited to the relative effects on the health outcomes in the assessment scope and the certainty of those effects. Member States must give due consideration to the reports but keep the competence to draw their own conclusions on overall clinical added value for their healthcare system. The HTA Coordination Group's scoping guidance adds that the listing of outcomes should be free of value judgement or ranking." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Regulation (EU) 2021/2282 of 15 December 2021 on health technology assessment, recital 28 and Articles 9(1) and 13(1)(a); HTA Coordination Group, Guidance on the scoping process, V1.0 of 13 November 2024 (adopted 28 November 2024), section 3.1.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "added-therapeutic-value" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:added-therapeutic-value .

heiro:6000074 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Addiction Treatment Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Crime Savings From Drug Misuse Treatment Reported Outside the Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's manual uses treatments to reduce drug misuse as its example of technologies with substantial benefits to other government bodies, such as reduced crime. Such benefits are counted only where agreed with the Department of Health and Social Care and set out in the remit, and are presented separately from the NHS and personal social services reference case. In the methadone and buprenorphine appraisal, reference-case ICERs against no treatment were £13,700 and £26,400 per QALY, and adding criminal justice and victim costs made methadone dominant. In the nalmefene appraisal, the wider perspective in the remit left nalmefene dominant." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section 4.2.10, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE technology appraisal guidance TA114, Methadone and buprenorphine for the management of opioid dependence (24 January 2007), sections 1.1 and 4.2.15 to 4.2.16; NICE technology appraisal guidance TA325, Nalmefene for reducing alcohol consumption in people with alcohol dependence (26 November 2014), section 4.12" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "addiction-treatment-economics" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:addiction-treatment-economics .

heiro:6000075 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO and United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) on Addiction Treatment Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005,
        heiro:5000008 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/331635"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO and United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Resource Allocation Guided by the Cost-Effectiveness of Treatment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The WHO and UNODC International Standards for the Treatment of Drug Use Disorders require resource allocation for treatment to be guided by evidence on the effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of treatment interventions. From a public budget perspective, the Standards call evidence-based treatment a smart investment because treating drug use disorders costs much less than untreated dependence, citing a NIDA estimate that savings can exceed investment by 12 to 1 through lower crime, justice, law enforcement and healthcare costs. Systems built on the service delivery pyramid, with most care in outpatient and community settings, are described as more cost-effective." ;
    heiro:2000018 "United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime and World Health Organization, International standards for the treatment of drug use disorders: revised edition incorporating results of field-testing (2020, ISBN 978-92-4-000219-7), chapter 2 (Principle 4, standard 4.1) and chapter 3, section 3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "addiction-treatment-economics" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:addiction-treatment-economics .

heiro:6000076 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Additive Interaction Scale"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/1f6b8a70-5ce0-404e-9066-120dc9a8df75_en?filename=hta_practical-guideline_direct-and-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Effect Modification Depends on the Scale of the Treatment Effect" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's guidelines for EU joint clinical assessments state that whether a variable is an effect modifier, and the size and direction of its effect, is specific to the scale on which the treatment effect is measured. In its example, a vaccine cuts risk from 10% to 5% in women and from 6% to 3% in men, so sex modifies the effect on the risk difference scale but not on the relative risk scale. Syntheses must pool relative treatment effects from each trial, and any analysis across levels of an effect modifier must be described and justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Coordination Group, Practical Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, sections 3, 3.2.1 and 4.1; Methodological Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted 8 March 2024, section 6.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "additive-scale" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:additive-scale .

heiro:6000077 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Additive Interaction Scale"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/scientific-guidelines/investigation-subgroups-confirmatory-clinical-trials"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Statistical Interactions Are Scale and Model Dependent" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA's guideline on subgroups in confirmatory trials states that statistical interactions are scale and model dependent: interactions in linear models are departures from additivity, meaning differences in treatment effects on an absolute scale, while those in logistic or Cox models are departures from a multiplicative model. A similar relative effect across subgroups can still give a larger absolute effect in severe disease that offsets the risks and a smaller one in mild disease that does not. Interactions should be explored first on the usual scale for the endpoint, with supplementary analyses on the complementary scale where inconsistency appears." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use, Guideline on the investigation of subgroups in confirmatory clinical trials (EMA/CHMP/539146/2013, adopted 31 January 2019, in effect from 1 August 2019), section 4.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "additive-scale" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:additive-scale .

heiro:6000078 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Additive Interaction Scale"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Subgroup Analyses Must State the Scale of Effect Modification" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's manual asks for subgroup analyses that specify the choice of scale on which any effect modification is defined. It notes that the overall net treatment effect depends on baseline risk and on the relative effects of the technology, so subgroups may differ in one or more contributors to absolute treatment effects, and relative effects from randomised trials may be applied to baseline risk data for the subgroups of interest. Standard trial subgroup analyses test for differences in relative treatment effects through interactions, and post hoc data dredging for subgroup effects is viewed sceptically." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.6.16 and 4.9.4 to 4.9.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "additive-scale" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:additive-scale .

heiro:6000079 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Adherence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42682"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adherence Defined as Following Agreed Recommendations" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's 2003 report on adherence to long-term therapies defines adherence as the extent to which a person's behaviour in taking medication, following a diet or making lifestyle changes corresponds with agreed recommendations from a health care provider, and separates it from compliance because it requires the patient's agreement. The report puts average adherence to long-term therapy in developed countries at 50%. It treats adherence as an important modifier of health system effectiveness: population health outcomes predicted by efficacy data will not be achieved unless adherence rates inform planning and evaluation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Adherence to long-term therapies: evidence for action (2003, ISBN 92 4 154599 2), Take-home messages, chapter I (Defining adherence) and chapter IV (Lessons learned)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adherence" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adherence .

heiro:6000080 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Adherence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg76/chapter/Introduction"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adherence Presumes Agreement Between Prescriber and Patient" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's guideline on medicines adherence defines adherence as the extent to which the patient's action matches the agreed recommendations, and says non-adherence should not be seen as the patient's problem. It cites estimates that a third to a half of medicines prescribed for long-term conditions are not taken as recommended, with costs that include wasted medicines and extra demand for healthcare. Professionals should routinely assess adherence without judgement, may use prescription re-ordering records and returned medicines to identify non-adherence, and should tailor support because no single intervention suits all patients." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence, Medicines adherence: involving patients in decisions about prescribed medicines and supporting adherence (CG76), published 28 January 2009, last updated September 2019, Introduction and recommendations 1.2.1 to 1.2.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adherence" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adherence .

heiro:6000081 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Adherence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Real-World Adherence and Medicine Wastage in Economic Evaluations" ;
    heiro:2000017 "KCE's Belgian guidelines ask analysts to consider whether adherence, compliance or persistence matter for the technology and differ between trials and the real world, since this can substantially change effectiveness and cost-effectiveness. The impact of poor adherence should be quantified from appropriate evidence and explored in sensitivity analyses, and the cost of medicine wastage, including wastage from non-compliance, belongs in the reference case. Adjusting only incremental costs for compliance is incorrect: the reference case uses trial evidence, with the effect of non-compliance on both incremental costs and effects shown in a scenario analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (first edition 8 May 2025, second edition 18 June 2025), sections 4.2.4 and 5.9.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adherence" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adherence .

heiro:6000082 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on ADME Studies"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/158178/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Mass Balance Study Expected for Every New Molecular Entity" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance calls a human radiolabelled mass balance study the most direct way to obtain quantitative information on a drug's absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, and says one should generally be conducted for all new molecular entities, with justification when it is not. Exceptions include biologics with known elimination pathways and drugs with 90% or more of the dose recovered unchanged in urine. The study should be run early, at the latest before late-phase trials. Total recovery should preferably exceed 90% of the dose, and metabolites above 10% of drug-related exposure should be structurally characterised." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, Clinical Pharmacology Considerations for Human Radiolabeled Mass Balance Studies: Guidance for Industry (final, July 2024), sections II, III, IV, V.G and V.I" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adme-studies" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adme-studies .

heiro:6000083 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on ADME Studies"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/scientific-guidelines/ich-m12-drug-interaction-studies"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ICH M12 in the EU, With Mass Balance Results Due Before Phase 3" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In the EU, ICH M12 on drug interaction studies came into effect on 30 November 2024, superseding most of EMA's own interaction guideline. M12 calls a well-performed clinical mass balance study the keystone for identifying a drug's principal elimination routes, recommends that its results be available before phase 3, and uses ADME properties to decide whether transporter data are needed. EMA keeps the mass balance appendix of its earlier guideline in force, which asks that recovery of radioactivity preferably exceed 90% of the dose and that metabolites above 10% of drug-related exposure be characterised." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, ICH M12 Guideline on drug interaction studies, Step 5 (EMA/CHMP/ICH/652460/2022; final CHMP adoption 30 May 2024, in effect 30 November 2024), section 1.4; Implementation strategy of ICH Guideline M12 on drug interaction studies (EMA/460496/2024, February 2025), Table 1; Guideline on the investigation of drug interactions, Revision 1 (CPMP/EWP/560/95/Rev. 1 Corr. 2**), Appendix V" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adme-studies" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adme-studies .

heiro:6000084 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Treatment Administration Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Administration Costs Included and Costed With the Right HRG Code" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's manual asks for all relevant costs, including treatment, monitoring, staffing and facilities, and calls Healthcare Resource Groups a valuable source, while warning that they may not suit technologies sharing an HRG with the comparator, when micro-costing may be more appropriate. It gives an administration cost as an example of a direct, intrinsic consequence of a technology. In the amivantamab appraisal, the committee rejected a community nursing code for subcutaneous dosing as unsuitable for an outpatient procedure and concluded that the SB12Z code should be used." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.4.9, 4.4.10 and 4.4.16, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE technology appraisal guidance TA1122, Amivantamab with lazertinib for untreated EGFR mutation-positive advanced non-small-cell lung cancer (21 January 2026), section 3.22" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administration-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administration-cost .

heiro:6000085 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Treatment Administration Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Drug Administration Costs Itemised and Included" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's national guidelines for economic evaluation in Ireland ask that drug administration costs be itemised and included where appropriate, together with the cost of drug wastage, for example from injection vials or patient non-compliance, and the cost of therapeutic drug monitoring. The reference-case drug cost should reflect the product, formulation and pack size with the lowest cost, provided this is a realistic choice in clinical practice. Resource use should be reported in physical units, and Irish cost data should be used where possible." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), sections 2.11.2 and 2.11.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administration-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administration-cost .

heiro:6000086 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Administrative Barrier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/06/03/2026-11094/medicaid-program-community-engagement-requirement-for-certain-individuals"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Ex Parte Checks and Faster Prior Authorisation to Limit Paperwork Losses" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS's interim final rule on the Medicaid community engagement requirement makes states first try to verify compliance ex parte from reliable electronic data, and notes that screening questions can reduce administrative barriers. Its impact analysis assumes that 7% of applicable people who meet the requirement would still lose coverage for administrative or procedural reasons, such as unanswered verification requests. Under its 2024 prior authorisation rule, Medicare Advantage, Medicaid and CHIP payers must decide expedited requests within 72 hours and standard requests within seven calendar days, generally from January 2026, and give a specific reason for each denial." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Medicaid Program; Community Engagement Requirement for Certain Individuals, interim final rule (CMS-2454-IFC), 91 FR 33348, 3 June 2026, effective 31 July 2026, sections II.I.6, II.I.7 and V.C; CMS Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule (CMS-0057-F), fact sheet, 17 January 2024" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-barrier" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-barrier .

heiro:6000087 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Administrative Censoring"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/appendix-1-guideline-evaluation-anticancer-medicinal-products-man-methodological-consideration-using-progression-free-survival-or-disease-free-survival-confirmatory-trials_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Administrative Censoring Generally Uninformative; Premature Censoring Checked" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EMA states that commonly used methods for comparing survival times are valid only if censoring is uninformative, and that this assumption generally holds for administrative censoring of patients with complete follow-up and no event at the data cut-off. For patients with incomplete follow-up at the cut-off, described as premature censoring, the assumption should be examined systematically, for example with Kaplan-Meier curves of time to censoring and checks of censoring patterns across covariates and treatment arms. The extent of informative censoring should be discussed in the clinical trial report." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Appendix 1 to the guideline on the evaluation of anticancer medicinal products in man: methodological consideration for using progression-free survival (PFS) or disease-free survival (DFS) in confirmatory trials, EMA/CHMP/27994/2008/Rev.1, adopted 13 December 2012, effective 1 July 2013, section Informative censoring" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-censoring" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-censoring .

heiro:6000088 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Administrative Censoring"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD14-Survival-analysis.updated-March-2013.v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Extrapolate Immature Survival Data With Parametric Models" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 14 states that standard survival methods are suitable only if censoring is uninformative, and that extrapolation is required unless trial survival data are complete or very close to complete. When some patients remain event-free at the end of follow-up, only a mean restricted to that time can be estimated directly, so parametric models should be used rather than restricted means unless data are almost entirely complete. With substantial censoring, a scenario based only on observed data can show how far the extrapolated period drives the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 14: Survival analysis for economic evaluations alongside clinical trials, extrapolation with patient-level data, June 2011, last updated March 2013, sections 2, 5 and 6.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-censoring" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-censoring .

heiro:6000089 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Administrative Censoring"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Extrapolating Beyond Trial Follow-Up and Assessing Hazard Plausibility" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE notes that a lifetime time horizon often requires extrapolating data beyond the duration of the available studies, and asks for scenarios using different statistical models, including one in which the technology provides no further benefit beyond its use. When extrapolating time-to-event data, the clinical plausibility of the underlying hazard functions should routinely be assessed, and functions with stable or decreasing variance over time are likely to underestimate uncertainty. Simple adjustments for treatment switching, such as censoring patients who cross over, should be avoided as very susceptible to selection bias." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.24, 4.6.25 and 4.6.26, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-censoring" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-censoring .

heiro:6000090 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of U.S. Department of Health and Human Services on Administrative Cost Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000024 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-B/part-158/subpart-B/section-158.210"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Medical Loss Ratio Floors of 85% and 80% With Premium Rebates" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Federal rules require health insurance issuers to pay rebates when their medical loss ratio falls below 85% in the large group market or 80% in the small group and individual markets, and a state may substitute a higher standard. The rebate equals premium after taxes and fees, adjusted for the risk programmes, multiplied by the shortfall. Administrative services, including cost containment that is not quality improvement, broker fees and general administration, are reported as non-claims costs, and all retrospective and concurrent utilisation review, fraud prevention and marketing cannot count as quality improvement." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Code of Federal Regulations, title 45, part 158, sections 158.150, 158.160, 158.210, 158.211 and 158.240, eCFR text up to date as of 1 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-cost-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-cost-ratio .

heiro:6000091 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development on Administrative Cost Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000009 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/01/tackling-wasteful-spending-on-health_g1g72f29/9789264266414-en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Administration Averaged Around 3% of Health Spending in 2014" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The OECD reports that member countries spent on average around 3% of health spending on administration in 2014, double that level in France and higher still in the United States. Single-payer systems had lower administrative costs than multi-payer systems, and among multi-payer systems those with free choice of insurer tended to cost more than those with automatic affiliation. Private insurance schemes spent from 9% of their spending on administration in Australia to more than 30% in Spain, the United Kingdom and Austria. The OECD cautions that some countries value private insurers' administration as the sum of costs, leaving out profits and brokerage fees." ;
    heiro:2000018 "OECD, Tackling Wasteful Spending on Health, OECD Publishing, Paris, 2017, chapter 6 (Administrative spending in OECD health care systems: where is the fat and can it be trimmed?), sections 1.1 and 1.3 and Box 6.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-cost-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-cost-ratio .

heiro:6000092 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of OECD, Eurostat and WHO on Administrative Cost Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005,
        heiro:5000009,
        heiro:5000010 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/03/a-system-of-health-accounts-2011_g1g75c9d/9789264270985-en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "OECD, Eurostat and WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Accounts Function HC.7 Defines Governance and Financing Administration" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The System of Health Accounts records administration under function HC.7, split into governance and health system administration, and administration of health financing. Financing administration covers enrolling members, billing and collecting contributions, risk equalisation, contracting with providers and claims processing; for private insurers it includes sales, claim adjudication, actuarial and legal functions, corporate overheads and risk charges. Administration inside hospitals and other providers is counted within the care they deliver, and the paperwork costs borne by consumers are excluded." ;
    heiro:2000018 "OECD, Eurostat and World Health Organization, A System of Health Accounts 2011: Revised Edition, OECD Publishing, Paris, 2017, chapter 5 (Classification of health care functions, ICHA-HC), classes HC.7, HC.7.1 and HC.7.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-cost-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-cost-ratio .

heiro:6000093 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Administrative Data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd9/chapter/introduction-to-real-world-evidence-in-nice-decision-making"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Administrative Data in the Real-World Evidence Framework" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's real-world evidence framework defines administrative data as data collected for administrative purposes by health and social care services, citing Hospital Episode Statistics, whose primary purpose is hospital reimbursement through payment by results, and treats claims data as a type of administrative data. Submissions should report the original purpose of data collection, the type of source, linkage methods and the completeness and accuracy of key variables, since imperfect linkage can exclude records or cause misclassification. NICE sets no minimum thresholds for completeness or accuracy." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence, NICE real-world evidence framework (ECD9), published 23 June 2022, last updated September 2025, chapters 'Introduction to real-world evidence in NICE decision making' and 'Assessing data suitability'" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-data" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-data .

heiro:6000094 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Administrative Data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Administrative Databases for Belgian Costs, Not for Added Benefit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "KCE's Belgian guidelines describe administrative databases as a potentially useful source for measuring and valuing resource use, since they can provide cost information reflecting real-world conditions in Belgium, but analysts should check that the data are reliable and fit for purpose. Real-world data from registries or administrative databases are, under usual circumstances, unsuitable for showing added therapeutic benefit because there is no randomised comparison group. Such data remain useful for valuing specific variables that an economic evaluation needs in the Belgian context." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (first edition 8 May 2025, second edition 18 June 2025), sections 4.2.4 and 5.9 (Guideline 9)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-data" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-data .

heiro:6000095 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Administrative Data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/152503/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Claims Data Need Coverage Checks and Validated Definitions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance on electronic health records and medical claims data notes that claims data, sometimes called administrative healthcare claims data, are information submitted to insurers to receive payment. Because a record exists only when a patient interacts with the health care system, sponsors should address continuity of insurance coverage and recognise that such data may miss non-prescription drugs and drugs not reimbursed by health plans. Operational definitions of study variables should be validated with measures such as sensitivity, specificity and positive predictive value." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, Real-World Data: Assessing Electronic Health Records and Medical Claims Data To Support Regulatory Decision-Making for Drug and Biological Products, final guidance for industry (July 2024), section I (footnote 6) and sections IV.B, IV.B.1 and IV.D" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-data" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-data .

heiro:6000096 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of U.S. Department of Health and Human Services on Administrative Simplification"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000024 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/03/24/2026-05676/administrative-simplification-adoption-of-standards-for-health-care-claims-attachments-transactions"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Claims Attachments Standard Adopted to Reduce Administrative Burden" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under HIPAA Administrative Simplification, HHS adopted standards for health care claims attachments transactions and electronic signatures in March 2026, effective 26 May 2026 with compliance required by 26 May 2028. HHS states that attachments still rely largely on fax, mail or portal uploads and that standardising them is intended to reduce administrative burden. After commenters warned of misalignment with existing prior authorisation standards, it did not finalise attachment standards for prior authorisation. Its impact analysis estimates annualised savings of 781.98millionagainstannualisedcostsof781.98 million against annualised costs of 781.98millionagainstannualisedcostsof478.23 million at a 7% discount rate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Secretary, Administrative Simplification; Adoption of Standards for Health Care Claims Attachments Transactions and Electronic Signatures, final rule (CMS-0053-F), 91 FR 14350, 24 March 2026, sections I.A, I.C and I.D" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-simplification" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-simplification .

heiro:6000097 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Administrative Simplification"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/key-initiatives/burden-reduction/administrative-simplification/hipaa"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "HIPAA Standards and FHIR Prior Authorisation to Cut Paperwork" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS states that HIPAA and later legislation set national standards for electronic transactions, code sets, unique identifiers and operating rules to reduce paperwork and streamline business processes across the health care system. Its 2024 interoperability and prior authorisation rule requires Medicare Advantage, Medicaid, CHIP and federally facilitated exchange plans to run a FHIR-based Prior Authorization API from January 2027 to reduce the burden of prior authorisation. HHS will not take HIPAA enforcement action against covered entities that use an all-FHIR API instead of the X12 278 standard." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, HIPAA and Administrative Simplification overview (page last modified 31 July 2026); CMS Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule (CMS-0057-F), fact sheet, 17 January 2024; HIPAA Transaction Enforcement Discretion FAQ (page last modified 21 September 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "administrative-simplification" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:administrative-simplification .

heiro:6000098 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Health Technology Adoption Curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/implementing-nice-guidance/cost-saving-resource-planning-and-audit/assessing-the-resource-impact-of-nice-guidance"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Resource Impact Tools Model Uptake to Maximum Implementation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's resource impact process manual says its resource impact tools usually cover 3 years after guidance is published and state when maximum implementation is assumed to be achieved. Costs are not discounted, and predictions of future uptake should not rely on a single source where possible, drawing on company submissions and previous uptake of similar technologies. The budget impact test is exceeded if the net annual NHS cost could be greater than £40 million in any of the first 3 years of use, and NHS England may then seek special arrangements with the company." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence, Assessing resource impact process manual (2026; Word document, January 2026 update), sections 2.1, 2.2, 3.5 and 4.2.3 to 4.2.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adoption-curve" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adoption-curve .

heiro:6000099 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Patented Medicine Prices Review Board (PMPRB) on Health Technology Adoption Curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000056 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.canada.ca/content/dam/pmprb-cepmb/documents/reports-and-studies/budget-impact-analysis-guidelines/PMPRB-BIA-Guidlines-en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Patented Medicine Prices Review Board (PMPRB)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Market Share Uptake Forecasts in Canadian Budget Impact Analyses" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PMPRB's 2020 budget impact analysis guidelines, which supersede its 2007 edition, say the potential use of a new drug strongly affects results. Market share should be estimated from historical data from Canadian private payers or foreign markets, or from a model of patient and physician awareness and switching, checked by expert opinion. Without better data, the new drug takes share from each comparator in proportion to that comparator's share. Analyses typically report a one-year baseline and a three-year forecast, without discounting or inflation, with sensitivity analysis on market share." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Patented Medicine Prices Review Board, Budget Impact Analysis Guidelines: Guidelines for Conducting Pharmaceutical Budget Impact Analyses for Submission to Public Drug Plans in Canada, second edition (June 2020), sections 6.2, 6.6, 6.8, 7.4.2 and 7.4.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adoption-curve" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adoption-curve .

heiro:6000100 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Advance Care Planning"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng142/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Service Policies to Offer Advance Care Planning at the End of Life" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's guideline on end of life care service delivery says providers should have policies so that advance care planning is offered to adults approaching the end of their life, taking account of under-served and vulnerable groups, and systems so that each person has a copy of their plan at home or with them if admitted. The committee found the evidence unclear but saw some benefit in helping people stay where they wish to be cared for and die, which may reduce the need for hospital services while increasing demand for community services." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence, End of life care for adults: service delivery (NG142), published 16 October 2019, last updated October 2021, recommendations 1.6.1 to 1.6.7 and their rationale" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "advance-care-planning" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:advance-care-planning .

heiro:6000101 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Advance Care Planning"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/universal-principles-for-advance-care-planning.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Six Universal Principles for Advance Care Planning in England" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Universal Principles for Advance Care Planning, published by NHS England with partner organisations in March 2022, describe it as a voluntary process of person-centred discussion about preferences and priorities for future care. Six principles put the person at the centre, with personalised conversations, shared decision making, a shareable plan, the chance to review it, and freedom for anyone involved to speak up if the principles are not followed. Advance statements guide later decisions but are not legally binding, unlike valid and applicable advance decisions to refuse treatment and lasting powers of attorney." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England and partner organisations, Universal Principles for Advance Care Planning (ACP), first published March 2022 (gateway reference B0845), Executive summary and 'What is advance care planning (ACP)?'" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "advance-care-planning" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:advance-care-planning .

heiro:6000102 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Advance Care Planning"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/files/document/mln-advanced-care-planning.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Medicare Pays for Advance Care Planning With No Frequency Limit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS pays for advance care planning, a voluntary face-to-face discussion of a patient's wishes should they become unable to make their own medical decisions, under CPT code 99497 for the first 30 minutes and 99498 for each additional 30 minutes. Discussions of 15 minutes or less are not billed as advance care planning. Medicare pays for it as an optional element of the annual wellness visit or as a separate Part B service, waives the deductible and coinsurance when it is billed with that visit under set conditions, and sets no limit on how often it is reported." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Medicare Learning Network fact sheet Advance Care Planning (MLN909289, March 2026), sections 'Billing Eligibility & Coding' and 'Billing & Payment'" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "advance-care-planning" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:advance-care-planning .

heiro:6000103 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Advanced Therapy Medicinal Product"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/human-regulatory-overview/advanced-therapy-medicinal-products-overview"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Central Authorisation With CAT Draft Opinion and Optional Classification" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA states that all advanced therapy medicinal products are authorised centrally through a single evaluation. Its Committee for Advanced Therapies prepares a draft opinion on quality, safety and efficacy, on which the CHMP bases its recommendation to the European Commission. Developers unsure whether a product is an ATMP can use an optional classification procedure, set up to settle borderline questions early, for example with medical devices. The committee issues a scientific recommendation within 60 days of receiving the request, after consulting the Commission, and summaries of outcomes are published." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Advanced therapy medicinal products: overview (web page, sections 'EMA role' and 'Committee for Advanced Therapies'); Advanced therapy classification (web page), under Article 17 of Regulation (EC) No 1394/2007" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "advanced-therapy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:advanced-therapy .

heiro:6000104 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Advanced Therapy Medicinal Product"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/expedited-programs-regenerative-medicine-therapies-serious-conditions"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "RMAT Designation Within 60 Days, With Accelerated Approval Options" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance on expedited programmes for regenerative medicine therapies says RMAT designation carries all the features of breakthrough therapy designation, including early discussion of surrogate or intermediate endpoints. Requests are made with an IND or later, ideally by the end-of-phase 2 meeting, and FDA responds within 60 calendar days. RMATs may gain accelerated approval on agreed surrogate or intermediate endpoints reasonably likely to predict long-term benefit, and post-approval requirements may be met through patient registries or other real-world evidence, or by monitoring all patients treated before approval." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, Expedited Programs for Regenerative Medicine Therapies for Serious Conditions: Guidance for Industry (final, February 2019), sections III.C and III.E; Regenerative Medicine Advanced Therapy Designation (web page, content current as of 4 November 2025)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "advanced-therapy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:advanced-therapy .

heiro:6000105 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of MHRA on Advanced Therapy Medicinal Product"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000026 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advanced-therapy-medicinal-products-regulation-and-licensing-in-uk"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "MHRA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "UK-Wide Licence Required, With Hospital Exemption for Unlicensed ATMPs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The MHRA states that the ATMP definition is set in the Human Medicines Regulations 2012 and that every ATMP placed on the UK market needs a marketing authorisation, which since the Windsor Framework took effect on 1 January 2025 must cover the whole UK. The MHRA classifies products where developers are unsure. Unlicensed ATMPs can be supplied under the hospital exemption, for products prepared on a non-routine basis and used in a hospital on a prescription for a specific patient, or under the specials scheme, and either route needs a manufacturer's licence." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency, Advanced therapy medicinal products: regulation and licensing in UK (published 26 January 2015, last updated 11 September 2026), sections 1, 2, 6 and 9; Guidance on the UK's hospital exemption scheme for advanced therapy medicinal products (updated 6 March 2025)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "advanced-therapy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:advanced-therapy .

heiro:6000106 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Advanced Therapy Medicinal Product"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/48974c78-1c37-4cf9-9fc9-a630fee9baac_en?filename=hta_mp_jca_sc-specifications_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ATMPs Subject to Joint Clinical Assessment From 12 January 2025" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the EU HTA Regulation, medicinal products regulated as ATMPs became subject to joint clinical assessment from 12 January 2025, alongside new active substances for treating cancer, with designated orphan medicines following from 13 January 2028 and all other medicines from 13 January 2030. The HTA Coordination Group confirms whether a product submitted to EMA is in scope as an ATMP using Regulation (EC) No 1394/2007. Unlike the cancer criteria, which it specifies in detail, the group sets no separate criteria for ATMPs because that regulation already lays them down." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment (HTACG), Scientific specifications of medicinal products subject to joint clinical assessments (agreed 10 June 2024), sections 1, 2 and 3.4; Regulation (EU) 2021/2282 on health technology assessment, Article 7" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "advanced-therapy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:advanced-therapy .

heiro:6000107 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Advanced Therapy Medicinal Product"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/innovation-models/cgt"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Medicaid Outcomes-Based Agreements for Sickle Cell Gene Therapy" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS's Cell and Gene Therapy Access Model is a voluntary, multi-year model in which CMS negotiates outcomes-based agreements with manufacturers on behalf of state Medicaid agencies, combining price discounts with outcomes-based rebates. Its initial focus is gene therapy for sickle cell disease, with two manufacturers, Genetix Biotherapeutics and Vertex Pharmaceuticals, and 34 Medicaid programmes, including the District of Columbia and Puerto Rico, which began participating between January 2025 and January 2026. Manufacturers must pay for defined fertility preservation services, since the conditioning chemotherapy typically causes infertility." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Cell and Gene Therapy (CGT) Access Model (web page, last modified 30 September 2026), sections on the model overview, gene therapy for sickle cell disease and participants" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "advanced-therapy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:advanced-therapy .

heiro:6000108 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Adverse Drug Event"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng5/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Fair Blame Reporting and PINCER Principles for Medicines Safety Incidents" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's medicines optimisation guideline defines medicines-related patient safety incidents as unintended or unexpected incidents related to medicines use that could have or did lead to harm, including medication errors, near misses and potentially avoidable adverse events and hospital admissions. Organisations should support a fair blame culture, use several methods to identify incidents, such as health record review, patient surveys and direct observation of administration, and consider applying the principles of the PINCER intervention, including dedicated pharmacist support, educational outreach and information technology support." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence, Medicines optimisation: the safe and effective use of medicines to enable the best possible outcomes (NG5), published 4 March 2015, last updated October 2021, recommendations 1.1.1 to 1.1.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-drug-event" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-drug-event .

heiro:6000109 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Adverse Drug Event"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/human-regulatory-overview/post-authorisation/pharmacovigilance-post-authorisation/medication-errors"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Errors With Harm Reported as Adverse Reactions, All Errors in PSURs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA describes medication errors as the most common preventable cause of undesired adverse events in medication practice. Under EU pharmacovigilance law, adverse reactions resulting from medication errors are reported to EudraVigilance like other suspected adverse reactions, while errors without harm, intercepted errors and potential errors should be recorded but are not valid individual case safety reports. Marketing authorisation holders summarise medication errors in periodic safety update reports whether or not harm occurred, and reflect the risk of errors in risk management plans." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Medication errors (web page, sections 'Legal requirements' and 'EMA role'); Good practice guide on recording, coding, reporting and assessment of medication errors (EMA/762563/2014, in effect 27 November 2015), sections 2, 4.3.2 and 5.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-drug-event" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-drug-event .

heiro:6000110 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Adverse Drug Event"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/343477"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Medication Without Harm in the Global Patient Safety Action Plan" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's Global Patient Safety Action Plan 2021 to 2030 asks governments to implement a programme based on its third Global Patient Safety Challenge, Medication Without Harm, launched in 2017 with the goal of halving severe, avoidable medication-related harm globally over five years. Governments should act early on high-risk situations, polypharmacy and transitions of care, designate a national coordinator and encourage reporting of adverse drug events and medication errors. Health care facilities should identify medication errors and harm through incident reporting systems and monitor progress in reducing medication-related harm." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Global patient safety action plan 2021-2030: towards eliminating avoidable harm in health care (3 August 2021, ISBN 9789240032705), section 1.1 and Strategic objective 3, Strategy 3.2; Medication Without Harm (WHO initiative web page)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-drug-event" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-drug-event .

heiro:6000111 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Adverse Drug Reaction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/regulatory-procedural-guideline/guideline-good-pharmacovigilance-practices-gvp-module-vi-collection-management-submission-reports-suspected-adverse-reactions-medicinal-products-rev-2_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Serious Suspected Reactions to EudraVigilance Within 15 Days" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA's pharmacovigilance guidance applies the EU definition of an adverse reaction as a noxious and unintended response to a medicinal product, covering use within or outside the marketing authorisation, including off-label use, overdose, misuse, abuse and medication errors, as well as occupational exposure. Spontaneous reports from healthcare professionals or consumers count as suspected adverse reactions unless the reporter excludes a causal link. Serious valid reports must be submitted to EudraVigilance within 15 days of receipt and non-serious ones within 90 days." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Guideline on good pharmacovigilance practices (GVP) Module VI: Collection, management and submission of reports of suspected adverse reactions to medicinal products (EMA/873138/2011 Rev. 2, legal effective date 22 November 2017), sections VI.A.1.1 and VI.C.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-drug-reaction" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-drug-reaction .

heiro:6000112 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Adverse Drug Reaction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-314/subpart-B/section-314.80"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Postmarketing 15-Day Alert Reports for Serious Unexpected Experiences" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's postmarketing reporting rule uses the term adverse drug experience, meaning any adverse event associated with the use of a drug in humans, whether or not considered drug related, including events from overdose, abuse or withdrawal and any failure of expected pharmacological action. Applicants must report each serious and unexpected adverse drug experience within 15 calendar days of first receiving the information, and report all others periodically: quarterly for three years after approval, then annually. An unexpected experience is one not listed in the current labelling." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, 21 CFR 314.80 Postmarketing reporting of adverse drug experiences (eCFR, up to date as of 1 October 2026), paragraphs (a), (c)(1)(i) and (c)(2)(i)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-drug-reaction" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-drug-reaction .

heiro:6000113 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of MHRA on Adverse Drug Reaction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000026 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/guidance/the-yellow-card-scheme-guidance-for-healthcare-professionals-patients-and-the-public"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "MHRA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Yellow Card: All Serious Reactions and All Black Triangle Reactions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The MHRA asks for all serious suspected adverse drug reactions to established medicines and vaccines to be reported to the Yellow Card scheme, even if the effect is well recognised, and for all suspected reactions to new medicines and vaccines under additional monitoring, marked with an inverted black triangle. It is particularly interested in reactions in children, in patients over 65, to biological medicines and vaccines, from delayed effects and interactions, and to herbal and homeopathic remedies. A reporter needs only a suspicion, not proof of causality." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency, The Yellow Card scheme: guidance for healthcare professionals, patients and the public (last updated 24 July 2025), sections 'What to report' and 'Black triangle scheme'" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-drug-reaction" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-drug-reaction .

heiro:6000114 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Adverse Event"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-312/subpart-B/section-312.32"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "IND Safety Reports Within 15 Days, or 7 for Fatal Reactions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's IND safety reporting rule defines an adverse event as any untoward medical occurrence associated with the use of a drug in humans, whether or not considered drug related, and a suspected adverse reaction as one where there is a reasonable possibility that the drug caused it. Sponsors must report serious and unexpected suspected adverse reactions within 15 calendar days and unexpected fatal or life-threatening ones within 7 calendar days. Any clinically important increase in the rate of a serious suspected adverse reaction over that listed in the protocol or investigator brochure must also be reported." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, 21 CFR 312.32 IND safety reporting (eCFR, up to date as of 1 October 2026), paragraphs (a), (c)(1)(i), (c)(1)(iv) and (c)(2)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event .

heiro:6000115 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of MHRA on Adverse Event"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000026 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/guidance/clinical-trials-for-medicines-collection-verification-and-reporting-of-safety-events"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "MHRA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "UK Trial Adverse Events Recorded From Consent, SUSARs to the MHRA" ;
    heiro:2000017 "MHRA guidance under the amended Clinical Trials Regulations, which took full effect on 28 April 2026, defines an adverse event as any untoward medical occurrence in a participant given a medicinal product, whether or not caused by it. Sponsors and investigators should record adverse events from informed consent to the end of the trial unless the protocol says otherwise, and investigators report serious adverse events to the sponsor within 24 hours unless the protocol or investigator's brochure exempts them. Fatal or life-threatening SUSARs go to the MHRA within 7 days and other SUSARs within 15 days." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency, Clinical trials for medicines: collection, verification and reporting of safety events (published 25 June 2025, last updated 15 July 2026), sections 'Definitions', 'Reporting adverse events (AEs) and serious adverse events (SAEs)' and 'Reporting suspected unexpected serious adverse reactions (SUSARs)'" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event .

heiro:6000116 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Adverse Event"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/a70a62c7-325c-401e-ba42-66174b656ab8_en?filename=hta_outcomes_jca_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Six Adverse Event Categories Reported Irrespective of Causality in JCA" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group requires joint clinical assessments of medicinal products to use the term adverse event, not adverse reaction or side effect, and to report adverse events irrespective of any causality assessment. The main report gives numbers and percentages per arm for all adverse events, serious adverse events, severe events such as CTCAE grade 3 or above, deaths related to adverse events, and treatment discontinuation and interruption due to adverse events. An appendix gives relative effects, including events by system organ class and preferred term with an incidence of at least 5% in any arm." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment (HTACG), Guidance on outcomes for joint clinical assessments (adopted 10 June 2024), sections 4.1 and 4.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event .

heiro:6000117 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Adverse Event"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Serious and Severe Adverse Events and Discontinuations in Benefit Assessment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods note that the term adverse events leaves causality open, whereas adverse effects assume a causal link, and treat harm as an equal component of benefit assessment. As a matter of principle, IQWiG uses the overall rates of serious adverse events, severe adverse events where applicable, such as CTCAE grade 3 or above, and discontinuations due to adverse events. Specific adverse effects are then selected for their relevance to patients and differences between treatment arms, using the most suitable operationalisation, such as a Standardised MedDRA Query instead of a single preferred term." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English version published 1 July 2026), section 3.1.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event .

heiro:6000118 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Adverse Event Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adverse Event Costs in Cost Comparisons and Disaggregated Results" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE asks that cost-comparison analyses capture the costs of differing health outcomes, such as managing adverse events, when relevant. Substantial differences between technologies in costs directly relating to health outcomes such as adverse events indicate that the technologies may not provide similar overall health benefits, so such differences must be clearly justified. For cost-utility analyses, results should include a table of disaggregated costs and of the decrements associated with further interventions and adverse events, presented with and without discounting." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.19, 4.2.20 and 4.10.4, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event-rate .

heiro:6000119 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of International Council for Harmonisation (ICH) on Adverse Event Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000037 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://database.ich.org/sites/default/files/E3_Guideline.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "International Council for Harmonisation (ICH)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adverse Event Rates Tabulated for All Events by Treatment Group" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICH E3 asks clinical study reports to display all adverse events occurring after study treatment starts in summary tables giving each event, the number of patients in each treatment group in whom it occurred and the rate of occurrence, including events not considered drug related. These rates should be used to compare treatment and control groups, and life table or similar analyses may be more informative than crude rates. ICH E2A defines an adverse event as an untoward medical occurrence that need not have a causal relationship with the treatment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICH E3 Structure and content of clinical study reports, Step 4 version dated 30 November 1995, sections 12.2.2 and 12.2.3; ICH E2A Clinical safety data management: definitions and standards for expedited reporting, Step 4 version dated 27 October 1994, section II.A.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event-rate .

heiro:6000120 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Adverse Event Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-312/subpart-B/section-312.32"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "IND Safety Reports When Event Rates Exceed Controls" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA defines an adverse event as any untoward medical occurrence associated with the use of a drug in humans, whether or not considered drug related. A sponsor must submit an IND safety report, no later than 15 calendar days after finding that it qualifies, when an aggregate analysis of specific events in a clinical trial indicates that they occur more frequently in the drug treatment group than in a concurrent or historical control group. Sponsors must also report any clinically important increase in the rate of a serious suspected adverse reaction over that listed in the protocol or investigator brochure." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Code of Federal Regulations, title 21, section 312.32 (IND safety reporting), paragraphs (a), (c)(1)(i)(C) and (c)(1)(iv), eCFR text up to date as of 1 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event-rate .

heiro:6000121 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of National Cancer Institute on Adverse Event Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000057 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://dctd.cancer.gov/research/ctep-trials/for-sites/adverse-events/ctcae-v6.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "National Cancer Institute" ;
    heiro:2000016 "CTCAE Version 6.0 Grades Adverse Event Severity From 1 to 5" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The National Cancer Institute's Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events version 6.0, published on 22 July 2025, gives a severity grading scale for each adverse event term. Grade 3 is severe or medically significant but not immediately life-threatening, with hospitalisation or its prolongation indicated, grade 4 has life-threatening consequences with urgent intervention indicated, and grade 5 is death related to the event. An adverse event is any unfavourable and unintended sign, symptom or disease temporally associated with a treatment, whether or not considered related to it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Cancer Institute, Cancer Therapy Evaluation Program, Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events (CTCAE) v6.0 (MedDRA 28.0), published 22 July 2025, Introduction (CTCAE Term; Grade)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event-rate .

heiro:6000122 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Adverse Event Reporting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-H/part-803/subpart-E/section-803.50"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Device Deaths and Serious Injuries Reported by Manufacturers Within 30 Days" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's medical device reporting rule requires manufacturers to report within 30 calendar days of becoming aware of information that reasonably suggests a device they market may have caused or contributed to a death or serious injury, or has malfunctioned in a way likely to do so if the malfunction recurred. A 5-day report is due when an event needs remedial action to prevent an unreasonable risk of substantial harm to public health. Device user facilities report deaths to FDA and the manufacturer, and serious injuries to the manufacturer, within 10 work days." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, 21 CFR Part 803 Medical device reporting (eCFR, up to date as of 1 October 2026), sections 803.30(a), 803.50(a) and 803.53" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event-reporting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event-reporting .

heiro:6000123 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of MHRA on Adverse Event Reporting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000026 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/medical-devices-post-market-surveillance-requirements/vigilance-reporting-requirements"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "MHRA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Device Serious Incidents Reported to the MHRA Within 2, 10 or 15 Days" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For medical devices placed on the Great Britain market from 16 June 2025, the MHRA requires manufacturers to report a serious incident no later than 2 calendar days after becoming aware of a serious public health threat, 10 days for a death or unanticipated serious deterioration in health, and 15 days for all other serious incidents, down from 30. Reports go through the MORE portal and should not be delayed by incomplete information. Manufacturers must also submit trend reports when incidents rise significantly in number or severity." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency, Medical devices: post-market surveillance requirements (published 15 January 2025, last updated 5 September 2025), 'Introduction and scope' and 'Vigilance reporting requirements', sections c), d) and h), under Part 4A of the UK Medical Devices Regulations 2002 as inserted by the Medical Devices (Post-market Surveillance Requirements) (Amendment) (Great Britain) Regulations 2024" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event-reporting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event-reporting .

heiro:6000124 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Adverse Event Reporting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/334323"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Incident Reporting Systems Should Yield Learning, Not Just Record Failure" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's technical report on patient safety incident reporting and learning systems states that reporting systems should yield learning to improve safety rather than simply communicate failure. Staff will report incidents if they are protected from blame and retribution and involved in follow-up. Under-reporting happens in health care as in other sectors, but this should not lead to incident reports being disregarded. A single report seldom gives a definitive view of causation, so further investigation and analysis are needed to establish why an incident happened." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Patient safety incident reporting and learning systems: technical report and guidance (16 September 2020, ISBN 9789240010338), 'Ten facts on reporting and learning systems', facts 1, 2, 3 and 6" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-event-reporting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-event-reporting .

heiro:6000125 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of U.S. Department of Health and Human Services on Adverse Selection"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000024 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-B/part-153/subpart-D"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Risk Adjustment Addresses Selection Across Health Plans" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HHS requires every risk adjustment methodology used by a State, or by HHS on a State's behalf, to be federally certified and published before the benefit year, including how plan average actuarial risk and the resulting payments and charges are calculated. HHS certifies a State alternate methodology only after considering whether it accounts for risk selection across metal levels. Risk adjustment data must be validated on a statistically valid sample from each issuer, and plan risk and transfers may be adjusted for errors found." ;
    heiro:2000018 "45 CFR Part 153, Subpart D, State Standards Related to the Risk Adjustment Program, sections 153.320, 153.330(b) and 153.350 (eCFR, up to date as of 25 September 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-selection" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-selection .

heiro:6000126 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Government and ABI on Adverse Selection"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000058,
        heiro:5000059 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69b291ae97f6fef21f687aad/code-on-genetic-testing-and-insurance-updated-march-2026.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Government and ABI" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Predictive Genetic Tests Limited to Huntington's Disease Above £500,000" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Code on Genetic Testing and Insurance lets insurers ask for, and take account of, a predictive genetic test result only for approved conditions and only above set financial limits. The only approved test is for Huntington's disease, in applications for life insurance totalling over £500,000 per person, and no test is approved for critical illness or income protection cover. The Code defines anti-selection as individuals buying insurance based on information that insurers do not know, and an application to add a test must show a material risk of it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Government and Association of British Insurers, Code on Genetic Testing and Insurance (first published October 2018, updated 19 March 2026), Commitment 2; Questions and answers 'How will the Code be kept up-to-date?' and 'What is the process for changing the list of relevant predictive genetic tests in Appendix I?'; Appendix I" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-selection" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-selection .

heiro:6000127 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Adverse Selection"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.zorginstituutnederland.nl/financiering/fondsbeheer-zvf-en-flz/zorgverzekeringsfonds/wat-is-risicoverevening"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Risk Equalisation Supports Open Enrolment and Uniform Premiums" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ZIN explains that Dutch health insurers must accept everyone for the basic insurance and charge the same basic premium to all, so they may not offer lower premiums to young, healthy people to attract them, a practice it calls risk selection. Because some insurers carry more high-risk enrolees, ZIN administers risk equalisation, paying each insurer a contribution from the Health Insurance Fund based on its enrolees' risk profiles. The profiles use age, sex, region and health indicators such as medicine use and hospital visits, with norm amounts set each year by the health minister." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Wat is risicoverevening? (Fondsbeheer, Zorgverzekeringsfonds), sections 'Gelijkheidsbeginsel in de zorg', 'Financiële compensatie voor hogere risico's' and 'Berekening van de vereveningsbijdrage', accessed 29 September 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "adverse-selection" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:adverse-selection .

heiro:6000128 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Affordability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/content/information/files/pbac-guidelines-version-5.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Patient Affordability Without PBS Subsidy Among Five Quantitative Factors" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines list five quantitative factors that influence the committee's recommendations on subsidising a medicine. Alongside comparative health gain, comparative cost-effectiveness and the projected annual net cost to the PBS and to the Australian Government health budget, the committee weighs patient affordability in the absence of PBS subsidy. Submissions present this as the cost per patient per course for acute or self-limited therapy, or the cost per patient per year for chronic or continuing therapy." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), 'Key factors influencing decision making by the PBAC' and subsections 3A.8.1, 4.4 and 4.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "affordability" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:affordability .

heiro:6000129 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Internal Revenue Service on Affordability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000051 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-drop/rp-26-26.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Internal Revenue Service" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Employer Cover Affordable Up to 9.96% of Income in 2026 and 10.22% in 2027" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For the US premium tax credit, the Internal Revenue Service treats an employer plan as affordable for an employee if the employee's required contribution for self-only coverage does not exceed the required contribution percentage of household income; for related family members, the contribution for family coverage is tested. The percentage is 9.96% for plan years beginning in 2026 and 10.22% for 2027, indexed using projections of premium and income growth. A person for whom eligible employer coverage is affordable generally cannot claim the credit." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Internal Revenue Service, 26 CFR 1.36B-2 Eligibility for premium tax credit (eCFR, up to date as of 1 October 2026), paragraphs (a) and (c)(3)(v)(A); Rev. Proc. 2025-25, section 3.02; Rev. Proc. 2026-26 (July 2026), sections 1 and 3.02" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "affordability" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:affordability .

heiro:6000130 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Affordability Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.zorginstituutnederland.nl/wat-wij-doen/programmas-en-samenwerkingsverbanden/horizonscan-geneesmiddelen/sluis-voor-dure-geneesmiddelen"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Expensive Hospital Medicines Enter the Lock From €20 Million a Year" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland describes a lock under which the Minister of Health, Welfare and Sport can temporarily keep new, expensive hospital medicines out of the basic health insurance package. A medicine becomes a candidate if it would cost €20 million or more a year across one or more new indications, or €10 million or more a year in one indication at €50,000 or more per patient per year, counting drug costs only. The institute then assesses necessity, effectiveness, cost-effectiveness and feasibility, and the medicine leaves the lock only after successful price negotiation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Sluis voor dure geneesmiddelen (web page), sections 'Wanneer komt een medicijn in de sluis?', 'Beoordeling door het Zorginstituut' and 'Definitief besluit van de minister'; criteria applied under article 2.4a of the Besluit zorgverzekering as restated in Staatscourant 2025, 1814 (Regeling of 6 January 2025)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "affordability-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:affordability-threshold .

heiro:6000131 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Affordability Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/jcms/r_1502595/en/comprendre-l-evaluation-economique-des-produits-de-sante"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Economic Evaluation Triggered by €20 Million Forecast Annual Sales" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS requires an efficiency analysis, reviewed by its economic and public health evaluation committee (CEESP), when a company claims an improvement in medical benefit of level I, II or III and the product is expected to have a significant impact on health insurance spending. For medicines, this means an advanced therapy, forecast sales of €20 million or more a year in the indication in the second year on the market, or a claimed effect on care organisation or practice. The resulting economic opinion goes to the CEPS to support price negotiation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Comprendre l'évaluation économique des produits de santé (web page, updated 16 January 2023), sections 'Missions de la HAS', 'Les conditions d'éligibilité à une évaluation économique' and 'Rôle de la CEESP', applying Collège decision n°2022.0212/DC/SED/SEM of 23 June 2022 under article R161-71-3 of the Code de la sécurité sociale" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "affordability-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:affordability-threshold .

heiro:6000132 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Australian Government Department of Health, Disability and Ageing on Affordability Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000038 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.pbs.gov.au/industry/listing/procedure-guidance/8-procedures-positive-recommendation-list/8-5-finalisation-of-the-budget-impact"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Australian Government Department of Health, Disability and Ageing" ;
    heiro:2000016 "PBS Listings Costing Over AUD 20 Million a Year Referred to Cabinet" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Australian Government procedure guidance for the PBS states that the financial impact of every listing is considered across government, with the finalised cost considered by the Department of Finance and included in the recommendation to the Minister. The Minister for Health, Disability and Ageing can approve PBAC-recommended listings with a financial impact of AUD 20 million or less. Where the net cost to the Australian Government exceeds AUD 20 million in any of the forward years, the Minister refers the listing to Cabinet." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Australian Government Department of Health, Disability and Ageing, PBS procedure guidance, section 8.5 'Finalisation of the budget impact', subsections 8.5.1 and 8.5.2 (last updated 1 October 2016); PBS web page 'Minister' (last updated 6 July 2022)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "affordability-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:affordability-threshold .

heiro:6000133 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Insurance Authority (Ireland) on Age Rating"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000043 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hia.ie/information/lifetime-community-rating-lcr"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Insurance Authority (Ireland)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Lifetime Community Rating Loads Late Entrants 2% a Year From Age 35" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Ireland's Health Insurance Authority explains that Lifetime Community Rating adds a loading of 2% of the gross cost of an inpatient health insurance policy for each year a person spent aged 35 or over without cover before first buying it. Loadings apply to people first buying cover after 30 April 2015, last for at most 10 years and are capped at 70% for first-time buyers aged 69 or over. People who already hold cover pay no more because of their age, and the rule aims to encourage people to join the market younger." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Insurance Authority (Ireland), Lifetime Community Rating (LCR) (web page), sections 'How much is the loading?', 'Will I have to pay the loading for the rest of my life?' and 'What is the maximum loading?'" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "age-rating" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:age-rating .

heiro:6000134 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Federal Office of Public Health (Switzerland) on Age Rating"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000039 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.bag.admin.ch/en/premiums-and-costs-answers-to-frequently-asked-questions"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Federal Office of Public Health (Switzerland)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Basic Cover Open at Any Age, With Lower Premiums for Children and Young Adults" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Switzerland's Federal Office of Public Health states that basic health insurers must accept anyone subject to compulsory insurance without reservation or waiting period, regardless of age or state of health, and charge the same premiums for all policyholders unless the law stipulates otherwise, with variation by region and insurance model. For 2027 the average monthly premium is CHF 487.60 for adults, CHF 338.80 for young adults and CHF 128.10 for children. Supplementary insurance, by contrast, may vary premiums by health, age and gender." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Federal Office of Public Health (Switzerland), Health insurance: Premiums and co-payment (web page); Health insurance premium costs: FAQs and useful links (web page); press release 'Krankenkassenprämien steigen durchschnittlich um 5,0 Prozent' (29 September 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "age-rating" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:age-rating .

heiro:6000135 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Age Weighting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/our-approach/methods-process/value-assessment-framework/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Equal Value of Life Years Gained (evLYG) as a Co-Base Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "To address concerns that the QALY undervalues gains for the elderly, disabled, or terminally ill, ICER reports an equal value of life years gained (evLYG) alongside the conventional cost-per-QALY. The evLYG values every life-year gained at the full value of a healthy year (a fixed utility, ~0.851) regardless of a person’s age or baseline health, while still crediting quality-of-life improvements, so that life extension is valued equally across patient groups." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER Value Assessment Framework (Equal Value of Life Years Gained)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "age-weighting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:age-weighting .

heiro:6000136 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Age Weighting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/gho-documents/global-health-estimates/ghe2023_daly_methods.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Age Weights Dropped From DALYs Since 2012" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's methods for its Global Health Estimates record that the standard DALYs of the original Global Burden of Disease study and earlier WHO updates used non-uniform age weights, giving less weight to years of healthy life lost at young and older ages. With the DALY defined purely as a measure of population health loss, WHO states that age weights are difficult to justify. After informal consultations in 2012, WHO adopted the GBD 2010 approach of no age weighting and a 0% discount rate, which raised the absolute number of DALYs and the share at younger and older ages." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2023, Global Health Estimates Technical Paper GHE/2026.3 (September 2026), sections 2 and 2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "age-weighting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:age-weighting .

heiro:6000137 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Age Weighting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Equal QALY Weights, With No Age Weighting in the Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's manual sets no age weights for QALYs. Its reference case gives an additional QALY the same weight regardless of the other characteristics of the people receiving it, except in specific circumstances, and the committee regards all QALYs as being of equal weight. The main exception is the severity modifier, which weights QALYs by 1.2 or 1.7 according to absolute or proportional QALY shortfall, both measured against the general population with the same age and sex distribution as people with the condition." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), table 4.1, sections 6.2.10 and 6.2.12 to 6.2.18, and table 6.1, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "age-weighting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:age-weighting .

heiro:6000138 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Agent-Based Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/329389"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Individual-Based Models for Vaccines When Risks Differ Across Small Groups" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's guide to economic evaluation of immunisation programmes treats agent-based models as individual-based models, in which each person has characteristics such as age, susceptibility, location and household that govern transmission. It notes their flexibility for dynamic stochastic transmission but also their computing demands and outcomes that are harder to validate and interpret, and suggests them when infection risks differ substantially between many small groups. Its recommendations call for a dynamic model when vaccination is likely to change the force of infection." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, WHO guide for standardization of economic evaluations of immunization programmes, 2nd edition (WHO/IVB/19.10, 17 October 2019), sections 6.3.1, 6.3.2.3 and 6.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "agent-based-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:agent-based-model .

heiro:6000139 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Agent-Based Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/content/information/files/pbac-guidelines-version-5.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Agent-Based Models for Herd Immunity Only Where Simpler Models Fall Short" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines ask applicants to use individual-level models only when a model cannot feasibly be built as a cohort model, and say more complex techniques are unnecessary if simpler models give plausible sensitivity and scenario analyses. In infectious diseases, dynamic or agent-based models that represent herd immunity may be justified if a simple non-dynamic model would not show cost-effectiveness accurately enough. Complex techniques may be less transparent, which can lower the committee's confidence, so their use should be discussed with the Pharmaceutical Evaluation Branch in advance." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), subsection 3A.2.3 'Computational methods'" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "agent-based-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:agent-based-model .

heiro:6000140 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Aggregate Data Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pooling With Heterogeneity Assessment and Baseline Risk Checks" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE asks that statistical pooling of study results be accompanied by an assessment of heterogeneity, which a random-effects model takes into account only to some extent, and that its degree and causes be explored as fully as possible, for example through subgroup analysis and meta-regression. Each included study should be described and a forest plot provided. If event risk differs substantially between control groups, submissions should assess whether the relative effect is constant over baseline risk, especially when the model's baseline rate is very different from the trial rates." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.4.9 and 3.4.10, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "aggregate-data-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:aggregate-data-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000141 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Cochrane on Aggregate Data Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000060 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cochrane.org/authors/handbooks-and-manuals/handbook/current/chapter-10"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Cochrane" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Inverse-Variance Pooling, Heterogeneity and Random Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Cochrane Handbook states that a generic inverse-variance meta-analysis needs an effect estimate and its standard error from each study, and that a fixed-effect analysis assumes all estimates target the same underlying effect. It warns that the chi-squared test for heterogeneity has low power when studies are few or small and that thresholds for interpreting I-squared can be misleading. Simulation studies favour other between-study variance estimators, such as Paule and Mandel or REML, over DerSimonian and Laird, and random-effects pooling gives relatively more weight to smaller studies." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions, version 6.5 (2024), chapter 10: Analysing data and undertaking meta-analyses, last updated November 2024, sections 10.3.1, 10.10.2, 10.10.4.1 and 10.10.4.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "aggregate-data-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:aggregate-data-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000142 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Aggregate Data Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD2-General-meta-analysis-corrected-2Sep2016v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Generalised Linear Model Framework for Aggregate Trial Data" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 2 sets out a single framework for synthesising aggregate data from randomised trials, mainly for probabilistic decision making, in which pairwise meta-analysis, indirect comparisons and network meta-analysis use the same core models with fixed or random effects. It uses Bayesian Markov chain Monte Carlo estimation and treats trial-specific baselines as nuisance parameters, because an incorrect baseline model would bias the relative treatment effects. Model fit is checked with the residual deviance, and fixed and random effects models are compared using the Deviance Information Criterion." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 2: A generalised linear modelling framework for pairwise and network meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials, August 2011, last updated September 2016, sections 1, 2.1.1 and 2.1.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "aggregate-data-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:aggregate-data-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000143 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of U.S. Department of Labor on Aggregate Stop-Loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000061 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.dol.gov/agencies/ebsa/employers-and-advisers/guidance/technical-releases/14-01"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "U.S. Department of Labor" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Low Aggregate Attachment Points Open to State Regulation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Department of Labor describes stop-loss cover that pays claims above an attachment point set for a single member or for aggregate claims over a set period, citing 125% of expected claims per plan year as an aggregate example, with the employer self-insuring claims below it. Such cover usually insures the employer against losses rather than guaranteeing benefits to participants. Very low attachment points give nearly all the protection of conventional health insurance without its consumer protections, and a state law barring attachment points below specified levels would not, in the Department's view, be pre-empted by ERISA." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Department of Labor, Employee Benefits Security Administration, Technical Release No. 2014-01: Guidance on State Regulation of Stop-Loss Insurance, 6 November 2014, sections I to III" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "aggregate-stop-loss" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:aggregate-stop-loss .

heiro:6000144 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of National Association of Insurance Commissioners on Aggregate Stop-Loss"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000062 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/model-law-092.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "National Association of Insurance Commissioners" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Model Act Floors of 120% and 110% of Expected Claims" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NAIC Stop Loss Insurance Model Act bars an annual aggregate attachment point below the greater of USD 4,000 times the number of group members, 120% of expected claims or USD 20,000 for groups of 50 or fewer, and below 110% of expected claims for groups of 51 or more. Expected claims are those projected for the group's health plan in the absence of stop-loss or other insurance. An NAIC white paper describes the aggregate layer as protection against claim frequency, with claims paid under specific cover not counted towards the aggregate attachment point." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Association of Insurance Commissioners, Stop Loss Insurance Model Act (Model 92), adopted 1995, amended 1999, sections 2 and 3; NAIC white paper Stop Loss Insurance, Self-Funding and the ACA, 2015, section II" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "aggregate-stop-loss" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:aggregate-stop-loss .

heiro:6000145 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Australian Treasury on Aging-Driven Cost Growth"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000063 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://treasury.gov.au/sites/default/files/2026-09/p2026-797917.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Australian Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Ageing Drives About a Third of Projected Health Spending Growth" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The 2026 Intergenerational Report projects Commonwealth health spending rising from 4 per cent of GDP in 2025-26 to 6.2 per cent by 2065-66, and attributes around 33 per cent of that increase to population ageing. Treasury estimates the ageing effect against a counterfactual that holds the age and gender mix at June 2026 shares, finding the largest effect in aged care, followed by health and pensions. Other drivers include higher-quality services and decisions to subsidise new health technologies, and public hospitals account for over 60 per cent of the rise." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Australian Treasury, 2026 Intergenerational Report (21 September 2026), Part III, chapter 12, Box 12.2 and section 12.2 'Health'" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "aging-driven-cost-growth" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:aging-driven-cost-growth .

heiro:6000146 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Canadian Institute for Health Information on Aging-Driven Cost Growth"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000040 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cihi.ca/en/national-health-expenditure-trends/nhex-trends-reports/nhex-trends-2025-snapshot"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Canadian Institute for Health Information" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Population Ageing Added About 0.6 Points a Year to Canadian Spending Growth" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CIHI describes population ageing as a modest but steady driver of health spending in Canada. From 2014 to 2019, total health care spending grew by an average of 3.9% a year, of which population growth contributed 1.2 percentage points, public-sector inflation 1.5, ageing 0.6 and other factors 0.6. CIHI uses the 2014 to 2019 ageing effect as a proxy for 2023 to 2025 and treats other factors, such as technology, service use and system efficiency, as a residual." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Canadian Institute for Health Information, National health expenditure trends, 2025: Snapshot (27 November 2025), sections on cost drivers and the table 'Annual growth of health care spending, Canada'" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "aging-driven-cost-growth" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:aging-driven-cost-growth .

heiro:6000147 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Aging-Driven Cost Growth"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/files/document/nhe-projections-forecast-summary.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Medicare the Fastest Growing Payer as Baby Boomers Reach Older Ages" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The CMS Office of the Actuary projects US national health spending growing 5.4% a year over 2025 to 2034, faster than GDP at 4.1%, raising health spending from 18.0% to 20.6% of GDP. Medicare grows fastest among major payers, at 7.7% a year on average. For 2029 to 2034, when Medicare growth is projected at 7.3% a year, the actuaries note that the baby boomer generation will enrol through 2029 and its oldest members will reach ages at which health care use typically increases." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Office of the Actuary, National Health Expenditure Projections 2025-2034: Forecast Summary (projections web page last modified 24 June 2026), 'Major findings' and 'Key trends in projected national health expenditures by payer: Medicare'" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "aging-driven-cost-growth" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:aging-driven-cost-growth .

heiro:6000148 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on AI Diagnostic Value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2022-02/guide_dm_vf_english_publi_appendix5.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Descriptive Grid Required for Each Machine Learning Function" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS asks any company submitting a medical device with at least one machine learning function to its device evaluation committee (CNEDiMTS) to complete a descriptive grid, one for each smart function, as part of the reimbursement dossier. The grid covers the claimed use and target population, the data used for initial learning or relearning and how training, validation and test sets were separated, the type of learning and retraining frequency, human involvement, and the metrics used to measure performance. It was added to the submission guide in September 2020." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, LPPR: Dossier submission to the Medical Device and Health Technology Evaluation Committee, Appendix 5 'Specific descriptive information to be provided for medical device functions relying on machine learning processes' (September 2020; grid web page updated 17 February 2022)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ai-diagnostic-value" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ai-diagnostic-value .

heiro:6000149 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on AI Diagnostic Value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/373421"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "External Validation on Representative Data and Risk-Graded Clinical Evidence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's overview of regulatory considerations on AI for health, which it presents as a discussion document rather than guidance, sets out 18 considerations. It advises documenting intended use and training data, and showing performance beyond the training and testing data through external validation on an independent dataset representative of the intended population and setting. Clinical validation should be graded by risk: randomised trials for the highest-risk tools, prospective real-world validation with a comparator elsewhere, and a period of more intense post-deployment monitoring." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Regulatory considerations on artificial intelligence for health (17 October 2023, ISBN 9789240078871), Executive summary, topic area 3 'Intended use, and analytical and clinical validation', and section 5.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ai-diagnostic-value" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ai-diagnostic-value .

heiro:6000150 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Akaike Information Criterion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/nice-dsu/tsds/survival-analysis"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "AIC and BIC Test Fit to Trial Data, Not Extrapolation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 14 states that AIC and BIC, unlike the negative 2 log likelihood, can compare parametric survival models that are not nested, and recommends presenting them alongside log-cumulative hazard plots and clinical plausibility. Model choice can rest on these tests only when survival data are very close to complete; with substantial censoring, external data, clinical plausibility and expert judgement are needed. TSD 21 adds that AIC and BIC give little information about how well a model extrapolates and should not be the sole basis for selection when the aim is to extrapolate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 14: Survival analysis for economic evaluations alongside clinical trials, extrapolation with patient-level data, June 2011, last updated March 2013, sections 3.3, 3.5, 5 and 6.1; NICE DSU Technical Support Document 21: Flexible methods for survival analysis, January 2020, updated March 2022, sections 3.2.2, 3.2.3 and 6.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "akaike-information-criterion" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:akaike-information-criterion .

heiro:6000151 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Akaike Information Criterion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "AIC and BIC Required When Extrapolating Survival Data" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ZIN requires all standard parametric distributions to be evaluated and reported when trial time-to-event data are extrapolated, with spline and cure models allowed only if these are insufficient. Statistical fit must be evaluated using the Akaike and Bayesian information criteria, after visual fit and the proportional hazards assumption, and the AIC and BIC for each distribution studied must be reported. ZIN states that these steps address internal validity only, so clinical and external plausibility of the extrapolation matters more." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland. Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), 16 January 2024, sections 4.5 and 5.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "akaike-information-criterion" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:akaike-information-criterion .

heiro:6000152 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Akaike Information Criterion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-4-model-transition-probabilities-or-variables-transformation-extrapolation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Goodness of Fit by Visual Inspection, AIC and BIC" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For economic models that extrapolate time-to-event data, the PBAC guidelines ask submissions to fit a range of parametric survival models, such as exponential, Weibull, log-normal, log-logistic, gamma and Gompertz, and more flexible approaches such as piecewise spline models. Goodness of fit should be assessed and discussed using visual inspection, Akaike's information criterion and the Bayesian information criterion. The most appropriate model for the base case must be justified, a number of the best-fitting models tested in sensitivity analysis, and the plausibility of predictions in the unobserved period addressed." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0, September 2016, subsection 3A.4.3 (Extrapolation)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "akaike-information-criterion" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:akaike-information-criterion .

heiro:6000153 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Community Preventive Services Task Force on Alcohol Policy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000064 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.thecommunityguide.org/findings/alcohol-excessive-consumption-increasing-alcohol-taxes.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Community Preventive Services Task Force" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Raise Alcohol Taxes to Cut Excessive Drinking and Related Harm" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Community Preventive Services Task Force recommends increasing the unit price of alcohol by raising taxes, on strong evidence from a review of 73 studies, and expects public health effects to be proportional to the size of the increase. Reported price elasticities were -0.50 for beer, -0.64 for wine, -0.79 for spirits and -0.77 for total alcohol, and higher prices were consistently linked to fewer motor vehicle crashes and less cirrhosis mortality. Taxes levied on volume erode with inflation unless adjusted regularly." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Community Preventive Services Task Force, The Guide to Community Preventive Services, Alcohol Excessive Consumption: Increasing Alcohol Taxes (systematic review with search period through October 2007), sections 'Findings and Recommendations', 'Intervention' and 'Summary of Results'" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alcohol-policy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alcohol-policy .

heiro:6000154 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Alcohol Policy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph24/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Consider a Minimum Price per Unit and Review It Regularly" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's prevention guideline on alcohol-use disorders states that making alcohol less affordable is the most effective way of reducing alcohol-related harm. Its first policy recommendation asks government to consider a minimum price per unit, set by taking account of the health and social costs of alcohol-related harm and its effect on consumption, and to review it and alcohol duties regularly so that alcohol does not become more affordable over time. Further policy recommendations cover licensing and marketing, with final decisions left to government." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence, Alcohol-use disorders: prevention (PH24), published 2 June 2010, 'Policy and practice' and recommendations 1 to 3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alcohol-policy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alcohol-policy .

heiro:6000155 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Department of Health (Ireland) on Alcohol Policy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000065 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://assets.gov.ie/206477/b77611ba-d074-4ece-8be2-dab68594141f.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Department of Health (Ireland)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Minimum Price of €0.10 per Gram of Alcohol Since January 2022" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Ireland's Public Health (Alcohol) Act 2018 sets a minimum price of €0.10 per gram of alcohol, so a product's floor price is that rate multiplied by its grams of alcohol, and selling or advertising below it is an offence. The Department of Health's industry guidance records that the section came into operation on 4 January 2022. The Minister may raise the rate by order, not earlier than three years after the section commenced, taking account of evidence on effectiveness, consumption, harm and the price and affordability of alcohol." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Department of Health (Ireland), Public Health (Alcohol) Act 2018 (Number 24 of 2018) Guidance for Industry: Section 11 (reference 2021/TACU/01 V1, December 2021), sections 'Commencement' and 'Minimum price of alcohol products'; Public Health (Alcohol) Act 2018, section 11" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alcohol-policy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alcohol-policy .

heiro:6000156 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Canadian Centre on Substance Use and Addiction on Alcohol Use Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000066 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://csuch.ca/substance-use-costs/current-costs/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Canadian Centre on Substance Use and Addiction" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Alcohol Cost Canada CAD 19.7 Billion in 2020, the Largest Substance Cost" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Canadian Substance Use Costs and Harms project, run by the Canadian Centre on Substance Use and Addiction with the University of Victoria, estimates that substance use cost Canada CAD 49.1 billion in 2020, with alcohol the largest contributor at CAD 19.7 billion, or 40.1% of the total. Costs are grouped into lost productivity, health care, criminal justice and other direct costs. Alcohol caused 17,098 deaths in 2020, and its cost per person rose from CAD 427 in 2007 to CAD 518 in 2020, the latest year with data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Canadian Centre on Substance Use and Addiction and Canadian Institute for Substance Use Research, Canadian Substance Use Costs and Harms (CSUCH), 'Substance Use Costs in Canada in 2020' (web page), drawing on the Canadian Substance Use Costs and Harms 2007-2020 report" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alcohol-use-economics" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alcohol-use-economics .

heiro:6000157 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Alcohol Use Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/medicare-coverage-database/view/ncd.aspx?ncdid=347"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Medicare Covers Annual Alcohol Screening and Four Counselling Sessions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS covers annual alcohol screening for Medicare beneficiaries, including pregnant women, and up to four brief face-to-face behavioural counselling interventions a year for those who screen positive for alcohol misuse without meeting criteria for dependence. Counselling must be furnished by qualified primary care practitioners in a primary care setting, which excludes emergency departments, inpatient hospital settings and skilled nursing facilities, and should follow the 5A's approach. CMS does not prescribe a screening tool, and based coverage on a grade B recommendation from the US Preventive Services Task Force." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, National Coverage Determination 210.8 Screening and Behavioral Counseling Interventions in Primary Care to Reduce Alcohol Misuse (effective for claims with dates of service on or after 14 October 2011), Item/Service Description and Indications and Limitations of Coverage" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alcohol-use-economics" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alcohol-use-economics .

heiro:6000158 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) on Alert Fatigue"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000067 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://healthit.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/SAFER-Guide-3.-CPOE-Final.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Limit Interruptive Alerts and Revise Those With High Override Rates" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ONC's SAFER Guide on computerised provider order entry with decision support recommends that alerts and reminders be informative, actionable and judiciously limited to the most significant, patient-specific notifications, tiered by severity. Organisations should set standards limiting interruptive alerts to the most critical warnings, and a multidisciplinary decision support governance committee should periodically review them and revise alerts in response to user feedback and high override rates. Alert overrides should be classified and analysed to identify improvements." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology, SAFER Guide: Computerized Provider Order Entry with Decision Support (2025 SAFER Guides; self-assessment dated August 2024), Recommended Practices 1.3, 2.1 and 3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alert-fatigue" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alert-fatigue .

heiro:6000159 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Clinical Algorithm Development"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/the-scope-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Evaluations Span the Whole Care Pathway and May Compare Alternative Sequences" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's technology evaluation manual treats the care pathway as the entire sequence of tests and treatments relevant to an evaluation, including the stages after diagnosis or treatment, and states that the range of treatment pathways must be understood to assess a technology's value. For diagnostic technologies used in sequence, every technology in the potential sequence should be included, and variants such as different thresholds may need separate evaluation. Where the technology and comparator both sit within a sequence, the evaluation may compare alternative sequences, counting all patient outcomes that change because of the test or sequence of tests." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 2.2.7, 2.2.14, 2.2.16 and 4.3.19, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "algorithm-development" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:algorithm-development .

heiro:6000160 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on All-Cause Mortality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/approaches-assessment-overall-survival-oncology-clinical-trials"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Overall Survival Defined by Death From Any Cause and Assessed for Harm" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's draft guidance on overall survival in oncology trials defines it as the time from randomisation until death from any cause, and describes it as both an efficacy and a safety endpoint that drug toxicity can lower. All randomised oncology trials should be designed to assess overall survival so that potential harm can be evaluated, with a pre-specified safety analysis even when overall survival is not an alpha-controlled efficacy endpoint. Interim analyses for futility or harm should be event driven, and crossover should generally be limited because it can confound treatment effect estimates." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, Approaches to Assessment of Overall Survival in Oncology Clinical Trials: guidance for industry, draft guidance (August 2025, docket FDA-2024-D-5850), sections II and III.A" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "all-cause-mortality" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:all-cause-mortality .

heiro:6000161 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on All-Cause Mortality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "All-Cause Mortality as the Mortality Indicator for Life-Years Gained" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS requires that, when the reference case is a cost-effectiveness analysis, the health outcome criterion is life-years and the mortality indicator is the all-cause mortality rate. If all interventions are equivalent on length of life, the reference case should be a cost-minimisation analysis. A surrogate that predicts survival is acceptable only with strong, established evidence of its predictive value; without that demonstration, the reference case should assume no difference in survival between the intervention and its comparators, with any assumed survival gain shown in a scenario analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation: methodological guidance (validated July 2020, English version), Guideline 9 and section 2.1.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "all-cause-mortality" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:all-cause-mortality .

heiro:6000162 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Maryland Health Services Cost Review Commission on All-Payer System"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000068 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Laws/StatuteText?article=ghg&section=19-219&enactments=false"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Maryland Health Services Cost Review Commission" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Hospital Rates Set Equitably Among All Purchasers Without Undue Discrimination" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Maryland law directs the Health Services Cost Review Commission to review hospital costs and rates so that each purchaser of hospital services is assured that total costs are reasonable, that aggregate rates are reasonably related to aggregate costs, and that rates are set equitably among all purchasers or classes of purchasers without undue discrimination or preference. Hospitals may charge only rates set under the statute and must comply with the all-payer model contract. The Commission may set rate levels and increases in aggregate or for each hospital, taking account of objective standards of efficiency and effectiveness." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Annotated Code of Maryland, Health-General Article, sections 19-211 and 19-219(a) to (c), the statutory basis of the Commission's rate setting (Maryland General Assembly statute text, accessed 4 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "all-payer-system" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:all-payer-system .

heiro:6000163 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on All-Payer System"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/innovation-models/ahead"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "AHEAD Holds States to Medicare and All-Payer Cost Targets Until 2035" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS describes the AHEAD Model as a voluntary state total cost of care model aimed at multi-payer alignment. The participating states, Maryland in Cohort 1, Connecticut and Hawaii in Cohort 2, and Rhode Island and New York in Cohort 3, are held accountable for statewide or sub-state cost targets for Medicare fee-for-service and across all payers, and for statewide quality targets, with state Medicaid agencies aligning with CMS on hospital global budgets. The model also selects Geo AHEAD entities by competitive bidding to take total cost of care accountability, and runs to 31 December 2035 for all cohorts." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, AHEAD (Achieving Healthcare Efficiency through Accountable Design) Model web page (page last modified 30 September 2026), model overview, components and participants" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "all-payer-system" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:all-payer-system .

heiro:6000164 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Allocation Concealment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/scientific-guidelines/ich-e9-statistical-principles-clinical-trials"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Next Allocation Released Only After Trial Entry Is Confirmed" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The ICH E9 guideline on statistical principles for clinical trials, adopted by the EMA, states that the treatment for the next subject should be allocated only once that subject's entry to the randomised part of the trial has been confirmed. Details that make the sequence predictable, such as block length, should be kept out of the protocol, investigators should generally be blind to block length, and the randomisation schedule should be filed securely by the sponsor or an independent party. In multicentre trials randomisation should be organised centrally, and deterministic dynamic allocation should be avoided." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, ICH E9 Statistical principles for clinical trials, Step 5 (CPMP/ICH/363/96, legal effective date 1 September 1998, current version), section 2.3.2 Randomisation" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "allocation-concealment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:allocation-concealment .

heiro:6000165 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Allocation Concealment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Concealed, Unforeseeable Allocation as a Core Risk of Bias Item" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods name adequate concealment, meaning that allocation to groups cannot be foreseen and is concealed, as a key aspect of its risk of bias assessment of randomised trials, with external randomisation given as an example for trials that cannot be blinded. The Institute stresses that in trials that cannot be blinded, adequate concealment of allocation is needed alongside outcomes that are assessed blind or independently of treating staff. Allocation concealment is assessed across outcomes together with generation of the randomisation sequence, and the risk of bias is then classified as high or low." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 (19 December 2025; English version published 1 July 2026), section 10.1.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "allocation-concealment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:allocation-concealment .

heiro:6000166 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Allocation Concealment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/publications/guidance-validity-clinical-studies-joint-clinical-assessments_en"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cochrane RoB 1 Required for Trials in Joint Clinical Assessments" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Guidance adopted by the HTA Coordination Group requires risk of bias in randomised trials used in EU joint clinical assessments to be assessed with the Cochrane RoB 1 tool, whose domains include biased allocation to interventions, which the guidance calls confounding bias rather than selection bias. Although RoB 2 was intended to replace RoB 1, the group judges RoB 1 currently preferable because it is well established and less time-consuming. A low overall rating requires low risk in all key domains, and assessors should describe the relevant trial characteristic, ideally verbatim, to make each judgement transparent." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment, Guidance on validity of clinical studies, V1.0 (adopted 19 September 2024 under Article 3(7)(d) of Regulation (EU) 2021/2282), section 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "allocation-concealment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:allocation-concealment .

heiro:6000167 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Allocative Efficiency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Technical and Allocative Cost-Effectiveness as Separate Evaluation Aims" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS states that, within its mandate, the prime objective of economic evaluation is to guide public decisions on allocating collective resources. That requires identifying the options on the cost-effectiveness frontier, which it calls technical cost-effectiveness, and the options that allow optimal allocation of available resources, which it calls allocative cost-effectiveness and defines as maximising individual health benefits in a constrained budget. For analyses whose outcome is not based on survival, HAS cautions that a frontier shows technical cost-effectiveness but that the size of an ICER or incremental net benefit is difficult to interpret in allocative terms." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation: methodological guidance (validated July 2020, English version), Guideline 1, sections 1.1 and 2.1.1, and glossary" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "allocative-efficiency" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:allocative-efficiency .

heiro:6000168 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Allocative Efficiency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240020689"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Benefit Packages Should Secure the Greatest Health Gain Within the Budget" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's principles for health benefit packages state that all countries must ensure, through their package selection process, that available resources are used in the most efficient manner by achieving the greatest possible health benefits within the budget constraint. Every decision within a finite budget carries an opportunity cost, because funding one intervention means that other services, which may have produced more health, protected the most vulnerable from impoverishment or improved access for the least advantaged, will not be provided. Where other criteria are prioritised, the resulting health loss must be clearly communicated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Principles of health benefit packages (2021, ISBN 978-92-4-002068-9), principle 3, page 8" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "allocative-efficiency" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:allocative-efficiency .

heiro:6000169 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of United Nations General Assembly on Alma-Ata Declaration"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000041 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/n23/306/84/pdf/n2330684.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "United Nations General Assembly" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Primary Health Care Reaffirmed With an Extra 1% of GDP Target" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The 2023 political declaration on universal health coverage recognises the fundamental role of primary health care, as declared at Alma-Ata and Astana, and calls it the most inclusive, effective and efficient approach to enhancing health and social well-being. It notes an estimate that about 75 per cent of projected health gains from the Sustainable Development Goals could be achieved through primary health care, saving over 60 million lives by 2030. Heads of State and Government committed to prioritise budget allocations for primary health care, recalling the recommended target of an additional 1 per cent of gross domestic product or more." ;
    heiro:2000018 "United Nations General Assembly, Political declaration of the high-level meeting on universal health coverage, resolution A/RES/78/4 (adopted 5 October 2023), paragraphs 30, 31 and 86" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alma-ata-declaration" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alma-ata-declaration .

heiro:6000170 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Alma-Ata Declaration"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/337641"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Funding and Allocation of Resources as a Core Lever for Primary Health Care" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO and UNICEF's operational framework for primary health care, requested by the World Health Assembly to turn the Astana commitments into action, sets out 14 levers, of which funding and allocation of resources is one of four core strategic levers. It calls primary health care the most efficient route to universal health coverage but finds funding insufficient: in 2016 none of 16 low-income countries with data spent more than US$50 per capita on it. Funds should be rationally allocated across primary care, public health interventions and community engagement, and tertiary facilities often receive disproportionately large shares of health budgets." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization and UNICEF, Operational framework for primary health care: transforming vision into action (14 December 2020, ISBN 978-92-4-001783-2), core strategic lever 'Funding and allocation of resources', pages 16 to 17; World Health Assembly resolution WHA72.2 (24 May 2019), paragraph 4(2)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alma-ata-declaration" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alma-ata-declaration .

heiro:6000171 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Alpha Spending"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/78495/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Lan-DeMets Spending With Safeguards on Analysis Timing" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance states that testing every look of a group sequential trial at the conventional one-sided 0.025 level would inflate the Type I error probability. The Lan-DeMets alpha-spending approach specifies how that probability is spent while allowing flexibility in the number and timing of interim analyses, but timing chosen in response to accumulating results can inflate error, so sponsors should set a targeted number of analyses, an approximate schedule and a decision framework independent of the treatment effect. Conventional estimates tend to be biased towards greater effects, and adjusted methods exist." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Adaptive Designs for Clinical Trials of Drugs and Biologics: Guidance for Industry, November 2019, section V.A (Group Sequential Designs)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alpha-spending" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alpha-spending .

heiro:6000172 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of International Council for Harmonisation (ICH) on Alpha Spending"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000037 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://database.ich.org/sites/default/files/E9_Guideline.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "International Council for Harmonisation (ICH)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Interim Analyses Planned in Advance With Type I Error Controlled" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICH E9 requires interim analyses to be carefully planned in advance and described in the protocol, with group sequential designs using statistical monitoring schemes as guidelines for stopping. The protocol should describe the schedule of interim analyses, or at least the considerations governing it, for example where flexible alpha spending function approaches are to be used. The procedures selected should always ensure that the overall probability of type I error is controlled, and the potential effects of early stopping on other important variables should be considered." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICH E9: Statistical Principles for Clinical Trials, current Step 4 version dated 5 February 1998, section 4.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alpha-spending" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alpha-spending .

heiro:6000173 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Alpha Spending"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/reflection-paper-methodological-issues-confirmatory-clinical-trials-planned-adaptive-design_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Flexible Spending of Type I Error With Realistic Interim Aims" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EMA's reflection paper on adaptive designs notes that interim analysis plans adjust the nominal significance level to maintain the overall level, and that statistical theory permits a flexible spending of the type I error over a trial. Stopping very early despite convincing efficacy may be unacceptable when data on safety or secondary endpoints are insufficient, so interim analyses without realistic objectives should be avoided. Interim data should generally pass from an independent statistician to an independent committee, and sponsor involvement is discouraged." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use, Reflection paper on methodological issues in confirmatory clinical trials planned with an adaptive design, CHMP/EWP/2459/02, 18 October 2007, sections 4.1.1 and 4.1.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "alpha-spending" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:alpha-spending .

heiro:6000174 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Ambulatory Care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-B/part-412/subpart-A/section-412.3"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Two-Midnight Benchmark for Admission and a Wider Day Surgery List" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under Medicare Part A, CMS treats an inpatient admission as generally appropriate when the admitting physician expects hospital care to cross two midnights, with shorter inpatient stays allowed on documented clinical judgement and inpatient-only procedures exempt from the benchmark. For ambulatory surgical centres, from 1 January 2026 CMS covers any surgical procedure separately paid under the outpatient prospective payment system that is not designated inpatient only, not reported with an unlisted code and not otherwise excluded. Former exclusions, such as extensive blood loss or major invasion of body cavities, became safety factors for physicians to weigh." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, 42 CFR 412.3 Admissions, paragraph (d), and 42 CFR 416.166 Covered surgical procedures, paragraphs (a), (b)(2) and (d) (eCFR, up to date as of 1 October 2026; as amended at 90 FR 54082 and 54084, 25 November 2025)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ambulatory-care" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ambulatory-care .

heiro:6000175 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Canadian Institute for Health Information on Ambulatory Care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000040 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cihi.ca/en/indicators/ambulatory-care-sensitive-conditions-hospitalizations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Canadian Institute for Health Information" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Hospitalisation for Sensitive Conditions Under 75 as a Signal of Access" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CIHI's indicator measures the age-standardised acute care hospitalisation rate for ambulatory care sensitive conditions per 100,000 population younger than 75, standardised to the 2021 Canadian population. The conditions are epileptic convulsions, chronic lower respiratory disease, asthma, diabetes, heart failure and pulmonary oedema, hypertension and angina, with discharges by death excluded and cardiac procedure cases excluded from some groups. CIHI reads a disproportionately high rate as a sign of problems obtaining appropriate primary care, while noting that the appropriate level of hospitalisation for these conditions is not known." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Canadian Institute for Health Information, Ambulatory Care Sensitive Conditions Hospitalizations, indicator library entry (web page, accessed 4 October 2026), 'Background, Interpretation and Benchmarks', 'Numerator' and 'Method of Adjustment'" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ambulatory-care" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ambulatory-care .

heiro:6000176 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Ambulatory Payment Classification"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-B/part-419/subpart-B/section-419.22"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Inpatient Only List Phased Out of OPPS Exclusions by 1 January 2029" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Medicare regulations list the hospital services that are not paid under the outpatient prospective payment system, and these have included services the Secretary designates as requiring inpatient care. From 1 January 2026 CMS is eliminating that inpatient only list through a three-year transition, with the list removed entirely by 1 January 2029, so that more procedures can be paid in the hospital outpatient setting. Procedures removed from the list since 2021 are exempt from certain medical review activities until CMS finds them more commonly performed as outpatient than as inpatient services." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, 42 CFR 419.22 Hospital services excluded from payment under the hospital outpatient prospective payment system, paragraph (n), and 42 CFR 412.3(d)(2) (eCFR, up to date as of 1 October 2026; as amended at 90 FR 54082, 25 November 2025)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ambulatory-payment-classification" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ambulatory-payment-classification .

heiro:6000177 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Medicare Payment Advisory Commission on Ambulatory Payment Classification"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000069 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.medpac.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Mar26_Ch3_MedPAC_Report_To_Congress_SEC.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Medicare Payment Advisory Commission" ;
    heiro:2000016 "One 2027 Update for IPPS and OPPS and Wider Site-Neutral Payment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "MedPAC's March 2026 report makes a single update recommendation for the inpatient and outpatient prospective payment systems, because their payment indicators are intertwined: update 2026 base rates for 2027 by the current-law amount and implement a Medicare Safety-Net Index with USD 1 billion added. It reports that site-neutral policies cut fee-for-service payments for off-campus outpatient services by USD 1.2 billion in 2024, and sees scope to extend them to on-campus clinic services, to more off-campus services and, as it recommended in June 2023, across hospital outpatient departments, ambulatory surgical centres and freestanding offices." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Medicare Payment Advisory Commission, Report to the Congress: Medicare Payment Policy (March 2026), chapter 3 'Hospital inpatient and outpatient services', Recommendation 3 and section 'Medicare should move toward site-neutral payments'" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ambulatory-payment-classification" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ambulatory-payment-classification .

heiro:6000178 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Academy of Managed Care Pharmacy on AMCP Dossier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000070 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.amcp.org/resource/amcp-format-formulary-submissions-guidance"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Academy of Managed Care Pharmacy" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Advisory Dossier Format, With Economic Value for Approved Products Only" ;
    heiro:2000017 "AMCP describes its Format for Formulary Submissions as advisory and not mandatory. It sets out the clinical and economic evidence that manufacturers present to health care decision-makers in a standard, living product dossier that runs from the pre-approval phase through the post-approval period. The Format recommends economic analyses and models that project budget and cost impact on the decision-maker's organisation and members and assess cost-effectiveness, with assessments of economic value applying only to approved product dossiers. Format 5.0 of April 2024 remains the current version; public comment on version 6.0 closed on 22 April 2026." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Academy of Managed Care Pharmacy, AMCP Format for Formulary Submissions web page (accessed 4 October 2026), covering Format 5.0 (April 2024) and the Format 6.0 public comment period (17 March to 22 April 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "amcp-dossier" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:amcp-dossier .

heiro:6000179 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on AMCP Dossier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/drug-and-device-manufacturer-communications-payors-formulary-committees-and-similar-entities"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Payer Economic Models May Extend Beyond Trial Duration and Setting" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's guidance on manufacturer communications with payers states that health care economic information relates to an approved indication if it concerns the disease, its manifestations or its symptoms in the labelled population, even when it draws on information absent from the labelling. Its examples include modelling economic consequences beyond the duration of the labelled studies where labelling sets no limit on use, extrapolating trial results to other settings, such as from hospital to ambulatory care, and using burden of illness studies. A June 2026 revised draft extends this to devices and will replace the June 2018 final guidance once finalised." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, Drug and Device Manufacturer Communications With Payors, Formulary Committees, and Similar Entities: Questions and Answers, final guidance (June 2018), question A.4, and revised draft guidance (June 2026, docket FDA-2016-D-1307), question 4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "amcp-dossier" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:amcp-dossier .

heiro:6000180 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of United Nations General Assembly on AMR Economic Burden"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000041 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://documents.un.org/doc/undoc/gen/n24/292/73/pdf/n2429273.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "United Nations General Assembly" ;
    heiro:2000016 "10% Cut in AMR-Associated Deaths by 2030 Against a 4.95 Million Baseline" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The 2024 political declaration on antimicrobial resistance commits Heads of State and Government to reduce global deaths associated with bacterial antimicrobial resistance by 10 per cent by 2030 against the 2019 baseline of 4.95 million, of which 1.27 million were directly attributable. Citing World Bank and other estimates, it notes that resistance could add USD 1 trillion a year in health care costs by 2050 and cause GDP losses of USD 1 trillion to 3.4 trillion a year by 2030, and that treating drug-resistant bacterial infections alone could cost up to USD 412 billion a year." ;
    heiro:2000018 "United Nations General Assembly, Political declaration of the high-level meeting on antimicrobial resistance, resolution A/RES/79/2 (adopted 7 October 2024), paragraphs 5, 6 and 16" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "amr-economic-burden" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:amr-economic-burden .

heiro:6000181 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on AMR Economic Burden"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/antimicrobial-resistance"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Costs Driven by Longer Stays and a Global Action Plan for 2026 to 2036" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO states that antimicrobial resistance drives health care costs largely through longer hospital stays and greater need for intensive care, along with more use of second-line antibiotics and extra diagnostic testing. It reports a projection that treating resistant bacterial infections alone could cost USD 412 billion a year up to 2035, with productivity losses of USD 443 billion a year. On 23 May 2026 the World Health Assembly adopted an updated global action plan on antimicrobial resistance for 2026 to 2036, which aims to enable the UN target of a 10% fall in bacterial AMR-associated deaths by 2030." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Antimicrobial resistance fact sheet (16 July 2026), 'A global concern' and 'WHO response'; World Health Assembly decision WHA79(19), Global action plan on antimicrobial resistance 2026 to 2036 (23 May 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "amr-economic-burden" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:amr-economic-burden .

heiro:6000182 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of UK Government Analysis Function on Analysis Error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000071 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/guidance/the-aqua-book"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "UK Government Analysis Function" ;
    heiro:2000016 "All Analysis Assured, With an Assurer Independent of the Analyst" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The 2025 AQuA Book, the UK government guide to producing quality analysis, states that all analysis shall be assured and that the assurer and the analyst shall be independent, with the degree of separation depending on the importance, size and complexity of the work. Quality assurance runs through the whole analytical life cycle and covers verification, checking that analysis is error-free and meets its specification, and validation, checking that it is fit for purpose. Assurance should be proportionate to the risks of the intended use, including financial, legal, operational and reputational effects." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Government Operational Research Service, Government Analysis Function, Government Actuary's Department and Office for National Statistics, The AQuA Book: guidance on producing quality analysis for government (published 30 July 2025), sections 1.3.1 to 1.3.3 and chapter 3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "analysis-error" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:analysis-error .

heiro:6000183 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Analysis Error"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "AdViSHE Validation Report Required for Model-Based Evaluations" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland's guideline requires validation of a model-based economic evaluation to cover the conceptual model, the input data, the software implementation and the model outcomes, reported with the AdViSHE checklist. Technical validation, also called code verification, means checking the correctness of all model calculations, for which TECH-VER can be used. The conceptual model needs at least cross validation against other models and earlier institute assessments, with expert face validation where differences are found, and operational validation compares model results with input data and with empirical data not used as input." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (English version, 16 January 2024), sections 4.8.1 to 4.8.1.4 and 5.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "analysis-error" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:analysis-error .

heiro:6000184 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Analytic Epidemiology"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/real-world-evidence-considerations-regarding-non-interventional-studies-drug-and-biological-products"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Non-Interventional Studies Must Plan for Confounding Before Design Choice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's draft guidance on non-interventional studies lists cohort, case-control and self-controlled designs as options for evidence on drug effectiveness or safety, and warns that inferences may be incorrect if estimates are affected by confounding or by other biases, such as patient selection, misspecified follow-up or differential outcome measurement. Before choosing such a design, sponsors should consider how likely it is to distinguish a true treatment effect from other influences. Protocols should state the approach to causal inference, such as target trial emulation, and to confounding, including assessment of unmeasured confounding." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, Real-World Evidence: Considerations Regarding Non-Interventional Studies for Drug and Biological Products, draft guidance (March 2024, docket FDA-2023-D-5470), sections II and III" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "analytic-epidemiology" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:analytic-epidemiology .

heiro:6000185 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Analytic Epidemiology"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/jcms/p_3284524/en/real-world-studies-for-the-assessment-of-medicinal-products-and-medical-devices"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Comparative Real-World Studies Must Address Indication Bias" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS's guide to real-world studies of medicinal products and medical devices favours descriptive, non-comparative observational studies for documenting use in routine practice, and comparative designs, whether real-world comparative studies, pragmatic trials or conventional trials, for comparing efficacy, safety or efficiency with other products. Without randomisation, HAS recommends measures to minimise indication bias, in particular causal inference methods such as adjustment, matching or weighting where their conditions are met. It also recommends a protocol drafted with a multidisciplinary scientific committee and the use of existing data, especially the national health data system." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Real-world studies for the assessment of medicinal products and medical devices: methodological guide (validated June 2021), sections 2.1 to 2.3 and appendix 3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "analytic-epidemiology" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:analytic-epidemiology .

heiro:6000186 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Closed-Form Analytical Method"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Base Case and Scenario Results Must Come From the Probabilistic Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Dutch guideline for economic evaluations requires the results of both the base case and the scenario analyses to be based on the probabilistic analysis, which varies all uncertain inputs at once by Monte Carlo simulation. The ICER is calculated by dividing mean incremental costs by mean incremental effects across all iterations, and the number of iterations must demonstrably give a stable result. A working random number generator and a fixed random seed are required so that model outcomes can be reproduced." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version, published 16 January 2024), sections 4.7.2.2 and 5.3.1 and the list of changes from the 2016 guideline" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "analytical-method" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:analytical-method .

heiro:6000187 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Anchor-Based MID"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/patient-focused-drug-development-incorporating-clinical-outcome-assessments-endpoints-regulatory"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Anchor-Based Thresholds, With Wider Ranges Where Uncertainty Is Greater" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's draft guidance on clinical outcome assessment endpoints lets sponsors use anchor-based methods to identify a meaningful score difference, mapping known meaningful differences on an external anchor, such as a patient's rating of change in symptom severity, onto the outcome measure. Distribution-based methods do not directly consider the patient voice and are insufficient as the sole basis. A wider range of thresholds should be chosen when uncertainty is greater, for example when the anchor is only weakly associated with the score differences." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, Patient-Focused Drug Development: Incorporating Clinical Outcome Assessments Into Endpoints for Regulatory Decision-Making, draft guidance (Guidance 4, April 2023, docket FDA-2023-D-0026), section III.B.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "anchor-based-mid" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:anchor-based-mid .

heiro:6000188 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Anchor-Based MID"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Responder Thresholds of at Least 15% of the Scale Range Instead of Published MIDs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods prefer patient-reported anchors for deriving minimal important differences in principle, but note that published MIDs for one instrument vary widely with the anchor, observation period and analysis, and that no established standard exists to judge the quality of MID studies. For benefit assessments IQWiG therefore uses responder analyses without further checks only when the response criterion is at least 15% of the instrument's scale range, a value derived from systematic reviews of MIDs; prespecified MIDs below 15% are as a rule not used." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English version published 1 July 2026), section 10.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "anchor-based-mid" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:anchor-based-mid .

heiro:6000189 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Anchor-Based MID"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/content/information/files/pbac-guidelines-version-5.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Anchor Approach Preferred for MCIDs on Scale-Based Patient Outcomes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC submission guidelines state that for patient-relevant outcomes measured on a scale, such as a quality of life instrument or the 6-minute walk distance test, the minimal clinically important difference may be established using an anchor approach, with statistical or consensus approaches less preferred. The MCID can then serve as the threshold beyond which an individual patient counts as a responder. Submissions tabulate each proposed MCID with its source, its method of derivation and any differences in population, outcome definition or baseline value." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), subsection 2.4.4 and Table 2.4.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "anchor-based-mid" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:anchor-based-mid .

heiro:6000190 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of US Environmental Protection Agency on Anchoring Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000027 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2024-12/chapter-7-guidelines-for-preparing-economic-analyses_final_508-compliant.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "US Environmental Protection Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Starting Point Bias in Bidding Games and Range Effects on Payment Cards" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EPA's Guidelines for Preparing Economic Analyses state that iterative bidding in stated preference surveys has been shown to suffer from starting point bias, with respondents' maximum willingness to pay systematically related to the opening dollar amount. They record concerns that the range and intervals of amounts on payment cards may influence answers, and that second responses to double-bounded dichotomous choice questions may not be independent of the first. EPA notes that researchers have generally turned from direct questions to other stated preference formats." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Environmental Protection Agency, Guidelines for Preparing Economic Analyses, 3rd edition (EPA-240-R-24-001, December 2024), chapter 7, section 7.3.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "anchoring" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:anchoring .

heiro:6000191 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Anchoring Bias in Elicitation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/nice-dsu/tsds/expert-elicitation-tsd"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Train Experts on Anchoring and Elicit Before Showing Any Hazard Scenario" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 26 recommends that standard training for experts judging long-term survival covers potential biases from anchoring, availability and overconfidence. When scenario testing is used, experts should give their own probability judgements about survival at the target time before any scenario is presented, so that their judgements are not anchored on it, and the scenario must be presented as an assumption, not a claim about the true hazard. The risk of an unduly influential expert should be discussed in training alongside anchoring effects." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Decision Support Unit, TSD 26: Expert elicitation for long-term survival outcomes (March 2025), sections 2.3, 2.8, 3.4 and 3.7.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "anchoring-bias-in-elicitation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:anchoring-bias-in-elicitation .

heiro:6000192 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Annualisation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42699"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Capital Costs Annualised Over Useful Life in Cost-Effectiveness Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The WHO guide to cost-effectiveness analysis defines capital goods as inputs lasting more than a year and says their cost must be spread over the study period. The economic cost combines the opportunity cost of the investment with depreciation, and where rental markets work poorly the item should be annualised from its purchase value net of resale value, the interest rate and its useful life. Start-up costs should be annualised over the programme's lifetime, with a 10-year period recommended where technologies are not expected to change over a short period." ;
    heiro:2000018 "WHO, Making choices in health: WHO guide to cost-effectiveness analysis (Tan-Torres Edejer and colleagues, editors), 2003, sections 3.2.5, 3.3.2 and 3.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "annualisation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:annualisation .

heiro:6000193 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Anonymization"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/regulatory-procedural-guideline/external-guidance-implementation-european-medicines-agency-policy-publication-clinical-data-medicinal-products-human-use-version-15_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Clinical Reports Anonymised to a Re-identification Risk Threshold of 0.09" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under its clinical data publication policy, EMA treats clinical reports as pseudonymised data that companies must render anonymous before publication, so that participants can no longer be identified by any means reasonably likely to be used by the controller or a third party. EMA advises a conservative re-identification risk threshold of 0.09, letting companies justify a different value, and favours quantitative measurement of risk. Anonymisation should maximise data utility while protecting the privacy of trial participants." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, External guidance on the implementation of the European Medicines Agency policy on the publication of clinical data for medicinal products for human use (EMA/90915/2016, version 1.5, 14 May 2025), sections 3.4.2, 3.4.3, 3.5.2 and 3.5.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "anonymization" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:anonymization .

heiro:6000194 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Antenatal Care Coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng201/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Booking by 10 Weeks and 10 or 7 Routine Antenatal Appointments" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's antenatal care guideline recommends offering a first antenatal booking appointment with a midwife by 10+0 weeks of pregnancy, and within 2 weeks if possible for women who contact or are referred to maternity services later than 9+0 weeks. It recommends planning 10 routine antenatal appointments with a midwife or doctor for nulliparous women and 7 for parous women, with additional or longer appointments offered according to the woman's medical, social and emotional needs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG201, Antenatal care (published 19 August 2021), recommendations 1.1.4, 1.1.5, 1.1.7, 1.1.8 and 1.1.10" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "antenatal-care-coverage" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:antenatal-care-coverage .

heiro:6000195 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of U.S. Department of Health and Human Services on Anti-Kickback Statute"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000024 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://oig.hhs.gov/documents/compliance-guidance/1135/HHS-OIG-GCPG-2023.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Inspector General's Key Questions: Referrals, Fair Market Value, Overuse and Steering" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Compliance guidance from the HHS Office of Inspector General advises that arrangements implicating the anti-kickback statute but falling outside a safe harbour be judged on the totality of facts and circumstances, including the parties' intent. Its key questions ask whether remuneration takes account of the volume or value of business generated, whether it is fair market value for necessary services actually rendered, and whether it could lead to overutilisation, skewed clinical decisions, cherry-picking healthy patients, lemon-dropping costly ones or steering." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Office of Inspector General, General Compliance Program Guidance (2023), section II.A, Federal Anti-Kickback Statute and Key Questions" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "anti-kickback-statute" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:anti-kickback-statute .

heiro:6000196 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Antibiotic Stewardship"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-G/part-482/subpart-C/section-482.42"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Hospital Antibiotic Stewardship Programmes as a Medicare Condition of Participation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "As a condition of participation in Medicare and Medicaid, a hospital must run an active hospital-wide programme for optimising antibiotic use through stewardship. The governing body appoints a qualified leader on the advice of medical staff and pharmacy leadership, and the programme must coordinate infection control, quality improvement, medical, nursing and pharmacy services, document evidence-based antibiotic use and improvements in it, follow nationally recognised guidelines and best practices, and reflect the scope and complexity of the hospital's services." ;
    heiro:2000018 "42 CFR 482.42, Condition of participation: Infection prevention and control and antibiotic stewardship programs, introductory text and paragraphs (b) and (c) (eCFR, up to date as of 1 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "antibiotic-stewardship" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:antibiotic-stewardship .

heiro:6000197 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of UK Health Security Agency on Antibiotic Stewardship"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000072 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/antimicrobial-stewardship-start-smart-then-focus/start-smart-then-focus-antimicrobial-stewardship-toolkit-for-inpatient-care-settings"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "UK Health Security Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Review Antimicrobials at 48 to 72 Hours and Record One of Five Outcomes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "UKHSA's Start Smart Then Focus toolkit for inpatient settings asks prescribers to document the indication, drug, dose, route and a treatment duration or review date. Clinicians should then review the diagnosis and the continuing need for antimicrobials at 48 to 72 hours and record a plan using five review outcomes: cease, amend (ideally to a narrower spectrum), refer to non-ward based antimicrobial therapy services, extend with a next review or stop date, or switch from intravenous to oral." ;
    heiro:2000018 "UK Health Security Agency, Start smart then focus: antimicrobial stewardship toolkit for inpatient care settings (last updated 12 September 2023), toolkit summary and section 3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "antibiotic-stewardship" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:antibiotic-stewardship .

heiro:6000198 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control on Antimicrobial Resistance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000073 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecdc.europa.eu/sites/default/files/documents/antimicrobial-resistance-eu-annual-epidemiological-report-2024.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control" ;
    heiro:2000016 "EU Unlikely to Meet All 2030 Resistance Targets Without Stronger Action" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ECDC's surveillance report on 2024 data finds the estimated EU incidence of MRSA bloodstream infections 20.4% below the 2019 baseline, already past its 2030 target of a 15% reduction. Bloodstream infections with third-generation cephalosporin-resistant E. coli were 5.9% higher, against a 10% reduction target, and carbapenem-resistant Klebsiella pneumoniae 61.0% higher, against a 5% reduction target. ECDC concludes that without stronger, swifter public health action the EU is unlikely to reach all its targets by 2030." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control, Antimicrobial resistance in the EU/EEA (EARS-Net): Annual Epidemiological Report for 2024 (November 2025), key facts and discussion" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "antimicrobial-resistance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:antimicrobial-resistance .

heiro:6000199 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Antimicrobial Resistance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/news/item/25-05-2026-the-world-health-assembly-adopts-updated-global-action-plan-on-antimicrobial-resistance-(2026-2036)"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Updated Global Action Plan Sets the One Health Framework to 2036" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Seventy-ninth World Health Assembly adopted the updated Global Action Plan on antimicrobial resistance for 2026 to 2036 on 23 May 2026, developed by the Quadripartite organisations FAO, UNEP, WHO and WOAH. WHO describes it as the global One Health framework for coordinated action to 2036, building on the 2015 plan and the 2024 UN political declaration. It aims to speed progress towards agreed targets and to help countries develop, implement and finance multisectoral national action plans, with weight on accountability, monitoring and sustainable financing." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, The World Health Assembly adopts updated Global Action Plan on Antimicrobial Resistance (2026 to 2036), departmental update, 25 May 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "antimicrobial-resistance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:antimicrobial-resistance .

heiro:6000200 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Australian Government Department of Health, Disability and Ageing on Appeal Process"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000038 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.pbs.gov.au/general/independent-review/independent-review-pbs-info-for-applicants"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Australian Government Department of Health, Disability and Ageing" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Independent Review of PBAC Rejections, Limited to Evidence Already Considered" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Australia's Independent Review (PBS), set up under the Australia-United States Free Trade Agreement, is open to any applicant whose PBAC submission did not lead to a recommendation to list, or whose requested extension of an existing listing was declined. The review rests only on information already before the PBAC; where new evidence is relevant, a resubmission must be made instead. A convenor appoints a reviewer free of conflicts of interest, and the PBAC reconsiders the submission with the review findings and the sponsor's comments." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Australian Government Department of Health, Disability and Ageing, Independent Review (PBS): information for applicants (PBS website, last updated 8 February 2019); PBS procedure guidance, section 7.2 Independent review" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "appeal-process" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:appeal-process .

heiro:6000201 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Appeals Design"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-B/part-422/subpart-C/section-422.122"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Specific Denial Reasons and Public Reporting of Approvals After Appeal" ;
    heiro:2000017 "From 1 January 2026, a Medicare Advantage organisation that denies a prior authorisation request must give the provider a specific reason for the denial, whatever the method of communication. Each year it must also post on its website prior authorisation data for the previous year, including the percentages of standard requests approved, denied and approved after appeal, and the average and median time from request to decision. CMS applies matching denial reason and reporting rules to other payers covered by its interoperability and prior authorisation final rule." ;
    heiro:2000018 "42 CFR 422.122, Prior authorization requirements, paragraphs (a) and (c) (eCFR, up to date as of 1 October 2026); CMS Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule (CMS-0057-F) fact sheet" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "appeals-design" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:appeals-design .

heiro:6000202 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Applicability Assessment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/content/information/files/pbac-guidelines-version-5.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Tabulate Trial Versus Australian Setting Differences and Test Those That Matter" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines ask each submission to tabulate important differences between the trial setting and the Australian setting after listing, covering the population, disease severity, circumstances of use, concomitant treatments and the health care system. Each difference is judged either unlikely to have an effect or as needing further investigation, which is then addressed through translation analyses in the economic evaluation. Differences that may affect comparative effectiveness or safety can undermine the assumptions needed for a cost-minimisation approach and then call for a supplementary analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 2.7.1 and 2.7.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "applicability-assessment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:applicability-assessment .

heiro:6000203 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Appraisal"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/jcms/c_1729421/en/transparency-committee"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Transparency Committee Rates Clinical Benefit and Added Value; Ministers Decide" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In France, a company seeking reimbursement of a medicine submits a dossier to HAS after marketing authorisation, and the Transparency Committee, HAS's dedicated appraisal committee, issues an opinion. Its rating of clinical benefit bears on the yes or no reimbursement decision and the reimbursement rate set by the national health insurance union, while its rating of clinical added value is used in price negotiations. The final listing decision rests with the ministers for health and social security and is published in the Official Journal." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Transparency Committee web page (6 March 2024), Assessment Process" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "appraisal" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:appraisal .

heiro:6000204 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Appraisal"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/content/information/files/pbac-guidelines-version-5.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Positive Advice for a Costlier Medicine Without a Significant Health Gain" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC recommends to the Minister which medicines should be subsidised and, under the National Health Act 1953, must consider effectiveness and cost against alternative therapies. It cannot recommend a medicine that is substantially more costly than an alternative unless satisfied that it also provides a significant improvement in health. Its decisions weigh five quantitative factors, including comparative health gain, cost-effectiveness and projected net cost to the PBS, with less readily quantified factors such as confidence in the evidence, equity, clinical need and severity." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), 'Key factors influencing decision making by the PBAC'" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "appraisal" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:appraisal .

heiro:6000205 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Appraisal Committee"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/our-approach/methods-process/value-assessment-framework/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Independent Committees Vote on Clinical Benefit, Then Long-Term Value for Money" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER convenes three standing appraisal committees, the New England and Midwest CEPACs and the California Technology Assessment Forum, whose members meet strict conflict of interest rules and need not include people affected by the condition. At each public meeting the committee votes on whether current evidence is adequate to show a greater net health benefit than the comparator, then rates long-term value for money at current or assumed pricing as high, intermediate or low, integrating cost-effectiveness, benefits beyond health and special ethical priorities." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023), sections 2.6 and 5; ICER Processes for Conducting Value Assessments (25 September 2023), Independent Appraisal Committees" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "appraisal-committee" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:appraisal-committee .

heiro:6000206 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Appraisal Committee"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/jcms/c_1729421/en/transparency-committee"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Transparency Committee of 29 Voting Members Plus 7 Advisory Members" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS's Transparency Committee has 29 voting members, 22 full members and 7 substitutes chosen for scientific expertise, including a chair drawn from the HAS Board and two vice-chairs. They include expert doctors and pharmacists, specialists in methodology and epidemiology, and two representatives of patient and user associations. Seven further members take part in an advisory role, representing bodies including the Social Security Directorate, the national health insurance fund and the medicines agency ANSM. Members serve three-year terms, renewable twice." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Transparency Committee web page (6 March 2024), Composition" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "appraisal-committee" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:appraisal-committee .

heiro:6000207 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Appropriate Care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/Regulations-and-Guidance/Guidance/Manuals/downloads/pim83c13.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reasonable and Necessary Means Care That Meets, but Does Not Exceed, Need" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS instructs the Medicare administrative contractors that write local coverage determinations to treat an item or service as reasonable and necessary only if it is safe and effective, not experimental or investigational, and appropriate. Appropriateness covers duration and frequency and requires that the service follows accepted standards of medical practice, is given in a setting suited to the patient's needs by qualified personnel, meets but does not exceed the patient's medical need, and is at least as beneficial as an available medically appropriate alternative." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Medicare Program Integrity Manual (Pub. 100-08), Chapter 13, Local Coverage Determinations (Rev. 863, issued 12 February 2019), section 13.5.4, applying Social Security Act section 1862(a)(1)(A)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "appropriate-care" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:appropriate-care .

heiro:6000208 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Area Under Curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-clinical-evaluation-diagnostic-agents-revision-1_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ROC Curves Mainly Used to Select Cut-off Points" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EMA guideline on diagnostic agents treats ROC curves mainly as a way to select cut-off points for prospective use in confirmatory trials. In comparative trials with an active comparator, the area under the ROC curve can illustrate diagnostic performance by turning sensitivity and specificity into a single variable, although observed differences may be hard to interpret clinically. The guideline also asks for sensitivity and specificity with confidence intervals, cut-off values fixed in the protocol before the data are evaluated, and discussion of the effect of disease prevalence on predictive values." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use, Guideline on clinical evaluation of diagnostic agents (CPMP/EWP/1119/98/Rev. 1), adopted 23 July 2009, in effect from 1 February 2010, sections 7.2, 7.2.1 and 7.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "area-under-curve" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:area-under-curve .

heiro:6000209 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Area Under Curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/77642/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ROC Summary Metric Recommended as Primary CADe Endpoint" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For reader studies of computer-assisted detection devices in radiology, FDA recommends an ROC summary performance metric, such as the area or partial area under the ROC curve or its FROC and LROC variants, as part of the primary analysis, with sensitivity and specificity at a clinical action point as secondary endpoints. Sensitivity and specificity should come from explicit clinical decisions by readers, not from the rating data used to build the curves. Where endpoints use the area or partial area, plots of the actual curves should accompany parametric and non-parametric summary results." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Clinical Performance Assessment: Considerations for Computer-Assisted Detection Devices Applied to Radiology Images and Radiology Device Data in Premarket Notification (510(k)) Submissions, Guidance for Industry and FDA Staff, 28 September 2022, section IV.A" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "area-under-curve" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:area-under-curve .

heiro:6000210 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Artificial Intelligence in Health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240029200"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Six Ethical Principles for AI in Health, With Humans in Control of Decisions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's guidance on the ethics and governance of artificial intelligence for health sets six consensus principles covering autonomy; human well-being, safety and the public interest; transparency, explainability and intelligibility; responsibility and accountability; inclusiveness and equity; and AI that is responsive and sustainable. Under the autonomy principle, humans should remain in full control of health care systems and medical decisions, and AI systems should be designed to help providers and patients make informed decisions, with meaningful human oversight that may depend on the risks of the system." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Ethics and governance of artificial intelligence for health: WHO guidance (28 June 2021, ISBN 9789240029200), executive summary and sections 5 and 5.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "artificial-intelligence-in-health" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:artificial-intelligence-in-health .

heiro:6000211 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Artificial Intelligence in Health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/reflection-paper-use-artificial-intelligence-ai-medicinal-product-lifecycle_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Risk-Based Scrutiny of AI in Medicines, With Models Frozen Before Unblinding" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA's reflection paper on AI in the medicinal product lifecycle makes the trial sponsor, applicant or marketing authorisation holder responsible for ensuring that all algorithms, models, datasets and data pipelines are fit for purpose, with scrutiny scaled to patient risk and regulatory impact. In late-stage confirmatory trials, models used for high-impact purposes such as the primary endpoint should be tested on prospectively generated data, incremental learning is not accepted, and the data pipeline and models should be frozen and documented in the statistical analysis plan before unblinding. AI applications affecting indication or posology count as high risk." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Reflection paper on the use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the medicinal product lifecycle (EMA/CHMP/CVMP/83833/2023, adopted, published 30 September 2024), sections 2.2, 2.3.3.3 and 2.3.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "artificial-intelligence-in-health" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:artificial-intelligence-in-health .

heiro:6000212 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on ASCOT"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg20/chapter/incorporating-economic-evaluation"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ASCOT Named Among Measures for Interventions With a Social Care Focus" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NICE guideline manual sets a reference case that varies with the decision problem. For interventions with a social care focus, its reference case table lists capability or social care-related quality of life measures, such as ICECAP or ASCOT, for interventions that result in both health and capability or social care outcomes, alongside health effects measured in QALYs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Developing NICE guidelines: the manual (PMG20), chapter 7 Incorporating economic evaluation, table 7.1 reference case" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ascot" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ascot .

heiro:6000213 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of European Commission Expert Group on Clinical Trials on Assent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000074 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/d721d6cb-687a-477f-b40f-8c7922e9ec9a_en?filename=2017_09_18_ethical_consid_ct_with_minors.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "European Commission Expert Group on Clinical Trials" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Minors' Refusal Respected and Agreement Sought Even Where Assent Is Not Law" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Recommendations of the expert group on clinical trials, published by the European Commission for the EU Clinical Trials Regulation, note that the Regulation lets national law require a capable minor's assent in addition to the legal representative's consent, and that the investigator must respect the explicit wish of a capable minor to refuse or withdraw. Where assent is not a legal requirement, the group recommends that investigators systematically seek the minor's agreement. Assent or agreement alone never suffices without consent from the parents or legally designated representative." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Commission, Ethical considerations for clinical trials on medicinal products conducted with minors: recommendations of the expert group on clinical trials for the implementation of Regulation (EU) No 536/2014, Revision 1 (18 September 2017), EudraLex Volume 10, sections 5.2, 5.4 and 7.1.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "assent" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:assent .

heiro:6000214 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences on Assent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000031 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://cioms.ch/wp-content/uploads/2017/01/WEB-CIOMS-EthicalGuidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Assent as a Process, and a Child's Deliberate Objection Respected" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The CIOMS international ethical guidelines require, before research with children or adolescents, both permission from a parent or legally authorised representative and the child's assent, obtained in keeping with capacity after information suited to the child's maturity. Assent is a process, not merely the absence of dissent, and is preferably written for literate children. A child's refusal or deliberate objection must be respected even if parents consent, unless the research offers treatment unavailable elsewhere with a clear prospect of clinical benefit and is judged the best available medical option." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences, International ethical guidelines for health-related research involving humans, fourth edition (2016), Guideline 17 and commentary" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "assent" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:assent .

heiro:6000215 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Tri-Council Agencies of Canada (CIHR, NSERC and SSHRC) on Assent"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000233,
        heiro:5000234,
        heiro:5000235 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://ethics.gc.ca/eng/tcps2-eptc2_2022_chapter3-chapitre3.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Tri-Council Agencies of Canada (CIHR, NSERC and SSHRC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Dissent Precludes Participation for People Who Lack Legal Capacity" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Canada's Tri-Council Policy Statement requires researchers, where an authorised third party has consented for a person who lacks legal capacity but can partly understand the research, to ascertain that person's wishes, and the prospective participant's dissent precludes participation. The approach rests on decision-making capacity, not age, and covers children whose capacity is developing and adults whose capacity is diminishing or fluctuating. Children unable to assent at enrolment must be asked for assent once they understand, and those who mature must give their own consent for participation to continue." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Canadian Institutes of Health Research, Natural Sciences and Engineering Research Council of Canada and Social Sciences and Humanities Research Council, Tri-Council Policy Statement: Ethical Conduct for Research Involving Humans, TCPS 2 (2022), Chapter 3, Articles 3.3 and 3.10" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "assent" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:assent .

heiro:6000216 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Assessment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/about-us/committees/scientific-advisory-board-war"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Scientific Board Checks the Assessment Before the Package Committee Advises" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In the Netherlands, the National Health Care Institute assesses whether care can be reimbursed from the basic health insurance package and receives advice from two independent committees. The Scientific Advisory Board reviews whether the Institute's conclusions on how well care works, for which patients, and on the quality of the cost-effectiveness assessment are scientifically well founded. The Insured Package Advisory Committee then advises from a societal perspective against the criteria of necessity, effectiveness, cost-effectiveness and feasibility, mainly where social impact is high, and the Minister of Health, Welfare and Sport decides." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Scientific Advisory Board (WAR) and Insured Package Advisory Committee (ACP) committee pages, English website (accessed 5 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "assessment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:assessment .

heiro:6000217 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of National Disability Insurance Agency on Assistive Technology Coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000076 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ndis.gov.au/participants/assistive-technology/types-assistive-technology/what-mid-cost-or-high-cost-assistive-technology"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "National Disability Insurance Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Assistive Technology Tiers at AUD 1,500 and AUD 15,000 Set the Evidence Needed" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Australia's National Disability Insurance Scheme funds assistive technology that is directly related to a participant's disability, helps towards their goals, is safe and is supported by evidence. Low-cost, low-risk items under AUD 1,500 can be bought from the consumables budget without quotes, assessments or prior approval. Mid-cost items of AUD 1,500 to 15,000 need a recommendation from an assistive technology advisor, and high-cost items above AUD 15,000, such as powered wheelchairs and prosthetics, need an assessment by a qualified assessor and may first have to be trialled or rented." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Disability Insurance Agency, NDIS website: What is low-cost assistive technology (page current as of 31 August 2026) and What is mid-cost or high-cost assistive technology (page current as of 30 June 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "assistive-technology-coverage" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:assistive-technology-coverage .

heiro:6000218 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Assumption Structure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/content/information/files/pbac-guidelines-version-5.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Justify Excluded Health States and Test Alternative Plausible Structures" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines ask that a model capture all relevant health states and clinical events, stay consistent with the clinical management algorithms, be informed by the literature and be checked for face validity. Submissions must justify excluding any potentially relevant state or event, discuss its effect on outputs and explain departures from existing models. Plausible alternative structures should be tested, structural assumptions parameterised where evidence or expert opinion allows and otherwise explored in scenario analyses, since probabilistic analysis cannot address structural uncertainty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 3A.2.2 and 3A.9" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "assumption-structure" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:assumption-structure .

heiro:6000219 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Assumption Structure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Explain Structural Choices and Test Extrapolation Assumptions Systematically" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS's guidance on economic evaluation asks that model type and structure represent patients' clinical progression and care without needless complexity, with the states, events and links chosen to capture differences in costs and outcomes and with attention to how intercurrent events such as adverse events and treatment stoppages are handled. When extrapolation is used, every assumption, especially on treatment duration and effect size, should be detailed and justified. Sensitivity analyses should quantify the impact of modelling assumptions, and the effect of extrapolation assumptions should be explored systematically." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation, methodological guidance (2020 version, adopted 2 July 2020), sections 4.2, 4.3 and 4.5 and Guidelines 22, 23 and 25" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "assumption-structure" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:assumption-structure .

heiro:6000220 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Atkinson Index"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/resources/support-document-health-inequalities-15313210669/chapter/distributional-cost-effectiveness-analysis-methods"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Health Inequality Aversion Weights Applied to QALYs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE accepts distributional cost-effectiveness analysis only as supporting evidence of a technology's effect on health inequalities, and only where there is clear evidence of a significant burden of inequality in the eligible population. Its support document states that results should not weight costs or benefits differently by the social characteristics of those affected, including through mathematical functions that use health inequality aversion parameters. Outputs are instead the distributions of health benefits, health opportunity costs and net health benefit across deprivation quintiles, with descriptive metrics such as gaps, ratios or simple regressions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.12.1 to 4.12.8, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE support document: Health inequalities, chapter Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis methods, last updated 13 May 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "atkinson-index" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:atkinson-index .

heiro:6000221 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of US Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) on Attachment Point"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000024 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-B/part-153"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "US Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reinsurance Attachment Point and the USD 1 Million High-Cost Risk Pool" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HHS rules define the attachment point as the threshold of an issuer's claims costs for one enrollee in a benefit year after which those costs become eligible for reinsurance payments. Under the transitional reinsurance programme for 2014 to 2016, each payment was the coinsurance rate multiplied by claims costs between the attachment point and the reinsurance cap. In the current risk adjustment programme, the high-cost risk pool pays 60% of an enrollee's claims above USD 1 million, a threshold HHS kept for the 2027 benefit year, funded by a charge on premiums." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Department of Health and Human Services, Code of Federal Regulations title 45, part 153 (eCFR current text), sections 153.20 and 153.230; HHS Notice of Benefit and Payment Parameters for 2027, final rule, 91 FR 29525 (20 May 2026), HHS risk adjustment section" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "attachment-point" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:attachment-point .

heiro:6000222 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Disease Control and Prevention on Attack Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000032 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/csels/dsepd/ss1978/lesson3/section2.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Attack Rate as Incidence Proportion in Outbreak Settings" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CDC's epidemiology training text lists attack rate, risk and cumulative incidence as synonyms for incidence proportion: new cases during a specified period divided by the population at the start, a proportion because every case is also in the denominator. In outbreaks the term means the risk over a period such as the outbreak's duration. An overall attack rate divides all new cases by the whole population, a food-specific rate uses people who ate a given food, and a secondary attack rate counts cases among contacts, often with household members minus primary cases as the denominator." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Principles of Epidemiology in Public Health Practice, third edition (SS1978), Lesson 3: Measures of Risk, Section 2: Morbidity Frequency Measures, page last reviewed 18 May 2012 (CDC archive)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "attack-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:attack-rate .

heiro:6000223 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of U.S. Department of Health and Human Services on Attributable Cost Estimation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000024 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ahrq.gov/hai/pfp/haccost2017-results.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Hospital Costs Counted Only if They Would Not Have Occurred Without the Condition" ;
    heiro:2000017 "AHRQ's estimates for hospital-acquired conditions define additional cost as the incremental hospital cost of the inpatient stay attributable to the condition, limited to costs that would not have occurred without it and excluding lost work days and readmissions. Charges were converted to costs with cost-to-charge ratios and per-case estimates pooled by meta-analysis in 2015 dollars, from USD 13,793 for catheter-associated urinary tract infection to USD 48,108 for central line-associated bloodstream infection. Excess mortality was estimated separately as a difference in probabilities of death." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality, Estimating the Additional Hospital Inpatient Cost and Mortality Associated With Selected Hospital-Acquired Conditions (November 2017), Results, Exhibits 7 and 8" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "attributable-cost-estimation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:attributable-cost-estimation .

heiro:6000224 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Attributable Fraction"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240096745"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Alcohol-Attributable Fractions: Deaths That Would Disappear Without Alcohol" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's global status report on alcohol defines alcohol-attributable fractions as the contribution of alcohol as a risk factor, interpreted as the proportion of deaths or disease burden that would disappear if there had been no alcohol consumption in a population. WHO quantifies the burden for 31 health conditions and estimates that 2.6 million deaths, 4.7% of all deaths, were attributable to alcohol in 2019. The fractions can be negative: for ischaemic stroke and diabetes mellitus the estimated net effect of alcohol was beneficial." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Global status report on alcohol and health and treatment of substance use disorders (25 June 2024, ISBN 9789240096745), sections 2.2.1 and 2.2.2 and Figure 2.18" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "attributable-fraction" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:attributable-fraction .

heiro:6000225 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Attributable Risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Risk Differences Are Study-Specific and Derived From a Plausible Baseline Risk" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods favour relative effect measures when grading the extent of added benefit, because a risk difference depends strongly on the baseline risk in the control group, which varies between regions, populations, comparator therapies and over time, and is capped by that baseline risk. A risk difference should therefore be read as a descriptive measure of a specific study, not a fixed property of a treatment. For health information, IQWiG converts pooled relative effects into absolute risk differences using a plausible baseline risk, usually the median control group risk of the included studies." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English version published 1 July 2026), Appendix A and section 7.7" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "attributable-risk" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:attributable-risk .

heiro:6000226 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Attributable Risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/content/information/files/pbac-guidelines-version-5.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Report Risk Differences With Relative Risks, Converting From Odds Ratios" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines ask submissions to report dichotomous outcomes and important adverse events as both relative risks and risk differences with 95% confidence intervals, and to present relative risk, odds ratio and risk difference separately in meta-analyses. For indirect comparisons, the outcome measure showing the least heterogeneity across trials should be applied; where the desired final outcome is an absolute risk difference but a relative measure is more consistent across trials, the comparison should be run on odds ratios and converted to a risk difference." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 2.5.1, 2.5.2, 2.6.2 and 2.6.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "attributable-risk" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:attributable-risk .

heiro:6000227 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Attrition"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Results Set Aside if Over 30% Are Excluded or Arms Differ by 15 Points" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods assess missing data in two steps. Results are generally not used in a benefit assessment if more than 30% of the participants who should be analysed are left out entirely, or if the proportions left out differ between groups by more than 15 percentage points, which indicates non-random exclusion; these limits are guidance, with exceptions where exclusions are shown to be completely at random. Losses to follow-up among analysed participants have no fixed limit and are judged by their number, timing and reasons." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English version published 1 July 2026), section 10.3.11" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "attrition" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:attrition .

heiro:6000228 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Attrition"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-missing-data-confirmatory-clinical-trials_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Maximum for Missing Data, but Ignoring It Is Not Acceptable" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA's guideline on missing data in confirmatory trials states that trials should aim to capture all data from all patients, including those who discontinue treatment, and that simply ignoring missing data is not an acceptable option. There is no rule on the maximum acceptable amount, but a substantial proportion can leave efficacy unestablished. Because every handling method rests on assumptions that cannot be verified, methods must be pre-specified in the protocol or analysis plan, and robustness should almost always be checked with sensitivity analyses making different assumptions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Guideline on missing data in confirmatory clinical trials (EMA/CPMP/EWP/1776/99 Rev. 1, adopted 2010, legal effective date 1 January 2011), executive summary and sections 5.1 and 7" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "attrition" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:attrition .

heiro:6000229 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Attrition"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Complete Case Analysis Only Below 5% Missing, Otherwise Multiple Imputation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Dutch guideline for economic evaluations requires missing data to be handled in the statistical analysis, since ignoring it can distort estimates and precision. Simple methods such as complete case analysis usually suffice only when under 5% of data are missing or data are missing completely at random; where data are missing at random, multiple imputation must be used, with scenario analyses testing that assumption because it cannot be told apart from missing not at random. Reports should state the amount of missing data, the likely mechanism and any imputation methods." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version, published 16 January 2024), sections 4.3 and 5.2.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "attrition" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:attrition .

heiro:6000230 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Auction Theory"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/02/2025-21767/medicare-and-medicaid-programs-calendar-year-2026-home-health-prospective-payment-system-hh-pps-rate"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Capacity-Based Bid Cut-Off and a 75th Percentile Single Payment Amount" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Medicare's DMEPOS competitive bidding ranks suppliers' composite bids from lowest and sets the cut-off at the bid where cumulative supplier capacity meets or exceeds projected demand; bidders at or below it become contract suppliers. CMS's 2025 final rule replaces the maximum winning bid for the lead item, used since Round 2021, with the 75th percentile of winning bids, after median pricing before 2021, and limits the number of contracts awarded, because awarding too many weakens the incentive to bid competitively. Lead item pricing itself was adopted to stop unbalanced bidding across items." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Medicare and Medicaid Programs; Calendar Year 2026 Home Health Prospective Payment System (HH PPS) Rate Update; ... DMEPOS Competitive Bidding Program Updates, final rule CMS-1828-F, 90 FR 55342 (2 December 2025), section VII.B; 42 CFR 414.416(b)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "auction-theory-health" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:auction-theory-health .

heiro:6000231 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Auction Theory"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240011878"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Tender Award Criteria and Single or Split Tenders for Medicines" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's pricing guideline conditionally recommends that countries use tendering for pharmaceutical products, with price considered alongside product quality, product characteristics, availability, supply security, supply reliability and supply chain charges, and with tendering combined with other pricing policies and complemented by price negotiation. Its implementation considerations ask purchasers to explore options for structuring the tender, such as single versus split tenders, to exploit market size and purchasing power while ensuring supply security, to base order quantities on reliable estimates of need, and to penalise intentional failure to supply." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, WHO guideline on country pharmaceutical pricing policies (ISBN 9789240011878, 28 September 2020), recommendations 6.A and 6.B and section 3.6 (Tendering and negotiation)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "auction-theory-health" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:auction-theory-health .

heiro:6000232 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Audit and Feedback"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng15/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Regular Feedback to Individual Prescribers in Antimicrobial Stewardship" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's antimicrobial stewardship guideline recommends that stewardship programmes consider regular feedback to individual prescribers in all care settings on their antimicrobial prescribing and related patient safety incidents, using professional regulatory numbers and cost centre codes, and integrating audit into existing quality improvement programmes. Organisations should consider updating individual prescribers and prescribing leads at least every year, with individual prescribing benchmarked against local and national rates and trends, and should support stewardship teams to give feedback and advice to prescribers who prescribe outside local guidelines without justification." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG15, Antimicrobial stewardship: systems and processes for effective antimicrobial medicine use (published 18 August 2015), recommendations 1.1.3, 1.1.6 and 1.1.9" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "audit-and-feedback" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:audit-and-feedback .

heiro:6000233 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Healthcare Quality Improvement Partnership on Audit and Feedback"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000077 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hqip.org.uk/guidance/best-practice-in-clinical-audit/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Healthcare Quality Improvement Partnership" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Clinical Audit as a Cycle of Measurement Against Standards and Action" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HQIP's guide to best practice in local clinical audit describes audit and feedback as improving care by reviewing clinical performance against explicit standards and directing action towards areas that fall short. It defines clinical audit as a quality improvement cycle of measuring care against agreed standards and acting to bring practice in line, run through stages of preparation and planning, measuring performance, implementing change and sustaining improvement. Audit works best within a board-approved quality improvement programme and should not be used when another method is more appropriate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Healthcare Quality Improvement Partnership, Best practice in clinical audit (April 2020; guidance page dated 14 May 2020), introduction, definition of clinical audit and prerequisites" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "audit-and-feedback" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:audit-and-feedback .

heiro:6000234 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Products Regulatory Authority on Automatic Substitution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000078 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://assets.hpra.ie/data/docs/default-source/external-guidance-document/aut-g0115-guide-to-interchangeable-medicines-v7.pdf?sfvrsn=c97fb259_10"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Products Regulatory Authority" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pharmacists May Substitute Only Medicines on the HPRA Interchangeable List" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the Health (Pricing and Supply of Medical Goods) Act 2013, the HPRA establishes and maintains the List of Interchangeable Medicines, and pharmacists may substitute a lower-cost medicine only within a group on that list. Interchangeable medicines share the same active substance and strength, pharmaceutical form and route of administration, contain no more than two active substances and, for generics, have shown bioequivalence. Biological medicines are excluded, as are products that cannot be safely substituted, such as some narrow therapeutic range medicines. A prescriber can prevent substitution by writing 'do not substitute' on the prescription." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Products Regulatory Authority, Guide to Interchangeable Medicines (AUT-G0115, version 7, 23 January 2023), sections 1.1, 1.2, 2 and 3, under the Health (Pricing and Supply of Medical Goods) Act 2013" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "automatic-substitution" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:automatic-substitution .

heiro:6000235 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Norwegian Medical Products Agency on Automatic Substitution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000030 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.dmp.no/en/public-funding-and-pricing/medicine-substitution-in-pharmacies/what-are-substitutable-medicines"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Norwegian Medical Products Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Biosimilars on the Substitution List, With Reservation Only for Medical Reasons" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Pharmacies in Norway may substitute generic, biosimilar or parallel-imported medicines only when the Norwegian Medical Products Agency has placed them on its substitution list, which has the status of a regulation. Listed products must contain the same active ingredient at the same strength in the same pharmaceutical form, be bioequivalent or biosimilar, and be judged medically equivalent; notes can limit substitution, for epilepsy medicines to the start of treatment. A physician may reserve a prescription against substitution only for compelling medical reasons recorded in the patient's record; otherwise a patient who declines substitution pays the price difference." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Norwegian Medical Products Agency, What are substitutable medicines? and The physician's role in the substitution scheme (web guidance, pages dated 10/03/2025), applying the Pharmacy Act section 6-6" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "automatic-substitution" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:automatic-substitution .

heiro:6000236 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Autonomy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/about-us/our-principles"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Right to Informed Choices Balanced Against Limited Resources" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that its committees balance the most overall benefit for the greatest number of people against fairness and respect for individual choice. One of the moral principles behind its guidance holds that people have the right to make informed choices about the care they receive. NICE adds that not everyone has the ability to make their own choices and that not everything people want will necessarily be available. Because resources are limited, decisions have to be made about which options are publicly funded." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Our principles (web page, replacing Social value judgements, second edition 2008), introduction and moral principles, accessed 14 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "autonomy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:autonomy .

heiro:6000237 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Average Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Hospital Stays Valued at National Cost Study Averages, Within Reliability Limits" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS requires resources to be valued at unit production costs, with hospital stays valued at the average cost per homogeneous patient group from the National Cost Study, corrected to exclude structural costs and products funded on top of the stay tariff. HAS notes that these averages can conceal sharp variation between institutions and does not recommend them when the survey rate is below 20%, the sampling error exceeds 30%, or the estimate rests on fewer than 30 stays or a single institution; stays are then valued at the applicable tariffs. Any modification to the average must be explained and justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation: methodological guidance (English version of the 2020 guide adopted on 2 July 2020), section 3.2.3, valuation of hospital costs" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "average-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:average-cost .

heiro:6000238 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Average Cost-Effectiveness Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Decisions Use Incremental Ratios in a Fully Incremental Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE frames cost-effectiveness for decisions in incremental terms. ICERs must be the ratio of expected additional total cost to expected additional QALYs compared with alternative technologies, following standard decision rules on dominance and extended dominance. Results should be presented in a fully incremental analysis with dominated and extendedly dominated technologies removed, with pairwise comparisons only when relevant and justified, and expected net health benefits should be reported alongside ICERs using values of £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY gained." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16 and 4.10.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "average-cost-effectiveness-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:average-cost-effectiveness-ratio .

heiro:6000239 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Average Cost-Effectiveness Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/e09a9dc3-4f57-4eb0-ad36-828df6ba8c68/content"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Interventions Evaluated First Against the Null, Then Incrementally" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The WHO guide to cost-effectiveness analysis recommends that analysts evaluate all interventions initially against the null, the situation if none of a set of interacting interventions were implemented, so that currently funded services can be assessed as well as new options. In the league table, the option with the lowest ratio against the null in each set of mutually exclusive interventions comes first and the others follow by incremental ratios, with weakly dominated options left out. A background paper in the guide warns that ranking interventions by average cost-effectiveness alone will in general give suboptimal allocations." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Making Choices in Health: WHO Guide to Cost-Effectiveness Analysis, edited by Tan-Torres Edejer T et al., World Health Organization, 2003, section 1.4 (pages 13 to 14), chapter 2 summary of recommendations (page 28), and background paper Development of WHO guidelines on generalized cost-effectiveness analysis (pages 128 to 137)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "average-cost-effectiveness-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:average-cost-effectiveness-ratio .

heiro:6000240 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development on Average Length of Stay"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000009 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2025/11/health-at-a-glance-2025_a894f72e/full-report/hospital-activity_8d89d2a3.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ALOS as an Efficiency Indicator, Mainly for Acute Care" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The OECD treats average length of stay as an indicator of efficiency in health service delivery: all else equal, a shorter stay reduces the cost per discharge and shifts care to less expensive settings, although some patients may be discharged too early. It measures ALOS as the total days stayed by all inpatients in a year divided by admissions or discharges, usually excluding day cases. Its 2025 edition reports 6.5 days for acute care across 36 OECD countries in 2023, from 4.7 days in Türkiye and Mexico to 15.7 days in Japan." ;
    heiro:2000018 "OECD (2025), Health at a Glance 2025: OECD Indicators, OECD Publishing, Paris, 13 November 2025, chapter 5, Hospital activity, including Definition and comparability and Figure 5.23" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "average-length-of-stay" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:average-length-of-stay .

heiro:6000241 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Average Length of Stay"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/PRN02348-26-27-nhs-payment-scheme-pay-award-annex-d-prices-and-cost-adjustments.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adjusted Length of Stay, Trimpoints and Short Stay Payments" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NHS Payment Scheme defines the adjusted length of stay of an admitted patient care spell as the number of changes of calendar day from admission to discharge, after deducting days in critical care, rehabilitation, specialist palliative care and delayed transfers of care. Each healthcare resource group has a trimpoint of the upper quartile plus 1.5 times the interquartile range of adjusted stay, with a minimum of 5 days, beyond which excess bed days are paid daily. Adult emergency stays of zero or one day attract a reduced price when the group's average non-elective stay is 2 days or more." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England, 2026/27 NHS Payment Scheme, Annex D: Prices and cost adjustments (PRN02348), pay award update August 2026, section 2.9 (paragraphs 61 to 63), Appendix 1 (paragraphs 105 and 106) and Appendix 3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "average-length-of-stay" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:average-length-of-stay .

heiro:6000242 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Medicaid and CHIP Payment and Access Commission on Average Manufacturer Price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000079 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.macpac.gov/wp-content/uploads/2018/06/Improving-Operations-of-the-Medicaid-Drug-Rebate-Program.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Medicaid and CHIP Payment and Access Commission" ;
    heiro:2000016 "AMP Should Reflect the Price Available to Wholesalers and Pharmacies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "MACPAC, the commission that advises Congress on Medicaid, recommended in June 2018 that Congress remove the requirement for manufacturers to blend the AMP of a brand drug with that of its authorised generic, so that rebates rest on the price available to wholesalers and pharmacies. It warned that a manufacturer could sell its authorised generic cheaply to a subsidiary, lowering the brand AMP and its rebate. MACPAC's recommendations tracker records that Public Law 116-59 now excludes authorised generics from AMP for the rebate programme, and that the rebate cap it recommended removing in June 2019 ended on 1 January 2024." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Medicaid and CHIP Payment and Access Commission, Report to Congress on Medicaid and CHIP (June 2018), chapter 1, Improving Operations of the Medicaid Drug Rebate Program, recommendation 1.1; MACPAC recommendations tracker, accessed 6 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "average-manufacturer-price" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:average-manufacturer-price .

heiro:6000243 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Office of Inspector General on Average Sales Price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000025 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://oig.hhs.gov/documents/evaluation/11843/OEI-03-26-00060.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Office of Inspector General" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Quarterly ASP and AMP Comparison Triggers Payment at 103% of AMP" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Social Security Act requires the HHS Office of Inspector General to compare each Part B drug's ASP with its AMP and notify the Secretary when ASP exceeds AMP by 5% or more. Under its November 2012 rule, CMS then substitutes 103% of AMP for the ASP-based payment when a drug code exceeds the threshold in two consecutive quarters or three of the previous four, using complete AMP data, unless that would raise payment or the drug is in short supply. OIG found nine qualifying codes in the first quarter of 2026 and reports $78 million saved by substitutions since 2013." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of Inspector General, Comparison of Average Sales Prices and Average Manufacturer Prices: Results for the First Quarter of 2026 (OEI-03-26-00060, 10 August 2026); Medicare Part B Drug Payments: Impact of Price Substitutions Based on 2024 Average Sales Prices (OEI-03-26-00080, 21 September 2026); Social Security Act section 1847A(d)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "average-sales-price" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:average-sales-price .

heiro:6000244 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Average Treatment Effect"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/nice-dsu/tsds/observational-data"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ATE Usually the Effect of Interest in NICE Appraisals" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 17 states that the treatment effect typically of interest in NICE technology appraisals is the average treatment effect, the expected gain from treatment for a randomly selected individual and the effect a randomised trial would identify, although in some cases the effect on the treated may be the parameter of interest. It notes that the ATE needs more demanding, often untestable, assumptions than the alternatives, and that the ATE, ATT and local average treatment effect coincide only when effects are homogeneous. Analysts should state the parameter of interest and justify how their method estimates it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 17: The use of observational data to inform estimates of treatment effectiveness in technology appraisal: methods for comparative individual patient data (Faria, Hernandez Alava, Manca and Wailoo, May 2015), sections 2.1.2, 4.2.2 and 5.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "average-treatment-effect" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:average-treatment-effect .

heiro:6000245 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Average Treatment Effect"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/1f6b8a70-5ce0-404e-9066-120dc9a8df75_en?filename=hta_practical-guideline_direct-and-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Chosen Estimand, ATE or ATT, Must Match the Inferential Goal" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's guideline for joint clinical assessments names the average treatment effect and the average treatment effect among the treated as the most common estimands for propensity score methods. Assessors should evaluate whether the chosen estimand matches the inferential goal, and whether the propensity score method suits that estimand, noting that matching primarily estimates the effect among the treated. The assumptions of positivity, overlap and covariate balance must be checked and reported, and a violation of balance stated if any absolute standardised difference is 0.25 or more." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Coordination Group, Practical Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 6.2.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "average-treatment-effect" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:average-treatment-effect .

heiro:6000246 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Avoidable Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/43f49471-4a8d-4e67-b1f9-d5ff1a9f5f7f/content"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Estimating Avoidable Costs Encouraged, Against a Stated Counterfactual" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's guide to economic impact studies in health states that isolating the share of a disease's or risk factor's economic costs that effective policies or interventions could avoid is a highly relevant undertaking that should be strongly encouraged. Every estimate must be measured against a stated counterfactual, and because the theoretical minimum risk can differ from the feasible minimum, the choice should follow the policy question. The guide adds that the size of an economic burden cannot set priorities on its own, which also need the costs of reducing it and the marginal effect of extra spending." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, WHO guide to identifying the economic consequences of disease and injury (2009, ISBN 9789241598293), sections 1.2 and 2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "avoidable-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:avoidable-cost .

heiro:6000247 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Background Mortality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Extrapolated Survival Must Not Exceed That of the General Dutch Population" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland's guideline for economic evaluations requires survival curves extrapolated beyond trial follow-up to be checked for clinical and external plausibility against longer trials, registries or clinical experts. If an extrapolation implies higher survival than that of the general Dutch population, it must be adjusted, so general population mortality sets a limit on modelled survival. Where data are immature, the base case uses the most conservative plausible curve, giving the smallest incremental difference in life expectancy, with scenario analyses for other distributions and shorter time horizons." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version, published 16 January 2024), section 4.5 (extrapolation techniques)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "background-mortality" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:background-mortality .

heiro:6000248 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of National Institute for Health and Disability Insurance (Belgium) on Balance Billing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000042 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.inami.fgov.be/fr/themes/soins-de-sante-cout-et-remboursement/les-prestations-de-sante-que-vous-rembourse-votre-mutualite/soins-dans-les-hopitaux"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "National Institute for Health and Disability Insurance (Belgium)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Fee Supplements in Shared Hospital Rooms; Possible in Single Rooms" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Belgium's National Institute for Health and Disability Insurance (INAMI-RIZIV) states that a doctor can never charge fee supplements above the regulated fees to a hospital patient in a double or shared room, while in a single room a supplement can normally be charged. No supplement may be charged for a stay in a single room if the patient chose a double or shared room in the admission declaration. Room supplements follow the same pattern, so the room chosen on admission determines whether the patient can face charges above the regulated tariff." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Disability Insurance (INAMI-RIZIV), Les frais d'hospitalisation (soins dans les hôpitaux), section on possible supplements, amounts applicable from 1 January 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "balance-billing" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:balance-billing .

heiro:6000249 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of National Institute for Health and Disability Insurance (Belgium) on Balance Billing Protection"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000042 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.inami.fgov.be/fr/professionnels/professionnels-de-la-sante/medecins/exercice-du-metier/medecins-interdiction-de-facturer-des-supplements-en-ambulatoire-aux-beneficiaires-de-l-intervention-majoree"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "National Institute for Health and Disability Insurance (Belgium)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Fee Supplements in Ambulatory Care for Patients With Increased Reimbursement" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Since 1 January 2025, doctors in Belgium, whether or not they have joined the national agreement, may not charge fee supplements for reimbursed ambulatory care to patients automatically entitled to increased reimbursement (BIM), and since 1 January 2026 the ban covers patients granted BIM after a means test. INAMI-RIZIV states that the aim is to guarantee access and price certainty for people in a fragile economic position, so the agreed tariff applies. The only exceptions are rounding of up to EUR 0.02 and documented special requests by the patient, and doctors may not refuse treatment over supplements." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Disability Insurance (INAMI-RIZIV), Médecins: interdiction de facturer des suppléments en ambulatoire aux bénéficiaires de l'intervention majorée, applying article 53 of the law of 14 July 1994 (as amended by the law of 29 November 2022) and the royal decree of 12 March 2024" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "balance-billing-protection" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:balance-billing-protection .

heiro:6000250 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Balanced Scorecard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://aqua.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/qsir-balanced-scorecard.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Eight Stages to Build a Scorecard Across Four Perspectives" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS England's Quality, Service Improvement and Redesign tools present the balanced scorecard as a performance management tool for turning a team's vision and strategy into objectives, measures and targets. Measures should cover finance, customers and patients, internal business processes, and learning and growth, with the public sector financial perspective tending to stress cost efficiency. Design runs through eight stages, from a sound strategic foundation and cause-and-effect links between objectives to targets, strategic initiatives and full implementation, and the tool works best with buy-in across the organisation and involvement of a wide range of staff." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England and NHS Improvement, Online library of Quality, Service Improvement and Redesign (QSIR) tools: Balanced scorecard (library hosted by AQuA, file dated July 2023)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "balanced-scorecard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:balanced-scorecard .

heiro:6000251 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Balancing Measure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://aqua.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/qsir-measuring-quality-care.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Improvement Projects Need Balancing Measures Alongside Outcome and Process" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS England's Quality, Service Improvement and Redesign tools add balancing measures to Donabedian's structure, process and outcome measures and state that improvement projects should have all of these types, as additions and not alternatives. Balancing measures capture unintended or wider consequences of a change, positive or negative, so that teams can measure them and reduce their impact where needed; monitoring emergency readmissions after work to reduce length of stay is the example given. The guidance suggests that listening to sceptics' concerns often reveals which balancing measures to track." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England and NHS Improvement, Online library of Quality, Service Improvement and Redesign (QSIR) tools: A model for measuring quality care, and Seven steps to measurement for improvement (library hosted by AQuA, files dated July 2023)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "balancing-measure" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:balancing-measure .

heiro:6000252 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Barrier to Implementation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg20/chapter/support-for-putting-the-guideline-recommendations-into-practice"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Committees Weigh Levers and Barriers, and NICE Supports Implementation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's guideline manual asks each guideline committee to consider factors that may help or hinder implementation, which it calls levers and barriers, and to judge how feasible its recommendations will be, taking account of the change in practice required, staff training needs, policy levers and funding streams. These judgements are documented in the guideline and its implementation resources. NICE's national implementation team then works with national partners, practitioners and commissioners to identify barriers to, and priorities for, implementation and develops support resources to help overcome them." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence, Developing NICE guidelines: the manual (PMG20), sections 1.5, 3.1, 9.1, 12.1 and 12.5, last updated 23 October 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "barrier-to-implementation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:barrier-to-implementation .

heiro:6000253 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Barrier to Implementation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/809b813f-fcfe-451e-b242-d914d580c111/content"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Greater Barriers Make a Strong Recommendation Less Likely" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The WHO handbook for guideline development treats feasibility as a determinant of recommendation strength: the greater the barriers to implementing an option, the less likely the intervention is to be strongly recommended, with feasibility shaped by available resources, programme considerations, infrastructure and training. Implementation should be considered from the start of guideline development, and the basic steps for national or subnational implementers include analysing local needs, identifying potential barriers and facilitating factors, and designing an implementation strategy. Qualitative evidence can inform questions on contextual barriers and facilitators." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, WHO handbook for guideline development, 2nd edition (2014, ISBN 9789241548960), sections 8.4, 10.2.8 and 13.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "barrier-to-implementation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:barrier-to-implementation .

heiro:6000254 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Base Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Probabilistic Base Case Unless the Model Is Linear" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that the committee's preferred cost-effectiveness estimate should be derived from a probabilistic analysis when possible unless the model is linear, and that any use of deterministic results must be clearly justified. Deterministic base-case or scenario analyses also need justification, although this matters less if the base case is shown to be linear or only moderately non-linear. All parameter values used in base-case, sensitivity, scenario and subgroup analyses should be clinically plausible and consistent with the data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.6.27, 4.7.12 and 4.7.13, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "base-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:base-case .

heiro:6000255 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Base Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Deterministic Conventional Base Case From a Health Care System Perspective" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's Reference Case sets out the methods for its conventional base-case cost-effectiveness analysis, which takes a health care system perspective and is run deterministically where appropriate for the model type. A modified societal perspective analysis is always included, and is presented alongside as a co-base case when relevant direct data on societal effects or costs exist and their impact is judged substantial. This most often applies when the ratio changes by more than 20%, by more than USD 200,000 per evLY or QALY gained, or crosses USD 100,000 or USD 150,000 per evLY or QALY gained." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, current as of 23 October 2025, section 1 (Perspective; Conditions to be met to consider the modified societal perspective as a co-base case; Presentation of Results)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "base-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:base-case .

heiro:6000256 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Base Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-8-results-of-the-base-case-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Stepped Derivation of the Base-Case ICER From Trial Data" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC asks submissions whose models go beyond the clinical data to show, step by step, how the comparative trial results are transformed into the modelled base-case estimate of incremental cost-effectiveness. Each step undertaken, such as applicability adjustments, translation of surrogate to clinical outcomes, extrapolation over time and conversion to QALYs, is presented with re-estimated costs and outcomes, and the final step must correspond to the base-case ICER. The stepped presentation identifies the assumptions that need fuller sensitivity analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee (PBAC Guidelines), version 5.0, subsections 3A.8.2 and 3A.8.4, September 2016" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "base-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:base-case .

heiro:6000257 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Base Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Base Case Results Based on the Probabilistic Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland's guideline requires the results of both the base case analysis and scenario analyses to be based on the probabilistic analysis, a change from the 2016 version, and now includes medical costs in life years gained in the base case. Base case reporting must give absolute and incremental costs and effects with confidence intervals, QALYs in every case, the ICER and the net monetary benefit. Deterministic and scenario results are always presented together with, and compared against, the base case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), 16 January 2024, Changes compared to the 2016 guideline and sections 5.3.1 and 5.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "base-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:base-case .

heiro:6000258 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Base Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Base-Case Analysis Within the Reference Case Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS keeps the two terms apart: its reference case analysis, which follows the methodological choices of the HAS frame of reference, comprises a base-case analysis, non-exploratory subgroup analyses and a thorough exploration of uncertainty through sensitivity analyses. The base-case analysis is defined as the analysis of the study population based on the middle values of the model parameters and the adopted assumptions and modelling choices. Sensitivity analyses then explore the uncertainty around the base-case result." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in methods for economic evaluation: methodological guidance, validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, HAS frame of reference summary and glossary (reference case analysis, base-case analysis, sensitivity analysis)" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "base-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:base-case .

heiro:6000259 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Federal Office of Public Health (Switzerland) on Base Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000039 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.bag.admin.ch/de/stationaere-tarifstrukturen"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Federal Office of Public Health (Switzerland)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Hospital Base Rate Agreed by Tariff Partners and Approved by the Canton" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Switzerland's Federal Office of Public Health explains that under SwissDRG the payment for each case group is its cost weight multiplied by a base rate, a kind of average value for inpatient treatment in a particular hospital that can differ between hospitals. Insurers and providers agree the base rate as tariff partners and the competent canton approves it, or sets it if the partners cannot agree. The national tariff structure is developed and updated annually by SwissDRG AG and must be submitted to the Federal Council for approval." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Federal Office of Public Health (FOPH/BAG), Stationäre Tarifstrukturen (web page, accessed 6 October 2026), applying the Federal Health Insurance Act (KVG/LAMal) article 49 and KVV article 59d" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "base-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:base-rate .

heiro:6000260 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Baseline Risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Absolute Effects Built on the Median Control Group Risk" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods state that, when results from a meta-analysis are presented as absolute effects in its health information, a plausible baseline risk is first selected, typically the median control group risk across the included studies. In justified cases it may come from one suitable study or a valid external source such as a German prevalence study or registry, with large uncertainty handled by the Newcombe and Bender method. The relative pooled effect is then applied to it, and where included studies have heterogeneous baseline risks, absolute effects are shown for each assumed risk, for example low and high." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English version published 1 July 2026), section 7.7" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "baseline-risk" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:baseline-risk .

heiro:6000261 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Baseline Risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/content/information/files/pbac-guidelines-version-5.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Check Baseline Risk Before Applying Trial Effects to the Target Population" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines tell applicants to take care when converting relative treatment effects across jurisdictions with different baseline risks, and to ensure that the baseline risk, meaning patients' prognostic characteristics, does not differ between the trial evidence and the target population. In indirect comparisons, where event rates in the common reference arms differ because of baseline risk, applicants should present evidence of a constant relative treatment effect across baseline risks and choose the outcome measure least affected by differences between trials in baseline risk." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), sections 2.6.3 and 3A.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "baseline-risk" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:baseline-risk .

heiro:6000262 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Baseline Risk"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Subgroup Analyses Required Where Baseline Risk Varies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland's guideline names heterogeneity in the baseline risk of events, with heterogeneity in the effectiveness of an intervention, as a type that can currently enter reimbursement decisions. Where such heterogeneity has a clinical rationale, subgroups can be distinguished in practice and differential reimbursement would be ethical and consistent with equality, subgroup analyses must be performed, with cost-effectiveness estimated and other inputs determined per subgroup. Insufficient statistical power is not a valid reason to report only average effects for the whole population." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version, published 16 January 2024), section 4.6 (subgroup analyses)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "baseline-risk" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:baseline-risk .

heiro:6000263 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Basket Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Basket Trials Should Assess Heterogeneity and Allow Borrowing" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE notes that some technologies suit alternative study designs, for example histology-independent cancer treatments studied in basket trials with a heterogeneous population. When basket trials are used, they should be appropriately designed and analysed, assess heterogeneity and allow borrowing between baskets. They should also include relevant comparators, allocate treatments randomly, use clinical endpoints with a validated relationship to overall survival and quality of life, and enrol all patient groups relevant to the indication." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.3.7 and 3.3.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "basket-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:basket-trial .

heiro:6000264 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Basket Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/120721/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "One Drug Tested Across Multiple Cancer Populations" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance describes a basket trial as a master protocol testing a single investigational drug or drug combination in different populations defined by cancer type, disease stage, histology, number of prior therapies, genetic or other biomarkers, or demographic characteristics. Substudies are usually single-arm, activity-estimating trials with overall response rate as the primary endpoint, and a strong response signal may allow a substudy to expand to generate data that could support a marketing application. Each substudy needs specific objectives, a rationale for each population and a statistical analysis plan with sample size justification and stopping rules." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Master Protocols: Efficient Clinical Trial Design Strategies to Expedite Development of Oncology Drugs and Biologics, Guidance for Industry, March 2022, section IV.A" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "basket-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:basket-trial .

heiro:6000265 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Basket Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-clinical-evaluation-anticancer-medicinal-products-revision-6_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pooling Baskets Needs a Strong Upfront Case for Homogeneity" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA describes basket designs as studying one drug or combination in a population made up of different diseases, usually defined in oncology by a presumed response-predictive biomarker; without an internal control, each basket resembles a separate single-arm trial. When baskets are pooled, a homogeneous treatment effect must be supported upfront by mechanistic, pre-clinical and pharmacodynamic data, and sponsors must make it convincingly plausible that interaction with tumour site or histology is negligible. Scientific advice is strongly recommended before master protocols are used to generate the main evidence for marketing authorisation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use, Guideline on the clinical evaluation of anticancer medicinal products (EMA/CHMP/205/95 Rev.6), adopted 18 November 2023, sections 8.2, 8.2.1 and 8.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "basket-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:basket-trial .

heiro:6000266 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Bathtub Hazard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Clinical Plausibility of Extrapolated Hazard Functions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that standard parametric and more flexible approaches, such as spline-based and cure models, are available for extrapolating time-to-event data, and that their appropriateness and the validity of their extrapolations should routinely be considered. When alternative models are compared, the clinical plausibility of their underlying hazard functions should routinely be assessed and uncertainty in the extrapolated portion of the hazard explored. Hazard plots based on observed data and model predictions should be presented in graphs and tables alongside Kaplan-Meier and parametric curves." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.6.25 and 4.10.5, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bathtub-hazard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bathtub-hazard .

heiro:6000267 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Bathtub Hazard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/nice-dsu/tsds/survival-analysis"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Standard Models Cannot Capture Two or More Turning Points" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 21 states that none of the standard parametric survival models can accurately model survival where the hazard has two or more turning points, such as a hazard that rises soon after trial entry, falls as sicker patients die and rises again with age-related mortality. It describes flexible parametric, mixture, poly-hazard, piecewise and cure models for such shapes. Fitted and extrapolated hazard and survival functions should always be presented with general population functions plotted alongside, and background mortality incorporated to avoid extremely implausible projections." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 21: Flexible methods for survival analysis (Rutherford, Lambert, Sweeting, Pennington, Crowther, Abrams and Latimer, January 2020, updated version March 2022), sections 2.1.1, 2.1.2, 3.2 and 7.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bathtub-hazard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bathtub-hazard .

heiro:6000268 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Office for Budget Responsibility on Baumol Effect"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000055 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://obr.uk/frs/fiscal-risks-and-sustainability-july-2026/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Office for Budget Responsibility" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Baumol Effect Drives Most Other Cost Pressures on Health Spending" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The OBR projects UK health spending from demographic change, income effects and other cost pressures, which add 1 percentage point a year to real growth. Its 2024 report attributed 0.72 points of this to the Baumol effect, multiplying projected economy-wide productivity growth of 1.5 per cent a year by an OECD coefficient of 0.48. The July 2026 report keeps these assumptions, projecting health spending rising from 8 per cent of GDP in 2030-31 to 13 per cent by 2075-76, but calls future relative productivity growth in health very uncertain; without these pressures spending reaches about 9 per cent." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Office for Budget Responsibility, Fiscal risks and sustainability, July 2026, chapter 3, paragraphs 3.15 to 3.17; Fiscal risks and sustainability, September 2024, chapter 3, paragraphs 3.26 to 3.28" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "baumol-effect" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:baumol-effect .

heiro:6000269 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Bayesian Adaptive Design"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/78495/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Same Principles as Other Adaptive Designs, With Simulated Performance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance describes Bayesian adaptive designs as those using features such as predictive modelling to govern the timing and decision rules of interim analyses, assumed dose-response relationships, explicit borrowing of external information through informative prior distributions, or posterior probabilities as success criteria. The same principles apply as for adaptive designs without Bayesian features. For trials using Bayesian inference with informative priors, Bayesian statistical properties are more informative than the type I error probability, and simulations of operating characteristics can be particularly resource intensive because many Bayesian methods rely on Markov chain Monte Carlo." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, Adaptive Designs for Clinical Trials of Drugs and Biologics: Guidance for Industry, November 2019, section VI.B (Bayesian Adaptive Designs)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bayesian-adaptive-design" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bayesian-adaptive-design .

heiro:6000270 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Bayesian Analysis CEA"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Preferred Estimate From Probabilistic Analysis Unless the Model Is Linear" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE expects the committee's preferred cost-effectiveness estimate to come from a probabilistic analysis when possible, unless the model is linear. Parameter distributions should represent the available evidence rather than be chosen arbitrarily, and enough simulations should be run to minimise Monte Carlo error. Models should quantify decision uncertainty and report the probability of cost-effectiveness at £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY gained. Informative prior distributions for relative effectiveness are not recommended unless under very specific circumstances, such as very sparse adverse event data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.4.19, 4.7.2, 4.7.10 to 4.7.12 and 4.7.15, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bayesian-analysis-cea" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bayesian-analysis-cea .

heiro:6000271 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Bayesian Analysis CEA"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD6-Software.final_.08.05.12.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Posterior Samples Passed Directly Through the Decision Model" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 6 sets out ways to embed evidence synthesis in probabilistic cost-effectiveness analysis, which it calls the preferred methodology for the NICE reference case. In a one-stage Bayesian approach, samples from the joint posterior go directly through the decision model with no assumption about their distributional form. In a two-stage approach the posterior samples are exported to another package, and all parameter values must come from the same Markov chain Monte Carlo iteration so that correlations are preserved. Frequentist alternatives sample from a multivariate normal distribution built from the estimates and their variance-covariance matrix." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 6: Embedding evidence synthesis in probabilistic cost-effectiveness analysis: software choices (Dias, Sutton, Welton and Ades, May 2011, last updated April 2012), executive summary and sections 2.1 to 2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bayesian-analysis-cea" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bayesian-analysis-cea .

heiro:6000272 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Bayesian Information Criterion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD14-Survival-analysis.updated-March-2013.v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "AIC and BIC Compare Non-Nested Survival Models on Internal Fit Only" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 14 recommends the Akaike and Bayesian information criteria for comparing the fit of alternative parametric survival models, which cannot be nested, and notes that the negative 2 log likelihood suits only nested models and was used erroneously in past NICE appraisals. Both criteria weigh improved fit against extra parameters, which BIC penalises more highly. They address internal validity, not the extrapolated portion of the curve, so with substantial censoring external data, clinical plausibility and expert judgement should guide model choice." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 14: Survival analysis for economic evaluations alongside clinical trials: extrapolation with patient-level data (Latimer, June 2011, last updated March 2013), sections 3.3, 3.5 and 6.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bayesian-information-criterion" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bayesian-information-criterion .

heiro:6000273 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Bayesian Information Criterion"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/1f6b8a70-5ce0-404e-9066-120dc9a8df75_en?filename=hta_practical-guideline_direct-and-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "AIC, BIC or DIC to Select Fractional Polynomial Survival Models" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's guideline for joint clinical assessments states that fractional polynomial network meta-analysis of time-to-event data requires selection of the most appropriate model for the hazard rates. This can be assessed using measures of statistical fit such as AIC, BIC or, in a Bayesian framework, DIC, together with visual fit to the observed hazards and survival functions and clinical plausibility. Because different fractional polynomial models can lead to different conclusions on relative treatment effects, sensitivity analysis is important." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Coordination Group, Practical Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 4.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bayesian-information-criterion" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bayesian-information-criterion .

heiro:6000274 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Bayesian Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Informative Heterogeneity Priors in Networks With Few Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE prefers the network meta-analysis methods of the DSU evidence synthesis series. External information should be used to help estimate between-study heterogeneity, and in networks with few included studies informative prior distributions for the heterogeneity parameter may be preferable, with distributions tailored to particular outcomes and disease areas. The source of the prior must be noted and justified, with sensitivity analysis over candidate priors. Informative priors for relative effectiveness are not recommended unless under very specific circumstances, such as very sparse adverse event data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.4.11 and 3.4.16 to 3.4.19, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bayesian-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bayesian-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000275 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Bayesian Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD2-General-meta-analysis-corrected-2Sep2016v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Vague Priors for Effects, With Caution on Between-Trial Variance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 2 takes a Bayesian approach to pairwise and network meta-analysis using Markov chain Monte Carlo and recommends vague priors, such as N(0, 100²), for trial baselines and relative effects, with informative priors for relative effects needing special justification. It warns that routinely vague priors leave the between-trial variance poorly identified without many large trials and give posteriors biased upwards, so external data or elicited informative priors may help. Where all trials are two-arm, frequentist methods are acceptable if parameter uncertainty and correlations are propagated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 2: A generalised linear modelling framework for pairwise and network meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials (Dias, Welton, Sutton and Ades, August 2011, last updated September 2016), sections 6.2 and 9" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bayesian-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bayesian-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000276 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Bayesian Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Bayesian Random-Effects Model as Standard for 3 or 4 Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods set a standard approach by number of studies. With 2 studies a fixed-effect model is used. With 3 or 4 studies a random-effects model is fitted for standardised mean differences, odds ratios, relative risks and hazard ratios as a Bayesian meta-analysis with uninformative priors for the treatment effect and informative priors for the heterogeneity parameter, checked against a qualitative summary. With 5 or more studies the Knapp-Hartung method applies, and Bayesian approaches with other adequately justified priors remain an option." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, section 10.3.7 (Meta-analyses)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bayesian-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bayesian-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000277 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Bayesian Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/1f6b8a70-5ce0-404e-9066-120dc9a8df75_en?filename=hta_practical-guideline_direct-and-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Bayesian Option for Very Few Studies, With Justified Priors" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's guideline for joint clinical assessments accepts a Bayesian approach as a general alternative to frequentist meta-analysis, provided the required prior distributions are available and can be justified, and notes that it is especially useful with very few studies. In that setting a random-effects Bayesian meta-analysis with a weakly informative prior for the heterogeneity parameter may help, but the choice of prior needs a clear rationale and its impact should be explored in sensitivity analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Coordination Group, Practical Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, sections 4.1.1 and 4.1.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bayesian-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bayesian-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000278 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS Wales on Bed Turnover"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000080 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.datadictionary.wales.nhs.uk/WordDocuments/bedusefactor.htm"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS Wales" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Bed Use Factor and Bed Turnover Interval Indicators" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NHS Wales Data Dictionary defines two bed indicators by reference to the QueSt 1 (QS1) return, each reported at specialty level. Bed use factor, which corresponds to the bed turnover rate, is the number of deaths and discharges in the period divided by the average daily number of available beds. Bed turnover interval is the average number of unoccupied beds multiplied by the number of days in the period, divided by the deaths and discharges during the period." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS Wales Data Dictionary, Indicator IP03 Bed use factor and Indicator IP04 Bed turnover interval (derivation: KSIs, Hospital Bed Use Statistics)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bed-turnover" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bed-turnover .

heiro:6000279 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Before-and-After Study"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Diagnostic Before-and-After Studies Inform the Care Pathway" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE names diagnostic before-and-after studies as a useful source of evidence on how clinical management changes after an index test is introduced into practice. It notes that such studies are often unavailable, particularly for a new test not yet in routine use, so expert clinical input on the usual care pathway is likely to matter. For relative treatment effects NICE strongly prefers randomised trials and regards non-randomised studies as tending to be at high risk of bias from confounding." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.3.2, 3.3.9 and 3.3.24, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "before-and-after-study" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:before-and-after-study .

heiro:6000280 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Benchmark Price"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/our-approach/methods-process/value-assessment-framework/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Benefit Price Benchmark (HBPB): a Suggested Value-Based Price Range" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER’s signature output is not an accept/reject decision but a Health Benefit Price Benchmark — the price range, net of discounts and rebates, at which the treatment’s incremental cost-effectiveness would fall between 100,000and100,000 and 100,000and150,000 per QALY (lower bound) and per evLYG (upper bound). ICER publishes whether a drug’s actual price sits above this benchmark and estimates the excess spending and health foregone when it does, framing a value-based \"fair price\" rather than a coverage ruling." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER Value Assessment Framework (Health Benefit Price Benchmark)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "benchmark-price" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:benchmark-price .

heiro:6000281 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Benefit-Cost Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#summary-metrics-of-social-value"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "BCR for Initial Ranking Only, Not Judged Against Thresholds" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Green Book defines the benefit-cost ratio as the present value of monetisable social benefits divided by the present value of monetisable social costs, and asks for it with net present social value and the return on public sector cost for each shortlisted option. These metrics give only an initial ranking. Practitioners should not judge value for money with ratio thresholds, such as rejecting proposals below two, and a ratio below one may still represent value for money if unmonetisable benefits are significant." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, The Green Book (2026), updated 5 February 2026, chapter 6 (Shortlist appraisal), Summary metrics of social value (table 10) and Identifying the preferred option" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "benefit-cost-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:benefit-cost-ratio .

heiro:6000282 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Best-Worst Scaling"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/92593/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Case 1 to Prioritise Endpoints and Case 3 to Quantify Trade-Offs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's final guidance on voluntary patient preference information for medical devices lists best-worst scaling case 3 among the methods commonly used to quantify the trade-offs patients are willing to make between attributes, alongside discrete choice experiments, the threshold technique, swing weighting and multi-dimensional thresholding. Where the main research question is to prioritise endpoints, best-worst scaling case 1 may be sufficient, because it asks which item is most or least important and yields an ordinal ranking. Sponsors planning a best-worst scaling study are encouraged to describe its experimental design in the study protocol." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration (CDRH and CBER), Incorporating Voluntary Patient Preference Information over the Total Product Life Cycle: Guidance for Industry, Food and Drug Administration Staff, and Other Interested Parties, issued 30 March 2026, section V.D and Appendix B" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "best-worst-scaling" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:best-worst-scaling .

heiro:6000283 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Beta Distribution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Distributions Must Represent the Evidence, Not Be Chosen Arbitrarily" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE requires distributions to be assigned to characterise uncertainty in the precision of mean parameter values, and these should not be chosen arbitrarily but should represent the available evidence on each parameter, with their use justified. Where alternative plausible distributions exist, separate probabilistic analyses should explore them. Evidence on correlation between parameters should be reflected, avoiding both artificial restriction of ordered parameters and unsupported assumptions of perfect correlation. The manual does not name particular distributions such as the beta." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.7.10 and 4.7.19, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "beta-distribution" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:beta-distribution .

heiro:6000284 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Beta Distribution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD12-Utilities-in-modelling-FINAL.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Normal Usually Adequate for Mean Utility, Rescaled Beta if Bounded" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 12 notes that preference-based quality of life data such as EQ-5D are typically skewed, bounded by the limits of the index and often include negative values, but that in most cases uncertainty in the mean can be adequately described by sampling from a normal distribution. Where it cannot, utilities can be expressed as decrements from full health and sampled from a log-normal or gamma distribution. If a lower limit is needed, such as minus 0.594 for the UK EQ-5D index, a standard beta distribution can be scaled up with a height parameter." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 12: The use of health state utility values in decision models (Ara and Wailoo, July 2011), section 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "beta-distribution" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:beta-distribution .

heiro:6000285 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Beta Distribution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-9-uncertainty-analysis-model-inputs-and-assumptions.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Beta Distributions a Natural Match for Transition Probabilities" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC Guidelines ask submissions to use commonly adopted statistical standards to represent uncertainty around the true value of each uncertain model input. As an example, they describe beta distributions as a natural match for transition probabilities, with log-normal distributions for relative risks or hazard ratios and gamma or log-normal distributions for costs and utility parameters. The use of alternative distributions should be justified, and interval estimates from the fitted distributions should define the ranges tested in deterministic sensitivity analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsection 3A.9.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "beta-distribution" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:beta-distribution .

heiro:6000286 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Between-Subject Variation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Separate Estimates for Each Relevant Subgroup When Benefit Differs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE notes that for many technologies the level of benefit differs between patients with differing characteristics, and asks cost-utility analyses to give clinical and cost-effectiveness estimates separately for each relevant subgroup. Subgroups should rest on a clearly justified expectation of a differential effect, and post hoc data dredging will be viewed sceptically. Analysts should specify the scale on which effect modification is defined and reflect the statistical precision of all subgroup estimates in the analysis of parameter uncertainty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.9.1, 4.9.3, 4.9.4 and 4.9.7, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "between-subject-variation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:between-subject-variation .

heiro:6000287 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Between-Subject Variation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD15_Patient-level_simulation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Keep Patient Heterogeneity Out of the Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 15 distinguishes patient heterogeneity, stochastic uncertainty and parameter uncertainty, and states that parameters reflecting patient heterogeneity should not be included within the probabilistic sensitivity analysis. Where patient characteristics relate non-linearly to model outcomes, a patient-level simulation that samples characteristics from population distributions should be used to obtain an unbiased estimate of mean outcomes, unless suitable subgroup analysis avoids the need. The estimate is unbiased only if enough patients are simulated and covariance between their characteristics is correctly taken into account." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15: Cost-effectiveness modelling using patient-level simulation (Davis, Stevenson, Tappenden and Wailoo, April 2014), sections 2.2 and 3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "between-subject-variation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:between-subject-variation .

heiro:6000288 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Beveridge Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/44371"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Beveridge Versus Bismarck No Longer Useful for Policy-Making" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's World Health Report 2010 states that financing labels can mislead, because two systems described as tax-based, or two based largely on insurance, may pool and use funds very differently, so the traditional division into tax-based and social health insurance systems, or Beveridge versus Bismarck, is no longer useful for policy-making. WHO's current health financing guidance instead analyses revenue raising, pooling and purchasing in every system, irrespective of whether it is labelled tax-based or health insurance." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, The world health report: health systems financing: the path to universal coverage (2010), chapter 1; Assessing country health financing systems: the health financing progress matrix, Health Financing Guidance No. 8 (December 2020), section 2.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "beveridge-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:beveridge-model .

heiro:6000289 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Bias-Variance Tradeoff"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD14-Survival-analysis.updated-March-2013.v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Information Criteria Weigh Better Fit Against Extra Parameters" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 14 states that the Akaike and Bayesian information criteria weigh the improved fit of alternative survival models against the potentially inefficient use of additional parameters, with BIC penalising extra parameters more heavily than AIC. It also points to split-sample validation, k-fold cross-validation and bootstrap resampling as helpful ways to validate a fitted model internally. These measures address only fit to the observed data, not the extrapolated portion of the curve, so they cannot be relied on alone when choosing a model." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 14: Survival analysis for economic evaluations alongside clinical trials: extrapolation with patient-level data (Latimer, June 2011, last updated March 2013), sections 3.3 to 3.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bias-variance-tradeoff" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bias-variance-tradeoff .

heiro:6000290 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Bibliographic Database"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Database Selection, Search Strategies and Search Documentation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods treat bibliographic databases, alongside trial registries, as a main source of study results. Citing the Cochrane Handbook, they name MEDLINE, Embase and CENTRAL as the 3 most important databases, though a search may justifiably be limited to MEDLINE and CENTRAL depending on the question. Strategies combine subject headings and free-text terms and are quality assured with the PRESS checklist before searching, and reports document the databases, providers and interfaces, search period, last search date and full strategies with all limits." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 9.1.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bibliographic-database" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bibliographic-database .

heiro:6000291 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Bibliographic Database"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg24/chapter/appendix-b-identification-selection-and-synthesis-of-clinical-evidence"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reproducible Database Searches in Company Evidence Submissions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's user guide for company evidence submissions expects a systematic literature search in line with the systematic review sections of its methods manual. Companies must report enough detail for the results to be reproduced, including a full list of information sources and the full electronic search strategies for all databases, with any limits applied. Searches for published cost-effectiveness studies must also be described in enough detail to be reproduced, with the rationale for inclusion and exclusion criteria." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Single technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies evaluation: User guide for company evidence submission template (PMG24), appendices B and E, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "bibliographic-database" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:bibliographic-database .

heiro:6000292 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Big Data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/minutes/hma-ema-joint-task-force-big-data-summary-report_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "HMA and EMA Definition of Big Data and Data Quality Priorities" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The joint HMA and EMA Big Data Taskforce found no commonly accepted definition and defined big data as extremely large datasets, possibly complex, multi-dimensional, unstructured and heterogeneous, which accumulate rapidly and can be analysed computationally to reveal patterns, trends and associations. A single clinical trial dataset does not strictly qualify, but pooled or linked datasets can. The taskforce held that data quality largely determines the validity of derived evidence, and recommended minimum data quality standards and distributed data networks for sharing sensitive healthcare data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HMA-EMA Joint Big Data Taskforce, summary report (EMA/105321/2019), 13 February 2019, sections 4, 6.3.2 and 6.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "big-data" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:big-data .

heiro:6000293 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Binomial Distribution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD2-General-meta-analysis-corrected-2Sep2016v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Binomial Likelihood Handles Zero Cells Without Continuity Corrections" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 2 models the number of events in each trial arm as binomial, with a logit link to the arm's event probability, in its core models for pairwise and network meta-analysis of randomised trials. When trials report the proportion with an event at different follow-up times, it keeps the binomial likelihood but uses a complementary log-log link, assuming a constant event rate. Because zero cells are allowed, the occasional trial with a zero count needs no special precautions, whereas adding an arbitrary 0.5 biases effect estimates. Several small trials with zero cells can make the models numerically unstable." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 2: A generalised linear modelling framework for pairwise and network meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials (Dias, Welton, Sutton and Ades, August 2011, last updated September 2016), sections 2.1.1, 3.2 and 6.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "binomial-distribution" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:binomial-distribution .

heiro:6000294 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Biosimilar"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/long-read/what-is-a-biosimilar-medicine/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Separate Appraisal; Reference Product’s Recommendation Applies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "A biosimilar does not require a separate technology appraisal — a positive NICE recommendation for the reference biologic applies to the biosimilar. NICE conducts surveillance around loss of exclusivity and may produce an evidence summary where a review is warranted, with product choice for the NHS driven by overall value (cost, indications, formulation, pathway)." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Health Technology Evaluations: The Manual (PMG36); NHS England biosimilar guidance" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "biosimilar" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:biosimilar .

heiro:6000295 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Biosimilar"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Minor Submission, No Economic Evaluation; Supports Pharmacy Substitution" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For a biosimilar of an already-listed reference biologic in the same indication, the PBAC accepts a minor submission with no economic evaluation required, listing on a cost-minimisation basis; where appropriate it recommends \"a\"-flagging, allowing pharmacy-level substitution of the biosimilar for the reference biologic." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC (biosimilars)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "biosimilar" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:biosimilar .

heiro:6000296 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Biosimilar"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Tailored Biosimilar Review, Including Switching Advice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CADTH applies a tailored approach to biosimilars rather than a full reimbursement review, focusing on the biosimilarity established at regulatory approval and, for some products, issuing advice on switching patients from the reference biologic to the biosimilar." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH (now CDA-AMC), biosimilars review approach" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "biosimilar" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:biosimilar .

heiro:6000297 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Break-Even Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Switching Values at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY Gained" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE allows threshold analysis to explore highly uncertain parameters when a switching value would inform decision makers: the value an input would need to take for the decision on whether a technology is a good use of NHS resources to change at a given threshold, such as £25,000 or £35,000 per QALY gained. The analysis should show how far the switching value is from the current best estimate. It is unsuitable for parameters highly correlated with other influential parameters and should not justify restricting the population to a subgroup. Implausible switching values are still reported with the plausible range." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.6.29, 4.7.22 and 4.7.23, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "break-even-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:break-even-analysis .

heiro:6000298 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Break-Even Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/ICER_2023_2026_VAF_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Threshold Prices and a Health Benefit Price Benchmark" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER calculates threshold prices, the prices at which a new intervention would reach given cost-effectiveness thresholds, and reports results at USD 50,000, 100,000, 150,000 and 200,000 per QALY and per evLY gained in all assessments. Its Health Benefit Price Benchmark is the range of prices, net of rebates and other concessions, needed to reach USD 100,000 to 150,000 per QALY and per evLY gained. When no non-negative price would make a treatment cost-effective, ICER presents a scenario that excludes health care costs not tied to the intervention or its comparators." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023, current web version), sections 3.1, 3.9, 3.10 and 3.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "break-even-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:break-even-analysis .

heiro:6000299 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England / NICE on Budget Impact Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001,
        heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/developing-the-guidance-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England / NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Budget Impact Test: £40 Million Trigger" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The budget impact test applies when the potential net budget impact of a medicine is expected to exceed £40 million per year in any of its first 3 financial years in the NHS. A June 2025 update to NICE's manual recorded the rise from £20 million to £40 million. When the test is triggered, NHS England offers commercial discussions with the company and may request longer than the standard 3 months to implement the funding requirement. Where the impact is expected to significantly exceed £40 million, NICE may consider a pause before publishing final guidance." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 5.10.1 to 5.10.6, 5.10.22 and Update information (June 2025), last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "budget-impact-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:budget-impact-analysis .

heiro:6000300 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Budget Impact Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-4-use-of-the-medicine-in-practice.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Financial Implications for Government Health Budgets" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC submissions present budget impact analyses giving the most likely extent of use of the proposed medicine and its financial impact on the Australian Government. Sponsors justify an epidemiological or market-share approach and use a standardised Excel workbook to estimate net changes for the PBS or RPBS, the MBS and the wider government health budget over six years. Unlike the economic evaluation, these financial analyses exclude health outcomes, apply no discounting and exclude costs from outside the identified budget. Where a special pricing arrangement is offered, costs are shown with and without it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0, Section 4 Use of the medicine in practice (introduction and subsections 4.2 to 4.5), page last updated September 2016" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "budget-impact-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:budget-impact-analysis .

heiro:6000301 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Budget Impact Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/our-approach/methods-process/value-assessment-framework/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Potential Budget Impact: $821 Million Threshold and Affordability Alerts" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER estimates the potential budget impact of a new treatment across all US health care spending over five years, assuming 20% of eligible patients start treatment each year, at net and threshold prices. For new drugs, results are compared with an annual threshold, which ICER lowered in October 2025 from 880millionto880 million to 880millionto821 million. When clinical experts expect uptake to exceed that threshold, the final report issues an Access and Affordability Alert. ICER states that the analysis is not intended to suggest a cap on spending." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023, with revisions to July 2026), section 6 Potential Budget Impact Analysis (6.1 to 6.3); ICER Value Assessment Framework web page, annual budget impact threshold update (October 2025)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "budget-impact-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:budget-impact-analysis .

heiro:6000302 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Budget Impact Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Budget Impact Analysis Mandatory Alongside Health Economic Evaluation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's methods make a budget impact analysis mandatory, from the statutory health insurance perspective, as a supplement to the health economic evaluation of drugs under German social law. The analysis estimates annual expenditure over at least 3 years, comparing a reference scenario without the drug with a scenario in which the drug is available, and reports total and incremental expenditure for each year. IQWiG treats it as information on the financial reasonableness of cost coverage, with additional scenarios showing the effect of uncertain inputs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 4.1, 4.2 (Table 5) and 4.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "budget-impact-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:budget-impact-analysis .

heiro:6000303 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Capacity Planning"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng94/evidence/39bed-occupancy-pdf-172397464704"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Plan Bed Capacity Locally With Dynamic Modelling" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's emergency and acute care guideline asks providers to plan capacity to minimise the risks associated with occupancy rates exceeding 90%. Its committee found the convention of an 85% safe upper limit rested on a 1999 theoretical model unlikely to reflect current NHS practice, judged that optimal occupancy depends on case mix, hospital size, ward type and seasonal effects, and concluded that organisations would need to evaluate their own occupancy levels with dynamic modelling tools. Health systems may set local criteria for safe maximum occupancy while monitoring case mix, care processes and outcomes." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG94, Emergency and acute medical care in over 16s: service delivery and organisation (published 28 March 2018), recommendation 1.3.1; evidence review 39 (Bed occupancy, March 2018), section 39.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "capacity-planning" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:capacity-planning .

heiro:6000304 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Capacity Planning"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/373473"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Workload-Based Staffing Requirements With the WISN Method" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's WISN method sets staffing requirements from the workload of a health facility rather than from population-to-staff ratios or fixed facility standards, which ignore local variation in demand and the work staff actually do. It estimates each cadre's available working time, sets standard workloads for its main activities, adds allowance factors for support and additional activities, and compares current with required staff, a smaller WISN ratio meaning greater work pressure. Using the workloads of planned future services allows the staff needed for those services to be calculated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Workload indicators of staffing need (WISN): user's manual, second edition (ISBN 9789240070066, October 2023), sections 1.1, 1.2, 3.2 to 3.8 and 4.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "capacity-planning" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:capacity-planning .

heiro:6000305 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Capacity Utilisation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng94/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Monitor Bed Occupancy and Avoid Rates Above 90%" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's emergency and acute care guideline asks healthcare providers to monitor total acute hospital bed occupancy, capacity, flow and outcomes in real time and to plan capacity to minimise the risks of occupancy above 90%, a pragmatic maximum chosen because adverse outcomes rose at that level. For costing, NICE's methods manual bases the average cost of a multi-use technology initially on its expected throughput for the uses evaluated, and allows an additional marginal cost analysis where the technology is already recommended for another purpose and enough spare capacity exists." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG94, Emergency and acute medical care in over 16s: service delivery and organisation (published 28 March 2018), recommendation 1.3.1 and evidence review 39 (Bed occupancy), section 39.6; NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.4.20 and 4.4.21, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "capacity-utilisation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:capacity-utilisation .

heiro:6000306 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Capacity Utilisation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://gettingitrightfirsttime.co.uk/medical_specialties/theatres-and-perioperative-medicine/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Minimum 85% Capped Theatre Utilisation for Systems and Providers" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS England's Getting It Right First Time programme targets integrated care systems and providers to achieve a minimum of 85% capped theatre utilisation, describing the target as a key enabler in operational planning guidance and central to elective recovery and the 18-week referral to treatment standard. It identifies variation in theatre utilisation and perioperative pathways across England as a priority, and sets out the main causes of low theatre productivity found through its data and direct work with providers." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England, Getting It Right First Time (GIRFT) programme, Theatres and perioperative medicine (programme web page, read 3 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "capacity-utilisation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:capacity-utilisation .

heiro:6000307 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Caregiver Quality of Life"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Carer HRQoL May Enter QALYs via Disutilities Where Evidenced" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Where an intervention has a substantial and evidenced effect on carers, carer health-related quality of life can be included in the QALY calculation — most commonly as an additive carer disutility linked to the patient’s health state — and the committee decides case by case whether it belongs in the base case. This is the health-effect counterpart to carer costs, which sit outside the reference case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Health Technology Evaluations: The Manual (PMG36); NICE DSU report on modelling carer HRQoL" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "caregiver-quality-of-life" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:caregiver-quality-of-life .

heiro:6000308 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Caregiver Quality of Life"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/publications/publications/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Carer HRQoL Included as Carer Utilities (Societal Perspective)" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Consistent with its societal perspective, ZIN includes effects on informal carers’ health-related quality of life, typically as carer utilities (anchored 0–1 like patient utilities) rather than disutilities, using care-related instruments; patient and carer QALYs are calculated and can be summed." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for Economic Evaluations in Healthcare (2024)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "caregiver-quality-of-life" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:caregiver-quality-of-life .

heiro:6000309 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Carer"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Carer Health Effects Count in the Reference Case When Evidenced" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE includes all relevant health effects for patients and, when relevant, for other people (mainly carers) in the reference-case perspective on outcomes, while costs take the NHS and personal social services (PSS) perspective. Carer health effects need evidence that the condition substantially affects carers' health-related quality of life and showing how the technology affects carers. When patients cannot report their own quality of life, measurements should come from their carer rather than healthcare professionals. Time spent by family carers may be costed only when the NHS or PSS might otherwise have provided the care, and such analyses are shown separately." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.7, 4.3.3, 4.3.17 and 4.4.24, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "carer" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:carer .

heiro:6000310 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Carer"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/RefCase_Sep2023_For-Publication_100124.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Caregiver Productivity and Disutility in the Modified Societal Perspective" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER takes a health care system perspective by default and expects each analysis to add a modified societal perspective. For most analyses that perspective should at least include productivity for the patient and caregiver and disutility for the caregiver. When direct data are lacking, ICER assumes caregiver time equals 75% of the patient's formal labour time lost, valued at a wage plus fringe benefits of $48.05 per hour. A societal co-base case may sit alongside the health system perspective when relevant direct data exist and societal effects, such as caregiver quality of life, are substantial." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, current as of 23 October 2025, Overview (Perspective) and section 1, Explanations (Perspective), Table 2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "carer" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:carer .

heiro:6000311 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Disease Control and Prevention on Case Fatality Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000032 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/csels/dsepd/ss1978/lesson3/section3.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention" ;
    heiro:2000016 "A Proportion of Cases Who Die, Measuring Severity" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CDC defines the case fatality rate as the proportion of persons with a particular condition who die from that condition, and treats it as a measure of the severity of the condition. Because it is a proportion, its numerator is restricted to deaths among the cases in its denominator, although the two time periods need not be the same, and some epidemiologists prefer the term case fatality ratio. It differs from the death-to-case ratio, whose deaths in a period need not come from the new cases counted in that period." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, Principles of Epidemiology in Public Health Practice, third edition, Lesson 3 (Measures of Risk), Section 3 (Mortality Frequency Measures), archived page last reviewed 18 May 2012" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "case-fatality-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:case-fatality-rate .

heiro:6000312 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Case Report"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/regulatory-procedural-guideline/guideline-good-pharmacovigilance-practices-gvp-module-vi-collection-management-submission-reports-suspected-adverse-reactions-medicinal-products-rev-2_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Weekly Literature Screening for Published Case Reports" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA expects marketing authorisation holders to search widely used reference databases for published reports of suspected adverse reactions no less frequently than once a week, and to create one individual case safety report for each identifiable patient. The first or corresponding author is treated as the primary source, and only products the authors identify as having at least a possible causal link need review. EMA itself monitors selected literature for certain active substances, and holders need not submit cases that this service already covers." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EMA and HMA, Guideline on good pharmacovigilance practices (GVP) Module VI, Collection, management and submission of reports of suspected adverse reactions to medicinal products (Rev 2), EMA/873138/2011 Rev 2, legal effective date 22 November 2017, sections VI.B.1.1.2 and VI.C.2.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "case-report" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:case-report .

heiro:6000313 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Case Report"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-314/subpart-B/section-314.80"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Serious Unexpected Literature Case Reports Within 15 Calendar Days" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA regulations require applicants with an approved drug application to review adverse drug experience information from every source, including reports in the scientific literature. Each adverse drug experience that is both serious and unexpected must be reported no later than 15 calendar days after initial receipt. For the literature, this requirement applies only to reports found in scientific and medical journals as case reports or as the result of a formal clinical trial, and each such report must be accompanied by a copy of the published article." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Code of Federal Regulations, Title 21, section 314.80, Postmarketing reporting of adverse drug experiences, paragraphs (b), (c)(1)(i) and (d), eCFR up to date as of 9 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "case-report" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:case-report .

heiro:6000314 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Case Report"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Consistent Case Reports Can Show a Dramatic Effect" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG regards the randomised controlled trial as the gold standard and, in drug benefit assessments, uses non-randomised or observational studies only in justified exceptional cases. One such exception is a disease that would be fatal within a short time without intervention, where several consistent case reports may give sufficient certainty that an intervention prevents that otherwise inevitable course. The study types able to give sufficient certainty for each question are fixed in advance in the report plan." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of Allgemeine Methoden; German original authoritative), DOI 10.60584/General-Methods_V8.0, section 3.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "case-report" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:case-report .

heiro:6000315 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Case Weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/medicare/payment/prospective-payment-systems/acute-inpatient-pps"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "DRG Relative Weights Multiply the Medicare Base Payment Rate" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the Medicare inpatient prospective payment system, each case is assigned to a diagnosis-related group, and each group has a payment weight based on the average resources used to treat Medicare patients in it. The base payment rate, with its labour-related share adjusted by the area wage index, is multiplied by the DRG relative weight. Percentage add-on payments follow for hospitals serving a high share of low-income patients and for approved teaching hospitals, and unusually costly outlier cases receive an additional payment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Acute Inpatient PPS (inpatient prospective payment system under section 1886(d) of the Social Security Act), web page last modified 3 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "case-weight" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:case-weight .

heiro:6000316 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Case Weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "HRG Unit Costs Useful for Resource Use, With Stated Limits" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE describes healthcare resource groups as standard groupings of clinically similar treatments that use common levels of healthcare resources, and calls them a valuable source of information for estimating resource use, with national average unit costs reported through the annual mandatory collection of reference costs. Analysts should consider all relevant HRGs, including critical care and unbundled costs outside the core HRG. HRG data may not be appropriate when the technology and its comparator fall in the same HRG, or when the mean cost does not reflect the technology's resource use; micro-costing may then be better." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.4.9 and 4.4.10, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "case-weight" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:case-weight .

heiro:6000317 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Categorical Data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg51/chapter/statement-on-using-the-eq-5d-5l-value-set"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "EQ-5D-5L Responses Valued With the New UK 5L Value Set" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE explains that EQ-5D levels are the multiple-choice options showing the extent of problems on each dimension, and that a value set is a mathematical model converting these questionnaire responses to utility values. Its interim methods statement makes the EQ-5D-5L descriptive system and the new UK 5L value set the preferred approach for adults, and the UK 5L value set should derive all adult utility values in reference-case analyses. This applies to topics starting after 27 August 2026, while ongoing topics continue mapping 5L data to 3L utility values." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51), published 27 August 2026, sections 1.5 to 1.7, 1.17 and 1.18" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "categorical-data" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:categorical-data .

heiro:6000318 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Categorical Data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD2-General-meta-analysis-corrected-2Sep2016v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Binomial and Multinomial Likelihoods for Categorical Trial Outcomes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The DSU's evidence synthesis framework matches the likelihood and link function to the type of data, with a binomial likelihood and logit link as the core model for binary outcomes. For ordered categorical outcomes, such as the proportions reaching ACR-20, ACR-50 and ACR-70, it uses a multinomial likelihood with a probit link, treating the categories as cut-offs on an underlying continuous scale. This assumes the same treatment effect at every cut-off, which should be checked by comparing effects across cut-offs and by the residual deviance." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 2: A generalised linear modelling framework for pairwise and network meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials (Dias, Welton, Sutton and Ades, August 2011, last updated September 2016), sections 2, 3 and 3.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "categorical-data" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:categorical-data .

heiro:6000319 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Categorical Data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/1f6b8a70-5ce0-404e-9066-120dc9a8df75_en?filename=hta_practical-guideline_direct-and-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Mantel-Haenszel, Peto and Beta-Binomial Methods for Binary Data" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's practical guideline names the Mantel-Haenszel method as the standard fixed-effect approach for meta-analysis of binary data. With rare events the Peto method can be applied, but not when treatment effects are large and trial arms are unbalanced, and with many double-zero studies a beta-binomial model can be used. The choice of method must be justified, with account taken of the number of events and of the effect scale, such as the odds ratio, risk ratio or risk difference." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Coordination Group, Practical Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 4.1.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "categorical-data" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:categorical-data .

heiro:6000320 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Causal Pathway"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Each Link From Surrogate or Test to Final Outcome Needs Evidence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's technology appraisal manual asks for the conceptual model behind a model structure to be transparent and justified, and says prior use of a structure is not enough. When surrogate end points stand in for final outcomes, it grades evidence from biological plausibility up to randomised trials showing a commensurate effect on the final outcome, and treats validation as specific to the population and technology. For diagnostics without direct outcome data, a linked evidence model should specify and review the links between diagnosis, treatment and final outcomes." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section(s) 4.6.3, 4.6.6 to 4.6.8 and 4.6.14, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "causal-pathway" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:causal-pathway .

heiro:6000321 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Causal Pathway"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/nice-dsu/tsds/multivariate-meta-analysis"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Surrogate Pathways Tested by Meta-Analysis Across Randomised Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 20 reports that satisfying the Prentice criteria does not guarantee a causal relationship between treatment effects on a surrogate and on the final outcome. It favours a meta-analytic approach based on several trials, because associations between effects derived directly from randomisation are average causal effects. A single trial cannot show that an association confirmed under one treatment will hold for other interventions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 20, Multivariate meta-analysis of summary data for combining treatment effects on correlated outcomes and evaluating surrogate endpoints, version 2, 22 October 2019, section 3.1.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "causal-pathway" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:causal-pathway .

heiro:6000322 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost-Effectiveness Acceptability Curve (CEAC)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "CEACs With the Acceptability Frontier and Probability at £25,000 to £35,000" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE lists cost-effectiveness acceptability curves among the appropriate ways of presenting parameter uncertainty, alongside scatter plots and confidence ellipses on the cost-effectiveness plane when only 2 alternatives are compared. The presentation of CEACs should include a representation and explanation of the cost-effectiveness acceptability frontier, which shows the probability that the technology with the highest expected net benefit is cost effective. Submissions should also give the probability that the treatment is cost effective at £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY gained and the error probability, particularly when there are more than 2 alternatives." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section 4.7.15, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ceac" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ceac .

heiro:6000323 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Cost-Effectiveness Acceptability Curve (CEAC)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "CEAC Required as a Reference Case Output for Probabilistic Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland's reference case, with which all economic evaluations for decision making must comply, specifies the CE-plane and the CEAC as the reported output of probabilistic or bootstrap analysis. In model-based evaluations and in bootstrap analyses of empirical evaluations, probabilistic results must be plotted on a cost-effectiveness plane, with the percentage of iterations in each quadrant reported, and must also be presented in a cost-effectiveness acceptability curve. Value of information results are reported separately as EVP(P)I curves." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare, 2024 version, chapter 1 (Reference case, table 2) and section 5.3.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ceac" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ceac .

heiro:6000324 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Cost-Effectiveness Acceptability Curve (CEAC)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/RefCase_Sep2023_For-Publication_100124.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Scatter Plots or CEACs for Results at $50,000 to $200,000 per evLY" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's reference case derives expected costs and outcomes for each intervention through probabilistic sensitivity analysis, using sufficient sampling to reflect distributional uncertainty. The output should include a table of the percentage of simulations that achieve 50,000,50,000, 50,000,100,000, 150,000and150,000 and 150,000and200,000 per evLY gained, together with graphs using scatter plots or cost-effectiveness acceptability curves. The CEAC is therefore one of two accepted graphical formats for probabilistic results rather than a mandatory display." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, current as of 23 October 2025, section 1 (Explanations), Analyses and Results: Uncertainty and Sensitivity Analyses" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ceac" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ceac .

heiro:6000325 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Cost-Effectiveness Acceptability Curve (CEAC)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-9-uncertainty-analysis-model-inputs-and-assumptions.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Acceptability Curves for Presenting Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC Guidelines state that a probabilistic sensitivity analysis may be provided in addition to deterministic sensitivity analysis, and note that it can characterise parameter uncertainty but cannot address translational or structural uncertainty. When a PSA is provided, applicants should use cost-effectiveness planes and acceptability curves to present its results, together with a tabulated presentation of interval estimates for the ICER or the incremental net benefits of the proposed medicine. The random seed behind the presented results should be supplied so the analysis can be replicated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee (PBAC Guidelines), version 5.0, section 3A.9.3, page last updated September 2016" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ceac" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ceac .

heiro:6000326 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Cost-Effectiveness Acceptability Curve (CEAC)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "CEAC as an Optional Display of Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods call for a probabilistic sensitivity analysis in health economic evaluations, with its results presented as a scatter plot of incremental cost effectiveness. The result may additionally be presented as a cost-effectiveness acceptability curve, termed a cost-effectiveness acceptance curve in the abbreviations list, or the scatter plot may be extended to include a confidence ellipse. The main result remains the ICER between the treatment alternatives, set out in tabular and graphical form." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, section 4.11 and table 5 (reference case components)" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ceac" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ceac .

heiro:6000327 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost-Effectiveness Acceptability Frontier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Acceptability Curves Must Show and Explain the Frontier" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE lists cost-effectiveness acceptability curves among appropriate ways of presenting parameter uncertainty, and states that their presentation should include a representation and explanation of the cost-effectiveness acceptability frontier, the region showing the probability that the technology with the highest expected net benefit is cost effective. Alongside expected costs, outcomes and ICERs, analyses should report the probability that the treatment is cost effective at maximum acceptable ICERs of £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY gained, and the error probability, particularly when there are more than two alternatives." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section 4.7.15, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ceaf" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ceaf .

heiro:6000328 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Cost-Effectiveness Acceptability Frontier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "The Frontier Follows the Option With the Highest Expected Net Benefit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's national guidelines explain that the option with the highest probability of being cost effective at a given threshold will not necessarily have the highest expected net benefit. The cost-effectiveness acceptability frontier therefore plots the option with the highest expected net benefit across values of the cost-effectiveness threshold. The guidelines caution that the frontier ignores the consequences of failing to select the truly preferred option, so it does not adequately reflect the importance of uncertainty, which the expected value of perfect information does capture." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), section 2.16.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ceaf" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ceaf .

heiro:6000329 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Chi-Square Distribution"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-2/2-6-trial-results-additional-analyses.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Chi-Square Test for Heterogeneity Reported With Every Meta-Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For meta-analyses of trial results, the PBAC Guidelines ask submissions to report the extent of statistical heterogeneity using the Cochran Q statistic, its degrees of freedom, the chi-square test for heterogeneity and the I-squared statistic with its 95% uncertainty interval. The example results table places the chi-square (Q) value, degrees of freedom and P value beside each pooled estimate. Group-level trial data are pooled with a DerSimonian-Laird random effects model, and any other method must be explained and justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsection 2.6.2 and Table 2.6.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "chi-square-distribution" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:chi-square-distribution .

heiro:6000330 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Choice-Based Conjoint"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/download/ga10-03_executive-summary-of-working-paper-11_conjoint-analysis.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pilot Found Choice-Based Weights Feasible for the Efficiency Frontier" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In a 2014 pilot project, IQWiG tested whether choice-based conjoint analysis could identify, weight and prioritise patient-relevant outcomes for its efficiency frontier method in chronic hepatitis C, using a discrete choice experiment, which it calls the choice-based variation of conjoint analysis. Patients and healthcare professionals chose between two fictitious treatments described by seven attributes, and the choices were analysed with logistic regression. IQWiG concluded that weighting patient-relevant outcomes in this way was feasible and that the linearity assumption could be tested, while some methodological issues remained unsolved." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), executive summary of IQWiG working paper GA10-03, Choice-based Conjoint Analysis: pilot project to identify, weight, and prioritize multiple attributes in the indication hepatitis C, version 1.1 (23 July 2014, English translation), sections Research objective, Methods and Conclusion" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "choice-based-conjoint" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:choice-based-conjoint .

heiro:6000331 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Clinical Benefit Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/jcms/c_1729421/en/transparency-committee"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Dual SMR/ASMR Rating Drives Reimbursement Rate and Price" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS’s Transparency Committee gives each drug two clinical ratings rather than an ICER: the actual clinical benefit (Service Médical Rendu, SMR), which determines whether and at what rate the drug is reimbursed (15%, 30%, 65% or 100%; insufficient SMR = not reimbursed); and the improvement in clinical benefit versus existing treatments (Amélioration du Service Médical Rendu, ASMR, on a I–V scale from major therapeutic progress to no improvement), which guides the price negotiation with the CEPS. Formal cost-effectiveness (by the CEESP) enters only for high-impact products." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Transparency Committee doctrine (SMR / ASMR)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "clinical-benefit-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:clinical-benefit-rate .

heiro:6000332 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Clinical Benefit Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-clinical-evaluation-anticancer-medicinal-products-revision-6_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Response or Prolonged Stable Disease, Normally 24 Weeks in Breast Cancer" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EMA guideline on the clinical evaluation of anticancer medicinal products defines the clinical benefit rate, also called clinical benefit response, as complete or partial response or prolonged stable disease, with prolonged stable disease defined by condition and normally at least 24 weeks in breast cancer. For exploratory phase II studies of non-cytotoxic compounds, it states that time to progression or progression-free survival and the clinical benefit rate without an internal reference may in some situations be accepted as a measure of benefit, and advises a systematic literature review in such cases." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use, Guideline on the clinical evaluation of anticancer medicinal products, EMA/CHMP/205/95 Rev. 6 (adopted 18 November 2023, first published 23 January 2024), sections 6.2.2 and 10" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "clinical-benefit-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:clinical-benefit-rate .

heiro:6000333 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Cohen's d"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cohen's d Thresholds for Minor, Considerable and Major Added Benefit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG grades the extent of added benefit on continuous outcomes using the standardised mean difference, expressed as Cohen's d or Hedges' g, with thresholds guided by Cohen's classification of 0.2, 0.5 and 0.8 as small, medium and large effects. The lower limit of the 95% confidence interval must exceed 0.2 for a minor benefit, 0.3 for serious or 0.4 for non-serious outcomes for a considerable benefit, and 0.5 for a major benefit, which applies to serious outcomes only. When no suitable responder analysis exists, Hedges' g is assessed against an irrelevance threshold of 0.2." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 3.3.3 (Table 4) and section 10.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cohens-d" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cohens-d .

heiro:6000334 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Cohen's d"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD2-General-meta-analysis-corrected-2Sep2016v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Standardised Mean Differences Add Heterogeneity Across Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 2 notes that standardised mean differences allow trials that measured the same quantity on different continuous scales to be pooled, and names Cohen's d and Hedges' adjusted g as the best known, differing in how the pooled standard deviation is defined and in Hedges' small-sample correction. Dividing by the sample standard deviation introduces extra heterogeneity, because standard deviations carry sampling error and trials with narrow inclusion criteria tend to give larger values. Dividing estimates from an instrument by a standard deviation from an external representative population sample would give more interpretable results." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 2: A generalised linear modelling framework for pairwise and network meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials (Dias, Welton, Sutton and Ades, August 2011, last updated September 2016), section 3.5.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cohens-d" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cohens-d .

heiro:6000335 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Cohort Simulation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-2-computational-methods-and-structure-of-economic_analysis.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cohort Models Preferred Unless the Structure Cannot Be Implemented" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC Guidelines ask for the least complicated modelling technique that can implement the model structure, moving from decision trees to cohort-based state transition models and then to individual-level models. Cohort-based state transition, or Markov, models suit longer time horizons when a manageable number of health states can represent the condition under the memoryless assumption, with tunnel states for time-varying transitions. A half-cycle correction is the default, and individual-level models are used only when a structure cannot feasibly be implemented as a cohort-based model." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsection 3A.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cohort-simulation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cohort-simulation .

heiro:6000336 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Cohort Simulation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD15_Patient-level_simulation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Choice Between Cohort and Patient-Level Models Must Be Justified" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 15 defines a cohort model as one that estimates outcomes for a group of patients without explicitly considering each individual, although subgroups can be tracked separately, and asks that the choice between cohort-level and patient-level approaches be carefully considered and justified. Using only average characteristics gives a biased mean when heterogeneous characteristics relate non-linearly to outcomes, and replicate or tunnel states eventually make a cohort Markov model unwieldy. Probabilistic sensitivity analysis needs only one simulation loop in a cohort-level model, so it usually runs faster than in an equivalent patient-level model." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15: Cost-effectiveness modelling using patient-level simulation (Davis, Stevenson, Tappenden and Wailoo, April 2014), sections 1, 2.1, 3.1, 3.2 and 3.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cohort-simulation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cohort-simulation .

heiro:6000337 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Cohort Simulation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cohort Model for Homogeneous Groups, Individual Model Otherwise" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS asks for the type of model to be justified, with the choice resting partly on whether the most appropriate statistical unit is a group of individuals with homogeneous characteristics, handled by a cohort model, or individuals with heterogeneous characteristics, handled by an individual-centred model. Its model-choice annex states that an individual simulation is probably necessary when too many dimensions, such as numerous risk factors or subdivided states, rule out a cohort approach. Its sample quality grid checks that the sensitivity analysis of a cohort simulation rests on parameter probability distributions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation, methodological guidance (2020, English version), Guideline 22 (section 4.2), Annex 2 and Annex 10" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cohort-simulation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cohort-simulation .

heiro:6000338 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Cointervention"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/ich-e9-r1-addendum-estimands-and-sensitivity-analysis-clinical-trials-guideline-statistical-principles-clinical-trials-step-5_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Additional or Concomitant Therapy Treated as an Intercurrent Event" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The ICH E9(R1) addendum adopted by EMA lists use of an additional or alternative therapy, including a change to background or concomitant therapy, among the intercurrent events that affect how trial outcomes are interpreted. Trials often classify such treatments as background treatment, rescue medication or prohibited medication so that each can be handled separately. Each estimand must make clear its strategy for these events, for example a treatment policy strategy that uses outcome values whether or not additional medication was taken." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICH E9 (R1) addendum on estimands and sensitivity analysis in clinical trials to the guideline on statistical principles for clinical trials, Step 5 (EMA/CHMP/ICH/436221/2017), adopted by CHMP 30 January 2020, in effect from 30 July 2020, sections A.3.1 and A.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cointervention" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cointervention .

heiro:6000339 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cointervention"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Additional Routine Care as a Potential Effect Modifier in Evidence Synthesis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE lists additional routine care, alongside patient characteristics, care setting and the year of the study, among the factors that can produce differences in relative effectiveness between studies in a systematic review. It asks for such potential effect modifiers to be identified before data analysis, either through a thorough review of the subject area or through discussion with experts in the clinical discipline." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section 3.4.7, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cointervention" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cointervention .

heiro:6000340 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Community Rating"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-B/part-147/section-147.102"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ACA Fair Premium Rules Allow Only Four Rating Factors" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Federal regulations allow premiums in the individual and small group markets to vary only by whether the plan covers an individual or a family, by rating area, by age and by tobacco use. Age variation may not exceed 3 to 1 for adults aged 21 and over, and tobacco variation may not exceed 1.5 to 1. The rate must not vary by any other factor, so health status and claims experience cannot be used. Family premiums are built by adding the premium for each member, counting no more than the three oldest children under 21." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Code of Federal Regulations, Title 45, section 147.102 (Fair health insurance premiums), paragraphs (a), (c) and (g), implementing Public Health Service Act section 2701, for plan years beginning on or after 1 January 2014" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "community-rating" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:community-rating .

heiro:6000341 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Insurance Authority on Community Rating"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000043 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hia.ie/information/lifetime-community-rating-lcr"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Insurance Authority" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Community Rating with Lifetime Community Rating Loadings" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Health Insurance Authority treats community rating as one of four principles of Irish private health insurance, alongside open enrolment, lifetime cover and minimum benefits. Insurers may not set premiums on the basis of an individual's claims risk, so older or less healthy people pay the same as the young and healthy, with limited discounts for children, young adults and groups. People who first buy cover aged 35 or over pay a loading of 2% of the gross premium for each year spent aged 35 or over without cover, up to 70%, for a maximum of 10 years." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Insurance Authority, Lifetime community rating (LCR), web page, accessed 2 October 2026; Department of Health and Health Insurance Authority, Public consultation: community-rated health insurance market in Ireland and proposed changes to the Risk Equalisation Scheme, November 2025 (consultation closed 5 December 2025), section 2; Health Insurance Act 1994 (revised to 1 April 2026), sections 1A and 7" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "community-rating" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:community-rating .

heiro:6000342 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Parliament of Australia on Community Rating"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000081 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.legislation.gov.au/C2007A00031/latest/text"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Parliament of Australia" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Principle of Community Rating in the Private Health Insurance Act 2007" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Private Health Insurance Act 2007 sets a principle of community rating so that everybody who chooses has access to health insurance. Insurers must not improperly discriminate between people on grounds including a chronic disease or other medical condition, gender, age, where a person lives, characteristics likely to increase the need for treatment, how often a person needs treatment, or the benefits claimed, except where the Act allows. One exception is Lifetime Health Cover: adults without hospital cover on the 1 July after turning 31 pay higher premiums, capped at 70% of the base rate and ending after 10 years of continuous cover." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Private Health Insurance Act 2007 (Cth), Compilation No. 42, compilation date 19 September 2026, sections 55-1 and 55-5 (principle of community rating), 34-1, 34-10 and 34-25 (lifetime health cover) and 37-15 (cap of 70% of base rates)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "community-rating" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:community-rating .

heiro:6000343 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Established NHS Practice Identified Through the Scope" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The scope identifies all potentially relevant comparators that are established practice in the NHS, and identification at that stage should be inclusive. Branded, generic and biosimilar medicines can be comparators, as can unlicensed treatments that are established clinical practice, but technologies recommended with managed access cannot. When choosing the most appropriate comparators, the committee considers established NHS practice in England, the natural history of the condition, existing NICE guidance, the comparator's cost effectiveness and its regulatory status. A cost-ineffective established practice can remain a comparator if other NICE guidance recommends it or it is deeply embedded in practice." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 2.2.12, 2.2.13, 2.2.15 and 6.2.2 to 6.2.4, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:comparator .

heiro:6000344 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Existing Practice and Least-Costly Practice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The drug should be compared with existing practice (the most prevalent clinical practice) and with minimum practice (the least-costly alternative or no treatment); other relevant comparators may also be included." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH (now CDA-AMC), Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition (2017)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:comparator .

heiro:6000345 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-1/1-1-clinical-issue-addressed_by-the-submission.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Therapy Most Likely to Be Replaced" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The main comparator is the therapy that prescribers would most replace with the proposed medicine, judged on what would be likely to happen in practice rather than what should happen. Where pharmacological analogues are already listed, the main comparator is usually the analogue prescribed on the PBS for the largest number of patients in the target population. Where no PBS medicine is listed for the population, or the medicine is used in addition to existing treatment, the comparator is usually clearly defined standard medical management." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for preparing a submission to the PBAC, Version 5.0 (September 2016), section 1.1.3 (Intervention and comparator)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:comparator .

heiro:6000346 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "All Clinically Relevant Interventions Must Be Identified" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The reference case analysis should identify all clinically relevant interventions in the population analysed, including options such as therapeutic abstention or non-medicinal therapy, and any exclusion must be justified. The choice should first rest on good practice guidelines published by scientific institutions free of conflicts of interest. Low use alone does not justify excluding a medically relevant intervention, medicines widely used without a marketing authorisation may be included, and the impact of excluding a comparator is tested in a sensitivity analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (April 2020), section 1.5 (Guideline 5)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:comparator .

heiro:6000347 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Standard or Usual Dutch Practice Plus the Most Cost-Effective Option" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The intervention must be compared with the standard or usual intervention in Dutch practice at the time. The standard intervention is the first choice under clinical guidelines or practice; where none exists, the usual intervention, meaning the one used most frequently, applies and may be watchful waiting with supportive or palliative care. If the standard or usual intervention is not the most cost-effective option available in the Netherlands, the most cost-effective intervention must also be compared, using a fully incremental analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland (National Health Care Institute), Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version, 16 January 2024), section 2.3.3 and table 2" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:comparator .

heiro:6000348 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Appropriate Comparator Therapy Specified by the G-BA" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In a health economic evaluation, the base case compares the drug with the appropriate comparator therapy specified by the Federal Joint Committee (G-BA) and used to demonstrate added benefit. This should preferably be a treatment established in practice and not excluded by the efficiency principle. Where several treatment options are equally appropriate, sensitivity analyses cover the range of their costs, in particular the cheapest and the most expensive. Other comparators may be added depending on the commission, which may require a new benefit assessment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, sections 3.3.3 and 4.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 6 ;
    heiro:2000020 "comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:comparator .

heiro:6000349 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Treatments Most Likely Used in the Absence of the Intervention" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The intervention should be compared with the available and feasible relevant treatments that would most likely be used for the target population in the absence of the intervention. These will often be specific alternative treatments, some of which may have been active comparators in clinical trials, or a generally defined usual care approach mixing active and supportive care. A do-nothing option, typically used for placebo comparisons in trials, may also be appropriate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, current as of 23 October 2025, section 1 (Decision Problem: Comparators)" ;
    heiro:2000019 7 ;
    heiro:2000020 "comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:comparator .

heiro:6000350 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Comparator"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/7be11d76-9a78-426c-8e32-79d30a115a64_en?filename=hta_jca_scoping-process_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Member States Define Comparators in the PICO Scope" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For joint clinical assessments, Member States define the comparators for each patient population they request, and more than one comparator can be relevant. Comparator treatments need not be licensed in the EU, can be drug classes, and should not specify dosage or regimen. Naming standard of care alone is not enough, because its components must be specified for the relevant health care system. Comparators can also include non-drug interventions, best supportive care or watchful waiting." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment (HTACG), Guidance on the scoping process, V1.0 of 13 November 2024 (adopted 28 November 2024), section 3.1.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 8 ;
    heiro:2000020 "comparator" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:comparator .

heiro:6000351 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Comparator Selection"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-1/1-1-clinical-issue-addressed_by-the-submission.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "\"Therapy Most Likely To Be Replaced in Practice\"; PBAC Can Reject the Nominated Comparator" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The submission must nominate as its main comparator the therapy prescribers would most replace with the proposed medicine — judged on what would likely happen in practice, not what should happen. A defined hierarchy applies (usually the pharmacological analogue already listed for the largest number of PBS patients in the target population). The comparator is not merely current best practice, and the PBAC can reject a sponsor’s nominated comparator and substitute another, which resets the entire clinical and economic evaluation built on it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC, Sections 1.1–2.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "comparator-selection" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:comparator-selection .

heiro:6000352 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Concentration Curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240102606"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Curve Plots Cumulative Health Share Against Ranked Population" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's 2024 handbook on health inequality monitoring uses the concentration curve to show how the absolute and relative concentration indices are calculated. Subgroups are ranked by an ordered social dimension, with the cumulative population share on the x-axis and the cumulative share of the health indicator on the y-axis. With no systematic difference in health across the dimension, the curve runs along the 45-degree diagonal, and the further it lies from that line, the greater the inequality. If health does not follow the subgroup ordering, these measures may suggest little inequality." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Health inequality monitoring: harnessing data to advance health equity (2024), ISBN 978-92-4-010260-6, Chapter 21 (Complex summary measures of health inequality: ordered disproportionality measures) and Annex 17" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "concentration-curve" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:concentration-curve .

heiro:6000353 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Concentration Index"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/379703"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Absolute and Relative Concentration Indices for Ordered Inequality" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's 2024 book on health inequality monitoring treats the concentration index as an ordered disproportionality measure, weighted by population size, for dimensions with a natural order such as wealth or education. The absolute concentration index is twice the area between the line of equality and the concentration curve, and the relative index divides it by the setting average, bounding it between minus 1 and plus 1. Zero means no inequality and positive values show concentration among advantaged subgroups. WHO notes the measures can suggest low inequality when health does not follow the subgroup ordering." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Health inequality monitoring: harnessing data to advance health equity (ISBN 9789240102606, December 2024), chapter 21, summary measures based on ordered inequality dimensions" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "concentration-index" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:concentration-index .

heiro:6000354 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of International Council for Harmonisation on Concurrent Control"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000037 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://database.ich.org/sites/default/files/E10_Guideline.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "International Council for Harmonisation" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Concurrent Control Needed in Most Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICH E10 defines a concurrent control group as one chosen from the same population as the test group, treated in a defined way within the same trial and over the same period. A similar group studied earlier may serve as a historical control only in unusual cases where the course of illness is predictable; in most situations a concurrent control is needed. Four of the five control types it describes are concurrent, while external controls are set apart because of serious concerns about comparability and bias." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICH E10, Choice of control group and related issues in clinical trials, Step 4 version, 20 July 2000 (adopted in the EU as CPMP/ICH/364/96), sections 1.2 and 1.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "concurrent-control" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:concurrent-control .

heiro:6000355 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Concurrent Control"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/174976/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Primary Comparisons Use Only Concurrently Eligible Controls" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA draft guidance on master protocols recommends that the control group for the primary comparison of each drug include only concurrently eligible participants, meaning those who could have been randomised to that drug but were concurrently randomised to control. Nonconcurrent controls risk bias from shifts over time in participants, trial conduct or standard of care. Other control data may be justified in rare circumstances, such as early-phase exploratory trials or rare diseases, and that choice should be agreed with FDA before the trial starts." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US FDA, Master Protocols for Drug and Biological Product Development, draft guidance for industry, Revision I, June 2026, docket FDA-2023-D-5259, section III.B (Control Group)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "concurrent-control" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:concurrent-control .

heiro:6000356 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Concurrent Control"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/concept-paper-platform-trials_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Non-Concurrent Controls Can Undermine Type I Error Control" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EMA concept paper on platform trials lists non-concurrently randomised controls among design features that may affect estimation, Type I error rates and uncertainty. It describes them as frequently used but not needed by design, and warns that they can violate key principles of confirmatory trials intended to support approval, such as strict Type I error control, and increase bias. A planned reflection paper is to discuss whether their use can be reasonable, and the concept paper remains the current EMA document on platform trials." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EMA CHMP, Concept paper on platform trials, EMA/CHMP/840036/2022, adopted for release for consultation 31 October 2022 (consultation closed 31 January 2023), section 2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "concurrent-control" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:concurrent-control .

heiro:6000357 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Conflict of Interest"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://a.storyblok.com/f/243782/x/522b952db1/declaring-managing-interests-for-advisory-committees.docx"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Declaring and Managing Interests on NICE Advisory Committees" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's policy asks committee members and other contributors to declare any interest that a reasonable person would see as relevant, covering the preceding 12 months and updated before and at each meeting, and states that declaring an interest does not mean there is a conflict. Responses range from open declaration through partial exclusion to complete exclusion. A member with a specific financial interest leaves for the relevant item unless the interest has ended with no scope to benefit, and people likely to be excluded from more than 50% of discussions should not be appointed." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Policy on declaring and managing interests for NICE advisory committees, version 1.7, current issue November 2025, sections Declaring interests, Responses to declared interests and Interests at appointment" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "conflict-of-interest" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:conflict-of-interest .

heiro:6000358 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Conflict of Interest"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/other/policy-44-european-medicines-agency-policy-handling-competing-interests-scientific-committees-members-experts_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Restrictions and Cooling-Off Periods for Competing Interests" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA's policy states that having interests is not the same as having a competing interest, which exists when an interest may affect, or be reasonably perceived to affect, impartiality. Restrictions depend on the nature of the interest, its timeframe and the type of activity. Current employment, consultancy on general matters or financial interests in a pharmaceutical company are generally incompatible with involvement, several past interests carry restrictions during a 3-year cooling-off period, and stricter rules may apply to chairs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency policy on the handling of competing interests of scientific committees' members and experts, POLICY/0044 (EMA/54457/2024), effective 1 May 2025, sections 1, 3.2.2 and 4.2.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "conflict-of-interest" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:conflict-of-interest .

heiro:6000359 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Conflict of Interest"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Disclosure and Assessment of External Experts' Relationships" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under German social law, external experts must disclose all connections to associations and contract organisations, particularly in the pharmaceutical and medical device industries, before IQWiG commissions them. IQWiG assesses whether the disclosures raise serious doubts about appropriate collaboration because of conflicts of interest, in which case collaboration on that commission is usually not possible or only possible under specific provisions. External reviewers must also disclose potential conflicts, and relationships are published only in summary form." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, sections 2.2.2 and 2.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "conflict-of-interest" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:conflict-of-interest .

heiro:6000360 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences on Conflict of Interest"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000031 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://cioms.ch/wp-content/uploads/2017/01/WEB-CIOMS-EthicalGuidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Conflicts of Interest in Health-Related Research Must Be Managed" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CIOMS defines conflicts of interest as conflicts between the primary goal of health-related research and secondary interests such as scientific recognition or financial gain. Research institutions should develop policies to mitigate them, researchers should disclose relevant interests to research ethics committees, and committees should evaluate each study in light of those interests. Measures should be proportional, from disclosure for a minor conflict to excluding a researcher, and a committee may decide not to approve a study when a serious conflict cannot be adequately mitigated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "International Ethical Guidelines for Health-related Research Involving Humans, fourth edition, prepared by CIOMS in collaboration with WHO, Geneva 2016, Guideline 25 and commentary" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "conflict-of-interest" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:conflict-of-interest .

heiro:6000361 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Conjoint Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/appendixes/appendix-6-including-nonhealth-outcomes-in-a-supplementary-analysis.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Conjoint Analysis to Value Nonhealth Benefits in a Supplementary Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC Guidelines allow direct patient benefits that are not health outcomes, such as a more convenient form of administration, to be valued in a supplementary analysis. Methods to estimate the monetary or other value of the nonhealth benefit may include a conjoint analysis or a discrete choice experiment with a monetary attribute. Where health outcomes do not otherwise differ substantively from the main comparator, the resulting estimate, such as willingness to pay, can enter a supplementary cost-benefit analysis, and any consumer surplus needs a nominated basis for sharing between the sponsor and the taxpayer." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), Appendix 6 (Including nonhealth outcomes in a supplementary analysis)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "conjoint-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:conjoint-analysis .

heiro:6000362 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Construct Validity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Poor Construct Validity Needed to Depart From the EQ-5D" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE prefers the EQ-5D for measuring health-related quality of life in adults. To argue that the EQ-5D is inappropriate, qualitative empirical evidence must show that key dimensions of health are missing, supported by evidence that the EQ-5D performs poorly on tests of construct validity, meaning it does not perform as would be expected, and on responsiveness in a particular patient population. This evidence should come from a synthesis of peer-reviewed literature, and any alternative measure must be accompanied by a detailed account of its methods." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.3.6 and 4.3.10, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "construct-validity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:construct-validity .

heiro:6000363 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Construct Validity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD8-Introduction-to-MVH_final.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Known Groups and Convergence as Tests of EQ-5D Construct Validity" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 8 defines construct validity as the extent to which a measure's scores agree with other measures or indicators of the dimensions of health-related quality of life relevant to the patient group. It names two common tests, known group differences and convergent validity, and states that validation is best performed at dimension level, although evidence is often available only for the index. Such tests can never prove validity, and groupings based on clinical measures such as visual acuity or respiratory function may relate only weakly to health-related quality of life." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 8: An introduction to the measurement and valuation of health for NICE submissions (Brazier and Longworth, August 2011), section 3.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "construct-validity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:construct-validity .

heiro:6000364 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Construct Validity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/77832/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Construct Validity as Evidence Against Prior Hypotheses" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's 2009 guidance on patient-reported outcome measures defines construct validity as evidence that relationships among items, domains and concepts conform to a priori hypotheses about logical relationships with other measures or patient groups, and reviews convergent, discriminant and known-groups evidence. Its October 2025 guidance on fit-for-purpose clinical outcome assessments treats validity as a unitary concept, describing sources of evidence instead of types of validity, and asks sponsors to show that correlations with other measures and differences between known groups match relationships hypothesised a priori." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Guidance for Industry. Patient-Reported Outcome Measures: Use in Medical Product Development to Support Labeling Claims, final guidance (December 2009), section III.E.2, Table 2 and Glossary; U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Patient-Focused Drug Development: Selecting, Developing, or Modifying Fit-for-Purpose Clinical Outcome Assessments, final guidance (Guidance 3, October 2025), sections IV.B and IV.H, footnotes 33 and 44, and Table 2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "construct-validity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:construct-validity .

heiro:6000365 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Consumer Surplus"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/appendixes/appendix-6-including-nonhealth-outcomes-in-a-supplementary-analysis.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Consumer Surplus From Nonhealth Benefits Shared With the Taxpayer" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC Guidelines allow direct patient benefits that are not health outcomes, such as a more convenient form of administration, to be valued in a supplementary analysis, for example through a conjoint analysis or a discrete choice experiment with a monetary attribute. Where health outcomes do not otherwise differ substantively from the main comparator, the resulting estimate, such as willingness to pay, can be included in a supplementary cost-benefit analysis. If that analysis results in a consumer surplus, the submission must nominate a suitable basis for sharing it between the sponsor and the taxpayer." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), Appendix 6 (Including nonhealth outcomes in a supplementary analysis)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "consumer-surplus" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:consumer-surplus .

heiro:6000366 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Contamination"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cluster Allocation Accepted Where Contamination Threatens Prevention Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG assesses the benefit of prevention programmes other than screening mainly through prospective comparative intervention studies, ideally with random allocation of participants to a strategy with or without the prevention measure, that report patient-relevant outcomes. Because of potential contamination between the intervention and control groups, it states that studies in which clusters were allocated to the study arms may also be eligible. Other designs, such as interrupted time series for mass-media campaigns, are judged case by case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of Allgemeine Methoden 8.0), section 3.7 Prevention" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "contamination" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:contamination .

heiro:6000367 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Contingency Table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/71147/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Report the Original 2x2 Table for Diagnostic Test Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance on diagnostic test studies recommends reporting the 2x2 table of results comparing the candidate test with the reference standard or comparative method, together with sensitivity and specificity pairs or agreement measures and their confidence intervals. Results should also be reported by site and by relevant clinical and demographic subgroups. FDA advises against revising the original 2x2 table through discrepant resolution, because the revised table rests on untested assumptions and its sensitivity and specificity are not valid estimates of performance." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US FDA, Statistical Guidance on Reporting Results from Studies Evaluating Diagnostic Tests, guidance for industry and FDA staff, issued 13 March 2007, docket 2003D-0044, sections 5, 6 and 7.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "contingency-table" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:contingency-table .

heiro:6000368 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Contingency Table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Effect Measures and Zero Cells in 2x2 Tables" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG treats binary outcomes as analyses of 2x2 tables and applies the usual effect and risk measures of such tables to categorical outcomes, with the statistical uncertainty described for every estimate. In meta-analyses with zero cells it adds a correction of 0.5 to each cell of the fourfold table, an approach it considers appropriate only while zero cells are few. For very rare events the Peto odds ratio needs no correction, but suits only effects that are not too large in a balanced design." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of Allgemeine Methoden; German original authoritative), DOI 10.60584/General-Methods_V8.0, sections 10.3.1 and 10.3.7" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "contingency-table" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:contingency-table .

heiro:6000369 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Continuous Data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD12-Utilities-in-modelling-FINAL.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Normal Sampling Usually Adequate for Uncertainty in Mean Utility" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The DSU notes that health-related quality of life data, particularly the EQ-5D preferred by NICE, are not normally distributed, being typically skewed, bimodal or trimodal, bounded by the index limits and often negative. It states that in most cases the uncertainty in the mean can still be described adequately by sampling from a normal distribution. Exceptions include patient-level simulation using a cohort with widely varying scores and a low or high mean, where utilities can be expressed as decrements from full health and sampled from a log-normal or gamma distribution." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 12: The use of health state utility values in decision models (Ara and Wailoo, July 2011), section 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "continuous-data" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:continuous-data .

heiro:6000370 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Continuous Data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Responder Analyses at 15% of Scale Range Preferred Over Continuous Data" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG uses pre-specified responder analyses whose response criterion is at least 15% of the instrument's scale range, and as a rule does not use criteria below that level. Where no suitable responder analysis exists but continuous data are available, it may use the standardised mean difference as Hedges' g, requiring the whole confidence interval to lie above an irrelevance threshold of 0.2, or a post hoc criterion of exactly 15%. When suitable responder analyses and continuous analyses are both available, the responder analyses are used." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of Allgemeine Methoden 8.0; the German original is authoritative), section 10.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "continuous-data" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:continuous-data .

heiro:6000371 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Continuous Data"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/a70a62c7-325c-401e-ba42-66174b656ab8_en?filename=hta_outcomes_jca_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Continuous Outcomes Analysed on Their Continuous Scale in JCAs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's outcomes guidance requires uncategorised data always to be presented, with outcomes conceived as continuous, such as health-related quality of life, assessed by measures and methods consistent with that property, for example change in scores over time. Responder definitions can aid interpretation, but because they discretise continuously measured variables they should complement continuous scores rather than replace them. One measure of treatment effect should be pre-specified as the primary analysis to avoid data dredging and an inflated type 1 error rate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Coordination Group, Guidance on outcomes for joint clinical assessments, adopted 10 June 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 5.3 (Interpretability)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "continuous-data" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:continuous-data .

heiro:6000372 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Continuous Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD15_Patient-level_simulation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Fixed-Cycle Models as Discrete Approximations to Continuous Processes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 15 describes a state-transition model as a discrete time approximation to clinical events that can occur at any point in time. Accuracy improves with continuity corrections such as the half-cycle correction and with shorter cycles, and reducing the cycle length until it has no appreciable effect on model outcomes is recommended. Incorrect discretisation can introduce bias, for example when several transitions can occur within one cycle, and where rapid events alternate with long quiet periods a discrete event simulation may be more efficient than very short cycles." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15: Cost-effectiveness modelling using patient-level simulation (Davis, Stevenson, Tappenden and Wailoo, April 2014), sections 2.6 and 3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "continuous-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:continuous-model .

heiro:6000373 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Continuous Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Discrete or Continuous Handling of Time Must Justify the Model Choice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS requires the choice of model type to be justified, mainly by how the model integrates time, including the duration of the simulation, the possibility of recurrence and whether progression over time is discrete or continuous. Its model-choice annex sets discrete-time forms beside continuous-time ones, such as continuous-time Markov chain models, system dynamics based on ordinary differential equations and continuous-time discrete event simulation. Where numerous events are likely to occur in one time unit, it advises a model with short time intervals or a continuous-time model." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation, methodological guidance (2020, English version), Guideline 22 (section 4.2) and Annex 10 (Tables 17 and 18)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "continuous-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:continuous-model .

heiro:6000374 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Convergent Validity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD8-Introduction-to-MVH_final.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Convergent Validity as Evidence on Whether EQ-5D Suits a Patient Group" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 8 names convergent validity, the extent to which a measure correlates with another measure of health, as one of two common tests of construct validity, alongside known group differences. It can be assessed by the strength of association between EQ-5D and another preference-based measure such as HUI3 or SF-6D, and a lack of association may suggest that EQ-5D is not valid in that group. Convergence with directly administered time trade-off, standard gamble or visual analogue scale values is a more limited test, because these reflect patient rather than general population values." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 8: An introduction to the measurement and valuation of health for NICE submissions (Brazier and Longworth, August 2011), section 3.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "convergent-validity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:convergent-validity .

heiro:6000375 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Convergent Validity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/159500/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Convergent Evidence for Clinical Outcome Assessments" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's final guidance on fit-for-purpose clinical outcome assessments uses the term convergent evidence, noting that it was previously called convergent validity, in line with testing standards that treat validity as a unitary concept. When a measure reflects the meaningful aspect of health only indirectly, sponsors may relate its scores to other variables more directly tied to the patient's experience, such as performance tests, and predict the expected correlation in advance. Correlation cutoffs should reflect the hypothesised relationship, and interpretation should allow for restriction of range and unreliability." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Patient-Focused Drug Development: Selecting, Developing, or Modifying Fit-for-Purpose Clinical Outcome Assessments, final guidance (Guidance 3, October 2025), section IV.H and footnotes 33, 42 and 43" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "convergent-validity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:convergent-validity .

heiro:6000376 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Conversion Factor"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/11/05/2025-19787/medicare-and-medicaid-programs-cy-2026-payment-policies-under-the-physician-fee-schedule-and-other"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Two 2026 Conversion Factors Turn Relative Value Units Into Payment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the Medicare Physician Fee Schedule, national relative values for work, practice expense and malpractice, adjusted for geographic cost differences, are multiplied by a conversion factor to give payment rates. From 2026 the statute requires two factors, and the final rule sets them at USD 33.5675 for qualifying alternative payment model participants and USD 33.4009 for others, against a single 2025 factor of USD 32.3465. Both include a positive budget neutrality adjustment of 0.49%, because changes in relative values may not shift Part B spending by more than USD 20 million." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Medicare and Medicaid Programs; CY 2026 Payment Policies Under the Physician Fee Schedule and Other Changes to Part B Payment and Coverage Policies, final rule CMS-1832-F (90 FR 49266, 5 November 2025, as corrected), sections II.A and VI.D.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "conversion-factor" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:conversion-factor .

heiro:6000377 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) on COPD Assessment Test"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000082 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://goldcopd.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/GOLD-REPORT-2026-v1.3-8Dec2025_WMV2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "CAT Score of 10 Marks the Symptom Cut-Point in COPD Assessment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "GOLD treats SGRQ scores of 25 or more as very uncommon in healthy persons and gives 10 as the equivalent cut-point for the COPD Assessment Test, which separates less symptomatic from more symptomatic patients in the ABE assessment tool. GOLD states that no equivalent cut-point can be calculated for the mMRC breathlessness scale and recommends a comprehensive symptom assessment. The 2026 report adjusted the criteria for groups A, B and E, with one moderate exacerbation now the threshold for considering treatment escalation, and states that the CAT and the Chronic Airways Assessment Test (CAAT) are equivalent with interchangeable scores." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease, Global Strategy for the Diagnosis, Management, and Prevention of Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease, 2026 Report, version 1.3, 8 December 2025, assessment of symptoms and the ABE assessment tool" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "copd-assessment-test" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:copd-assessment-test .

heiro:6000378 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Core Outcome Set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg20/chapter/developing-review-questions-and-planning-the-evidence-review"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Core Outcome Sets Should Be Used in Guideline Reviews When Suitable" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In developing NICE guidelines, core outcome sets should be used if they are suitable based on quality and validity, and the COMET database is named as one source for finding them. The suitability of an identified core outcome set is assessed with the Core Outcome Set Standards for Development (COS-STAD) and the Core Outcome Set Standards for Reporting (COS-STAR). The guidance sits in the manual's advice on outcomes for review questions about the effectiveness of interventions, and applies to the evidence reviews behind NICE guidelines." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence, Developing NICE guidelines: the manual (PMG20), chapter 4 Developing review questions and planning the evidence review, last updated 23 October 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "core-outcome-set" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:core-outcome-set .

heiro:6000379 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "NHS and PSS Resources Valued at the Prices the NHS Pays" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Reference-case costs cover resources under the control of the NHS and personal social services, valued at the prices the NHS actually pays, including price reductions available across the NHS such as patient access schemes. National average HRG unit costs from reference costs are a valuable source, with micro-costing where HRGs do not reflect the technology. Costs from earlier years are adjusted with indices such as the NHS cost inflation index. Productivity costs are excluded from the reference case and may be presented separately." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.4.1, 4.4.4, 4.4.9, 4.4.10, 4.4.12 and 4.4.23, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost .

heiro:6000380 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Publicly Funded Health Care Payer Perspective" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In the reference case, costs should be identified, measured, and valued from the perspective of the publicly funded health care payer; broader societal costs may be included only as a non-reference case analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH (now CDA-AMC), Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition (2017), Guideline Statement 11.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost .

heiro:6000381 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-6-health-care-resource-use-and-costs.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Care Resource Items Costed in Australian Dollars for a Stated Year" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Submissions identify each direct health care resource item whose use changes when the proposed medicine replaces the main comparator, and quantify it in natural units such as packs dispensed or admissions. The amount provided, not the amount consumed, is the relevant measure, so wastage is costed. Unit costs are in Australian dollars for a stated year close to the submission date, using the Manual of resource items and their associated costs, and historical costs are inflated with a relevant Australian health price index. Productivity impacts appear only in supplementary analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing submissions to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 3A.6.1 and 3A.1.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost .

heiro:6000382 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "SHI Cost Components, Friction Costs and Consumer Price Index Adjustment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "From the reference-case perspective of the community of statutory health insurance (SHI) members, direct reimbursable costs and insured persons' own expenses, including time costs, are counted, while transfer payments such as sickness allowance are left out. Resources are valued with standardised methods, using regulated prices such as per-case flat rates or negotiated drug prices. Under a societal perspective, productivity losses from paid work are valued by the friction cost approach, with friction costs set at 80% of wage costs. Costs from different years are adjusted with the German consumer price index." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, sections 4.3.4, 4.7.1, 4.7.2 and 4.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost .

heiro:6000383 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health System Costs, Net Drug Prices and PCE Health Care Inflation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The base case includes all relevant health system costs paid by third-party payers, including future related and unrelated health care costs, and any excluded cost must be justified. Branded drug acquisition costs use an estimated net price, with the wholesale acquisition cost kept for calculating the discount needed to reach each price benchmark, and publicly available sources such as Medicare fee schedules are preferred for other costs. All costs are converted to current-year United States dollars with the Personal Consumption Expenditures health care services price index. Patient and caregiver productivity is counted in the modified societal perspective." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale (current as of 23 October 2025), Perspective; Resource Use and Costs; Converting to Current Year US Dollars; Appendix D" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost .

heiro:6000384 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Unit Production Costs in France, With Tariffs as the Fallback" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Only direct costs enter the reference case, identified, measured and valued for the resources linked to the intervention over the time horizon. Resources are valued at their unit production cost in France, with French tariffs used where production costs are unavailable or unsuitable, and hospital stays are valued through the National Cost Study when its precision allows. Each resource is expressed in euros for a reference year using INSEE consumer price indices for health products and services. Indirect costs, mainly productivity losses, may be presented only in a supplemental analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (April 2020), Guidelines 18 to 20, sections 3.1 to 3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 6 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost .

heiro:6000385 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Societal Costs Valued With Costing Manual Reference Prices" ;
    heiro:2000017 "All relevant costs are included irrespective of who bears them: health care costs, including those in life years gained, costs for patients and family such as travel and informal care, and costs in other sectors, each calculated as volume times price. The methods of the costing manual must be used to identify, measure and value costs, and its reference prices are the starting point for valuation. Productivity losses from paid and unpaid work are part of the reference case and are valued with the friction cost method. Prices from different years are indexed with the Dutch consumer price index." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), section 3.2; Costing manual: Methods and Reference Prices for Economic Evaluations in Healthcare (2024 version)" ;
    heiro:2000019 7 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost .

heiro:6000386 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of TLV on Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000028 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.tlv.se/download/18.467926b615d084471ac3230c/1510316374332/TLVAR_2017_1.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "TLV" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Societal Costs Shown With and Without Production Losses" ;
    heiro:2000017 "All relevant costs in society linked to treatment and illness should be identified, quantified and valued, whoever bears them, whether county council, municipality, state, patient or relative, and inputs should reflect Swedish conditions. Results should be calculated both with and without indirect costs such as lost production, estimated with the human capital method. Unit costs and quantities should be presented separately where possible, the price year should be stated, and medicines should be costed at the pharmacy retail price (AUP)." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Tandvårds- och läkemedelsförmånsverket (TLV), general guidelines on economic evaluations (TLVAR 2003:2), reprinted as amended by TLVAR 2017:1 (in force 15 February 2017), points 2 and 6" ;
    heiro:2000019 8 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost .

heiro:6000387 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Cost-Benefit Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#shortlist-appraisal-1"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Social Cost-Benefit Analysis as the Recommended Shortlist Method" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Green Book names social cost-benefit analysis as the recommended approach for detailed comparison of shortlisted options, counting costs and benefits to UK society as a whole. Real monetisable costs and benefits are discounted at the social time preference rate of 3.5% for years 1 to 30 and summarised as net present social value, benefit-cost ratio and return on public sector cost. These metrics give only an initial ranking, and proposals should not be rejected simply because a benefit-cost ratio falls below a set threshold." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, The Green Book (2026), updated 5 February 2026, chapter 6 Shortlist appraisal: sections Methods of analysis, Scope, Discounting, Summary metrics of social value (Table 10) and Identifying the preferred option" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-benefit-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-benefit-analysis .

heiro:6000388 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Disease Control and Prevention on Cost-Benefit Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000032 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cdc.gov/polaris/php/economics/cost-benefit.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Public Health Cost-Benefit Analysis and Net Benefits" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CDC's POLARIS economics guidance describes cost-benefit analysis as comparing the costs and benefits of an intervention with both expressed in monetary units. Unlike cost-effectiveness analysis, it places a monetary value on health outcomes, counting benefits such as medical costs averted, productivity gains and the monetary value of health improvements. The output is net benefit, benefits minus costs, which indicates the economic value an intervention provides to society and allows health and non-health interventions to be compared." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, POLARIS Economics: Cost-Benefit Analysis (web page, 20 September 2024), sections What is cost-benefit analysis?, What inputs are included?, What output does a cost-benefit analysis provide? and How can decision makers use this information?" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-benefit-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-benefit-analysis .

heiro:6000389 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Cost-Benefit Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240057906"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Valuing Health Co-Benefits of Climate Action in Cost-Benefit Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "A 2023 WHO technical document, developed with the International Institute for Sustainable Development, gives guidance on valuing the health co-benefits of climate change mitigation and adaptation action for inclusion in sector-specific cost-benefit analysis. It structures the analysis around investments, avoided costs and added benefits, with avoided health costs valued through cost of illness, the value of a statistical life and the value of a life year lost. It treats cost-benefit and cost-effectiveness analysis as complementary methods drawing on the same inputs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, A framework for the quantification and economic valuation of health outcomes originating from health and non-health climate change mitigation and adaptation action (technical document, 2023, ISBN 9789240057906), chapter 4 How to prioritize interventions based on CBA and CEA, section 4.1 and Tables 6 to 9" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-benefit-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-benefit-analysis .

heiro:6000390 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of US Environmental Protection Agency on Cost-Benefit Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000027 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2024-12/chapter-1-guidelines-for-preparing-economic-analyses_final_508-compliant.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "US Environmental Protection Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Benefit-Cost Analysis and the Potential Pareto Criterion" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EPA Guidelines focus on benefit-cost analysis and economic impact analysis, which they describe as the two mainstays of the agency's economic analyses. Benefit-cost analysis assesses economic efficiency using the Potential Pareto criterion: a policy moves towards efficiency when those who gain could fully compensate those who lose and remain better off, so net benefits are positive. Benefits and costs that cannot be quantified or monetised should still be described, and distributional effects are assessed separately from the efficiency analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Environmental Protection Agency, Guidelines for Preparing Economic Analyses, 3rd edition (2024), EPA-240-R-24-001, Chapter 1 Introduction, sections 1.2, 1.3 and 1.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-benefit-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-benefit-analysis .

heiro:6000391 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CPB and PBL (Netherlands) on Cost-Benefit Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000044,
        heiro:5000045 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.pbl.nl/uploads/default/downloads/pbl-cpb-2015-general-guidance-for-cost-benefit-analysis_01512.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CPB and PBL (Netherlands)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Social Cost-Benefit Analysis as an Ex Ante Policy Information Tool" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Dutch general guidance treats social cost-benefit analysis as an information tool for the ex ante assessment of policy options. It sets out the effects, risks and uncertainties of a measure and values the resulting costs and benefits to society as a whole in euros, including effects without a market price such as nature and community safety, to give a balance of benefits minus costs. The analysis does not value how different groups experience these effects but can reveal them, and it is not considered appropriate where human dignity or moral values are the main consideration." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CPB Netherlands Bureau for Economic Policy Analysis and PBL Netherlands Environmental Assessment Agency, General Guidance for Cost-Benefit Analysis (2015), Summary" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-benefit-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-benefit-analysis .

heiro:6000392 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Office for Health Improvement and Disparities on Cost-Consequence Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000083 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/guidance/cost-consequence-analysis-health-economic-studies"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Office for Health Improvement and Disparities" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Disaggregated Costs and Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "OHID guidance on evaluating digital health products says a cost consequence analysis should report costs and effects in a simple, disaggregated format, with each cost and effect component shown separately at a level of detail that lets decision makers see the likely impact across distinct dimensions. Trade-offs are left to the decision maker, but incremental differences between the product and each alternative should still be reported. The guidance recommends CCA when a product has multiple health and non-health effects that would be difficult to combine into a single measure." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Office for Health Improvement and Disparities, Cost consequence analysis: health economic studies (Evaluating digital health products collection), sections 'What to use it for' and 'Reporting the results', published 22 July 2020" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-consequence-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-consequence-analysis .

heiro:6000393 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost-Consequence Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg20/chapter/incorporating-economic-evaluation"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "CCA When Outcomes Cannot Be Combined" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's guidelines manual defines cost-consequences analysis as presenting costs and outcomes in discrete categories without aggregating or weighting them. It may be considered when a cost-utility analysis is not possible, and is useful when different outcomes cannot be incorporated into an index measure, ideally with a table summarising all costs and outcomes. For public health and social care interventions with multiple outcomes, a cost-consequences analysis is usually needed, although the base case should still be a cost per QALY analysis where possible." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Developing NICE guidelines: the manual (PMG20), chapter 7 Incorporating economic evaluation, sections 7.3 (box 7.1) and 7.7, last updated 23 October 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-consequence-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-consequence-analysis .

heiro:6000394 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Cost-Consequence Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "CCA for Complex Interventions but Not Efficiency Estimates" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HAS methods guide limits the reference case to cost-utility and cost-effectiveness analysis, but states that other economic approaches may aid decisions when the question is broader than efficiency. It describes cost-consequence analysis as comparing interventions across several dimensions without an aggregate measure, with costs and other criteria presented separately. HAS considers it appropriate for detailing the diverse impacts of a complex intervention, such as a public health evaluation, but not suitable for estimating cost-effectiveness because it cannot assess opportunity cost." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (Choix méthodologiques pour l'évaluation économique à la HAS), methodological guidance 2020, adopted by decision 2020.0145/DC/SEESP of 2 July 2020, section 1.2 Choice of the evaluation method" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-consequence-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-consequence-analysis .

heiro:6000395 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost-Effectiveness"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Range of Maximum Acceptable ICERs Reflecting Displaced NHS Care" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE does not use a precise maximum acceptable ICER above or below which a technology is automatically judged cost effective or not. Given the fixed NHS budget, the appropriate maximum reflects the opportunity cost of programmes displaced by new, more costly technologies, and because this cannot be defined precisely NICE uses a range, giving other factors more weight as the ICER nears the top. Results must follow standard decision rules on dominance and extended dominance, with expected net health benefits presented at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16 and 6.3.1, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness .

heiro:6000396 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Cost-Effectiveness"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ICER Is Categorised but No Universal Threshold Is Applied" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG reports the cost-effectiveness ratio as the outcome of its health economic evaluations, which are cost-effectiveness or cost-utility analyses accompanied by a budget impact analysis. The ICER is the main result and is categorised, presented with the uncertainty from sensitivity analyses and alongside results of other evaluations of comparable questions. Because no generally accepted threshold exists for Germany, IQWiG applies no universal threshold, draws no dichotomous conclusion on cost-effectiveness and gives no single decisive price recommendation, leaving the decision-maker to weigh the information and judge affordability." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 1.4, 4.4 and 4.12" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness .

heiro:6000397 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Cost-Effectiveness"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Effectiveness Judged on the Efficiency Frontier" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS requires the evaluation of cost-effectiveness to identify the interventions on the cost-effectiveness frontier, meaning those for which no other intervention gives a better or identical health outcome at lower cost. A position on the frontier shows an economically rational choice but says nothing about acceptability in terms of what the community is willing to pay for an extra unit of health. Results are presented as the ICER or as net benefit, and because France has no predefined reference value, net benefit is shown graphically across willingness-to-pay values." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation, methodological guidance (2020, English version), Guideline 26 and sections 5.1.1 and 5.1.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness .

heiro:6000398 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost-Effectiveness Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reference Case Uses Fully Incremental Cost-Utility Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE uses cost-effectiveness analysis in its cost-utility form to judge whether differences in expected costs between technologies are justified by changes in expected health effects, reflecting its focus on maximising health gain from a fixed NHS and personal social services budget. The reference case requires fully incremental analysis that follows standard decision rules on dominance and extended dominance, reporting ICERs alongside expected net health benefits valued at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY gained. Technologies likely to give similar or greater health benefits at similar or lower cost than their comparators are assessed by cost-comparison analysis instead." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.11 to 4.2.21 and table 4.1, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-analysis .

heiro:6000399 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Cost-Effectiveness Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/42699"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Generalised Cost-Effectiveness Analysis Against a Null Scenario" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The WHO guide to cost-effectiveness analysis sets out generalised CEA, in which a set of related interventions is first evaluated against a null scenario, the situation that would exist if none of those interventions were implemented. Because currently funded and new interventions are assessed together, the approach can identify existing allocative inefficiencies instead of taking the current mix of care as given. Results are first presented in a single league table, and WHO-CHOICE measures health effects in DALYs, recommending that other analysts do the same for comparability." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Making Choices in Health: WHO Guide to Cost-Effectiveness Analysis, edited by Tan-Torres Edejer T et al., World Health Organization, 2003, sections 1.4, 2.3 and 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-analysis .

heiro:6000400 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CDA-AMC on Cost-Effectiveness Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CDA-AMC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reference-Case Incremental Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Canadian guidance places CEA within an explicit decision problem and reference case, with requirements for comparators, perspective, time horizon, uncertainty and incremental analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies Canada 4th Edition" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-analysis .

heiro:6000401 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Cost-Effectiveness Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/RefCase_Sep2023_For-Publication_100124.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost per evLY Gained and Cost per QALY Gained as Primary Outcomes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's reference case makes cost-utility analysis the default, with incremental cost per equal value life-year (evLY) gained and incremental cost per QALY gained reported as the primary outcomes. Analysts should also report cost per life-year gained and, when appropriate and feasible, cost per clinical outcome in natural units, such as cost per event averted. If cost per QALY and cost per evLY gained differ markedly, the report explains which characteristics of the treatment and condition cause the difference." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, current as of 23 October 2025, section 1 (Explanations), Outcomes and Presentation of Results" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-analysis .

heiro:6000402 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Cost-Effectiveness Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Utility Analysis as Standard for Reimbursement Decisions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ZIN's guideline describes cost-effectiveness analysis and cost-utility analysis as comparing incremental costs with incremental effects, the former using clinical effect sizes such as life years gained and the latter QALYs. A cost-utility analysis should be performed as standard for evaluations supporting reimbursement decisions, because it allows comparison across patient populations and interventions. Results must report the ICER in costs per QALY gained and the net monetary benefit, with a fully incremental analysis when more than two interventions are compared." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare, 2024 version (16 January 2024), sections 2.4 and 5.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-analysis .

heiro:6000403 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost-Effectiveness Plane"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Scatter Plots on the Plane and Net Benefit for South-West Results" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE lists confidence ellipses and scatter plots on the cost-effectiveness plane, when the comparison is restricted to two alternatives, as appropriate ways to present parameter uncertainty, alongside cost-effectiveness acceptability curves and the acceptability frontier. Expected net health benefits, valued at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY, may be particularly informative for technologies in the south-west quadrant, which provide less health benefit at lower cost. For such technologies, committees consider the usual cost-effectiveness levels of £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY together with any relevant additional factors and modifiers." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.7.15, 4.10.8 and 6.3.9, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-plane" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-plane .

heiro:6000404 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Cost-Effectiveness Plane"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Plane and Acceptability Curve Required, With Quadrant Shares Reported" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre requires every economic evaluation to present a cost-effectiveness plane and a cost-effectiveness acceptability curve, with uncertainty around incremental costs, incremental effects and ICERs shown by confidence intervals. When simulations are spread over different quadrants of the plane, the percentage of simulations in each quadrant should be reported. On the plane, incremental life years or QALYs sit on the horizontal axis and incremental costs on the vertical axis, with the comparator at the centre, giving four quadrants for the intervention." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (published 8 May 2025), Guideline 16 (sections 5.16 and 5.16.2) and Appendix 6.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-plane" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-plane .

heiro:6000405 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Cost-Effectiveness Plane"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Probabilistic Results on the CE-Plane With Quadrant Percentages" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland requires the results of the probabilistic analysis in a model-based economic evaluation, or of the bootstrap analysis of statistical uncertainty in an empirical one, to be presented graphically in a cost-effectiveness plane. The percentage of iterations that fall in each of the four quadrants must be reported. The same results must also be shown as a cost-effectiveness acceptability curve, alongside total and incremental costs and effects, the ICER and the net monetary benefit." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare, 2024 version (English translation), Table 2 and section 5.3.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-plane" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-plane .

heiro:6000406 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost-Effectiveness Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "£25,000 to £35,000 per QALY Range" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE does not apply a precise maximum ICER; because the NHS budget is fixed, the relevant benchmark is the opportunity cost of programmes displaced. Below a most plausible ICER of £25,000 per QALY gained (£100,000 for highly specialised technologies), a recommendation is normally based on the cost-effectiveness estimate. From £25,000 to £35,000 the committee makes explicit reference to factors such as uncertainty and benefits not captured in the QALY, and above £35,000 it needs an increasingly stronger case. This range replaced £20,000 to £30,000 in the manual update of 31 March 2026." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.3.1 to 6.3.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-threshold .

heiro:6000407 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Cost-Effectiveness Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/ICER_2023_2026_VAF_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "$100,000 to $150,000 per QALY and evLY Gained" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER sets a Health Benefit Price Benchmark for every assessment at prices that reach 100,000to100,000 to 100,000to150,000 per QALY and per evLY gained, and the top of this range is usually read as a ceiling price. ICER favours an opportunity cost view of thresholds and judges that current research points to an operational threshold of about 100,000perQALY.Evidencereportsalsoshowresultsfrom100,000 per QALY. Evidence reports also show results from 100,000perQALY.Evidencereportsalsoshowresultsfrom50,000 to $200,000, and ICER states that a single threshold should not be used as a blunt decision rule." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023, current web version), sections 3.9 and 3.13; ICER Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale (current as of 23 October 2025), Uncertainty and Sensitivity Analyses" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-threshold .

heiro:6000408 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Cost-Effectiveness Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/11/26/beoordelingskader-kosteneffectiviteit-van-zorg/Beoordelingskader+kosteneffectiviteit+van+zorg.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reference Values by Burden of Disease (€20,000 to €80,000 per QALY)" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland compares an ICER with one of three reference values that rise with burden of disease, measured by the proportional shortfall method: up to €20,000 per QALY for a burden of 0.10 to 0.40, up to €50,000 for 0.41 to 0.70 and up to €80,000 for 0.71 to 1.00. Each value is in principle the maximum society is willing to pay per QALY gained. The values have not been indexed since their introduction in 2015, and the institute supports redetermining them." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Beoordelingskader kosteneffectiviteit van zorg: afweging van effecten en kosten (definitief, 26 November 2024), section 3.2.2, Table 1 and Box 5; burden of disease method in Ziektelast in de praktijk (7 May 2018)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-threshold .

heiro:6000409 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Cost-Effectiveness Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Single Explicit Threshold" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CADTH does not declare a single fixed cost-effectiveness threshold; committees weigh the magnitude of the ICER alongside clinical benefit, budget impact, and other contextual factors rather than applying a fixed cost-per-QALY cut-off." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH (now CDA-AMC), Procedures and guidance for reimbursement reviews" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-effectiveness-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-effectiveness-threshold .

heiro:6000410 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Cost Minimisation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3b-cost-minimisation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Minimisation Where Non-Inferiority and Equivalent Safety Are Established" ;
    heiro:2000017 "A cost-minimisation approach is appropriate where the therapeutic claim is non-inferiority (or superiority) with an equivalent or superior safety profile in both nature and magnitude, and the medicine costs the same or less; the comparison is then reduced to costs. The non-inferiority assumption must be well justified, and where adverse-effect profiles differ significantly a full economic evaluation is required instead." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC, Section 3B" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-minimisation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-minimisation .

heiro:6000411 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost Minimisation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Comparison Case Where Health Benefits Are at Least Equivalent" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Where a technology is expected to provide similar or greater health benefits at similar or lower cost than technologies already recommended for the same indication, NICE may use a cost-comparison case rather than a full cost-utility analysis, focusing the appraisal on the cost difference while still requiring evidence that outcomes are at least equivalent." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Health Technology Evaluations: The Manual (PMG36) (cost-comparison case)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-minimisation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-minimisation .

heiro:6000412 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Cost-Minimisation Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3b-cost-minimisation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Minimisation in Submission Guidance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC guidance treats a cost-minimisation approach as appropriate where the therapeutic claim is non-inferiority or superiority, the safety profile is equivalent or superior in nature and magnitude, and the proposed medicine is expected to cost the health system the same or less. The non-inferiority assumption for both effectiveness and safety must be well justified. If adverse effect profiles differ significantly in nature, the approach is unlikely to suffice and a full economic evaluation is expected. Submissions must establish equi-effective doses and identify differences in prescribing, administration, monitoring and adverse-event costs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), Section 3 Economic evaluation (introduction) and Section 3B Cost minimisation" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-minimisation-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-minimisation-analysis .

heiro:6000413 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost-Minimisation Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Comparison Analysis for Similar Health Benefits" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE uses cost-comparison analysis in technology appraisals for technologies likely to provide similar or greater health benefits at similar or lower cost than comparators recommended in published NICE guidance for the same population. The analysis compares costs and resource use, while health effects are captured in the clinical-effectiveness evidence rather than in the analysis itself. Costs arising from differing health outcomes, such as managing adverse events, should be included where relevant. Substantial differences in outcome-related costs must be clearly justified because they suggest overall health benefits may not be similar." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.13 and 4.2.18 to 4.2.20, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-minimisation-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-minimisation-analysis .

heiro:6000414 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Cost-Minimisation Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost Minimisation When Length of Life Is Equivalent" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS bases its reference case on cost-utility or cost-effectiveness analysis, using cost-effectiveness analysis with life-years as the outcome when health-related quality of life is not a major consequence. If all interventions are shown to be equivalent on length of life, the reference case analysis should be based on cost minimisation. An analysis using another health outcome criterion may then be presented for information as a supplemental analysis, with the choice of criterion duly justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation: methodological guidance (validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020; English version of the 2020 guide), Guideline 2 (section 1.2) and section 2.1.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-minimisation-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-minimisation-analysis .

heiro:6000415 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Cost-of-Illness Study"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241598293"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-of-Illness Approach Not Recommended for Societal Economic Impact" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO does not recommend the cost-of-illness approach for estimating the economic impact of disease or injury at the societal level. It describes traditional cost-of-illness studies as static, partial and inconsistent, citing an undefined quantity of interest, an unclear counterfactual, neglect of capital and human capital effects, and unrealistic human-capital valuation of lost production. Direct-cost elements may still answer narrower questions, such as spending on a given disease by households, firms or government." ;
    heiro:2000018 "WHO guide to identifying the economic consequences of disease and injury (World Health Organization, 2009), sections 3.1 and 3.4, Box 4 and Appendix B" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-of-illness-study" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-of-illness-study .

heiro:6000416 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost-of-Illness Study"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg20/chapter/incorporating-economic-evaluation#reviewing-economic-evaluations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-of-Illness Studies Usually Excluded from Guideline Economic Reviews" ;
    heiro:2000017 "When NICE reviews published economic evidence for a guideline, the review should usually focus on economic evaluations that compare both the costs and consequences of alternative interventions. Cost-of-illness studies, burden of disease studies and other non-comparative costing studies should usually be excluded. Some non-comparative costing studies may be included for service delivery questions or flagged for economic modelling, and inclusion criteria may be broadened when published economic evidence is extremely sparse." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Developing NICE guidelines: the manual (PMG20), section 7.5 (Reviewing economic evaluations: Selecting relevant economic evaluations), last updated 23 October 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-of-illness-study" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-of-illness-study .

heiro:6000417 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of OECD, Eurostat and WHO on Cost-of-Illness Study"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005,
        heiro:5000009,
        heiro:5000010 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/03/a-system-of-health-accounts-2011_g1g75c9d/9789264270985-en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "OECD, Eurostat and WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Disease Expenditure Accounts Cover Direct Health Costs Only" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The System of Health Accounts treats current health expenditure by disease as comparable with the direct health cost component of cost-of-illness accounts. Direct non-health costs, such as family travel, lodging or childcare during a hospital stay, fall outside it, as do the indirect productivity costs and intangible costs that cost-of-illness studies may add. Disease accounts use top-down allocation so that spending across all diseases sums to current health expenditure, whereas specific cost-of-illness studies usually build estimates bottom-up from patient-level data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "A System of Health Accounts 2011: Revised edition (OECD, Eurostat and WHO, 2017), chapter 4 (Consumer health interface, notes 19 and 20) and chapter 10 (Possible methodological approaches)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-of-illness-study" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-of-illness-study .

heiro:6000418 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Most Plausible ICER Read Against £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Below a most plausible ICER of £25,000 per QALY gained, NICE normally recommends a technology on its cost-effectiveness estimate, and a negative decision must refer to the plausibility of the model inputs or the certainty of the ICER. Between £25,000 and £35,000, decisions refer explicitly to uncertainty, uncaptured benefits and health inequalities, and above £35,000 an increasingly stronger case is needed. Highly specialised technologies use a single level of £100,000 per QALY, and the usual £25,000 to £35,000 levels also apply to technologies giving less benefit at lower cost." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.3.4 to 6.3.9, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-per-qaly" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-per-qaly .

heiro:6000419 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Cost per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/ICER_2023_2026_VAF_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Benchmark Prices at USD 100,000 to USD 150,000 per QALY and evLY" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER reports base-case results as incremental cost per evLY gained, per QALY gained, per life-year gained and per condition-specific outcome, with results at USD 50,000, USD 100,000, USD 150,000 and USD 200,000 in every assessment. Its Health Benefit Price Benchmark uses USD 100,000 to USD 150,000 per evLY and per QALY, reflecting its judgement that opportunity cost and willingness-to-pay research both point to an operational threshold of about USD 100,000 per QALY. The upper bound of USD 150,000 per evLY leaves room for benefits beyond health and special ethical priorities." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023), sections 3.9 and 3.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-per-qaly" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-per-qaly .

heiro:6000420 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Cost per QALY"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Costs per QALY Gained Reported With Net Monetary Benefit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Dutch guideline requires base-case results to show absolute and incremental costs and effects with confidence intervals, together with the ICER expressed in costs per QALY gained and the net monetary benefit, all based on the probabilistic analysis. QALYs must be presented whatever the time horizon. Net monetary benefit needs a reference value, or cost-effectiveness threshold, which can be based on the burden of disease in the indication, and value of information curves must cover the ICER when it exceeds that reference value." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare, 2024 version (English translation), sections 5.3.1 and 5.3.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-per-qaly" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-per-qaly .

heiro:6000421 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Cost Sharing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.healthcare.gov/glossary/out-of-pocket-maximum-limit/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Annual Limit on Cost Sharing in ACA Marketplace Plans" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Federal rules cap the cost sharing that an insured person pays in a plan year. Deductibles, copayments and coinsurance for covered in-network care count towards the limit, after which the plan pays 100% of covered benefits; premiums, uncovered services, out-of-network care and charges above the allowed amount do not count. For Marketplace plans the limit is 10,600foranindividualand10,600 for an individual and 10,600foranindividualand21,200 for a family in 2026, rising to 12,000and12,000 and 12,000and24,000 in 2027. The self-only limit is uprated each year by the premium adjustment percentage, and the family limit is twice the self-only limit." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HealthCare.gov glossary, Out-of-pocket maximum/limit, accessed 2 October 2026; Code of Federal Regulations, Title 45, section 156.130 (Cost-sharing requirements), paragraphs (a), (c) and (e), as amended to 20 May 2026 (91 FR 29874)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-sharing" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-sharing .

heiro:6000422 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS Business Services Authority on Cost Sharing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000084 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nhsbsa.nhs.uk/sites/default/files/2026-04/HC12%20%28V24%29%2004.2026.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS Business Services Authority" ;
    heiro:2000016 "NHS Prescription Charge of £9.90 per Item, with Exemptions and Prepayment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NHS Business Services Authority sets out the prescription charge in England as £9.90 for each prescribed item. People under 16, those aged 16 to 18 in full-time education, those aged 60 or over, people receiving certain income-related benefits, NHS inpatients and holders of valid medical or maternity exemption certificates do not pay. A prescription prepayment certificate, costing £32.05 for 3 months or £114.50 for 12 months, covers all of a person's NHS prescriptions for that period, which limits what regular users pay." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS Business Services Authority, HC12: A quick guide to help with health costs including charges and optical voucher values, version 24, effective from April 2026, pages 2, 3 and 17" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-sharing" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-sharing .

heiro:6000423 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO Regional Office for Europe on Cost Sharing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000006 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/4409faaa-9a7a-466d-a8a0-30b6e3746768/content"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO Regional Office for Europe" ;
    heiro:2000016 "User Charge Design: Exemptions, Annual Caps and Low Fixed Co-payments" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The WHO Regional Office for Europe finds that the countries with the strongest financial protection apply user charges very sparingly, citing the United Kingdom, or protect people through exemptions for poor people and regular users of health services, annual caps on all co-payments per person, and low fixed co-payments in place of percentage co-payments. It calls exemptions for poor people the single most effective co-payment design feature for access and financial protection. It also states that user charges are not an effective rationing instrument, because they reduce necessary and unnecessary use in equal measure." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Thomson S, Cylus J, Evetovits T. Can people afford to pay for health care? New evidence on financial protection in Europe. WHO Regional Office for Europe, 2019 (ISBN 9789289054058), Executive summary and chapter The story behind the numbers, section User charges (co-payments), pages 83 to 96" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-sharing" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-sharing .

heiro:6000424 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cost-Utility Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "QALY-Based Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case expresses health effects in QALYs, combining health-related quality of life and length of life in a single index. Changes in health-related quality of life should be reported by patients, or by carers where that is not possible, and valued using public preferences from a representative UK sample through a choice-based method. The EQ-5D is the preferred measure in adults. For topics with an invitation to participate issued after 27 August 2026, the UK EQ-5D-5L value set must be used." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.3.1 to 4.3.6, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE Interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51), sections 1.7 and 1.17, published 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-utility-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-utility-analysis .

heiro:6000425 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CDA-AMC on Cost-Utility Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CDA-AMC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Transparent Utility Evidence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Canadian guidance uses QALYs in reference-case economic evaluation while requiring transparent reporting of utility sources, assumptions and uncertainty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies Canada 4th Edition" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-utility-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-utility-analysis .

heiro:6000426 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Cost-Utility Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-1-overview-and-rationale-of-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Utility Analysis Preferred in Submission Guidance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC Guidelines prefer a cost-utility analysis to a cost-effectiveness analysis, particularly where a submission claims incremental life-years gained, where quality but not quantity of life improves, or where direct randomised trials report a multiattribute utility instrument. Where transformations or external data are needed to estimate QALYs, a stepped transformation from the cost-effectiveness analysis to the cost-utility analysis should be presented. Australian-based preference weights are preferred when scoring utility instruments." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 3A.1.2 and 3A.5.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-utility-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-utility-analysis .

heiro:6000427 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Cost-Utility Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Utility Analysis as the Dutch Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland's reference case specifies a cost-utility analysis, which should be performed as standard for economic evaluations supporting reimbursement decisions. Effects are expressed in QALYs, calculated by multiplying health-related quality of life valuations by life years. Quality of life should be measured with the EQ-5D-5L and valued using the Dutch tariff, with the EQ-5D-Y for children aged 8 to 12. Alternative instruments may be supplied alongside the reference case where the EQ-5D-5L is argued to be inadequate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), chapter 1 (table 2) and paragraphs 2.4 and 3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-utility-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-utility-analysis .

heiro:6000428 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Cost-Utility Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Utility Analysis as a Reference Case Option" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's reference case for health economic evaluations of drugs in German statutory health insurance allows either a cost-effectiveness analysis based on selected benefit assessment outcomes or a cost-utility analysis using QALYs, each with an additional budget impact analysis. IQWiG describes the QALY as the most widely used preference-based outcome measure while acknowledging its limitations. Utilities should be based primarily on valuations by patients, and indirect valuation methods should be used only where a validated German tariff is available." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 4.3.3, 4.4 and 4.6 and table 5" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-utility-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-utility-analysis .

heiro:6000429 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Cost-Utility Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Utility Analysis Where Quality of Life Is a Major Consequence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS uses a cost-utility analysis for the reference case when health-related quality of life is a major consequence of the intervention, with the health outcome expressed in QALYs; otherwise a cost-effectiveness analysis based on length of life is used. A cost-utility analysis should always be accompanied by a cost-effectiveness analysis. Utility scores should come from a generic questionnaire completed by patients and valued using general population preferences, with the EQ-5D-5L and the prevailing French value set recommended." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (methodological guidance), validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, sections 1.2, 2.1.2 and 2.3.1 (guidelines 2, 10 and 15)" ;
    heiro:2000019 6 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cost-utility-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cost-utility-analysis .

heiro:6000430 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Cox-Snell Residual"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD14-Survival-analysis.updated-March-2013.v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cox-Snell Residuals Among Further Tests of Survival Model Fit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 14 lists tests of residuals such as Cox-Snell, martingale or Schoenfeld residuals among further statistical tests that may be useful when assessing fitted survival models, beside the methods it treats as particularly relevant. Its model selection algorithm begins with log-cumulative hazard plots or suitable residual plots to judge the type of hazard and whether proportional hazards hold. Its review of NICE appraisals found tests of residuals only occasionally, and it stresses that fit to the observed data says nothing about the extrapolated portion, which needs external data and clinical validity." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 14: Survival analysis for economic evaluations alongside clinical trials, extrapolation with patient-level data (Latimer, June 2011, last updated March 2013), sections 3, 3.5, 3.6, 4.4 and 6.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cox-snell-residual" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cox-snell-residual .

heiro:6000431 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Cream Skimming"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/files/document/report-congress-risk-adjustment-medicare-advantage-december-2024.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Risk Adjustment to Counter Plan Selection of Healthier Members" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS states that, without an adjustment for health status, Medicare Advantage plans would have a strong incentive to target the enrolment of beneficiaries who are healthier than average, and that selection can arise from plan practices such as high copayments for specialist visits. It counters this with risk adjustment and benefit-related policies. For the individual and small group markets, CMS describes risk adjustment payments to issuers whose plans attract higher-risk members, such as people with chronic conditions, as reducing the incentive to design benefits or marketing to avoid them." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CMS, Report to Congress: Risk Adjustment in Medicare Advantage, December 2024, section 2.1 (The Function of Risk Adjustment); CMS Center for Consumer Information and Insurance Oversight, Interim Summary Report on Individual and Small Group Market Risk Adjustment for the 2025 Benefit Year, published 13 March 2026, section I (Background)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cream-skimming" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cream-skimming .

heiro:6000432 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Insurance Authority on Cream Skimming"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000043 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hia.ie/sites/default/files/2025-11/hia-public-consultation-community-rated-health-insurance-market-in-ireland-and-proposed-changes-to-the-risk-equalisation-scheme.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Insurance Authority" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Risk Equalisation to Remove the Incentive to Avoid Older and Sicker People" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Department of Health and the Health Insurance Authority state that, because community rating stops insurers varying prices with risk, insurers would otherwise be incentivised to avoid older and sicker people. The Risk Equalisation Scheme pays insurers credits, funded by a stamp duty on all health insurance contracts, that subsidise the higher claims costs of these members. One stated aim of the scheme is to reduce the incentives for risk selection, so that insurers are indifferent, or at least less incentivised, to target less risky and more profitable people." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Department of Health and Health Insurance Authority, Public consultation: community-rated health insurance market in Ireland and proposed changes to the Risk Equalisation Scheme, November 2025 (consultation closed 5 December 2025), sections 3 and 4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cream-skimming" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cream-skimming .

heiro:6000433 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Cream Skimming"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.zorginstituutnederland.nl/financiering/fondsbeheer-zvf-en-flz/zorgverzekeringsfonds/wat-is-risicoverevening"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Risk Equalisation Alongside the Acceptance Obligation and Equal Premiums" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland explains that Dutch insurers must accept everyone for the basic insurance and may not charge higher premiums to people who are chronically ill or at higher risk, nor lower premiums to attract young, healthy people, which it calls risk selection. It carries out risk equalisation so that insurers with many members from high-risk groups, such as older people and people with chronic conditions, are compensated through the Health Insurance Fund. Payments reflect expected costs based on age, sex, region and health indicators such as medicine use and hospital visits, and insurers still bear financial risk." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Wat is risicoverevening? (Zorgverzekeringsfonds), web page, accessed 2 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cream-skimming" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cream-skimming .

heiro:6000434 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Criterion Validity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/77832/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Criterion Validity Reviewed Only When a Gold Standard Exists" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA defines criterion validity as the extent to which the scores of a patient-reported outcome instrument are related to a known gold standard measure of the same concept. It reviews this property only in rare cases, such as a new sleep scale compared with polysomnography, because for most patient-reported outcomes no criterion measure can exist, as with any symptom known only to the patient. Where a criterion is used, sponsors should give a rationale for it, and FDA reviews the correlation with the instrument and the sensitivity, specificity and predictive value of the criterion measure." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Guidance for Industry. Patient-Reported Outcome Measures: Use in Medical Product Development to Support Labeling Claims, final guidance (December 2009), section III.E.2 and Glossary" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "criterion-validity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:criterion-validity .

heiro:6000435 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Cross-Price Elasticity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240019188"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cross-Price Elasticity Needed to Forecast Tobacco Tax Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO advises policy-makers to know the own-price, cross-price and income elasticities of demand for tobacco products in their country, and to update the estimates regularly, so that the effect of a tax rise on consumption and revenue can be anticipated. It defines cross-price elasticity as the change in demand for one product when the price of another changes, with a positive value signalling substitutes, such as hand-rolling tobacco and manufactured cigarettes. Most studies find e-cigarettes and conventional cigarettes to be partial substitutes, while several studies in India found bidis and manufactured cigarettes to be complements." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, WHO technical manual on tobacco tax policy and administration (2021), ISBN 978-92-4-001918-8, sections 2.2.3 (Key takeaway 14) and 2.4.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cross-price-elasticity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cross-price-elasticity .

heiro:6000436 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cross-Sectional Design"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd9/chapter/methods-for-real-world-studies-of-comparative-effects"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cross-Sectional Studies Less Reliable Where Timing Matters" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's real-world evidence framework describes cross-sectional studies as collecting information on current exposures and outcomes at a single time point. It accepts that they can be used to estimate intervention effects but regards them as less reliable than longitudinal designs such as cohort studies when a clear temporal separation of exposure and outcome is needed. When choosing a statistical model for a real-world evidence study, analysts should consider whether the data are cross-sectional or longitudinal, alongside the outcome distribution, sample size, clustering and heterogeneity." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE real-world evidence framework (ECD9), chapters on methods for real-world studies of comparative effects (types of non-randomised study design, cross-sectional studies) and conduct of quantitative real-world evidence studies (choosing study design and analytical methods), published 23 June 2022, last updated September 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cross-sectional-design" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cross-sectional-design .

heiro:6000437 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Cross-Sectional Design"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cross-Sectional Studies for Prevalence and Test Accuracy" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods state that the prevalence of diseases can be estimated from population-based cross-sectional studies. When diagnostic tests are compared on accuracy, the highest certainty of results comes from cohort and cross-sectional studies in which the tests and the reference test are conducted independently in the same patients under mutual blinding, whereas prognostic markers can be studied in cohort rather than cross-sectional studies. In assessments of potential, results of non-comparative studies, including cross-sectional studies, carry only minimum qualitative certainty of results." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 3.5.2, 3.8.1 and 10.1.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cross-sectional-design" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cross-sectional-design .

heiro:6000438 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cumulative Hazard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Log-Cumulative Hazard Plots to Assess Proportional Hazards" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that the proportional hazards assumption should always be assessed when relative treatment effects on survival or other time-to-event outcomes are measured with hazard ratios. It prefers log-cumulative hazard plots, as advised in Decision Support Unit Technical Support Document 14, together with visual inspection of hazard plots or hazard ratios over time and the proportional hazards tests reported in trial publications. If the assumption holds and is clinically plausible during extrapolation, hazard ratios may be pooled, and if it fails in some studies, the alternative methods in Technical Support Document 21 should be considered." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.6.22 to 4.6.24, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cumulative-hazard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cumulative-hazard .

heiro:6000439 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Cumulative Hazard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD14-Survival-analysis.updated-March-2013.v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Log-Cumulative Hazard Plots as the First Step in Survival Model Choice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 14 makes log-cumulative hazard plots, or suitable residual plots, the first step of its survival model selection algorithm, showing whether hazards are constant, monotonic or non-monotonic and whether proportional hazards is reasonable. Approximately straight plots identify parametric models to fit, parallel arms point to proportional hazards models, and curved plots call for piecewise or more flexible approaches. TSD 21 notes that flexible parametric models are usually fitted on the log cumulative hazard scale. TSD 14 adds that such plots show fit to the observed data only and say nothing about the period beyond trial follow-up." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 14: Survival analysis for economic evaluations alongside clinical trials, extrapolation with patient-level data (Latimer, June 2011, last updated March 2013), sections 3.2, 3.5 and 6.1; NICE DSU Technical Support Document 21: Flexible methods for survival analysis (Rutherford et al., 23 January 2020), section 3.2.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cumulative-hazard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cumulative-hazard .

heiro:6000440 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Cumulative Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cumulative Meta-Analysis Not Used as Standard in Review Updates" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG notes a continuing debate over whether meta-analyses in repeatedly updated systematic reviews should be calculated and presented as cumulative meta-analyses with correction for multiple testing. As a standard it applies the usual type of meta-analysis and normally does not draw on cumulative methods. It will consider cumulative meta-analysis with correction for multiple testing only if commissioned to update a review regularly until a decision can be made on the basis of a statistically significant result." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of Allgemeine Methoden 8.0), section 10.3.7 Meta-analyses, part E (Cumulative meta-analyses)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cumulative-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cumulative-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000441 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Cure Rate Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cure Models Only When Standard Parametric Distributions Fall Short" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland requires extrapolation in model-based evaluations when trial follow-up is shorter than the relevant time horizon, and all standard parametric distributions (exponential, Weibull, Gompertz, log-logistic, lognormal, gamma and generalised gamma) must be evaluated and reported. More flexible methods such as spline and cure models are possible but are allowed only if the standard distributions are insufficient, and any decision to use them must be properly substantiated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), section 4.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cure-rate-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cure-rate-model .

heiro:6000442 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Cure Rate Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cure Approaches Among Flexible Extrapolation Options" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's methods manual lists cure approaches, alongside spline-based models, among the more flexible options for extrapolating time-to-event data beyond standard parametric models. The appropriateness of each approach and the validity of its extrapolations should routinely be considered, the clinical plausibility of the underlying hazard functions assessed when comparing models, and uncertainty in the extrapolated portion explored. Where proportional hazards do not hold, the manual points to the alternative methods in NICE DSU technical support document 21." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.6.24 and 4.6.25, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cure-rate-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cure-rate-model .

heiro:6000443 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Cycle Length"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-2-computational-methods-and-structure-of-economic_analysis.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Justify the Cycle Length and Test Alternatives" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC asks sponsors using cohort state transition models to consider the likely impact of alternative cycle lengths on the model outputs and to describe the factors that determined the selected cycle length. A half-cycle correction is the default way to represent the timing of transitions between states, although another correction factor may be proposed with justification. Values from all sources of evidence should be adjusted to the model structure, for example by translating reported rates or cumulative probabilities into probabilities for the timeframe of a model cycle." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 3A.2.3 and 3A.4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cycle-length" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cycle-length .

heiro:6000444 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Cycle Length"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD15_Patient-level_simulation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Shorten the Cycle Until Results Stop Changing" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 15 describes state-transition models as a discrete-time approximation in which patients are counted at fixed points set by the cycle length and time horizon. Accuracy improves with continuity corrections such as a half-cycle correction and with shorter cycles, and reducing the cycle length until there is no appreciable effect on model outcomes is recommended. Shorter cycles also reduce the bias from more than one transition occurring within a cycle, but diseases that combine rapid events with long quiet periods may then need models that are large and slow to evaluate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15: Cost-effectiveness modelling using patient-level simulation (Davis, Stevenson, Tappenden and Wailoo, April 2014), sections 2.6 and 3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "cycle-length" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:cycle-length .

heiro:6000445 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Decision"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Recommendation Types Matched to the Strength of the Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE committees normally choose between recommending a technology as an option, recommending it only in specific circumstances, recommending it with managed access for medicines, recommending use during an evidence generation period for HealthTech, recommending it only in a research context, or not recommending it. The type depends on how far the potential benefits are supported by evidence. When considering uncertainty, the committee should take into account the likelihood of decision error and its consequences for patients and the NHS, and it cannot make recommendations on pricing." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.1.15, 6.2.32, 6.4.1 and table 6.3, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision .

heiro:6000446 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Decision Criteria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Committee Factors Around a £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY Range" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE committees do not use a precise maximum acceptable ICER, and cost-effectiveness is necessary but not the only basis for decisions. Below a most plausible ICER of £25,000 per QALY gained the decision normally rests on the cost-effectiveness estimate; above it, the committee considers the certainty around the ICER, uncaptured benefits and non-health factors, and health inequalities, and above £35,000 it needs an increasingly strong case. Severity can add QALY weights of 1.2 or 1.7 based on absolute or proportional shortfall." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.2.10 to 6.2.18, 6.3.1 and 6.3.4 to 6.3.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-criteria" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-criteria .

heiro:6000447 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Decision Criteria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/information/about-the-guidelines.html#Key-factors"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Quantitative and Less Readily Quantifiable Decision Factors" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the National Health Act 1953 the PBAC must consider the effectiveness and cost of a proposed medicine compared with alternative therapies, and cannot recommend one that is substantially more costly than an alternative unless it provides a significant improvement in health. Five quantitative factors influence its decisions: comparative health gain, comparative cost-effectiveness, patient affordability without subsidy, and predicted use and financial implications for the PBS and for the government health budget. Other factors include confidence in the evidence, equity, effective alternatives, severity and targeting of therapy." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee (PBAC Guidelines), version 5.0, About the Guidelines: Key factors influencing decision making by the PBAC, September 2016" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-criteria" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-criteria .

heiro:6000448 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Decision Criteria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/11/26/beoordelingskader-kosteneffectiviteit-van-zorg/Beoordelingskader+kosteneffectiviteit+van+zorg.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Four Package Criteria With Burden-Based Reference Values" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland bases advice on the basic benefit package on four package criteria: effectiveness, cost-effectiveness, necessity and feasibility. Its reference values for cost-effectiveness rise with burden of disease, measured by proportional shortfall: up to 20,000 euros per QALY for a burden of 0.10 to 0.40, up to 50,000 euros for 0.41 to 0.70 and up to 80,000 euros for 0.71 to 1.00. After comparing the ICER with the reference value, it considers arguments for accepting a higher or lower cost per QALY and weighs all four criteria together." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Beoordelingskader kosteneffectiviteit van zorg, 26 November 2024, sections 1.1, 3.2.2 and 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-criteria" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-criteria .

heiro:6000449 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Decision Criteria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2019-07/doctrine_de_la_commission_de_la_transparence_-_version_anglaise.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Clinical Benefit and Clinical Added Value Ratings" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HAS Transparency Committee advises ministers on the reimbursement and pricing of medicines using two ratings. Clinical benefit (SMR) is assessed on five factors: efficacy and adverse effects, place in the therapeutic strategy, seriousness of the disease, whether the product is preventive, curative or symptomatic, and public health benefit, and it bears on whether and how much a medicine is reimbursed. Clinical added value (ASMR) is rated from level I (major) to level V (no improvement) and frames price negotiations, with the final decision resting with the ministers." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Transparency Committee doctrine: principles of medicinal product assessments and appraisal for reimbursement purposes, adopted 2 December 2020, sections 2.1 and 3.1; HAS Transparency Committee web page, posted 6 March 2024" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-criteria" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-criteria .

heiro:6000450 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Decision Criteria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Added Benefit Graded by Probability and Extent" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In benefit assessments of new drugs, IQWiG compares the drug with an appropriate comparator therapy in three steps. It first classifies the probability of an effect on each outcome as a hint, an indication or proof, then grades the extent of the effect as major, considerable, minor or non-quantifiable, and finally reaches an overall conclusion in one of six categories, from major added benefit to no added benefit proven or less benefit than the comparator. Its reports serve as recommendations to the G-BA, which can also weigh aspects beyond the benefit assessment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of the authoritative German text), sections 1.3.2 and 3.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-criteria" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-criteria .

heiro:6000451 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Decision Criteria"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241507158"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Effectiveness, Priority to the Worse Off and Financial Protection" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The WHO Consultative Group on Equity and Universal Health Coverage recommends that countries sort services into high, medium and low priority classes using criteria related to cost-effectiveness, priority to the worse off and financial risk protection. It suggests starting with cost-effectiveness estimates and then integrating the concern for the worse off and other relevant criteria, and finds an exclusive focus on cost-effectiveness generally indefensible. The criteria should be specified and balanced through public deliberation and participatory procedures." ;
    heiro:2000018 "WHO, Making fair choices on the path to universal health coverage: final report of the WHO Consultative Group on Equity and Universal Health Coverage, 2014, executive summary and chapter 3 (service selection criteria)" ;
    heiro:2000019 6 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-criteria" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-criteria .

heiro:6000452 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Decision Making"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Committee Judgement Within a Structured and Transparent Process" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's committee bases its recommendations on the evidence presented, stakeholder statements and expert views, considering the factors it judges most appropriate within the Health and Social Care Act 2012 and NICE's equality duties. It will not recommend technologies whose benefits are unproven or that are not a good use of NHS resources, and it uses a range rather than a precise maximum acceptable ICER. Above a most plausible ICER of £25,000 per QALY gained, decisions specifically consider uncertainty, uncaptured benefits and health inequalities, and the committee's reasoning must be explained so that the process is transparent." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.1.1 to 6.1.3, 6.1.8, 6.3.1 and 6.3.5, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-making" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-making .

heiro:6000453 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Decision Making"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Institute Assesses the Evidence, the Federal Joint Committee Decides" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG submits the results of work commissioned by the Federal Joint Committee (G-BA) as recommendations, which the G-BA must by law consider in its decision-making. Where studies of adequate validity are lacking, IQWiG's task is to describe and classify the situation, while the G-BA takes the uncertainty into account and also weighs aspects such as the efficiency of interventions and the needs and values of people, which matter more when the evidence is uncertain. In health economic evaluations no universal threshold is applied, so the decision-maker weighs the information and judges affordability." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 1.1, 1.3.4 and 1.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-making" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-making .

heiro:6000454 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Decision Making"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240020689"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Separating Data, Dialogue and Decision in Benefit Package Design" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO holds that decisions on health benefit packages should be evidence-based, unbiased, impartial and fair, and seen as fair by all affected parties. Package design should respect the difference between data, dialogue and decision: rigorous scientific assessment free of conflicts of interest, a transparent deliberative appraisal involving all appropriate stakeholders, and a final political decision by those accountable for it, who may weigh considerations beyond expert evaluation. Deliberations, reasoning and trade-offs should be made public and communicated to decision makers and citizens." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Principles of health benefit packages (2021), ISBN 978-92-4-002068-9, principles 1, 5 and 8, pages 5 to 13" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-making" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-making .

heiro:6000455 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Decision Problem"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Decision Problem Consistent With the NICE Scope" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In NICE's reference case the decision problem is defined by the scope developed by NICE, which covers the population, technology, care pathway, comparators, outcomes, costs and time horizon. The economic evaluation should start with a clear statement of the decision problem that defines the technologies compared and the relevant patient groups, and any differences from the scope must be justified. For medicines, the NICE technical lead and the external assessment group check whether a submission specifies the decision problem appropriately, and the committee considers whether the model reflects it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 2.1.2, 4.2.4, 4.2.5, table 4.1, 5.6.2 and 6.2.27, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-problem" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-problem .

heiro:6000456 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Decision Problem"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Decision Problem Framed as a PICOTS Research Question" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ZIN requires the framework of an economic evaluation to be defined before the analyses start, beginning with its objective and its future users, whose decision-making problem should be linked to that objective. Once the user and the societal perspective are set, the research question is formulated with the PICOTS method: patient population, intervention, comparison, outcomes, time span and setting. The report must clearly formulate and justify the PICOTS and the choice of an empirical or model-based study design." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland. Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), 16 January 2024, sections 2.1 to 2.3 and 5.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-problem" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-problem .

heiro:6000457 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Decision Rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Fixed Maximum ICER; Other Factors Weigh More Near the Top of the Range" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's committee does not use a precise maximum acceptable ICER, and cost-effectiveness is necessary but not the only basis for its decisions, so other factors carry more weight as the ICER nears the top of the range. Below a most plausible ICER of £25,000 per QALY gained (£100,000 for highly specialised technologies), the decision is normally based on the cost-effectiveness estimate. From £25,000 to £35,000 the committee makes explicit reference to factors such as the certainty around the ICER, uncaptured benefits and health inequalities, and above £35,000 it needs an increasingly stronger case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.3.1 to 6.3.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-rule" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-rule .

heiro:6000458 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Canada’s Drug Agency on Decision Rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Canada’s Drug Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Canadian interpretation of incremental economic evidence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Canada’s Drug Agency guidance requires transparent incremental analysis and characterisation of uncertainty. A threshold imported from another setting should not be presented as a universal Canadian rule." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-rule" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-rule .

heiro:6000459 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Decision Rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/information/about-the-guidelines.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Statutory Cost Test and Five Quantitative Factors in PBAC Decisions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC must consider the effectiveness and cost of a proposed medicine compared with alternative therapies, and it cannot recommend one that is substantially more costly than an alternative unless satisfied that it provides a significant improvement in health. Its decisions are influenced by five quantitative factors: comparative health gain, comparative cost-effectiveness, patient affordability without subsidy, and projected net costs to the PBS and to the Australian Government health budget. Less readily quantifiable factors include confidence in the evidence, equity, the presence of alternatives, severity and the ability to target therapy." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee (PBAC Guidelines), version 5.0 (September 2016), About the Guidelines: Key factors influencing decision making by the PBAC; section 5.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-rule" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-rule .

heiro:6000460 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Institute for Clinical and Economic Review on Decision Rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/ICER_2023_2026_VAF_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Price Benchmarks at $100,000 to $150,000 per QALY, Not a Blunt Decision Rule" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER sets Health Benefit Price Benchmarks at prices reaching 100,000to100,000 to 100,000to150,000 per QALY and per evLY gained, and the top of this range is usually read as a ceiling price. ICER states that single cost-effectiveness thresholds should not be used as a blunt decision rule, since decision-makers may weigh their own opportunity costs, benefits beyond health and special ethical priorities. Potential budget impact over five years is compared separately with an annual threshold for each new drug, updated to $821 million in October 2025, to signal affordability concerns rather than to cap spending." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023, current web version with revisions to July 2026), sections 3.9, 3.13 and 6; ICER Value Assessment Framework web page, Potential Budget Impact Threshold Updated for 2025-2026 (October 2025)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-rule" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-rule .

heiro:6000461 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Decision Rule"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/11/26/beoordelingskader-kosteneffectiviteit-van-zorg/Beoordelingskader+kosteneffectiviteit+van+zorg.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reference Value as the Starting Point for an Integral Package Decision" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland compares a usable ICER with the reference value for the burden of disease involved: a point estimate below it is in principle favourable, while an ICER above it makes cost-effectiveness unlikely. The closer the ICER lies to the reference value, the better the methodological quality and the smaller the uncertainty must be. The reference values are a starting point for discussion rather than a cut-off, and after this test the institute, advised by its Package Advisory Committee, weighs arguments for accepting higher or lower costs per QALY alongside effectiveness, necessity and feasibility." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Beoordelingskader kosteneffectiviteit van zorg: afweging van effecten en kosten (definitief, 26 November 2024), sections 1.3, 3.2.2, 3.2.3, 3.6 and 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-rule" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-rule .

heiro:6000462 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Decision Tree"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-1-overview-and-rationale-of-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "A Decision Tree Diagram Framing the Economic Evaluation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines ask each economic evaluation to identify the primary decision it addresses and include a decision tree or analytic diagram. The diagram should give a conceptual overview rather than the full computational structure, defining after the decision point the alternative choices, uncertain events, probabilities where practical, and outcomes, with complex branches collapsed and pathways consistent with the clinical management algorithms. For computation, decision trees are described as useful for models with short time horizons, with cohort state transition models used for longer horizons." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee (PBAC Guidelines), version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 3A.1.3 and 3A.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-tree" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-tree .

heiro:6000463 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Decision Tree"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Decision Trees for Simple Models and Very Short Time Horizons" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's national guidelines list decision-tree analysis among the available modelling techniques, alongside state-transition or Markov models, discrete-event simulation, system dynamic models and partitioned survival models, and hold that no single technique is optimal, so the choice should depend on the research question. Decision trees can be useful for relatively simple models or decision problems with special characteristics, such as very short time horizons, whereas state-transition models suit pathways that can be represented as mutually exclusive states." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), section 2.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "decision-tree" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:decision-tree .

heiro:6000464 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Deliberative Process"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/pcodr-expert-review-committee-perc-0"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Separate Oncology/Non-Oncology Committees; pERC’s Four-Quadrant Deliberative Framework" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CDA-AMC runs two parallel reimbursement-review streams with distinct expert committees: the Common Drug Review with the Canadian Drug Expert Committee (CDEC) for non-oncology drugs, and the pan-Canadian Oncology Drug Review with the pCODR Expert Review Committee (pERC) for cancer drugs. pERC structures every oncology recommendation around a defined four-quadrant deliberative framework — clinical benefit, alignment with patient values, cost-effectiveness, and feasibility of adoption — and advises provincial cancer agencies in addition to federal, provincial and territorial drug plans (Quebec’s INESSS reviews separately)." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CDA-AMC, Canadian Drug Expert Committee and pCODR Expert Review Committee; pCODR deliberative framework" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "deliberative-process" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:deliberative-process .

heiro:6000465 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Demand Forecasting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-4/4-2-estimation-of-use-and-financial-impact-of-the-proposed-medicine.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Six-Year Utilisation Forecasts by Epidemiological or Market-Share Approach" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC asks sponsors to estimate use of a proposed medicine for six years after listing, either through an epidemiological approach that converts incidence or prevalence data into patients with the condition, those eligible under the restriction and those likely to take the medicine, or through a market-share approach based on utilisation of the medicines likely to be substituted. Growth assumptions must be justified, linear growth may be inappropriate, and projections should be based on patient numbers rather than dispensed packs wherever possible. Uptake estimates must be justified and varied in sensitivity analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 4.2, 4.2.1 and 4.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "demand-forecasting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:demand-forecasting .

heiro:6000466 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Demand Forecasting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Target Population and Market Share Projections in Budget Impact" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For budget impact analyses, the Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre requires the anticipated size of the target population to be specified and the estimation method justified, preferably with Belgian epidemiological data, and its evolution over time with and without the new technology considered, including shifts in incidence and prevalence. Market share and the degree of implementation must be justified, with evidence-based estimates preferred. Where future patient numbers or market share rest on expert assumptions, comprehensive deterministic sensitivity analyses, including best and worst cases, are recommended." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (published 8 May 2025), Guideline 18 (section 6.3) and Guideline 24 (section 6.9)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "demand-forecasting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:demand-forecasting .

heiro:6000467 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Dependent Censoring"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd9/chapter/methods-for-real-world-studies-of-comparative-effects"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Informative Censoring Must Be Considered and Addressed" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's real-world evidence framework states that censoring can create bias if it is informative, that is, related to the outcomes and treatment assignment, as when people stop an experimental drug because of a perceived lack of benefit. Developers should consider this bias and address it where needed, using methods similar to those for time-varying confounding, such as marginal structural models with weighting, or methods for missing data. For trials with treatment switching, the technology appraisal manual advises against simply censoring or excluding patients who cross over, because these methods are very susceptible to selection bias." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE real-world evidence framework (ECD9), chapter on methods for real-world studies of comparative effects (analysis: informative censoring), published 23 June 2022, last updated September 2025; NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section 4.6.26, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "dependent-censoring" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:dependent-censoring .

heiro:6000468 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Dependent Censoring"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/68716099"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Re-Censoring and Weighting to Correct Censoring Linked to Switching" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 24 states that simple methods, such as censoring patients at the time of switch or excluding switchers, are prone to bias and not recommended, because switching is often related to prognosis. Inverse probability of censoring weights can correct the bias introduced by censoring switchers, provided the switching model is appropriately specified and there is no unmeasured confounding. Adjusting censoring times only in switchers also creates informative censoring, so rank preserving structural failure time models, iterative parameter estimation and two-stage estimation should be applied with and without re-censoring in submissions to NICE." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 24: Adjusting survival time estimates in the presence of treatment switching, an update to TSD 16 (Bell Gorrod, Latimer and Abrams, published April 2024), sections 4.2, 4.2.1, 4.2.2 and 4.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "dependent-censoring" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:dependent-censoring .

heiro:6000469 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Dependent Censoring"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Censoring at Treatment Switch Raises a High Risk of Bias" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods note that adverse events and patient-reported outcomes in oncology studies are often recorded only during treatment, so observations are censored when treatment is discontinued or switched. Survival time methods are then needed for the different observation periods, but this incomplete data collection raises the problem of informative censoring, which may result in a high risk of bias. To avoid it, data on all outcomes relevant to the assessment should be collected completely, even after discontinuation or switching of treatment, since treatment policy and intention-to-treat analyses are required." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 10.3.12" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "dependent-censoring" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:dependent-censoring .

heiro:6000470 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Detection Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Detection Bias Named Among the Risks of Non-randomised Evidence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE lists detection bias, with limits in data quality and selection bias, among the reasons non-randomised studies tend to be at high risk of bias, and asks for the risk of bias in each such study to be assessed with a validated tool such as ROBINS-I. For randomised trials, internal validity includes blinding where appropriate, which prevents knowledge of treatment allocation from influencing behaviours, and the selection and measurement of outcomes. NICE notes that blinding is often not possible in trials of medical devices or diagnostics." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.3.5, 3.3.6, 3.3.9 and 3.3.12, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "detection-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:detection-bias .

heiro:6000471 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Detection Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Blinded Outcome Assessment Where Treatment Cannot Be Blinded" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG describes detection bias as the result of unequal observation of participants and treats blinding as an effective protection against it. Its risk-of-bias assessment of randomised trials, closely following the Cochrane tool, rates blinding of outcome assessors for each outcome. Where patients and physicians cannot be blinded, outcomes should be independent of the treating staff or assessed blind, and failing that a preferably objective outcome should be chosen. Results of unblinded trials are interpreted more cautiously than those of blinded trials." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 10.1.4 and 10.3.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "detection-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:detection-bias .

heiro:6000472 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Detection Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/9f9dbfe4-078b-4959-9a07-df9167258772_en?filename=hta_clinical-studies-validity_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Joint Clinical Assessments Rate Detection Bias With Cochrane RoB 1" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For outcomes drawn from randomised trials, the HTA Coordination Group requires risk of bias in a joint clinical assessment to be judged with the Cochrane RoB 1 tool, whose domains include detection bias, both for each domain and overall. Judgements are made separately for outcomes where bias from lack of blinding differs, although outcomes sharing data collection, blinding and evaluation may be grouped. Reports should state who was blinded, such as participants, care providers or outcome assessors, instead of using the terms single blind or double blind." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EU HTA Coordination Group, Guidance on Validity of Clinical Studies, V1.0 (4 July 2024, adopted 19 September 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282), sections 3.1 and 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "detection-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:detection-bias .

heiro:6000473 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Detection Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/ich-e-9-statistical-principles-clinical-trials-step-5_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Blinding Protects End Point Assessment, With Blind Assessors in Open Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under ICH E9, as adopted by the EMA, blinding is intended to limit conscious and unconscious bias arising from knowledge of treatment, including in the assessment of end points, and the double-blind trial is the optimal approach. Where only a single-blind or open-label trial is possible, clinical assessments should be made by medical staff who are not involved in treating the participants and who remain blind to treatment. Primary variables in such trials should be as objective as possible." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICH Topic E9, Statistical principles for clinical trials, Step 5 (CPMP/ICH/363/96, September 1998), section 2.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "detection-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:detection-bias .

heiro:6000474 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Deviance Residual"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD2-General-meta-analysis-corrected-2Sep2016v2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Deviance Residuals to Find Poorly Fitting Data Points" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 2 assesses the fit of generalised linear models for pairwise and network meta-analysis through the posterior mean residual deviance, to which each data point should contribute about 1, so the total can be compared with the number of independent data points. A deviance residual is calculated for each data point. Where the residuals suggest poor fit, plotting each point's contribution to the effective number of parameters against its signed deviance residual shows whether poorly fitting points materially affect the model, and points beyond the curve marking a contribution of 3 to the deviance information criterion generally indicate poor fit." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 2: A generalised linear modelling framework for pairwise and network meta-analysis of randomised controlled trials (Dias, Welton, Sutton and Ades, August 2011, last updated September 2016), section 2.1.2 and Figures 1 and 2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "deviance-residual" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:deviance-residual .

heiro:6000475 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Diagnostic Accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg48/chapter/methods-for-guidance-produced-in-the-nice-healthtech-programme"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Test Accuracy as an Intermediate Outcome in Linked Evidence Models" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's HealthTech programme manual notes that meta-analysis of test accuracy data is complicated by the correlation between sensitivity and specificity, by heterogeneity in setting, population, reference standard, equipment and operator skill, and by cut-off points that differ between studies, so the chosen method must be justified, with Decision Support Unit Technical Support Document 25 as the methods guide. Because most outcomes of testing follow from treatment started, modified or stopped, a linked evidence model should connect test results, care decisions and final outcomes, with test accuracy treated as an intermediate outcome." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE HealthTech programme manual (PMG48), sections 2.2.15, 2.3.14 and 2.3.15, published 14 July 2025, last updated 17 December 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "diagnostic-accuracy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:diagnostic-accuracy .

heiro:6000476 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Diagnostic Accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/68716507"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Bivariate Synthesis of Sensitivity and Specificity for Decision Models" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 25 sets out meta-analysis of diagnostic test accuracy for NICE decision models, pooling sensitivity and specificity and discouraging the pooling of predictive values, which depend critically on prevalence. Its methods allow for heterogeneity between studies, the expected negative correlation between sensitivity and specificity and small cell counts, using the binomial bivariate random effects model, equivalent to the HSROC model, and a multiple thresholds model when studies report several cut-offs. Correlated posterior samples should feed a probabilistic decision model, since plugging in point estimates fails to carry the correlation through." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 25: Evidence synthesis of diagnostic test accuracy for decision making (Derezea et al., November 2024), sections 3, 4.1, 5.1 and 6.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "diagnostic-accuracy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:diagnostic-accuracy .

heiro:6000477 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Diagnostic Accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Test Accuracy Studies Mainly Relevant Through Linked Evidence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG assesses diagnostic tests primarily through comparative intervention studies with patient-relevant outcomes, because a test's benefit is in essence realised only through the treatment or prevention that follows. Studies of sensitivity and specificity are mainly relevant within the linked evidence approach, with the highest certainty from studies applying the index tests and reference test independently in the same patients under mutual blinding, appraised mainly with QUADAS-2. Meta-analyses of diagnostic accuracy are therefore of limited relevance; random-effects models are usually advisable, and bivariate models show advantages when a summary ROC curve is of interest." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 3.5, 3.5.1, 3.5.2 and 10.3.7" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "diagnostic-accuracy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:diagnostic-accuracy .

heiro:6000478 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Diagnostic Accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/71147/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Accuracy Needs a Reference Standard, Otherwise Report Agreement" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA defines the diagnostic accuracy of a new qualitative test as the extent of agreement between its outcome and the reference standard, the best available method for establishing whether the target condition is present. It recommends using a reference standard to estimate sensitivity and specificity, adjusted for verification bias when it is applied to only some subjects. Where only a non-reference comparator exists, results should be reported as positive and negative percent agreement rather than sensitivity and specificity, and FDA advises against discrepant resolution and the elimination of equivocal results." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Statistical Guidance on Reporting Results from Studies Evaluating Diagnostic Tests, guidance for industry and FDA staff, final (13 March 2007), sections 3, 4.1 and 6" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "diagnostic-accuracy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:diagnostic-accuracy .

heiro:6000479 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Hierarchy of Diagnostic Efficacy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg48/chapter/methods-for-guidance-produced-in-the-nice-healthtech-programme"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Diagnostic Outcomes Arise Through Changes in Treatment and Care" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that diagnostic technologies may bring some direct benefit from the knowledge gained and some direct harm from testing, but that most outcomes come after testing, when treatment or preventive measures are started, modified or stopped. Trials of tests are unusual, so where direct data on final outcomes are lacking a linked evidence modelling approach should be used. The links between test results, care decisions and final outcomes must be specified and justified, and companies relying on intermediate outcomes such as test accuracy should supply supporting evidence for their use." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE HealthTech programme manual (PMG48), sections 2.3.14 and 2.3.15, published 14 July 2025, last updated 17 December 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "diagnostic-efficacy-hierarchy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:diagnostic-efficacy-hierarchy .

heiro:6000480 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Hierarchy of Diagnostic Efficacy"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Benefit of Diagnostic Tests Judged on Patient Relevant Outcomes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG holds that a diagnostic test realises its health benefit or harm mainly through the therapeutic or preventive procedures that follow it, and that diagnostic information without medical consequences as a rule has no benefit. It therefore assesses tests primarily through comparative intervention studies of the whole diagnostic and therapeutic chain with patient-relevant outcomes. Linked evidence, which joins test accuracy studies to treatment studies, is used only when direct evidence cannot be expected and is generally considered weaker." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English version published 1 July 2026), sections 3.5, 3.5.1 and 3.5.1.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "diagnostic-efficacy-hierarchy" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:diagnostic-efficacy-hierarchy .

heiro:6000481 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Difference-in-Differences"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD17-DSU-Observational-data-FINAL.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Difference in Differences for Natural Experiments in Appraisal" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 17 describes difference in differences as comparing the change in outcomes before and after a natural event between treatment and control groups, using longitudinal data or repeated cross-sections, which in general identifies the average treatment effect on the treated. The estimator assumes common trends across the groups and no selection on unobserved time-varying shocks, an assumption that fails if people change behaviour in anticipation of treatment. In its algorithm for choosing methods, patient-level data from a natural experiment suit such designs, which would be preferred in the first instance if their assumptions are reasonable." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 17: The use of observational data to inform estimates of treatment effectiveness in technology appraisal, methods for comparative individual patient data (Faria, Hernandez Alava, Manca and Wailoo, May 2015), sections 2.5.1 and 4.1 and Figure 2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "difference-in-differences" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:difference-in-differences .

heiro:6000482 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Differential Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Same 3.5% Rate for Costs and Health Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE requires costs and health effects in the reference case to be discounted at the same rate of 3.5% per year. A rate of 1.5% may be considered only as a non-reference-case analysis, and it then applies to both costs and health effects, so NICE does not use differential discounting. The committee can consider the lower rate only if the technology is for people who would otherwise die or have a very severely impaired life, is likely to restore them to full or near-full health, and has benefits likely to be sustained over a very long period." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.5.1 to 4.5.5, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "differential-discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:differential-discounting .

heiro:6000483 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Differential Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "3% for Costs and 1.5% for Effects in the Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland requires future costs to be discounted at a constant 3% and future effects at a constant 1.5% in the reference case whenever data are collected or modelled over more than one year. The cost rate builds on the 2.25% rate for social cost benefit analysis, adjusted upwards for current market developments. The lower rate for effects reflects the view that the value of health grows over time, and equals the cost rate minus an assumed 1.5% annual growth in the consumption value of health, set slightly below the average of an estimated Dutch range of 0.6% to 2.9%." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare, 2024 version (English translation), section 4.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "differential-discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:differential-discounting .

heiro:6000484 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Differential Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Lower Outcome Rate to Avoid Penalising Future Benefits" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre requires future costs to be discounted at 3% and future outcomes at 1.5%, a lower outcome rate intended to avoid penalising too strongly interventions that generate most of their outcomes in the future, such as screening and vaccination. Both rates are kept from earlier guidelines for consistency, since the change in the value of health over time is highly uncertain. Mandatory scenarios apply equal rates of 0%, 3% and 5% to costs and outcomes, the 3% scenario reflecting the base case in many other countries' guidelines." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (published 8 May 2025), Guideline 12 (section 5.12)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "differential-discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:differential-discounting .

heiro:6000485 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Differential Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Identical 3% Rate, With Differential Rates in Sensitivity Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods require costs and benefits lasting longer than one year to be discounted after the first year at an identical constant rate of 3% per year in the base case, with identical rates of 0% and 5% in sensitivity analyses. Noting that the literature discusses differential discounting in certain cases, especially where the consumption value of health is assumed to rise over time, the Institute allows differential rates of 3% for costs and 1.5% for benefits to be examined in sensitivity analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 4.2 (Table 5) and 4.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "differential-discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:differential-discounting .

heiro:6000486 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Differential Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "One Public Discount Rate for Costs and Health Outcomes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS requires future costs and health outcomes to be discounted whenever the time horizon exceeds 12 months, using the public discount rate that applies to all public investment decisions, which was 2.5% when the guidance was published and falls gradually to a floor of 1.5% beyond 30 years. Costs and health outcomes are discounted at the same rate in the reference case, because the relative price of the health outcome is taken not to vary over time. Sensitivity analysis applies at least a 4.5% rate and a zero rate to costs and outcomes together." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation, methodological guidance (2020, English version), Guideline 7 (section 1.7)" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "differential-discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:differential-discounting .

heiro:6000487 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Digital Adoption"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd7/chapter/how-to-meet-the-standards"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Uptake Profile From Pilot Data and an Agreed Plan to Track Ongoing Use" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's evidence standards framework asks companies in all three evidence tiers to describe a digital health technology's expected uptake profile, meaning the proportion of the target population expected to use it and their usage rates. The profile should be calculated from pilot or other usage data, validated by relevant UK professionals and allow for subgroups whose uptake differs or changes over time. The company and evaluator should agree a plan to report ongoing usage against the usage profile agreed at commissioning, and for tier B and C technologies the budget impact analysis should include uptake estimates." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Evidence standards framework for digital health technologies (ECD7), published 10 December 2018, last updated 9 August 2022, how to meet the standards, standards 10, 16 and 17" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-adoption" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-adoption .

heiro:6000488 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Digital Adoption"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240020924"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Digital Health Is Adopted When It Is Accessible, Efficient and Improves Care" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's global strategy on digital health, endorsed by the World Health Assembly in 2020, sets a vision of accelerating the development and adoption of appropriate, accessible, affordable, scalable and sustainable person-centric digital health solutions. It states that digital health will be valued and adopted if it supports equitable and universal access to quality health services, makes health systems more efficient and sustainable, and strengthens services from health promotion to palliative care while protecting the privacy of patient information. A strategic objective calls for inclusive approaches to adoption, including digital health literacy and preparing health workers to use the technologies." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Global strategy on digital health 2020-2025 (2021, ISBN 978-92-4-002092-4), Vision (page 10) and strategic objective 4 (pages 26 and 27)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-adoption" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-adoption .

heiro:6000489 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Digital Divide"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd7/chapter/how-to-meet-the-standards"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Digital Literacy, Connectivity and Device Access Shape Expected Uptake" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's evidence standards framework says the expected uptake of a digital health technology may be affected by digital literacy within the intended user population, the availability of necessary connectivity and access to the necessary hardware or devices. Companies should show that their uptake profile is mindful of subgroups with different expected uptake rates and how these may change over time. The budget impact analysis should include any expected variations for different groups of service users, and the framework points companies to NHS Digital's guide on digital inclusion when considering health inequalities." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Evidence standards framework for digital health technologies (ECD7), published 10 December 2018, last updated 9 August 2022, how to meet the standards, standards 4, 10 and 17" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-divide" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-divide .

heiro:6000490 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Digital Divide"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240020924"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "WHO Defines the Digital Divide as a Gap in Access to Information Technology" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's global strategy on digital health defines the digital divide as the gap between demographics and regions that have access to modern information and communications technology and those that do not or have restricted access, including the telephone, television, personal computers and the internet. The strategy is designed for use by all Member States, including those with limited access to digital technologies, goods and services. It promotes digital technologies as adaptable digital public goods that support equity in access to digital resources so that no one is left behind, and lists improving digital health literacy at population level among possible actions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Global strategy on digital health 2020-2025 (2021, ISBN 978-92-4-002092-4), Purpose (page 11), Guiding principles (page 16), strategic objective 4 (page 26) and Glossary (page 39)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-divide" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-divide .

heiro:6000491 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Digital Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd7/chapter/how-to-meet-the-standards"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Inequalities and Algorithmic Bias Considered in Technology Design" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's evidence standards framework includes a design standard, applying to digital health technologies in all three evidence tiers, that asks companies to factor health inequalities into design and to describe expected positive impacts and efforts to reduce negative ones. A technology claiming to address a health or care inequality should also show that it challenges health inequalities or improves access to care among hard-to-reach populations, and that it promotes equality for people with protected characteristics under the Equality Act 2010. Data-driven technologies, including those using artificial intelligence, should describe design steps taken to mitigate algorithmic bias that could cause unequal impacts between groups." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Evidence standards framework for digital health technologies (ECD7), published 10 December 2018, last updated 9 August 2022, Section C (table 3) and how to meet the standards, standard 4" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-equity .

heiro:6000492 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Digital Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240020924"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Digital Health Must Not Aggravate Inequality and Should Guarantee Access" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's global strategy on digital health asks that, when digital health interventions are planned and prioritised, relevant factors of inequality are assessed so that introducing the technologies does not aggravate them, a \"do no harm\" test, and that access for specific population groups is guaranteed. It calls for stronger gender equality and health equity approaches and accessibility for people with disabilities, to promote an inclusive digital society with better digital health skills. The strategy adds that, designed properly, digital solutions can promote inclusiveness because digital connectivity can transcend physical barriers." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Global strategy on digital health 2020-2025 (2021, ISBN 978-92-4-002092-4), strategic objective 4, policy option 4 (page 27)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-equity .

heiro:6000493 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Digital Evidence Framework"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd7/chapter/section-c-evidence-standards-tables"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "21 Evidence Standards Tiered by Intended Purpose and Financial Risk" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's evidence standards framework sets 21 standards in 5 groups for the evidence a digital health technology should have to show its value in the UK health and care system. Technologies are classified by intended purpose into tiers A, B and C, with the evidence needed proportionate to the potential risk to service users. A budget impact analysis is expected in all tiers, and a cost-utility or cost-consequence analysis where the costs of commissioning, purchasing or implementing the technology are substantial for the relevant budget. The framework is not mandated, so commissioners are not required to use it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Evidence standards framework for digital health technologies (ECD7), published 10 December 2018, last updated 9 August 2022, Introduction, Section B, Section C (tables 3 to 7) and how to meet the standards, standards 17 and 18" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-evidence-framework" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-evidence-framework .

heiro:6000494 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices (BfArM) on Digital Evidence Framework"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000033 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.bfarm.de/SharedDocs/Downloads/DE/Medizinprodukte/diga_leitfaden.pdf?__blob=publicationFile"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices (BfArM)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "DiGA Listing Requires Proof of a Positive Healthcare Effect" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under German law and BfArM's guide, a digital health application is listed for statutory reimbursement only if it meets requirements on safety, functionality, quality, interoperability and data protection and shows a positive healthcare effect, either a medical benefit or a patient-relevant improvement of structure and processes in care. Staff workload and economic indicators do not count as patient-relevant endpoints. Lower-risk applications can be listed provisionally for up to 12 months, extendable by up to 12 more, while a comparative study runs, but class IIb applications must show a medical benefit at application. BfArM decides within three months of a complete application." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Bundesinstitut für Arzneimittel und Medizinprodukte, Das Fast-Track-Verfahren für digitale Gesundheitsanwendungen (DiGA) nach § 139e SGB V, Ein Leitfaden für Hersteller, Leistungserbringer und Anwender (version 3.6, 10 December 2025), sections 1.1, 2.3 and 4.1; Fifth Book of the Social Code (SGB V) section 139e(2) to (4)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-evidence-framework" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-evidence-framework .

heiro:6000495 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Digital Health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240020924"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Digital Health Defined as a Field of Knowledge and Practice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's global strategy on digital health, endorsed by the World Health Assembly in 2020, understands digital health as the field of knowledge and practice associated with the development and use of digital technologies to improve health. The definition encompasses eHealth and extends it to digital consumers with a wider range of smart and connected devices, and to the Internet of Things, advanced computing, big data analytics, artificial intelligence including machine learning, and robotics. WHO states that digital health should be an integral part of health priorities and benefit people in ways that are ethical, safe, secure, reliable, equitable and sustainable." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Global strategy on digital health 2020-2025 (2021, ISBN 978-92-4-002092-4), Introduction (page 8) and Purpose (page 11)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-health" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-health .

heiro:6000496 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Digital Health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd7/chapter/section-b-classification-of-digital-health-technologies"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Digital Health Technologies Classified by Intended Purpose Into Three Tiers" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's evidence standards framework treats digital health technologies as digital products intended to benefit people or the wider health and social care system, such as smartphone apps, standalone software, online tools and programmes that analyse data from medical devices. It excludes software embedded in a medical device, training tools for professionals and tools for collecting research data. Technologies are classified by intended purpose into tier A for system services, tier B for communicating about health, health diaries and promoting good health, and tier C for technologies that inform or guide clinical management, diagnose or treat, where most regulated medical devices fall." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Evidence standards framework for digital health technologies (ECD7), published 10 December 2018, last updated 9 August 2022, Section A and Section B (table 1)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-health" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-health .

heiro:6000497 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices (BfArM) on Digital Health"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000033 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.bfarm.de/SharedDocs/Downloads/DE/Medizinprodukte/diga_leitfaden.pdf?__blob=publicationFile"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices (BfArM)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "What Counts as a Digital Health Application Under German Statutory Insurance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "BfArM's guide defines a digital health application (DiGA) as a medical device of risk class I, IIa or IIb whose main function rests on digital technologies and which the patient uses alone or together with a care provider. It must support the detection, monitoring, treatment or alleviation of disease, and it cannot serve primary prevention or be equipment used only by providers, such as video consultation software. Since the Digital Healthcare Act came into force on 19 December 2019, about 73 million people in statutory health insurance can receive listed applications on prescription, with the cost reimbursed by their insurer." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Bundesinstitut für Arzneimittel und Medizinprodukte, Das Fast-Track-Verfahren für digitale Gesundheitsanwendungen (DiGA) nach § 139e SGB V, Ein Leitfaden für Hersteller, Leistungserbringer und Anwender (version 3.6, 10 December 2025), sections 1.1 and 2.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-health" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-health .

heiro:6000498 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Digital Implementation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd7/chapter/how-to-meet-the-standards"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Deployment Standards and Implementation Costs for Digital Health Technologies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's evidence standards framework for digital health technologies includes three deployment standards, which apply to all tiers and aim to ensure that claimed benefits can be realised in practice. Companies should set out a data flow map and minimum infrastructure requirements, describe their planned approach to training end users, and show that load testing matches expected uptake. For tier B and C technologies, the budget impact analysis should include all direct costs of implementing the technology, including staffing, training and supportive IT infrastructure." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Evidence standards framework for digital health technologies (ECD7), section C and How to meet the standards, standards 17, 19, 20 and 21, published 10 December 2018, last updated 9 August 2022" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-implementation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-implementation .

heiro:6000499 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Digital Implementation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240010567"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Costed Implementation Plans for Digital Health Interventions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's Digital Implementation Investment Guide sets out a process for countries to produce a costed implementation plan for integrating digital interventions into health programmes. It warns that the full costs of digital health implementations are frequently underestimated because budgets focus on the initial demonstration or deployment and omit long-term operation and maintenance. Planners are asked to estimate the total cost of ownership across development and setup, deployment, integration and interoperability, scale and sustained operations, identifying the cost drivers for each phase." ;
    heiro:2000018 "WHO, Digital implementation investment guide (DIIG): integrating digital interventions into health programmes, 2020 (ISBN 978-92-4-001056-7), sections 1.2, 7.1 and 7.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-implementation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-implementation .

heiro:6000500 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of U.S. Department of Health and Human Services on Digital Interoperability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000024 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-D/part-171"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Information Blocking Rules for Electronic Health Information" ;
    heiro:2000017 "US federal regulations define information blocking as a practice likely to interfere with access, exchange or use of electronic health information, unless required by law or covered by an exception. Health IT developers, networks and exchanges are liable if they know or should know this, while health care providers are liable only if they know the practice is unreasonable. Fees, including a reasonable profit margin, are permitted when based on objective, uniformly applied criteria and reasonably related to the actor's costs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "45 CFR Part 171, Information Blocking (Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology), sections 171.103 and 171.302, current eCFR text (85 FR 25955, 1 May 2020, as amended at 89 FR 101810, 16 December 2024)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-interoperability" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-interoperability .

heiro:6000501 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of European Commission on Digital Interoperability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000017 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/ehealth-digital-health-and-care/european-health-data-space-regulation-ehds_en"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "European Commission" ;
    heiro:2000016 "European Health Data Space Interoperability Rules for EHR Systems" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The European Commission states that the European Health Data Space Regulation, published on 5 March 2025 and in force from 26 March 2025, establishes a harmonised legal and technical framework for electronic health record systems. Manufacturers must certify that their systems meet security and interoperability criteria. Exchange of patient summaries and ePrescriptions is due in all Member States from March 2029, with medical images, laboratory results and hospital discharge reports following by March 2031." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Commission, European Health Data Space Regulation (EHDS) web page on Regulation (EU) 2025/327, sections on the regulation, the implementation timeline and data protection and security, accessed 14 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-interoperability" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-interoperability .

heiro:6000502 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Digital Therapeutic"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-H/part-882/subpart-F/section-882.5801"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Class II Special Controls for Prescription Digital Therapy Devices" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA classifies computerised behavioural therapy devices for psychiatric disorders as prescription-only Class II devices, used as an adjunct to clinician-supervised outpatient treatment. Special controls require clinical data validating the therapy model as the device implements it, and labelling that warns the device is not a standalone therapy or a substitute for medication. A 2024 classification for ADHD digital therapy devices adds clinical performance testing with a validated measure of effectiveness and capture of all adverse events." ;
    heiro:2000018 "21 CFR 882.5801, Computerized behavioral therapy device for psychiatric disorders (82 FR 61167, 27 December 2017), and 21 CFR 882.5803, Digital therapy device for Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (89 FR 71156, 3 September 2024), paragraphs (a) and (b), current eCFR text" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-therapeutic" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-therapeutic .

heiro:6000503 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices (BfArM) on Digital Therapeutic"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000033 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.bfarm.de/SharedDocs/Downloads/DE/Medizinprodukte/diga_leitfaden.pdf?__blob=publicationFile"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Federal Institute for Drugs and Medical Devices (BfArM)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "DiGA Fast Track Listing and Proof of Positive Care Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "BfArM's guide to the DiGA fast track states that it assesses an application within three months of a complete submission, checking product requirements and the manufacturer's evidence of positive care effects. Once listed, provisionally or permanently, a DiGA is funded by statutory health insurance. Manufacturers without a suitable study may apply for provisional listing with a trial phase of up to twelve months, but this route is not open to risk class IIb products, which must show a medical benefit before listing." ;
    heiro:2000018 "BfArM, Das Fast-Track-Verfahren für digitale Gesundheitsanwendungen (DiGA) nach § 139e SGB V, Ein Leitfaden für Hersteller, Leistungserbringer und Anwender, Version 3.6, 10 December 2025, sections 1.1, 2.3, 2.3.1 and 2.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-therapeutic" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-therapeutic .

heiro:6000504 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Digital Therapeutic"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/jcms/p_3376633/fr/dispositifs-medicaux-numeriques-la-prise-en-charge-anticipee"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Early Reimbursement (PECAN) for Therapeutic Digital Medical Devices" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS explains that a decree of 30 March 2023 allows early reimbursement of digital medical devices presumed to be innovative, including therapeutic devices intended for the LPPR list. Coverage lasts one year and cannot be renewed. Its device committee, CNEDiMTS, gives an opinion within 60 days on CE marking in the claimed indication and on presumed innovation in clinical benefit or organisation of care, judged on early data and relevant comparators. The manufacturer must then file an LPPR application within six months." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Dispositifs médicaux numériques : la prise en charge anticipée, guide pratique, online 1 May 2024, updated 22 September 2026 (décret n° 2023-232 du 30 mars 2023)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-therapeutic" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-therapeutic .

heiro:6000505 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Digital Therapeutic"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/files/document/mm14315-medicare-physician-fee-schedule-final-rule-summary-cy-2026.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Medicare Payment for Digital Mental Health Treatment Devices" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For 2026, CMS extended its payment codes G0552 to G0554 for digital mental health treatment devices to FDA-classified digital therapy devices for ADHD, alongside computerised behavioural therapy devices. Payment applies when the device is furnished incident to professional behavioural health services, as an adjunct to ongoing clinician-supervised treatment under a plan of care. The billing practitioner bears the cost of the device as a supply, and Medicare pays only for use indicated under the device's FDA classification." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CMS, MLN Matters MM14315, Medicare Physician Fee Schedule Final Rule Summary: CY 2026, release date 5 December 2025, Behavioral Health Services, pages 3 and 4" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "digital-therapeutic" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:digital-therapeutic .

heiro:6000506 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Direct Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "NHS and PSS Perspective on Costs in the Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case takes the perspective of the NHS and personal social services on costs, because its evaluations aim to support efficient use of those resources. Costs should relate to resources under NHS and PSS control and be valued at prices relevant to them, and productivity costs are excluded. Costs paid by patients may be included when the NHS or PSS reimburses them, and unreimbursed costs may be presented separately. Carer time may be costed when the NHS or PSS might otherwise have provided that care, in a separate analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.7, 4.2.9, 4.4.1, 4.4.23 and 4.4.24, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "direct-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:direct-cost .

heiro:6000507 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Direct Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/costing-in-the-nhs/glossary/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Direct Costs in NHS Patient-Level Costing" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS England's costing glossary for the Approved Costing Guidance defines direct costs as those that directly relate to the delivery of patient care, such as medical and nursing staff costs. Indirect costs are not directly determined by patient numbers or mix, a term the glossary notes is common in management accounting but not used in the guidance. Patient-facing costs are driven by patient events and have a clear activity-based allocation method, while overheads such as finance and HR are allocated by a prescribed method such as actual usage or headcount." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England, Approved Costing Guidance, Costing glossary (web version updated 2 June 2023, listed in the 2026 guidance as dated 5 March 2025), entries Direct costs, Indirect costs, Overheads and Patient-facing costs" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "direct-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:direct-cost .

heiro:6000508 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Disability Weight"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/gho-documents/global-health-estimates/ghe2021_daly_methods.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Disability Weights Measure Healthfulness, Not the Worth of Persons" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's Global Health Estimates compute years lived with disability using disability weights on a scale from 0 for perfect health to 1 for a state equivalent to death. WHO holds that the weights should reflect general population judgements about the healthfulness of defined states, not judgements of quality of life, the worth of persons or stigma. Its estimates for 2000 to 2021 draw on Global Burden of Disease 2021 weights, derived from large population surveys of paired comparisons, with WHO's own revisions for conditions including infertility, intellectual disability, vision and hearing loss, dementia and low back pain." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2021, Global Health Estimates Technical Paper WHO/DDI/DNA/GHE/2024.3 (May 2024), sections 1.4, 2, 3.1 and 3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "disability-weight" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:disability-weight .

heiro:6000509 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Discount Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Same 3.5% Annual Rate for Costs and Health Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In the NICE reference case, costs and health effects are discounted at the same rate of 3.5% a year. The committee may consider a non-reference-case rate of 1.5% a year for both only if all stated criteria are met: the technology is for people who would otherwise die or have a very severely impaired life, is likely to restore them to full or near-full health, and has benefits likely to be sustained over a very long period. The committee must also be confident of long-term benefit and satisfied that irrecoverable costs are captured or mitigated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.5.1 to 4.5.5, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discount-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discount-rate .

heiro:6000510 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CDA-AMC on Discount Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CDA-AMC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Canadian Reference-Case Rates" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Canadian guidance specifies reference-case discounting of future costs and outcomes and requires transparent timing, rationale and sensitivity analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discount-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discount-rate .

heiro:6000511 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Discount Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-1-overview-and-rationale-of-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "5% Base-Case Rate With 3.5% and 0% Sensitivity Analyses" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC asks submissions to discount both costs and outcomes at a uniform, compounding annual rate of 5% for all costs and health outcomes that occur or extend beyond one year in the base case. Sensitivity analyses should use fixed rates of 3.5% and 0% a year, applied to both costs and outcomes. Other discounting methods, such as a different uniform rate, differential rates or time-varying rates, may be presented as supplementary analyses if the alternative approach is justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing submissions to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), section 3A.1.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discount-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discount-rate .

heiro:6000512 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Zorginstituut Nederland on Discount Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Zorginstituut Nederland" ;
    heiro:2000016 "3% for Costs and 1.5% for Effects in the Dutch Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland requires data collected or modelled over more than one year to be discounted, with future costs at a constant 3% and future effects at a constant 1.5% in the reference case. The 3% cost rate builds on the 2.25% rate used in Dutch social cost-benefit analysis, adjusted upward for current market developments. The effects rate is the cost rate minus an assumed 1.5% annual growth in the consumption value of health. The 2024 revision adjusted the cost rate to 3%." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare, 2024 version (16 January 2024), section 4.2 and the reference-case summary table" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discount-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discount-rate .

heiro:6000513 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Dental and Pharmaceutical Benefits Agency (TLV) on Discount Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000028 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.tlv.se/download/18.467926b615d084471ac3230c/1510316374332/TLVAR_2017_1.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Dental and Pharmaceutical Benefits Agency (TLV)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "3% for Costs and Health Effects, Tested at 0% and 5%" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TLV's general guidelines on economic evaluations state that both costs and health effects should be discounted at 3%. The sensitivity analysis should also report results at 0% and at 5%, together with a calculation that discounts costs at 3% and health effects at 0%. The guidelines are addressed to companies that attach a health economic analysis to an application for a medicine to be included in the Swedish pharmaceutical benefits scheme." ;
    heiro:2000018 "TLV general guidelines on economic evaluations (TLVAR 2003:2), as amended and reprinted by TLVAR 2017:1 of 26 January 2017, point 9 (in Swedish)" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discount-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discount-rate .

heiro:6000514 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Institute for Clinical and Economic Review (ICER) on Discount Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review (ICER)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Constant 3% Rate for Both Costs and Outcomes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's reference case states that all models should apply constant-rate discounting to both costs and outcomes at 3% a year, as recommended by the Second Panel on Cost-Effectiveness in Health and Medicine. The rate is intended to account for time value and to keep results comparable across studies. Costs, life-years, QALYs and equal value life-years gained are reported both undiscounted and discounted." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, current as of 23 October 2025, Explanations: Model Parameters and Data Inputs, Discounting (page 23) and overview table (page 5)" ;
    heiro:2000019 6 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discount-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discount-rate .

heiro:6000515 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Discount Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#discounting"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "3.5% STPR Declining After Year 30, With 1.5% for Health Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HM Treasury's Green Book sets the social time preference rate at 3.50% in real terms for years 1 to 30 of an appraisal, 3.00% for years 31 to 75 and 2.50% from year 76 onwards. Effects on the health or life of individuals are discounted at a lower 1.5% in every year, because the wealth effect component of the rate is excluded. The rate is real, so values are converted to real terms before discounting, and inflation and discount rates should not be added together." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, The Green Book (2026), published 5 February 2026, chapter 6 (Shortlist appraisal), Discounting" ;
    heiro:2000019 7 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discount-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discount-rate .

heiro:6000516 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Costs and Health Effects Discounted at 3.5% a Year" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In the reference case, NICE requires costs and health effects to be discounted at the same rate of 3.5% per year, so that results reflect present values over the time horizon. A committee may consider a non-reference-case rate of 1.5% for both costs and health effects only when the technology is for people who would otherwise die or have a very severely impaired life, is likely to restore full or near-full health and has benefits likely to last a very long time. The committee must then see a highly plausible case that benefits are maintained." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.5.1 to 4.5.5, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discounting .

heiro:6000517 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book--2/the-green-book-2026#discounting"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Social Time Preference Rate of 3.5% and 1.5% for Health Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Green Book discounts social costs and benefits at the social time preference rate, set at 3.5% in real terms for years 1 to 30, 3.0% for years 31 to 75 and 2.5% from year 76. Effects on health or life are discounted at a lower rate of 1.5%, because the wealth effect component of the rate is excluded. The rate is real and applies only to values with general inflation removed, and adding the inflation and discount rates together is described as mathematically incorrect." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, The Green Book (2026), updated 5 February 2026, chapter 6 (Discounting) and chapter 8 (Costs and benefits for society, Life and health)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discounting .

heiro:6000518 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Costs Discounted at 3% and Health Effects at 1.5%" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Dutch guideline requires reference-case future costs to be discounted at a constant 3% and future effects at a constant 1.5% whenever data are collected or modelled over more than one year. The lower rate for effects reflects the view that the value of health grows over time, and it equals the cost rate minus an assumed 1.5% annual growth in the consumption value of health. A scenario with no discounting must always be performed." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), sections 4.2 and 4.7.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discounting .

heiro:6000519 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Public Discount Rate of 2.5%, Falling to 1.5% After 30 Years" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS requires future costs and health outcomes to be discounted to present values whenever the time horizon exceeds 12 months, using the public discount rate that applies to all public investment decisions in France. When its guidance was published in 2020 that rate was 2.5% for horizons under 30 years, declining gradually beyond that to a floor of 1.5%. Costs and health outcomes must be discounted at the same rate in the reference case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation, methodological guidance validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, section 1.7 (Guideline 7)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discounting .

heiro:6000520 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-1-overview-and-rationale-of-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Base Case Rate of 5% With 3.5% and 0% Sensitivity Analyses" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC Guidelines ask for costs and health outcomes occurring or extending beyond one year to be discounted at a uniform annual compounding rate of 5% in the base case. Sensitivity analyses at fixed rates of 3.5% and 0%, applied to both costs and outcomes, are also requested, and other methods such as differential or time-varying rates may be offered as justified supplementary analyses. In April 2026 the PBAC reported that discount rates are one of two focus areas in its review of the Guidelines." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing submissions to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), section 3A.1.5; Update from the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, April 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discounting .

heiro:6000521 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Discrete Event Simulation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Choice of Individual Patient Simulation Must Be Justified" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE requires the chosen type of model, naming a Markov cohort model and an individual patient simulation as examples, to be justified for each new decision problem. Stating that a structure has previously appeared in published model reports or been accepted in earlier NICE submissions is not enough. The conceptual modelling process that informs the structure, including how experts were involved, should be transparent and justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section(s) 4.6.3, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discrete-event-simulation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discrete-event-simulation .

heiro:6000522 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Discrete Event Simulation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/nice-dsu/tsds/patient-level-simulation"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "When Patient-Level Simulation Suits NICE Appraisals and How to Size It" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 15 advises that a patient-level simulation, such as a discrete event simulation, may be preferable to a cohort model when outcomes vary non-linearly with patient characteristics, when risks depend on time since an event or on past events, when bursts of events alternate with long quiet periods, or when patients compete for constrained resources. The choice of approach should be properly justified. Modellers are expected to justify the number of patients simulated, and probabilistic sensitivity analysis usually needs two nested simulation loops." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15: Cost-effectiveness modelling using patient-level simulation (Davis, Stevenson, Tappenden and Wailoo, April 2014), sections 1, 3.1 to 3.6 and 5.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discrete-event-simulation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discrete-event-simulation .

heiro:6000523 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Discrete Event Simulation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Stable Simulated Population Size for Microsimulation Models" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Dutch guideline lists discrete event simulation among the commonly used model types, with the choice depending largely on the research question and the nature of the disease. For microsimulation models, analysts should clarify what simulated population size gives stable results, for example by plotting outcomes against an increasing population size with a 95% confidence interval. Uncertainty in the parameters of distributions describing variability should be included, preferably by bootstrapping, without mixing variability or heterogeneity with uncertainty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), sections 4.1.2, 4.7.2 and 4.7.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discrete-event-simulation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discrete-event-simulation .

heiro:6000524 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Discrete Event Simulation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Model Type Justified Against Individual Simulation and DES Criteria" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS requires the selected model type to be justified through an analysis of the possible options, based mainly on how the model handles time, whether individuals interact, whether a cohort or an individual-centred unit suits the heterogeneity of the population, and whether parameter randomness must be captured. Its guidance table prefers individual simulation when parameters interact or when too many dimensions rule out a cohort approach, and describes discrete event simulation as useful when a marginal parameter change can produce a non-linear change in performance, such as a full intensive care unit." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (methodological guidance, April 2020), Guideline 22 in section 4.2, and Annex 10, Table 18" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "discrete-event-simulation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:discrete-event-simulation .

heiro:6000525 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Disease-Free Survival"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/71195/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Disease-Free Survival Can Support Approval as a Surrogate or as Direct Clinical Benefit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The FDA generally defines disease-free survival (DFS) as the time from randomisation until disease recurrence or death from any cause. The most frequent use of the endpoint is in the adjuvant setting after definitive surgery or radiotherapy, and DFS can be an important endpoint in situations where survival may be prolonged, making a survival endpoint impractical. A treatment effect measured by DFS or event-free survival can be a surrogate endpoint to support accelerated approval, a surrogate endpoint to support traditional approval, or it can represent direct clinical benefit." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, Clinical Trial Endpoints for the Approval of Cancer Drugs and Biologics, Guidance for Industry, December 2018, section on disease-free survival and event-free survival" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "disease-free-survival" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:disease-free-survival .

heiro:6000526 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Distribution-Based MID"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/77832/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Distribution-Based Methods Only Support Anchor-Based Responder Definitions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's guidance on patient-reported outcome measures states that the empirical evidence for any responder definition is derived using anchor-based methods. Distribution-based methods, using for example the between-person standard deviation or the standard error of measurement, can classify changes as small, moderate or large and can be combined with anchor-based estimates, but they are supportive only and not appropriate as the sole basis for a responder definition. FDA's April 2023 draft guidance on endpoints, not yet final, likewise finds them insufficient as the sole basis for a meaningful score difference." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Guidance for Industry. Patient-Reported Outcome Measures: Use in Medical Product Development to Support Labeling Claims, final guidance (December 2009), section IV.E; Patient-Focused Drug Development: Incorporating Clinical Outcome Assessments Into Endpoints for Regulatory Decision-Making, draft guidance (Guidance 4, April 2023), section III.B.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "distribution-based-mid" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:distribution-based-mid .

heiro:6000527 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Distribution-Based MID"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Fixed 15% Response Threshold Instead of Study-Derived MIDs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods state that anchor-based methods are preferred for determining a minimally important difference, with distribution-based methods also in use, but that no established standard exists for judging the quality of studies that derive such values. Because published values vary widely, the Institute accepts responder analyses only where the response criterion is at least 15% of the instrument's scale range. Otherwise it uses standardised mean differences (Hedges' g), treating an effect as at least small when the confidence interval lies wholly above an irrelevance threshold of 0.2." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 10.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "distribution-based-mid" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:distribution-based-mid .

heiro:6000528 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Distribution-Based MID"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/a70a62c7-325c-401e-ba42-66174b656ab8_en?filename=hta_outcomes_jca_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Distribution-Based MIDs Secondary to Anchor-Based Estimates" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EU HTA Coordination Group's guidance on outcomes for joint clinical assessments regards anchor-based methods, which link a change in score to the patient's own global rating of change, as the most appropriate way to estimate a minimal important difference. Distribution-based methods use only the overall variability in scores and are criticised for not referring to what a change means to patients, although they are still used as secondary approaches to triangulate a plausible range. The guidance names Cohen's d values of 0.2 and 0.5 and one standard error of measurement as the commonest yardsticks." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment (HTACG), Guidance on outcomes for joint clinical assessments, adopted 10 June 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 5.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "distribution-based-mid" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:distribution-based-mid .

heiro:6000529 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Distributional Cost-Effectiveness"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/resources/support-document-health-inequalities-15313210669/chapter/distributional-cost-effectiveness-analysis-methods"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "DCEA Only as Supporting Evidence, Without Inequality Aversion Weights" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE describes distributional cost-effectiveness analysis (DCEA) as a framework showing how costs and benefits vary across population groups and how a technology may affect the health inequality gap. A company should include one only with clear quantitative evidence of a significant burden of health inequalities in the eligible population, and only as supporting evidence. NICE's preferred methods stratify groups by Index of Multiple Deprivation, assume equal uptake unless evidence shows otherwise, apply no inequality aversion weights to QALYs, share opportunity costs equally with gradient scenarios, and report net health benefit by quintile." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.12.1 to 4.12.8, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE support document: Health inequalities, chapter Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis methods, last updated 13 May 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "distributional-cost-effectiveness" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:distributional-cost-effectiveness .

heiro:6000530 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Disutility"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD12-Utilities-in-modelling-FINAL.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Combining Utility Decrements and Adverse Event Disutilities" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 12 sets out the additive, multiplicative and minimum methods for estimating utility in a combined health state from single-condition data, and states that assuming a baseline of perfect health is inappropriate, with age and sex adjustment an absolute minimum. Where utilities for the combined condition are unavailable, the multiplicative method should be used. Submissions to NICE should justify including or excluding adverse event disutilities, treat decrements for grade 3 to 4 events as particularly important, and avoid double counting decrements for grade 1 to 2 events." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 12: The use of health state utility values in decision models (Ara and Wailoo, July 2011), executive summary and sections 2.1, 3.2, 3.2.1, 5.2.1 and 5.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "disutility" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:disutility .

heiro:6000531 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Disutility"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Disutilities of Side Effects Measured With the EQ-5D-5L at Onset" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland notes that medicines and surgical procedures can cause side effects or complications that affect quality of life, and that those with long-term and short-term consequences should be distinguished. Their effects on quality of life, usually referred to as disutilities, must be measured with the EQ-5D-5L, or the EQ-5D-Y, completed at the moment the side effects or complications occur. Where this is impossible, the disutility values and their durations should be based on scientific literature." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare, 2024 version (English translation), section 3.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "disutility" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:disutility .

heiro:6000532 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Disutility"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Rules for Combining Disutilities Must Be Justified and Tested" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre defines a disutility as the decrement in utility due to a particular symptom or complication. It states that combining utilities and disutilities for multiple adverse events or multimorbidity should be treated with caution, and names the additive approach, which applies a marginal disutility for each additional state, the multiplicative approach and the minimum approach. Because none of these methods is free of bias, the chosen approach must be justified and alternative plausible assumptions tested in scenario analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (published 8 May 2025), Guideline 10 (section 5.10.2.2) and Glossary" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "disutility" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:disutility .

heiro:6000533 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Disutility"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Utility Loss From Rare Adverse Events Needs a Planned Approach" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS notes that trials are generally ill-suited to estimating utility scores for rare events such as adverse effects or acute events. Where such events have a major impact, a pragmatic data collection approach should be planned before the trial; otherwise a systematic literature review should identify the utility score for each event, with extreme values tested in multivariate sensitivity analysis. A mean utility difference between people who did and did not experience the event may be used when trial data or published disutilities are not reliable enough, with its limitations discussed." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation, methodological guidance (2020, English version), section 2.3.3 (rare events)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "disutility" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:disutility .

heiro:6000534 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on DLQI"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta534/chapter/1-Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "DLQI Reduction of at Least 4 Points Forms Part of the Dupilumab Stopping Rule" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE recommends dupilumab for moderate to severe atopic dermatitis in adults only if the disease has not responded to at least one other systemic therapy, such as ciclosporin, methotrexate, azathioprine or mycophenolate mofetil, or these are contraindicated or not tolerated, and the company provides it according to the commercial arrangement. Dupilumab should be stopped at 16 weeks if the atopic dermatitis has not responded adequately, meaning at least a 50% reduction in the Eczema Area and Severity Index score (EASI 50) and at least a 4-point reduction in the Dermatology Life Quality Index (DLQI) from when treatment started." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal guidance TA534, Dupilumab for treating moderate to severe atopic dermatitis, recommendations 1.1 and 1.2, published 1 August 2018" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "dlqi" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:dlqi .

heiro:6000535 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Dominance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Fully Incremental Analysis With Dominated Options Removed" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE asks that costs and QALYs be combined using standard decision rules that reflect dominance or extended dominance where it exists, presented through incremental cost-utility analysis. Results should be shown in a fully incremental analysis with dominated technologies, meaning those more costly and less effective than another technology, and extendedly dominated technologies removed. Pairwise comparisons may be added when relevant and justified, and expected net health benefits should also be presented when appropriate, using values of £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY gained." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16 and 4.10.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "dominance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:dominance .

heiro:6000536 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Dominance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Fully Incremental Analysis Flagging Strongly and Weakly Dominated Options" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ZIN states that a fully incremental analysis should be used to report outcomes whenever more than two interventions are compared. Interventions are ranked in a table from low to high costs or QALYs, those that are strongly or weakly dominated are identified, and ICERs are calculated only for the interventions that are not dominated. The results can be presented as an efficiency frontier, and the same approach applies when several usual interventions exist or the usual intervention is not the most cost-effective option." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), sections 2.3.3 and 5.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "dominance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:dominance .

heiro:6000537 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Dominance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Effectiveness Frontier Built From Non-Dominated Interventions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS holds that evaluating cost-effectiveness requires identifying the interventions on the cost-effectiveness frontier, which comprises all non-dominated interventions under strict or extended dominance. Its guidance defines strict dominance as an intervention being less costly for identical or higher effectiveness, or more effective for identical or lower cost. A place on the frontier indicates an economically rational choice but gives no indication of acceptability against willingness to pay, and where the frontier cannot be built a side-by-side comparison of costs and outcomes is presented instead." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (April 2020), Guideline 26, section 5.1.1 and Glossary" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "dominance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:dominance .

heiro:6000538 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Dominance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Efficiency Frontier Applying Absolute and Extended Dominance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For health economic evaluations conducted outside the statutory framework of §35b SGB V, IQWiG states that when more than two treatments are considered, the cost-effectiveness ratios can be plotted as one or more efficiency frontiers. The treatments forming the frontier are those most efficient in benefits and costs, taking into account the criterion of absolute and extended dominance. IQWiG adds that the final segment of the frontier no longer needs to be extended, since that extension was previously intended for deriving a price recommendation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 4.15" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "dominance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:dominance .

heiro:6000539 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Dose Response"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/ich-e-4-dose-response-information-support-drug-registration-step-5_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Dose Response Assessment as an Integral Part of Drug Development" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The ICH E4 guideline, in operation at the EMA since November 1994, states that knowledge of the relationships among dose, blood concentration and clinical response, both effectiveness and undesirable effects, is important for safe and effective use, and that dose-response assessment should be an integral part of drug development. It notes that drugs have often been marketed first at excessive doses on the plateau of the dose-response curve, and that no single dose is necessarily optimal for all patients. The randomised parallel dose-response study is described as simple in concept and widely used with considerable success." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICH Topic E4, Dose response information to support drug registration, note for guidance CPMP/ICH/378/95 (ICH harmonised tripartite guideline, Step 5, in operation November 1994), EMA scientific guideline, sections 1, 2 and 3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "dose-response" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:dose-response .

heiro:6000540 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Dose Response"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/164555/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Compare Multiple Oncology Dosages Before Approval" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's 2024 guidance on oncology dosages states that the maximum tolerated dose paradigm suited cytotoxic chemotherapy with steep dose-response relationships, whereas targeted therapies often show different dose-response relationships, so doses below the maximum may give similar activity with fewer toxicities. Dose- and exposure-response relationships should be evaluated early and used, with safety, tolerability and activity data, to select dosages for each trial. Multiple dosages should be compared before approval, and a randomised, parallel dose-response trial is one recommended design." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Optimizing the Dosage of Human Prescription Drugs and Biological Products for the Treatment of Oncologic Diseases, guidance for industry, final (August 2024), sections II, III.A and III.B" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "dose-response" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:dose-response .

heiro:6000541 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on DRG Creep"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/04/2026-15833/medicare-program-hospital-inpatient-prospective-payment-systems-for-acute-care-hospitals-ipps-and"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Separating Real Case-Mix Growth From Coding Effects in DRG Payment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In its FY 2027 inpatient prospective payment final rule, CMS distinguishes real case-mix change, meaning actual changes in the mix and resource needs of Medicare patients, from changes in documentation and coding that assign cases to higher-weighted DRGs without higher resource requirements. Federal law lets CMS adjust payments to remove the effect of coding or classification changes that do not reflect real case mix. With real case-mix growth projected at 0.5% a year, CMS sought public input on a default maximum threshold above which growth could prompt a prospective payment reduction." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Medicare Program; Hospital Inpatient Prospective Payment Systems for Acute Care Hospitals (IPPS) and the Long-Term Care Hospital Prospective Payment System and Policy Changes and Fiscal Year (FY) 2027 Rates, final rule, 91 FR 49570 (4 August 2026), Addendum sections II.A.4.h and III.A.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "drg-creep" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:drg-creep .

heiro:6000542 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on DRG Creep"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/08-nhssc-technical-guidance-2627.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Neutralising the Payment Impact of Counting and Coding Changes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NHS Standard Contract requires providers to follow national clinical coding guidance and bars local changes in counting and coding practice without the commissioner's agreement. A locally proposed change needs at least six months' notice and normally takes effect on 1 April, and where any coding change alters the value of services, prices must be adjusted so that its financial impact is neutral in the short term. NHS England's technical guidance explains that national prices assume activity is recorded on the same basis as before, and that most disputes have concerned coding drift in non-elective care." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England, 2026/27 NHS Standard Contract Service Conditions (Full Length), version 1, January 2026, SC28.7 to SC28.15; 2026/27 NHS Standard Contract Technical Guidance, version 2, January 2026 (PRN02124), section 44" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "drg-creep" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:drg-creep .

heiro:6000543 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on ECOG Performance Status"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta638/chapter/1-Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Recommendations Limited to ECOG 0 or 1 Where Trial Evidence Covers Only Those Grades" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE has recommended some cancer medicines only for adults with an Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) performance status of 0 or 1. In TA638 atezolizumab with carboplatin and etoposide for untreated extensive-stage small-cell lung cancer is recommended only for this group, because the clinical trial evidence was in people with an ECOG score of 0 or 1. TA818 places the same condition on nivolumab with ipilimumab for untreated unresectable malignant pleural mesothelioma. The TA638 committee also asked healthcare professionals to take account of physical, sensory or learning disabilities, or communication difficulties, that could affect the ECOG score and to make appropriate adjustments." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Atezolizumab with carboplatin and etoposide for untreated extensive-stage small-cell lung cancer (TA638), section 1 Recommendations, published 1 July 2020; Nivolumab with ipilimumab for untreated unresectable malignant pleural mesothelioma (TA818), section 1 Recommendations, published 17 August 2022, https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta818/chapter/1-Recommendations" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "ecog-performance-status" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:ecog-performance-status .

heiro:6000544 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Economic Evaluation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reference Case With Cost-Utility or Cost-Comparison Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE requires economic evaluations considered in its technology appraisals and highly specialised technologies evaluations to include an analysis using its reference-case methods, so that results are consistent across technologies and disease areas. Two forms are available: cost-utility analysis when a full analysis of costs and health benefits is needed, and cost-comparison analysis for technologies likely to provide similar or greater health benefits at similar or lower cost. Non-reference-case analyses may also be presented if they are justified and clearly distinguished." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.1, 4.2.2 and 4.2.11 to 4.2.13, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "economic-evaluation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:economic-evaluation .

heiro:6000545 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Economic Evaluation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240020689"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Effectiveness as One Criterion for Benefit Packages" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO states that one overarching goal of health benefit package design is to maximise population health within the available budget. Cost-effectiveness, meaning the value for money of an intervention, is listed as one possible selection criterion alongside burden of disease, equity, financial risk protection, budget impact, feasibility and political acceptability. Trade-offs between criteria should be explicit, and where other criteria are prioritised the resulting health loss should be clearly communicated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization. Principles of health benefit packages (2021), ISBN 978-92-4-002068-9, principle 3 and table 2, pages 7 to 8" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "economic-evaluation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:economic-evaluation .

heiro:6000546 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Economic Evaluation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Societal Perspective Reference Case for Dutch Evaluations" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ZIN treats cost-effectiveness as one of the four criteria for the Dutch basic healthcare package, and its 2024 guideline sets a reference case with which all economic evaluations carried out for decision-making must comply. Evaluations are conducted and reported from a societal perspective, covering all relevant societal costs and benefits irrespective of who bears the costs or receives the benefits. Other choices may be explored in justified scenario analyses, but these cannot replace the reference case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland. Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), 16 January 2024, preface, chapter 1 and section 2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "economic-evaluation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:economic-evaluation .

heiro:6000547 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of European Union (HTA Regulation) on Economic Evaluation"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "The Institution field, \"European Union (HTA Regulation)\", names Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, a rule, not a body, so the record names the regulation."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000019 heiro:5000238 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2021/2282/oj/eng"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "European Union (HTA Regulation)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Economic Evaluation Outside EU Joint Clinical Assessment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the EU health technology assessment regulation, joint clinical assessments carried out through the Member State Coordination Group cover only clinical domains: the health problem and current technologies, technical characteristics, relative clinical effectiveness and relative safety. Cost and economic evaluation is defined as a non-clinical domain, and Union-level work on non-clinical assessment is limited to voluntary cooperation between Member States. The regulation does not interfere with national pricing and reimbursement decisions, which remain a national competence." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Regulation (EU) 2021/2282 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 15 December 2021 on health technology assessment, recital 14, Article 1(2), Article 2(6) and (7) and Article 23(1)(a)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "economic-evaluation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:economic-evaluation .

heiro:6000548 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Economic Evaluation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#methods-of-analysis"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Social Cost-Benefit Analysis as the Recommended Appraisal Method" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HM Treasury's Green Book names two main methods for comparing shortlisted options in UK government appraisal. Social cost-benefit analysis, which values social costs and benefits in monetary terms, is the recommended approach, while social cost-effectiveness analysis is used when the main social benefits cannot be monetised or options deliver the same output by different means. The scope is UK society, and health and life effects are discounted at 1.5% rather than the standard social time preference rate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury. The Green Book (2026), published 5 February 2026, chapter 6 (Shortlist appraisal: methods of analysis, scope and discounting)" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "economic-evaluation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:economic-evaluation .

heiro:6000549 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of European Observatory on Health Systems and Policies on Efficiency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000086 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://eurohealthobservatory.who.int/publications/i/health-system-efficiency-how-to-make-measurement-matter-for-policy-and-management-study"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "European Observatory on Health Systems and Policies" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Making Efficiency Measurement Useful for Decisions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The European Observatory on Health Systems and Policies describes the core idea of efficiency as maximising valued outputs relative to inputs, while noting that it is often hard to make operational, particularly at health system level. It distinguishes allocative inefficiency, where the wrong mix of services or inputs is used given societal preferences, from technical inefficiency, which arises mainly at provider and practitioner level. It argues that efficiency measures must take account of external factors the health sector cannot control, such as the social determinants of health." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Cylus J, Papanicolas I, Smith PC (eds). Health system efficiency: how to make measurement matter for policy and management. Health Policy Series No. 46. European Observatory on Health Systems and Policies, 2016. Preface; chapter 1, sections 1.2, 1.3 and 1.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "efficiency" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:efficiency .

heiro:6000550 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Efficiency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564021"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "More Health for the Money by Reducing Inefficiency" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO defines efficiency as the quality or quantity of output, such as health outcomes or services, for a given level of input, and states that seeking efficiency gains does not mean simply cutting costs. It estimates that 20% to 40% of all health spending is wasted through inefficiency, describing this as a conservative, indicative figure based on relatively limited data. It identifies ten leading causes of inefficiency and argues that the resources released could be used to extend coverage towards universal health coverage." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization. The world health report: health systems financing: the path to universal coverage. Geneva: WHO, 2010. Executive summary; chapter 4 (More health for the money), key messages, Using resources wisely, Table 4.1 and Conclusion" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "efficiency" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:efficiency .

heiro:6000551 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Efficiency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Guidance Aims at Efficient Use of NHS and PSS Resources" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that it does not set the NHS budget and that the objective of its evaluations is guidance representing an efficient use of available NHS and personal social services resources, so the reference-case cost perspective is that of the NHS and PSS. Its committees take into account the Health and Social Care Act 2012, which expects NICE to be aware of the broad balance between the benefits and costs of providing services. Committees consider how their advice may allow more efficient use of healthcare resources, requiring greater certainty as the impact on NHS resources increases." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.9, 6.1.2 and 6.2.31, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "efficiency" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:efficiency .

heiro:6000552 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development on Efficiency"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000009 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/01/tackling-wasteful-spending-on-health_g1g72f29/9789264266414-en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Tackling Wasteful Spending on Health to Release Resources" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The OECD finds that a significant share of health spending in OECD countries is at best ineffective and at worst wasteful, and that existing estimates suggest one-fifth of health spending could be channelled towards better use. It treats as wasteful services and processes that are harmful or deliver no benefit, and costs that could be avoided by substituting cheaper alternatives with identical or better benefits. It groups such spending into wasteful clinical care, operational waste and governance-related waste." ;
    heiro:2000018 "OECD. Tackling Wasteful Spending on Health. Paris: OECD Publishing, 2017. Executive summary; chapter 1 (Ineffective spending and waste in health care systems: framework and findings)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "efficiency" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:efficiency .

heiro:6000553 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Efficiency Frontier"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/en/about-us/methods/methods-paper/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Indication-Specific Efficiency Frontier Sets the Maximum Reimbursable Price" ;
    heiro:2000017 "When a formal cost-benefit assessment is undertaken, IQWiG uses an efficiency-frontier method rather than a fixed cross-indication threshold: existing interventions in the same indication are plotted with net cost on one axis and value of benefit on the other, the non-dominated options are linked to form the efficiency frontier, and the maximum reimbursable price for a new drug is inferred from its position relative to that frontier (its willingness-to-pay is indication-specific, derived from the slope of the frontier / the \"proportional rule\")." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods; Caro et al., efficiency frontier approach (Health Economics, 2010)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "efficiency-frontier" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:efficiency-frontier .

heiro:6000554 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on EPDS"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg192/chapter/recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "EPDS or PHQ-9 Used for Full Assessment and Monitoring After Brief Depression Questions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE guideline CG192 recommends considering two depression identification questions and the GAD-2 at a woman's first contact with primary care or her booking visit and in the early postnatal period. If she responds positively, is at risk or there is clinical concern, practitioners should consider using the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale or the PHQ-9 as part of a full assessment, or refer her to her GP or, where a severe problem is suspected, to a mental health professional. At later contacts the EPDS or the PHQ-9 can be used as part of monitoring." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Antenatal and postnatal mental health: clinical management and service guidance (CG192), first published 2014, updated 2020, recommendations 1.5.4, 1.5.5 and 1.5.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "epds" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:epds .

heiro:6000555 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on EQ-5D-3L Value Set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg51/chapter/statement-on-using-the-eq-5d-5l-value-set"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "UK EQ-5D-5L Value Set Replaces the Three-Level Set for Adults" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that from 27 August 2026 the EQ-5D-5L descriptive system and the UK value set should be used for all adult reference-case analyses, so the UK EQ-5D-3L value set no longer supplies reference-case utilities for adults in new work. The interim methods statement PMG51 replaced the earlier position statement on the EQ-5D-5L value set for England, which had not recommended the 2018 England set, and adopts the UK value set published by Rowen and colleagues in 2026. PMG51 takes precedence over the manual until PMG36 is revised later in 2026." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), overview notice, last updated 13 October 2026; Interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51), statement on using the EQ-5D-5L value set, published 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "eq-5d-3l-value-set" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:eq-5d-3l-value-set .

heiro:6000556 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on EQ-5D-5L Value Set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg51/chapter/statement-on-using-the-eq-5d-5l-value-set"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "UK EQ-5D-5L Value Set Required for All Adult Reference-Case Utilities" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's interim methods statement makes the EQ-5D-5L descriptive system and the UK value set by Rowen and colleagues its preferred approach for measuring and valuing health-related quality of life in adults. The UK value set should be used to derive all adult utility values in reference-case analyses, including general population utilities for QALY shortfall under the severity modifier. Utilities based on the EQ-5D-3L value set should not appear in sensitivity or scenario analyses, and 3L data should be mapped to 5L utility values. The statement replaces the 2019 position statement that rejected the 2018 England value set." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE interim methods statement (PMG51), Statement on using the EQ-5D-5L value set, published 27 August 2026; takes precedence over the NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), last updated 13 October 2026, until the manual is updated" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "eq-5d-5l-value-set" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:eq-5d-5l-value-set .

heiro:6000557 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on EQ-HWB"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/what-nice-does/our-research-work/our-projects-and-partners/science-policy-projects"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Not Developed as a Replacement for the EQ-5D" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE describes the EQ-HWB (EQ Health and Wellbeing instrument) as a new standardised measure of health and wellbeing created following the Extending the QALY research project, to which NICE contributed. NICE states that the EQ-HWB has not been developed as a replacement for the EQ-5D, but as a different instrument that captures a broader range of impacts on people, their families and carers. Research is under way to validate the EQ-HWB and compare it with existing instruments, and NICE says this research will help it decide whether and how to use the EQ-HWB to inform its evaluations in the future." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Science policy and methods projects (web page, EQ-HWB entry), accessed 17 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "eq-hwb" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:eq-hwb .

heiro:6000558 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences on Equipoise"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000031 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://cioms.ch/wp-content/uploads/2017/01/WEB-CIOMS-EthicalGuidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Credible Uncertainty Permits Comparison Against an Established Intervention" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The CIOMS ethical guidelines, prepared with the World Health Organization, hold that participants in a control group should, as a general rule, receive an established effective intervention. When there is credible uncertainty about whether that intervention is superior to an investigational agent, which the guidelines call clinical equipoise, the two may be compared directly, because participants are not deprived of care believed to be effective. Withholding an established intervention instead requires compelling scientific reasons and no more than a minor increase above minimal risk." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences, International Ethical Guidelines for Health-related Research Involving Humans (Geneva 2016, prepared in collaboration with WHO), Guideline 5, Choice of control in clinical trials, and commentary" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equipoise" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equipoise .

heiro:6000559 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/health-topics/health-equity"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Identifying and Eliminating Unfair Health Differences" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO defines equity as the absence of unfair, avoidable or remediable differences among groups of people, whether those groups are defined socially, economically, demographically, geographically or by dimensions such as sex, ethnicity or disability. It states that monitoring health inequality must be central to national health information systems, beginning with data disaggregated by age, sex, education, income and disability. It calls for services that are available, accessible and acceptable to everyone, and for action on the structural determinants that shape the distribution of power and resources." ;
    heiro:2000018 "WHO health topics page: Health equity, Overview (no publication date shown on the page)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity .

heiro:6000560 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Equal QALY Weight With Structured Consideration of Health Inequalities" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In the reference case NICE regards all QALYs as being of equal weight, and health inequalities are handled through structured decision making rather than as a decision-making modifier. Since a May 2025 update, if robust evidence shows a technology substantially affects health inequalities, the committee can apply flexibility to the range normally considered cost effective, but must consider healthcare displacement and opportunity cost. A distributional cost-effectiveness analysis is expected only where there is clear evidence of a significant burden of health inequalities, and it must not weigh costs or benefits differently by social characteristics." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.2.10, 6.2.35 to 6.2.38, 4.12.2, 4.12.3 and 4.12.7, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity .

heiro:6000561 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/11/26/beoordelingskader-kosteneffectiviteit-van-zorg/Beoordelingskader+kosteneffectiviteit+van+zorg.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reference Values Rise With Burden of Disease" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ZIN treats maximising health gain from premium income as a fair starting point, while recognising a competing view that resources should narrow differences in health, giving priority to patients with a high burden of disease. Citing research on solidarity, it states that society considers higher costs per QALY fair for more severe disease. Burden is measured with the proportional shortfall method, and the reference value is up to €20,000 per QALY for a burden of 0.10 to 0.40, €50,000 for 0.41 to 0.70 and €80,000 for 0.71 to 1.00." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Beoordelingskader kosteneffectiviteit van zorg: afweging van effecten en kosten, final version 26 November 2024, sections 1.3 and 3.2.2 (Kader 4 and Tabel 1)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity .

heiro:6000562 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "QALYs Not Weighted by Personal Characteristics" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS lists equitable access to healthcare among the aims that economic evaluation helps public decision makers balance, alongside health improvement, quality of care, capacity to integrate innovation and control of public spending. In the reference case cost-utility analysis, QALYs are not weighted according to the individual characteristics of the people concerned, such as socio-demographic factors or severity. HAS describes such weighting as still debated, with methodological and ethical issues unresolved, and does not currently recommend it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in Methods for Economic Evaluation, methodological guidance, 2020 version (English edition), Introduction and section 2.1.2 (Guideline 10)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity .

heiro:6000563 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Equity-Efficiency Trade-off"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Equal QALY Weights, a Severity Modifier and Visible Health Costs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case gives all QALYs equal weight, but its committees may weight QALYs by 1.2 or 1.7 for severe conditions, judged by absolute or proportional QALY shortfall, whichever implies greater severity. To keep the opportunity cost visible, net health benefit must be shown at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY with and without the weighting. NICE's support document on health inequalities asks for results by deprivation quintile but rules out inequality aversion weights, leaving health inequalities to committee deliberation above £25,000 per QALY." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.2.10, 6.2.12 to 6.2.18, 6.3.2 and 6.3.5, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE support document: health inequalities, distributional cost-effectiveness analysis methods (last updated 13 May 2025)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity-efficiency-trade-off" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity-efficiency-trade-off .

heiro:6000564 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Equity-Efficiency Trade-off"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240020689"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Benefit Package Trade-Offs Between Criteria Made Explicit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's principles for health benefit packages list cost-effectiveness and equity, including priority to the worse off, among possible selection criteria alongside burden of disease, financial risk protection and budget impact. WHO states that trade-offs between conflicting goals may be necessary and that explicit priority setting makes them transparent. Packages should achieve the greatest possible health benefit within the budget, and where other criteria are prioritised, the health loss resulting from the trade-off must be clearly communicated to decision makers and the public." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization. Principles of health benefit packages (2021), ISBN 978-92-4-002068-9, principles 3 and 8 and table 2, pages 7 to 8 and 13" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity-efficiency-trade-off" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity-efficiency-trade-off .

heiro:6000565 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Equity-Efficiency Trade-off"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Equity Weights in the Reference Case, QALYs Shown by Group" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre requires all outcomes, such as QALYs, to be weighted equally in the reference case, whoever receives or is affected by the intervention. It notes that no societal equity weights have been set in Belgium and that such weights could shift with changing governments and health policy priorities. So that decision makers can discuss equity during appraisal, reports should show the QALYs gained in each target population in disaggregated form, for example highlighting unmet needs of disadvantaged groups." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (published 8 May 2025), Guideline 13 (section 5.13)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity-efficiency-trade-off" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity-efficiency-trade-off .

heiro:6000566 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Equity-Weighted Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Equal QALY Weights Apart From the Severity Modifier" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case gives an additional QALY the same weight whatever the characteristics of the person receiving it, except in specific circumstances. Decision-making modifiers can be applied through committee discussion or through QALY weighting, and the severity modifier weights QALYs by 1.2 or 1.7 according to absolute and proportional QALY shortfall. Net health benefits must be shown at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY with and without the weighting. Distributional analyses must not weight costs or benefits by social characteristics; where health inequality impacts are substantial, the committee can instead apply flexibility to the acceptable ICER range." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), table 4.1, table 6.1 and sections 4.12.7, 6.2.10 to 6.2.18, 6.2.38 and 6.3.2, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity-weighted-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity-weighted-analysis .

heiro:6000567 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Equity-Weighted Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/ICER_2023_2026_VAF_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Formal Equity Weights, With a Distribution Index for Deliberation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's 2023 value assessment framework notes that data to apply methods measuring effects on inequality across racial or socio-economic groups are lacking in the US, and that no other HTA agency has adopted such methods, including the equity weights used in distributional cost-effectiveness analysis, as a standard. Its reports instead include the Health Improvement Distribution Index, the prevalence of a condition in a subpopulation divided by its prevalence overall, as one input to deliberation. Severity and other modifiers are handled through deliberation rather than quantified weights, although ICER has said it will explore severity weighting methods used elsewhere." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023), sections 3.3 and 3.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity-weighted-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity-weighted-analysis .

heiro:6000568 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Equity-Weighted Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Equity Weights Not Applied in the Irish Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's national guidelines require additional QALYs to be treated as of equal value in the reference case, regardless of the characteristics of the population, so equity weights should not be applied to outcomes. The guidelines acknowledge proposals to build equity weights into QALY calculations to reflect societal concerns about severity and the ability to benefit, but cite significant methodological issues in deriving and applying them. Reports should instead highlight equity considerations, such as unmet needs of disadvantaged groups, and describe the technology's potential impact on them." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), section 2.17" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity-weighted-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity-weighted-analysis .

heiro:6000569 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Equity-Weighted Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Equity Weights in the Belgian Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "KCE's Belgian guidelines state that no equity weights should be applied to outcomes in the reference case, so all outcomes such as QALYs are weighted equally regardless of who receives them. The guidelines note that Belgium has no standardised way to weight outcomes for equity, since no societal equity weights have been determined, and that such weights might change with governments and policy priorities. To support appraisal, reports should show QALY gains disaggregated by target population, highlighting unmet needs of disadvantaged groups." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (first edition 8 May 2025, second edition 18 June 2025), Guideline 13 (section 5.13)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "equity-weighted-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:equity-weighted-analysis .

heiro:6000570 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Evidence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "All Evidence Types Considered, RCTs Preferred for Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE considers all types of evidence, including unpublished and non-UK data, registries, real-world evidence and other observational sources, and the preferred source depends on the use being considered. For relative treatment effects there is a strong preference for high-quality randomised controlled trials, while real-world evidence may be preferred for natural history, treatment patterns or patient experiences. Evidence should be relevant to the patient groups, comparators, perspective, outcomes and resource use in the scope, and where empirical evidence is absent, structured expert elicitation is preferred." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.2.1, 3.3.1, 3.3.2 and 3.3.21, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "evidence" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:evidence .

heiro:6000571 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Evidence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Certainty of Results Graded by Study Design and Bias" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG normally bases its recommendations only on studies with sufficient certainty of results, judged by internal validity, external validity and statistical precision. Results on an outcome carry high qualitative certainty when they come from a randomised study with low risk of bias, moderate certainty from a randomised study with high risk of bias, and low certainty from a non-randomised comparative study. For each patient-relevant outcome the evidence then supports a proof, an indication or a hint of added benefit or harm, or none of these." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, sections 1.3.4 and 3.1.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "evidence" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:evidence .

heiro:6000572 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Evidence-Based Decision Making"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Committee Recommendations Must Be Explained Against All the Evidence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's technology appraisal committee bases its recommendations on the evidence presented, including stakeholder statements and expert views, and considers advice from NICE on scientific and social value decisions. The manual states that the credibility of NICE guidance depends on transparency, so each decision must be explained with reference to all the available evidence, expert contributions and consultation comments. Cost effectiveness is weighed without a precise maximum acceptable ICER, and below £25,000 per QALY gained the decision is normally based on the cost-effectiveness estimate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section(s) 6.1.3, 6.1.7, 6.1.8, 6.3.1 and 6.3.4, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "evidence-based-decision-making" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:evidence-based-decision-making .

heiro:6000573 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Evidence-Based Decision Making"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240039872"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Decisions Informed by the Best Research Evidence and Wider Factors" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The WHO guide for evidence-informed decision-making says decisions should be informed by the best available research evidence together with context, public opinion, equity, feasibility of implementation, affordability, sustainability and acceptability to stakeholders. It describes the approach as systematic and transparent, applying structured and replicable methods to identify, appraise and use evidence. The guide treats tacit evidence, such as expert opinion, as complementary to research evidence rather than in competition with it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Evidence, policy, impact. WHO guide for evidence-informed decision-making (ISBN 978-92-4-003987-2), World Health Organization 2021, Glossary and chapter 1 sections What is evidence-informed decision-making? and What types of evidence are needed for evidence-informed decision-making?" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "evidence-based-decision-making" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:evidence-based-decision-making .

heiro:6000574 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Evidence Table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg20/chapter/reviewing-evidence"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Evidence Tables Built From Standard Extraction Templates" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's guidelines manual says evidence tables help identify similarities and differences between studies, and that data from each study are extracted to standard templates for inclusion in them. Content depends on the study type and review question, typically covering design, population, intervention, comparator, outcomes, key findings with confidence intervals and quality comments. Example tables are given, but NICE states that no fixed template suits all topics, asks for headings to be agreed at the protocol stage, and requires extra calculations to be marked 'calculated by the review team'." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Developing NICE guidelines: the manual (PMG20), published 31 October 2014, last updated 23 October 2025, sections 6.2 and 6.5 (Evidence tables) and appendix H (Appraisal checklists, evidence tables, GRADE and economic profiles)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "evidence-table" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:evidence-table .

heiro:6000575 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Evidence Table"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/3943ae6a-1bca-4afd-b05c-fd9a54a252d7_en?filename=hta_jca_mp_dossier-template_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Tabular Overview of Every Included Study in JCA Dossiers" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's dossier guidance for medicinal products requires an overview in tabular format of the study design and study population for every study included for any PICO. The tables must cover study type and design, dates and duration, the enrolled population with key eligibility criteria and locations, intervention and comparator characteristics, endpoints, any data cut-off, sample size and analysis methods. A supplementary table template collection sets the minimum information, and detailed study methods go in an appendix." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Coordination Group, Guidance on filling in the joint clinical assessment (JCA) dossier template, Medicinal products, V1.0, adopted 28 November 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 5.2 (Characteristics of included studies)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "evidence-table" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:evidence-table .

heiro:6000576 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Expanded Disability Status Scale"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta767/chapter/3-Committee-discussion"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "EDSS Health States in the Ponesimod Appraisal Model" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In the ponesimod appraisal the company used a Markov transition model with 20 health states, 10 EDSS states for relapsing-remitting MS, 9 for secondary progressive MS and death, with natural history from the British Columbia Multiple Sclerosis registry. Treatment effects from network meta-analyses adjusted progression through each EDSS state using 6-month confirmed disability accumulation. The committee noted that fatigue was not included explicitly in the model and was instead captured through measuring health-related quality of life by EDSS score." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal guidance TA767 (2022), Ponesimod for treating relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis, section 3 Committee discussion" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "expanded-disability-status-scale" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:expanded-disability-status-scale .

heiro:6000577 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Expected Net Benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Preferred Estimate From Probabilistic Analysis Unless the Model Is Linear" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that the committee's preferred cost-effectiveness estimate should be derived from a probabilistic analysis when possible, unless the model is linear, and that any use of deterministic results must be clearly justified. ICERs must be the ratio of expected additional total cost to expected additional QALYs. Scenario analyses should in general also be probabilistic, and enough simulations should be run to minimise Monte Carlo error, which can be judged by reviewing the variance of outputs such as net benefits as the number of simulations rises." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16, 4.7.11, 4.7.12 and 4.7.13, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "expected-net-benefit" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:expected-net-benefit .

heiro:6000578 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Expected Net Benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Base Case Results Built From Means Across Probabilistic Iterations" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Dutch guideline requires base case results, including the ICER and net monetary benefit, to be based on the probabilistic analysis, with the ICER calculated by dividing mean incremental costs by mean incremental effects across all iterations. The number of iterations must demonstrably give a stable result, and every scenario analysis must also be run probabilistically. Ranking by net monetary benefit can be used to identify the most cost-effective intervention, and the EVPI is defined as the expected cost of uncertainty from deciding on current evidence." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), sections 4.7.2.2, 4.7.2.3, 4.7.2.4 and 5.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "expected-net-benefit" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:expected-net-benefit .

heiro:6000579 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Expected Net Benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/RefCase_Sep2023_For-Publication_100124.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Deterministic Base Case With Probabilistic Expected Values Alongside" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's reference case asks for a deterministic base case analysis where appropriate for the model type, reporting incremental cost per QALY and per evLY gained, and it makes no reference to net benefit. In addition to deterministic sensitivity analyses, the model should derive expected values of costs and outcomes for each intervention through probabilistic sensitivity analysis, using sufficient sampling to reflect distributional uncertainty. These expected values sit within the uncertainty and sensitivity analyses, separate from the base case results." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, current as of 23 October 2025, sections Presentation of Results and Uncertainty and Sensitivity Analyses" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "expected-net-benefit" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:expected-net-benefit .

heiro:6000580 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Expected Value"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Probabilistic Means Preferred Unless the Model Is Linear" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE requires reported incremental cost-effectiveness ratios to be the ratio of expected additional total cost to expected additional QALYs. The committee's preferred cost-effectiveness estimate should come from a probabilistic analysis when possible unless the model is linear, and any use of deterministic results must be clearly justified. Distributions for mean parameter values should reflect the available evidence, and submissions should present the expected value of each cost component, expected total costs and expected QALYs for each option compared, alongside expected net health benefits." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16, 4.7.10, 4.7.12 and 4.10.6, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "expected-value" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:expected-value .

heiro:6000581 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Expected Value Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Decisions Rest on Expected Costs and Expected QALYs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE requires reported incremental cost-effectiveness ratios to be the ratio of expected additional total cost to expected additional QALYs, following standard decision rules for dominance and extended dominance, with expected net health benefits also presented. The committee's preferred estimate should come from a probabilistic analysis when possible unless the model is linear, and any use of deterministic results must be justified. Submissions should present the expected value of each cost component, expected total costs and expected QALYs for each option compared." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16, 4.7.12 and 4.10.6, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "expected-value-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:expected-value-analysis .

heiro:6000582 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Explanatory Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Trial Results Judged on Generalisability to Routine NHS Practice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE regards high-quality randomised controlled trials against relevant comparators as the most valid evidence of relative efficacy, but warns that results may not generalise to the population of interest because of the comparator, population, setting or treatment pathway used. Each trial is judged on internal validity and on external validity, meaning whether its results apply to wider patient groups and to routine clinical practice. When a randomised trial is not available or appropriate, the source and methods used to estimate relative effects must be justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.3.6 and 3.3.8, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "explanatory-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:explanatory-trial .

heiro:6000583 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Explanatory Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/regulatory-procedural-guideline/ich-guideline-e8-r1-general-considerations-clinical-studies_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Narrow Populations Maximise Sensitivity, Later Trials Should Be Representative" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The ICH E8(R1) guideline adopted by the EMA says a study population may be narrowly defined to reduce risk to participants or to maximise the sensitivity of the study for detecting an effect, or broadly defined to represent the diverse populations for whom the drug is intended. Studies early in development tend to use more homogeneous populations. Studies in later phases or after approval should involve participants who are representative of the diverse populations that will receive the intervention in clinical practice." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICH guideline E8 (R1) on general considerations for clinical studies, Step 5 (EMA/CHMP/ICH/544570/1998, adopted by CHMP 14 October 2021, in effect from 14 April 2022), section 5.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "explanatory-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:explanatory-trial .

heiro:6000584 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Extended Dominance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Extendedly Dominated Technologies Removed From Fully Incremental Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE requires economic evaluation results to be presented in a fully incremental analysis from which extendedly dominated technologies are removed. It defines a technology as extendedly dominated when a combination of 2 or more other technologies would be more cost effective. Standard decision rules for combining costs and QALYs should reflect extended dominance where it exists, and reported ICERs must be the ratio of expected additional total cost to expected additional QALYs. Expected net health benefits at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY should also be presented when appropriate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16 and 4.10.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "extended-dominance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:extended-dominance .

heiro:6000585 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Externality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/who-guide-for-standardization-of-economic-evaluations-of-immunization-programmes-2nd-ed"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Include Indirect Vaccination Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO defines externalities as costs or benefits of an individual's production or consumption decision that indirectly affect the well-being of others. Its guide counts indirect effects in unvaccinated or unsuccessfully vaccinated people, arising from reduced pathogen circulation, as part of total vaccine effectiveness, and states that ignoring effects such as herd immunity, serotype replacement or antimicrobial resistance is not best practice. Where vaccination directly reaches epidemiologically influential groups and negative externalities could exceed positive ones, its model-choice flow chart requires a dynamic transmission model." ;
    heiro:2000018 "WHO Guide for Standardization of Economic Evaluations of Immunization Programmes, 2nd Edition (WHO/IVB/19.10), October 2019, Glossary (Externalities), section 5.2 and section 6.3.2.1 (Figure 5)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "externality" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:externality .

heiro:6000586 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Externality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#case-for-change"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Externalities as a Market Failure Rationale for Intervention" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Green Book lists externalities among the market failures that economists often treat as an important rationale for government intervention, defining them as positive or negative impacts of market transactions on parties other than the buyer and seller. It gives pollution as a typical negative externality and education as a typical positive one. For valuation, it states that a good with negative externalities is priced too low from a social value perspective, leading to over-consumption compared with the optimal economic outcome." ;
    heiro:2000018 "The Green Book: UK Government Guidance on Appraisal (2026), HM Treasury, published 5 February 2026, paragraphs 4.6 and 8.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "externality" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:externality .

heiro:6000587 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of UK Review on Antimicrobial Resistance on Externality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000087 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://amr-review.org/sites/default/files/160518_Final%20paper_with%20cover.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "UK Review on Antimicrobial Resistance" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Antibiotic Resistance as a Negative Externality" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Review, commissioned by the UK government and chaired by Jim O'Neill, names externalities, imperfect information and an unwillingness to pay for public goods as the main market failures behind antimicrobial resistance. It places antibiotic consumption among goods with negative externalities: individuals take and may benefit from the drugs, but the resistance they contribute to affects all of society. It accepts some positive externality from curing infections that would otherwise spread, but judges the resistance externality more pronounced and weakly regulated, leading to overuse in patients and animals." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Tackling Drug-Resistant Infections Globally: Final Report and Recommendations, Review on Antimicrobial Resistance chaired by Jim O'Neill, May 2016, chapter 1, box 'An outline of the market failures affecting AMR' (page 16)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "externality" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:externality .

heiro:6000588 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Extrapolation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/nice-dsu"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Parametric Survival Extrapolation With Fit and Plausibility Checks (DSU TSD 14/21)" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Where trial data are censored, the base case should extrapolate survival using fitted parametric distributions (e.g. exponential, Weibull, Gompertz, log-logistic, log-normal, generalised gamma), selecting models by statistical goodness of fit and clinical/biological plausibility, and validating extrapolated hazards against background mortality and external data. TSD 21 cautions against over-relying on AIC/BIC beyond the observed follow-up." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Decision Support Unit Technical Support Documents 14 and 21 (Survival Analysis)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "extrapolation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:extrapolation .

heiro:6000589 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Extrapolation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-8-results-of-the-base-case-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Justified Extrapolation With Comprehensive Sensitivity Analyses" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Extrapolation of health outcomes and resource use over the time horizon should be justified and, where the main benefit is achieved by extrapolating beyond the observed data, examined through comprehensive sensitivity analyses around the extrapolation methods; heavily extrapolated results carry greater uncertainty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC, Section 3A.4/3A.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "extrapolation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:extrapolation .

heiro:6000590 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Extrapolation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Parametric Extrapolation to the Lifetime Horizon, Validated" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Because a lifetime horizon is generally required, survival typically must be extrapolated beyond the trial by fitting standard parametric models, with validation of the extrapolated output against external evidence and attention to treatment-effect waning and proportional-hazards assumptions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH (now CDA-AMC), Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition (2017); Extrapolating Clinical Evidence report" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "extrapolation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:extrapolation .

heiro:6000591 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Fee-for-Service"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/medicare/physician-fee-schedule/search/overview"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "The Medicare Physician Fee Schedule" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS uses the Medicare Physician Fee Schedule to determine payment for covered clinician services. The schedule assigns relative values to services and adjusts payment for geographic differences in practice costs, providing a large operational example of fee-for-service rate setting." ;
    heiro:2000018 "PFS Look-up Tool Overview" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "fee-for-service" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:fee-for-service .

heiro:6000592 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Fee-for-Service"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications-detail-redirect/analytical-guide-to-assess-a-mixed-provider-payment-system"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Fee-for-service within mixed provider payment systems" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO treats fee-for-service as one component of a wider provider payment system rather than an isolated method. Its assessment framework examines how payment methods interact, what incentives they create and whether the combined arrangement supports health-system and universal health coverage objectives." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Analytical Guide to Assess a Mixed Provider Payment System" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "fee-for-service" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:fee-for-service .

heiro:6000593 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development on Fee-for-Service"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000009 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://doi.org/10.1787/9789264258211-en"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Moving from volume towards better provider payment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The OECD identifies provider payment as an important policy lever and examines reforms intended to move beyond rewarding service volume alone. It emphasises that payment reform requires clear tariff setting, quality information, stakeholder involvement and evaluation rather than a simple replacement of one method with another." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Better Ways to Pay for Health Care" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "fee-for-service" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:fee-for-service .

heiro:6000594 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Focus Group"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/131230/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Focus Groups for Eliciting Patient Experience in Drug Development" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's patient-focused drug development guidance describes a focus group as a conversation with a group of participants led by a moderator, generally using a semi-structured discussion guide, that elicits a range of experiences and allows participant interaction. The number of groups depends on the complexity and sensitivity of the topic, sample diversity and planned subgroups, and groups of 5 to 10 participants are common. Focus groups can explore a broad topic before one-on-one interviews, which suit sensitive topics and detailed individual experiences." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Patient-Focused Drug Development, Methods to Identify What Is Important to Patients, guidance for industry, FDA staff and other stakeholders (Guidance 2), February 2022, sections III.A, III.A.2 and III.A.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "focus-group" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:focus-group .

heiro:6000595 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Focus Group"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Focus Groups with People Affected in IQWiG Assessments" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG lists individual interviews and focus groups among its verbal methods for involving the perspectives of people affected. For benefit assessments with longer processing times, discussions with people affected take place as individual interviews or focus groups of no more than 6 people, conducted by specially trained staff with open-ended questions, ideally before the report plan is developed. IQWiG also counts focus groups among the instruments of qualitative research used to understand the experiences of people affected." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, sections 8.1.4, 8.2.2.1 and 8.2.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "focus-group" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:focus-group .

heiro:6000596 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on GAD-7"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2018/06/nhs-talking-therapies-manual-v7.1-updated.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "GAD-7 Caseness Cut-Off of 8 for Recovery in NHS Talking Therapies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS England's manual for NHS Talking Therapies for anxiety and depression asks patients to complete measures of both depression and anxiety at every session. Table 9 sets a clinical cut-off of 8 and a reliable change index of 4 for the GAD-7, against 10 and 6 for the PHQ-9. Recovery, reliable improvement, reliable recovery and reliable deterioration are calculated from the pair of measures marked in bold for each presenting problem, and when the marked anxiety measure is missing the calculation uses the PHQ-9 and GAD-7 together." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS Talking Therapies for anxiety and depression manual, version 7, Table 9 (Clinical cut-offs and reliable change index), first published June 2018, updated March 2024" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "gad-7" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:gad-7 .

heiro:6000597 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Grounded Theory"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd9/chapter/appendix-4-conduct-of-qualitative-research-studies"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Constant Comparison in Grounded Theory Among Recognised Analytical Approaches" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's real-world evidence framework names constant comparison in a grounded theory approach as one of the common analytical approaches in qualitative health research, alongside thematic, content and framework analysis, and notes that each has strengths and limitations for particular research aims. It records that thematic analysis has been the primary approach in evidence submitted to NICE and centres its recommendations on that method. Study reports should give the reasons for stopping data collection, whether data saturation or another reason." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE real-world evidence framework (ECD9), appendix 4 (Conduct of qualitative research studies, added January 2025), introduction and reporting and assessment of qualitative studies; framework published 23 June 2022" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "grounded-theory" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:grounded-theory .

heiro:6000598 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Grounded Theory"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Grounded Theory Studies Included in Qualitative Evidence Syntheses" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods state that, when producing qualitative evidence syntheses for evidence reports, data from studies using different methodological approaches, such as grounded theory, ethnography and content analysis, must be included and summarised into synthesis findings. Aggregative synthesis methods such as content analysis, meta-summary or thematic synthesis are generally considered most suitable. Confidence in each synthesis finding is then assessed with the GRADE-CERQual tool." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English version published 1 July 2026), sections 10.4.3.1 and 10.4.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "grounded-theory" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:grounded-theory .

heiro:6000599 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on HAQ-DI"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta375/chapter/4-Evidence-and-interpretation"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "HAQ Progression and a HAQ to EQ-5D Mixture Model Underpin the Biological DMARD Appraisal" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In its multiple technology appraisal of biological DMARDs for rheumatoid arthritis, NICE accepted data from the Early Rheumatoid Arthritis Study to estimate underlying disease progression on conventional DMARDs, in place of the linear HAQ progression assumed in earlier appraisals. The Assessment Group simulated a pain score for each patient from HAQ and estimated EQ-5D values with a mixture model developed by Hernández Alava and colleagues on data from 16,011 patients in the National Data Bank for Rheumatic Diseases. The group noted that this assumes treatments affect pain in proportion to their effect on HAQ." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal guidance TA375, Adalimumab, etanercept, infliximab, certolizumab pegol, golimumab, tocilizumab and abatacept for rheumatoid arthritis not previously treated with DMARDs or after conventional DMARDs only have failed, section 4 Evidence and interpretation, published 26 January 2016" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "haq-di" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:haq-di .

heiro:6000600 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Hazard Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Assess Proportional Hazards Before Relying on Hazard Ratios" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE notes that studies with time-to-event outcomes often measure relative treatment effects as hazard ratios, which may be constant over time or change. The proportional hazards assumption should always be assessed, preferably with log-cumulative hazard plots, visual inspection of hazard plots or hazard ratios over time, and tests reported in the original trials. Hazard ratios may be pooled if proportional hazards hold within the trial and are clinically plausible during extrapolation; otherwise the alternative methods in DSU Technical Support Document 21 should be considered." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.6.22 to 4.6.25, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "hazard-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:hazard-ratio .

heiro:6000601 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Hazard Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/nice-dsu/tsds/survival-analysis"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Justify Proportional Hazards or Model Time-Dependent Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 14 advises that proportional hazards modelling should only be used if the assumption can be clearly justified with log-cumulative hazard plots, external information and clinical expert opinion, and that the source of any hazard ratio should be clearly stated. It notes that log-logistic and log normal models do not produce a single hazard ratio. TSD 21 adds that assuming a proportional treatment effect is restrictive, that flexible parametric models can relax it through time-dependent effects, and that plotting the implied hazard ratio shows what is assumed beyond follow-up." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 14: Survival analysis for economic evaluations alongside clinical trials, extrapolation with patient-level data (last updated March 2013), sections 2.9 and 5; NICE DSU Technical Support Document 21: Flexible methods for survival analysis (updated March 2022), section 3.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "hazard-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:hazard-ratio .

heiro:6000602 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Hazard Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Hazard Ratio Confidence Interval Sets the Extent of Added Benefit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For time-to-event outcomes in drug benefit assessments, IQWiG uses the two-sided 95% confidence interval of the hazard ratio, or of the pooled meta-analytic hazard ratio, to determine the extent of effect. The same thresholds apply as for the relative risk: for all-cause mortality, the upper confidence limit must fall below 0.85 for a major, 0.95 for a considerable and 1.00 for a minor extent. If a hazard ratio cannot be interpreted meaningfully, for example because of relevant violation of the proportional hazard assumption, a relative risk at a meaningful time point should be examined." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 3.3.3 (time to event) and Table 3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "hazard-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:hazard-ratio .

heiro:6000603 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Hazard Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/1f6b8a70-5ce0-404e-9066-120dc9a8df75_en?filename=hta_practical-guideline_direct-and-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pooling Hazard Ratios Requires Proportional Hazards in Every Comparison" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group states that a meta-analysis or network meta-analysis of hazard ratios requires the proportional hazards assumption to hold for all pairwise comparisons, assessed for every included study, preferably using individual or reconstructed patient data. If the assumption is implausible for any comparison, hazard ratios should not be meta-analysed, and restricted mean survival time or flexible survival models are the preferred alternatives. Submissions should report log-cumulative hazard plots, Schoenfeld residual plots and any statistical tests of the assumption." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Coordination Group, Practical Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted 8 March 2024, sections 4.3.1 to 4.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "hazard-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:hazard-ratio .

heiro:6000604 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Hazard Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/appendix-1-guideline-evaluation-anticancer-medicinal-products-man-methodological-consideration-using-progression-free-survival-or-disease-free-survival-confirmatory-trials_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Hazard Ratio as One Measure of Effect Size in PFS and DFS Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In confirmatory oncology trials using progression-free or disease-free survival, the EMA expects the size of effect to be quantified by plotting estimated survivor functions, estimating the hazard ratio, estimating the median and other percentiles, and estimating the percentage of patients event free at particular time points. It cautions against over-reliance on differences in medians. Sensitivity analyses should be planned for important assumptions, including proportional hazards." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EMA, Appendix 1 to the guideline on the evaluation of anticancer medicinal products in man: methodological consideration for using progression-free survival (PFS) or disease-free survival (DFS) in confirmatory trials, EMA/CHMP/27994/2008/Rev.1, adopted 13 December 2012, in effect from 1 July 2013, sections Primary and sensitivity analyses, and Size of effect" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "hazard-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:hazard-ratio .

heiro:6000605 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Health Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Statutory Duty to Weigh the Benefits and Costs of NHS Services" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE committees make recommendations on the clinical effectiveness and value for money of technologies for NHS use, and will not recommend technologies that are not considered a good use of NHS resources. Under the Health and Social Care Act 2012, NICE must have regard to the broad balance between the benefits and costs of health services or social care in England, alongside the degree of need and the desirability of promoting innovation. Because NICE considers the overall resources available to the NHS, decisions must account for the opportunity cost of programmes that a new technology may displace." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.1.1, 6.1.2 and 6.2.25, last updated 31 March 2026; Health and Social Care Act 2012, section 233(1)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-economics" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-economics .

heiro:6000606 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Health Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://apps.who.int/gb/ebwha/pdf_files/WHA67/A67_R23-en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Intervention and Technology Assessment for Universal Coverage" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The World Health Assembly treats the efficient use of resources as a factor in the sustainability of health systems moving towards universal health coverage, citing an estimate from The world health report 2010 that as much as 40% of health spending is wasted. Its 2014 resolution urges Member States to consider national systems of health intervention and technology assessment, covering cost-effectiveness and efficiency among other dimensions, to inform priority-setting, procurement, benefit packages and formularies. It asks the Director-General to integrate these methods into WHO work on health financing and universal health coverage." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Assembly resolution WHA67.23, Health intervention and technology assessment in support of universal health coverage, adopted 24 May 2014, preamble and operative paragraphs 1(1) and 2(3)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-economics" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-economics .

heiro:6000607 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Health Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/health-topics/health-equity"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Everyone Able to Attain Their Full Potential for Health" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO describes health equity as achieved when everyone can attain their full potential for health and well-being, and equity as the absence of unfair, avoidable or remediable differences among groups defined socially, economically, demographically, geographically or by other dimensions such as sex, gender, ethnicity, disability or sexual orientation. It states that health and health equity are determined by the conditions in which people are born, grow, live, work, play and age, and that monitoring health inequality, with data disaggregated by age, sex, education, income, disability and other factors, must be central to national health information systems." ;
    heiro:2000018 "WHO health topics page: Health equity, Overview (no publication date shown on the page)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-equity .

heiro:6000608 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Health Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Equal QALY Weights With Flexibility for Substantial Inequality Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In NICE's reference case all QALYs carry equal weight. If robust evidence shows that a technology substantially affects health inequalities, the committee considers this when judging whether it is an effective use of NHS resources, and can apply flexibility to the range normally considered cost effective, taking account of displacement and opportunity cost and giving stakeholders a rationale. Aspects relating to health inequalities are among the factors specifically considered above a most plausible ICER of £25,000 per QALY gained, or £100,000 per QALY gained for highly specialised technologies." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.2.10, 6.2.35, 6.2.38 and 6.3.5, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-equity .

heiro:6000609 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Health Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/about/equality/equality-hub/national-healthcare-inequalities-improvement-programme/core20plus5/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Statutory Duty to Reduce Inequalities and the Core20PLUS5 Approach" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under section 13G of the National Health Service Act 2006, NHS England must have regard to the need to reduce inequalities between persons in their ability to access health services and between patients in the outcomes achieved for them. Its Core20PLUS5 approach targets the most deprived 20% of the national population by the Index of Multiple Deprivation, plus locally identified groups such as ethnic minority communities and inclusion health groups, across five clinical areas: maternity, severe mental illness, chronic respiratory disease, early cancer diagnosis, and hypertension and lipid management." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Health Service Act 2006, section 13G (Duty as to reducing inequalities), as amended by the Health and Care Act 2022; NHS England, Core20PLUS5 (adults): an approach to reducing healthcare inequalities (web page, accessed 2 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-equity .

heiro:6000610 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Health Inequality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/df0f410e-7e5d-4081-9418-bb41f93d3e4e/content"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Measured Inequality as an Indirect Gauge of Inequity" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's handbook on health inequality monitoring treats health inequity, the unjust differences in health between social groups, as a normative concept that cannot be precisely measured, and health inequality, the observable differences between subgroups within a population, as something that can be measured and monitored as an indirect means of evaluating inequity. It advises that reports present disaggregated subgroup means alongside summary measures, cover both absolute and relative inequality, and show national averages with the inequality data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Handbook on health inequality monitoring: with a special focus on low- and middle-income countries, 2013, ISBN 978 92 4 154863 2, executive summary (page xvi), section 1.3 (page 6) and section 4" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-inequality" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-inequality .

heiro:6000611 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Health Inequality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/resources/support-document-health-inequalities-15313210669/chapter/distributional-cost-effectiveness-analysis-methods"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Distributional Analysis by Deprivation Quintile, Without Aversion Weights" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE uses distributional cost-effectiveness analysis to show how a technology's costs and benefits vary across social groups and its effect on the health inequality gap, but only as supporting evidence and only where there is clear evidence of a significant burden of health inequalities in the eligible population. Groups are stratified by Index of Multiple Deprivation, and outputs cover health benefits, health opportunity costs and net health benefit for each quintile, with descriptive metrics such as gaps and ratios between the top and bottom of the distribution or simple regressions. Health inequality aversion weights are not applied." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.12.1 to 4.12.3 and 4.12.7, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE support document: Health inequalities, chapter Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis methods, last updated 13 May 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-inequality" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-inequality .

heiro:6000612 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Health Inequality"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/about/equality/equality-hub/national-healthcare-inequalities-improvement-programme/what-are-healthcare-inequalities/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Inequalities Defined as Unfair and Avoidable Differences" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS England defines health inequalities as unfair and avoidable differences in health across the population and between different groups within society, including how long people are likely to live, the health conditions they may experience and the care available to them. It links them to the wider determinants of health and identifies people living in areas of high deprivation, Black, Asian and minority ethnic communities and inclusion health groups as most at risk. In this usage the term carries a judgement of unfairness, unlike the descriptive sense used in inequality measurement." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England, National Healthcare Inequalities Improvement Programme, What are healthcare inequalities? (web page, accessed 2 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-inequality" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-inequality .

heiro:6000613 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Health Inequity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/4f9b5741-608b-468b-bc24-812eb5618785/content"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Systematic Differences Avoidable by Reasonable Action Are Unjust" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The WHO Commission on Social Determinants of Health labels as health inequity the systematic differences in health that are judged to be avoidable by reasonable action, which it calls unfair in its executive summary and unjust in the main report, and treats reducing them as a matter of social justice. It recommends that national governments and international organisations, supported by WHO, set up health equity surveillance systems for routine monitoring of health inequity and the social determinants of health, and evaluate the health equity impact of policy and action." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Commission on Social Determinants of Health, Closing the gap in a generation: health equity through action on the social determinants of health, final report, World Health Organization, 2008, executive summary and chapter 1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-inequity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-inequity .

heiro:6000614 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Health Inequity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/resources/support-document-health-inequalities-15313210669/chapter/evidence-on-health-inequalities"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Social Characteristics and Structural Barriers Frame Inequality Evidence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's support document names socioeconomic status, level of deprivation and ethnicity as social characteristics related to health inequalities, and its preferred analysis stratifies by the Index of Multiple Deprivation, with other stratification allowed where inequalities concentrate in groups such as ethnic or inclusion health groups and there is an evidence-based rationale. Where robust evidence shows that structural or social barriers to accessing care or taking part in research cause uncertainty or bias in the inequality evidence, committees may accept a higher level of uncertainty. This consideration is separate from NICE's legal obligations under the Equality Act 2010." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.2.36 and 6.2.37, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE support document: Health inequalities, chapters Evidence on health inequalities and Distributional cost-effectiveness analysis methods, last updated 13 May 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-inequity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-inequity .

heiro:6000615 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Health Insurance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564021"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Prepayment and pooling support financial protection" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO treats health financing as central to ensuring that people can obtain needed services without financial hardship. Its approach emphasises prepayment, pooling and equitable coverage rather than relying heavily on direct payment when care is needed, while recognising that insurance arrangements must also use available resources efficiently and provide effective access to services." ;
    heiro:2000018 "The World Health Report 2010: Health Systems Financing: The Path to Universal Coverage" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-insurance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-insurance .

heiro:6000616 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Department of Health and Social Care on Health Needs Assessment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000029 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6304e6fdd3bf7f3664b65b35/Statutory-Guidance-on-Joint-Strategic-Needs-Assessments-and-Joint-Health-and-Wellbeing-Strategies-March-2013.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Department of Health and Social Care" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Joint Strategic Needs Assessment as the Statutory Local Assessment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Statutory guidance for England defines joint strategic needs assessments as assessments of the current and future health and social care needs of the local community, produced by health and wellbeing boards. Their purpose is to improve health and wellbeing and reduce inequalities through a continuous process of strategic assessment and planning. Boards should cover the whole population, give mental health equal priority with physical health, use quantitative and qualitative evidence and consider wider factors such as housing and air quality, with no mandatory template or data set." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Department of Health, Statutory Guidance on Joint Strategic Needs Assessments and Joint Health and Wellbeing Strategies, March 2013, with note dated 24 August 2022 on the Health and Care Act 2022, sections 2 and 3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-needs-assessment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-needs-assessment .

heiro:6000617 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Health Spending as GDP Share"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/379750"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Current Health Expenditure as a Share of GDP in Global Spending Reports" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's annual global spending report measures health spending as current health expenditure reported by Member States under the System of Health Accounts 2011, excluding capital expenditure, and compiled in the Global Health Expenditure Database with a two-year lag. Global spending fell in 2022 to US$ 9.8 trillion, or 9.9% of global GDP, while average health spending across countries was 6.7% of GDP. WHO distinguishes the two measures: the average is taken across countries, whereas the global share divides total health spending by total GDP." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Global spending on health: emerging from the pandemic (ISBN 9789240104495, December 2024), key messages and chapter 1, including notes 1 and 9" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-spending-as-gdp-share" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-spending-as-gdp-share .

heiro:6000618 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Health System Financing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/337938"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Nineteen Desirable Attributes Across the Health Financing Functions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO analyses health financing through the functions of revenue raising, pooling and purchasing, with benefit design as a fourth policy area, whatever label a system carries, such as tax-based or health insurance. Its Health Financing Progress Matrix sets nineteen desirable attributes as benchmarks for progress towards universal health coverage, including funding that is predominantly public or compulsory, pooling that enhances redistribution of prepaid funds, purchasing that reflects population health needs and keeps budgetary control, and benefit changes preceded by cost-effectiveness and budget impact assessments." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Assessing country health financing systems: the health financing progress matrix, Health Financing Guidance No. 8 (ISBN 9789240017405, December 2020), sections 2.1 and 2.2 and table 1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-system-financing" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-system-financing .

heiro:6000619 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of INAHTA on Health Technology Assessment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000088 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://htaglossary.net/health-technology-assessment"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "INAHTA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "International Definition of Health Technology Assessment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "INAHTA co-led, with HTAi, the international joint task group that agreed the 2020 definition of HTA as a multidisciplinary process using explicit methods to determine the value of a health technology at different points in its lifecycle, to inform decisions that promote an equitable, efficient and high-quality health system. The accompanying notes describe a formal, systematic and transparent process, list value dimensions from clinical effectiveness and safety to costs, ethical, social, legal, organisational and environmental aspects, and apply HTA from pre-market stages through to disinvestment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Glossary (INAHTA, HTAi and partners), entry 'health technology assessment', definition and notes 1 to 4; O'Rourke B, Oortwijn W, Schuller T. The new definition of health technology assessment: a milestone in international collaboration. Int J Technol Assess Health Care 2020;36(3):187-190, Box 1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-technology-assessment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-technology-assessment .

heiro:6000620 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Health Technology Assessment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240110878"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Technology Assessment of Medical Devices, Second Edition" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's 2025 guidance adopts the 2020 international definition of HTA and places HTA between regulatory approval for market access and the management of a technology across its lifetime. Regulators focus on quality, safety and performance, whereas HTA must also consider effectiveness, value for money, equity, acceptability to patients and providers, organisational impact and ethical issues. The guidance is aimed mainly at policy-makers in low- and middle-income countries and applies HTA from pre-market approval through procurement to reassessment and disinvestment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization. Health technology assessment of medical devices, second edition (WHO medical device technical series), published 30 May 2025, ISBN 9789240110878; Glossary, Executive summary and section 2.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-technology-assessment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-technology-assessment .

heiro:6000621 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of National Institute for Health and Care Excellence on Health Technology Assessment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Technology Appraisal and Highly Specialised Technologies Evaluation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE evaluates new and existing medicines and HealthTech, including devices, diagnostics and digital technologies, using clinical utility and cost-effectiveness analysis. A scope defines the population, comparators, care pathway and outcomes, and an independent committee makes recommendations to NICE. Recommendation types range from recommended, recommended in specific circumstances ('optimised') and, for medicines, recommended with managed access, to recommended only in research or not recommended. When NICE recommends funding, the NHS must normally make the technology available within 3 months." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections Introduction to health technology evaluation, 2.1.1, 6.1.1 and 6.4.1 to 6.4.16 (table 6.3), last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-technology-assessment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-technology-assessment .

heiro:6000622 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of European Commission / HTACG on Health Technology Assessment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016,
        heiro:5000017 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/health-technology-assessment/implementation-regulation-health-technology-assessment/joint-clinical-assessments_en"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "European Commission / HTACG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Joint Clinical Assessments Under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Regulation (EU) 2021/2282 applies from 12 January 2025, when new cancer medicines and advanced therapy medicinal products became subject to EU joint clinical assessments; orphan medicines follow from 13 January 2028 and all new centrally authorised medicines from 13 January 2030, with selected high-risk medical devices also covered. The assessments address clinical domains only, without value judgements or reimbursement conclusions, which remain with Member States. National authorities must give due consideration to published reports and may add evidence such as cost-effectiveness." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Commission, Implementation of the Regulation on health technology assessment and Joint Clinical Assessments web pages (accessed 29 September 2026); Joint Clinical Assessment for Medicinal Products factsheet (January 2025); Implementing the EU Health Technology Assessment Regulation factsheet (2023); Regulation (EU) 2021/2282" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "health-technology-assessment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:health-technology-assessment .

heiro:6000623 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Historical Control"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/ich-e-10-choice-control-group-clinical-trials-step-5_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "External and Historical Controls Only for Highly Predictable Diseases" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA applies ICH E10 as its scientific guideline on choosing a control group. It treats a historical control, a well-documented group of patients observed at an earlier time, as one form of external control, and warns that the lack of randomisation and blinding makes substantial bias inherent and impossible to quantify. An externally controlled trial should generally be considered only when belief in the superiority of the test therapy is very strong and the disease has a well-documented, highly predictable course. The control group, and any matching or adjustment, should be chosen before comparative analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICH E10 Choice of control group in clinical trials, Note for guidance CPMP/ICH/364/96, Step 5, effective January 2001, sections 1.3.5, 2.5.1, 2.5.2 and 2.5.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "historical-control" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:historical-control .

heiro:6000624 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Historical Control"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/164960/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Historical Controls as a Subset of External Controls in Drug Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's draft guidance on externally controlled trials describes a historical control as a group of people, treated or untreated, from an earlier time, and treats it as a subset of external controls. Suitability needs case by case assessment, and historical information can potentially serve as the control when the natural history of a disease is well defined and the disease is known not to improve without intervention. Sponsors should finalise the protocol, including the choice of external control arm and analytic approach, before the trial begins. The guidance remains a draft and is not for implementation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Considerations for the Design and Conduct of Externally Controlled Trials for Drug and Biological Products, draft guidance for industry, February 2023, docket FDA-2022-D-2983, sections I, II and III.A.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "historical-control" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:historical-control .

heiro:6000625 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Historical Control"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd9/chapter/methods-for-real-world-studies-of-comparative-effects"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "External Controls from Previous Trials or Real-World Data" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's real-world evidence framework recognises external controls formed from real-world data or from data from previous clinical trials. Earlier trials may offer greater similarity in inclusion criteria, follow up and outcome determination, but often provide only aggregate data. Differences in patients, settings, care, data collection and time periods can limit comparability, so developers should select and curate data to minimise these differences, design studies to emulate a target trial and consider bias analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE real-world evidence framework (ECD9), published 23 June 2022, updated September 2025, chapter Methods for real-world studies of comparative effects, sections Overview, Cohort studies, The target trial approach and Assessing robustness of studies" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "historical-control" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:historical-control .

heiro:6000626 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Historical Control"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Historically Controlled Studies Give Very Low Certainty of Results" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods grade a result from a non-randomised comparative study as having low qualitative certainty in benefit assessments. In assessments of potential, where lower evidence requirements apply, historically controlled studies are listed among designs giving very low certainty, and one-arm studies compared with literature controls give minimum certainty. Benefit can also be inferred from well-documented case series when a dramatic effect reverses a certainly or almost certainly predictable disease course that no available treatment can influence." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, sections 3.1.4, 3.2.2 and 3.8.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "historical-control" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:historical-control .

heiro:6000627 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Historical Control"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/4ec8288e-6d15-49c5-a490-d8ad7748578f_en?filename=hta_methodological-guideline_direct-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Historical Controls Highly Problematic in Joint Clinical Assessment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Coordination Group's guideline on direct and indirect comparisons notes that single-arm evidence is sometimes compared with historical controls or an unrelated contemporaneous study. The comparison then depends on every other aspect that differs between the groups and studies, so exchangeability probably does not hold, and no gold-standard method exists. Use of such evidence in joint clinical assessment is described as highly problematic, with a high risk of not providing valid and reliable relative effect estimates, and naive comparisons between arms of different studies should not be performed." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Methodological Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis, Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted by the HTA Coordination Group on 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, Part II sections 1, 2 and 6.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "historical-control" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:historical-control .

heiro:6000628 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Department of Health and Social Care on Horizontal Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000029 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-nhs-constitution-for-england/the-nhs-constitution-for-england#principles-that-guide-the-nhs"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Department of Health and Social Care" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Access Based on Clinical Need, Not Ability to Pay" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NHS Constitution for England sets as a guiding principle that access to NHS services is based on clinical need, not an individual's ability to pay, and that services are free of charge except in limited circumstances sanctioned by Parliament. It also states that the NHS provides a comprehensive service available to all, irrespective of gender, race, disability, age, sexual orientation, religion, belief, gender reassignment, pregnancy and maternity or marital or civil partnership status." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Department of Health and Social Care, The NHS Constitution for England, updated 17 August 2023, Principles that guide the NHS, principles 1 and 2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "horizontal-equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:horizontal-equity .

heiro:6000629 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Horizontal Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2006/41/section/13G"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Statutory Duty to Reduce Inequalities in Access to Services" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS England must, in exercising its functions, have regard to the need to reduce inequalities between persons with respect to their ability to access health services. A second limb requires it to have regard to the need to reduce inequalities between patients in the outcomes achieved for them by the provision of health services." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Health Service Act 2006, section 13G (Duty as to reducing inequalities), inserted by the Health and Social Care Act 2012 and amended by the Health and Care Act 2022, sections 6 and 186(6), with effect from 1 July 2022" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "horizontal-equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:horizontal-equity .

heiro:6000630 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Hyperbolic Discounting"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Hyperbolic Discounting Permitted as a Secondary Analysis Beyond 30 Years" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's national guidelines require costs and outcomes to be discounted at a standard rate of 4.0% a year in the reference case, reflecting society's rate of time preference, with the rate varied between 0% and 10% in sensitivity analysis. For economic evaluations with a time horizon extending beyond 30 years, hyperbolic discounting is permitted as a secondary analysis. Following a review by the Department of Public Expenditure, NDP Delivery and Reform, it should be applied using the published discount factors in the Public Spending Code." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), section 2.14" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "hyperbolic-discounting" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:hyperbolic-discounting .

heiro:6000631 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on ICECAP-A"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg20/chapter/incorporating-economic-evaluation"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Capability Measures Such as ICECAP for Interventions With a Social Care Focus, Table Under Revision" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NICE guideline manual sets a reference case that varies with the decision problem. In table 7.1, the column for interventions with a social care focus names capability or social care-related quality of life measures, such as ICECAP or ASCOT, when an intervention results in both health and capability or social care outcomes. NICE will consider social care QALYs if they are validated. The manual notes that an interim methods statement takes precedence and that section 7.4, which holds table 7.1, will be updated, so this position is under revision. NICE has not adopted a threshold for capability gains." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Developing NICE guidelines: the manual (PMG20), chapter 7 Incorporating economic evaluation, section 7.4 and table 7.1, last updated 23 October 2025 (sections 7.4, 7.7 and 7.9 under update)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "icecap-a" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:icecap-a .

heiro:6000632 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on ICECAP-O"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg20/chapter/incorporating-economic-evaluation"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ICECAP Listed for Non-Health Effects of Social Care Focused Interventions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NICE guidelines manual sets out a reference case for economic evaluation in guideline development, with separate columns for different kinds of intervention. As the page stood in October 2026, the column for interventions with a social care focus lists capability or social care-related quality of life measures, such as ICECAP or ASCOT, as the measure of non-health effects when an intervention results in both health and capability or social care outcomes. The manual names the ICECAP family in general and does not single out the ICECAP-O version for older people." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Developing NICE guidelines: the manual (PMG20), chapter 7 Incorporating economic evaluation, section 7.4, table 7.1 (column for interventions with a social care focus), page checked 17 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "icecap-o" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:icecap-o .

heiro:6000633 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Expected Costs Over Expected QALYs, Judged as the Most Plausible ICER" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE requires the ICER to be the ratio of expected additional total cost to expected additional QALYs, presented through incremental cost-utility analysis that reflects dominance and extended dominance, with expected net health benefits at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY alongside it. Committees then work from the most plausible ICER. Below £25,000 per QALY, a decision not to recommend must state the committee's view on the plausibility of the modelling inputs or the certainty around the ICER, and as the ICER rises from £25,000 to £35,000 committees refer explicitly to uncertainty, uncaptured benefits and health inequalities." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16 and 6.3.4 to 6.3.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "icer" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:icer .

heiro:6000634 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/richtlijn-voor-het-uitvoeren-van-economische-evaluaties-in-de-gezondheidszorg/Richtlijn+voor+het+uitvoeren+van+economische+evaluaties+in+de+gezondheidszorg+%28versie+2024%29.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ICER From Mean Probabilistic Costs and Effects, Reported With NMB" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland requires the base-case ICER, expressed as costs per QALY gained, to be based on the probabilistic analysis and calculated by dividing the mean incremental costs by the mean incremental effects across all iterations. The net monetary benefit must be reported alongside the ICER, using a reference value that can be set from the burden of disease of the indication. Deterministic sensitivity analyses must show the minimum and maximum discounted ICER together with the incremental costs and effects behind them." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Richtlijn voor het uitvoeren van economische evaluaties in de gezondheidszorg (versie 2024, published 16 January 2024), sections 4.7.2.2, 5.3.1 and 5.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "icer" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:icer .

heiro:6000635 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Institute for Clinical and Economic Review on Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost per QALY and per evLY Gained, Benchmarked at $100,000 to $150,000" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Institute for Clinical and Economic Review makes incremental cost per evLY gained and incremental cost per QALY gained the primary outcomes of its reference case, with discounted ratios calculated for each intervention-versus-comparator pair and an explanation given when the two differ markedly. Threshold analyses find the prices that reach 50,000,50,000, 50,000,100,000, 150,000and150,000 and 150,000and200,000 per QALY and per evLY gained. The Health Benefit Price Benchmark is reported at the standard range of 100,000to100,000 to 100,000to150,000 per QALY and per evLY gained." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale (current as of 23 October 2025), Outcomes, Presentation of Results, and Uncertainty and Sensitivity Analyses" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "icer" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:icer .

heiro:6000636 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-8-results-of-the-base-case-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Stepped Derivation of the Base-Case ICER" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC asks submissions to present the base-case incremental cost, incremental effectiveness and ICER, with the ICER calculated as the incremental costs divided by the incremental health outcomes. The ICER is built up in steps from the trial data and recalculated after each transformation, such as surrogate to clinical outcome, translation to the Australian setting, extrapolation and conversion to QALYs, so that the steps with the largest effect on the ICER are visible. An ICER based on an outcome other than life-years or QALYs gained should be compared with earlier PBAC decisions using the same outcome." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 3A.8.2 and 3A.8.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "icer" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:icer .

heiro:6000637 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Incremental Cost-Effectiveness Ratio (ICER)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ICER Against the Appropriate Comparator Therapy as the Main Result" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In health economic evaluations of drugs, IQWiG treats the ICER, the difference in costs divided by the difference in the benefit assessment outcome or in QALYs, as the main result. The base-case ICER compares the drug with the appropriate comparator therapy, and the effect of varying the drug price is examined first. IQWiG applies no universal threshold, so it draws no dichotomous conclusion on cost-effectiveness. When more than two treatments are compared outside the §35b SGB V procedure, the ratios can be plotted as an efficiency frontier, which is no longer extended to derive a price recommendation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 1.4, 4.2 (Table 5), 4.10 and 4.15" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "icer" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:icer .

heiro:6000638 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Incremental Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Incremental cost in NICE economic evaluation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE expects incremental costs to be estimated consistently with the applicable reference-case perspective, comparator, time horizon and discounting requirements." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Health Technology Evaluations: The Manual (PMG36)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "incremental-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:incremental-cost .

heiro:6000639 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Canada’s Drug Agency on Incremental Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Canada’s Drug Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Incremental cost in the Canadian reference case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Canada’s Drug Agency guidance requires transparent comparative costing under its reference case and presentation alongside incremental outcomes and uncertainty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "incremental-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:incremental-cost .

heiro:6000640 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Incremental Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "PBAC incremental economic evaluation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC describes economic evaluation as a comparison of incremental costs and outcomes between a proposed medicine and its main comparator. Component costs and cost offsets should be transparent." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC — Section 3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "incremental-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:incremental-cost .

heiro:6000641 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CHEERS 2022 on Incremental Cost"@en ;
    obo:IAO_0000116 "The Institution field names \"CHEERS 2022\", a reporting statement and not a body, so the record relates to no body. For the curator to review."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2021-067975"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CHEERS 2022" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reporting incremental costs transparently" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CHEERS 2022 supports transparent reporting of valuation methods and disaggregated and aggregated costs and outcomes. It is reporting guidance rather than a jurisdiction-specific reference case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Consolidated Health Economic Evaluation Reporting Standards 2022" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "incremental-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:incremental-cost .

heiro:6000642 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Incremental Cost-Utility Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Ratio of Expected Costs to Expected QALYs, Judged at £25,000 to £35,000" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE asks for results through incremental cost-utility analysis, with each incremental cost-effectiveness ratio calculated as expected additional total cost divided by expected additional QALYs, after removing dominated and extendedly dominated options. Below a most plausible ratio of £25,000 per QALY gained, a recommendation normally rests on the cost-effectiveness estimate. Between £25,000 and £35,000, committees refer explicitly to uncertainty, uncaptured benefits and health inequalities, and above £35,000 they need an increasingly stronger case. Expected net health benefits should be presented alongside the ratio." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16, 4.10.8 and 6.3.4 to 6.3.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "incremental-cost-utility-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:incremental-cost-utility-ratio .

heiro:6000643 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Incremental Cost-Utility Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-1-overview-and-rationale-of-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost-Utility Analysis With QALYs Preferred" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC's guidelines prefer a cost-utility analysis, with outcomes measured in QALYs, over a cost-effectiveness analysis, particularly where life-years are gained, where quality but not quantity of life improves, or where direct randomised trials report a multiattribute utility instrument. Where another outcome is chosen, sponsors must justify why it is not translated into QALYs. The base case must present the incremental cost, incremental effectiveness and the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio, calculated as incremental costs divided by incremental health outcomes, with a stepped derivation from the trial data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 3A.1.2, 3A.8.2 and 3A.8.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "incremental-cost-utility-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:incremental-cost-utility-ratio .

heiro:6000644 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Pharmaceutical Management Agency (PHARMAC) on Incremental Cost-Utility Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000089 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.pharmac.govt.nz/assets/pfpa-2-2.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Pharmaceutical Management Agency (PHARMAC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Utility-Cost Ratio Preferred, With ICURs Reported Alongside" ;
    heiro:2000017 "New Zealand's PHARMAC prefers to report cost-utility results as incremental utility-cost ratios, the incremental QALYs gained per NZD 1 million of net expenditure, because they reflect opportunity cost within a fixed budget. Incremental cost-utility ratios, the incremental cost per QALY gained, are described as the long-established international unit and in effect the inverse of the utility-cost ratio, and should be reported alongside it. QALYs in these ratios should not be weighted for health need or disease severity, to keep the ratios as value free as possible." ;
    heiro:2000018 "PHARMAC, Prescription for Pharmacoeconomic Analysis: Methods for cost-utility analysis, version 2.2 (2015; web edition last updated 15 May 2020), sections 6.1.3, 9 and 9.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "incremental-cost-utility-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:incremental-cost-utility-ratio .

heiro:6000645 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Incremental Cost-Utility Ratio"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/ICER_2023_2026_VAF_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost per QALY Reported Alongside Cost per evLY" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER uses the equal value life year as its usual measure of health gain, but for benchmarking with US and international academic standards and other HTA work it also presents cost per QALY gained as a primary measure of cost-effectiveness. Results are given at USD 50,000, 100,000, 150,000 and 200,000 per QALY or evLY gained, and its health benefit price benchmark is the price that would achieve USD 100,000 to 150,000 per QALY or evLY gained." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023), sections 3.1 and 3.9 and Glossary" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "incremental-cost-utility-ratio" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:incremental-cost-utility-ratio .

heiro:6000646 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Incremental Net Monetary Benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Net Monetary Benefit Reported With the ICER at a Burden-Based Reference Value" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland requires base-case results to report absolute and incremental costs and effects with confidence intervals, together with the ICER in costs per QALY gained and the net monetary benefit, all based on the probabilistic analysis. Calculating net monetary benefit needs a reference value, a cost-effectiveness threshold that can be based on the burden of disease for the indication. When more than two interventions are compared, a fully incremental analysis is required, and ranking on net monetary benefit can also identify the most cost-effective intervention." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), paragraph 5.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "incremental-net-monetary-benefit" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:incremental-net-monetary-benefit .

heiro:6000647 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Incremental Net Monetary Benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Net Monetary Benefit Where the ICER Is Negative" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's national guidelines note that when an intervention is less costly and more effective than its comparator, or more costly and less effective, the ICER is negative and hard to interpret consistently. In these cases analysts should identify the quadrant of the cost-effectiveness plane in which the results fall and consider them as net monetary benefit, calculated at a specific willingness-to-pay threshold. Because Ireland has no stated threshold, results presented as net monetary benefit should describe clearly the threshold values used." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), section 2.19.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "incremental-net-monetary-benefit" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:incremental-net-monetary-benefit .

heiro:6000648 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Indirect Treatment Comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adjusted Indirect Comparisons That Preserve Randomisation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Where technologies have not been compared within a single randomised trial, NICE expects the head-to-head trials to be presented with a network meta-analysis if appropriate, a term that includes adjusted indirect comparisons through a common comparator, and the committee weighs the extra uncertainty of indirect evidence. Trial randomisation must be preserved: comparing single arms from different trials is not acceptable, and such data are treated as observational. Population adjustment needs individual patient data from at least one trial, and inconsistency between direct and indirect evidence must be reported and explored." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.4.11 to 3.4.15 and 3.4.20, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "indirect-treatment-comparison" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:indirect-treatment-comparison .

heiro:6000649 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Indirect Treatment Comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Only Adjusted Indirect Comparisons via Adequate Common Comparators" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG regards indirect comparisons as resting on stronger assumptions than direct comparisons, so in benefit assessments they generally give only hints or indications rather than proofs and need adequate justification, although health economic evaluations of several interventions often require them. Only adjusted indirect comparisons via adequate common comparators, such as the Bucher method or network meta-analysis, are accepted; naive comparisons of single arms and modelling with strong assumptions about unknown effects are rejected. If serious doubts exist about similarity, homogeneity or consistency, indirect comparisons should not be used." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 10.3.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "indirect-treatment-comparison" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:indirect-treatment-comparison .

heiro:6000650 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Indirect Treatment Comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-2/2-6-trial-results-additional-analyses.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Indirect Comparison via a Common Reference, With Transitivity Assessed" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines ask submissions relying on an indirect comparison to identify the trials used, draw a network diagram and justify excluding trials whose differences may affect transitivity. The method should be described: the Bucher method for trials sharing a common reference arm is widely used, and network meta-analyses may be presented as supplementary analyses. Naive comparisons of single arms are difficult to interpret and reduce the PBAC's confidence, and where patient-level data exist for at least one study, matching-adjusted indirect comparisons or simulated treatment comparisons should be used to correct for trial differences." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee (PBAC Guidelines), version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 2.2.4 and 2.6.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "indirect-treatment-comparison" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:indirect-treatment-comparison .

heiro:6000651 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Indirect Treatment Comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adjusted Indirect Comparison Required and Documented" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CADTH accepts indirect treatment comparisons when direct evidence is insufficient, preferring adjusted methods over naive comparisons, and its submission template requires sponsors to summarise and attach any indirect-comparison reports." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH (now CDA-AMC), Indirect Evidence: Indirect Treatment Comparisons in Meta-Analysis; reimbursement review templates" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "indirect-treatment-comparison" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:indirect-treatment-comparison .

heiro:6000652 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Indirect Treatment Comparison"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/4ec8288e-6d15-49c5-a490-d8ad7748578f_en?filename=hta_methodological-guideline_direct-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Only Anchored Indirect Comparisons, Resting on Exchangeability" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EU HTA Coordination Group guideline uses indirect comparison as the broadest term, covering network meta-analysis, population-adjusted methods and comparisons in disconnected networks. Where no direct trial evidence exists or several treatments must be compared, only anchored indirect comparisons through a shared comparator are appropriate, since they respect randomisation, while naive comparisons break it. These rely on exchangeability, assessed through similarity, homogeneity and consistency, and if any fails the result is unlikely to be meaningful. The Bucher method suits simple networks and should be avoided when random-effects pooling feeds it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Methodological Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted by the HTA CG on 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, sections 1, 5 and 5.1 (Key Points 3)" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "indirect-treatment-comparison" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:indirect-treatment-comparison .

heiro:6000653 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) on Information Asymmetry"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000009 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/does-healthcare-deliver_c8af05a5-en/full-report/building-trustworthy-21st-century-healthcare-systems_2139da94.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Building trustworthy 21st century healthcare systems" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The OECD notes that patients may have limited ability to verify the quality of healthcare services, making trust especially important where information is unevenly distributed. It emphasises high-quality patient-centred care, clear communication, care coordination and support for self-management as practical ways to strengthen patients’ knowledge, confidence and trust." ;
    heiro:2000018 "OECD (2025), Does Healthcare Deliver?: Results from the Patient-Reported Indicator Surveys (PaRIS), Chapter 6" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "information-asymmetry" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:information-asymmetry .

heiro:6000654 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of UK National Screening Committee on Informed Choice"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000090 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/uk-national-screening-committee-information-development-guidance/guidance-for-the-development-production-and-review-of-information-to-support-uk-population-screening-programmes"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "UK National Screening Committee" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Personal Informed Choice as the Overarching Principle of Screening" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The UK National Screening Committee states that all approved screening programmes should aim to facilitate a personal informed choice, its overarching and guiding principle. It defines this as a decision to accept or decline a screening test based on access to accessible, accurate, evidence-based information on the condition, the testing process, potential benefits, harms, limitations and uncertainties, and potential outcomes. People offered screening must be able to discuss their options with an appropriately trained healthcare professional, and a choice to accept or decline is equally respected." ;
    heiro:2000018 "UK National Screening Committee, UK NSC informed choice principles (Guidance for the development, production and review of information to support UK population screening programmes, GOV.UK), principles 1, 2, 4 and 5, updated 10 December 2024" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "informed-choice" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:informed-choice .

heiro:6000655 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Informed Choice"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng197/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Shared Decision Making Supports Informed, Values-Based Decisions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's shared decision making guideline asks healthcare professionals to share and discuss the information people need to make informed decisions and to make sure they understand the choices available, including doing nothing. Discussions should cover the risks, benefits and consequences of each option, including choosing no treatment. Professionals should check understanding and check that the person's choice is consistent with what matters to them. Patient decision aids support informed, values-based decisions and do not advise people to choose one option over another." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG197, Shared decision making, recommendations 1.1.13, 1.2.10 and 1.2.11 and Terms used in this guideline (patient decision aids), published 17 June 2021" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "informed-choice" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:informed-choice .

heiro:6000656 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Intervention"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/the-scope-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Technology Use Specified in the Scope, Including Sequences" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's scope specifies the technology being evaluated, where it will be used and how and in what circumstances, particularly when this differs from alternatives or standard care. Diagnostic technologies must be described precisely because variants such as different thresholds may need separate evaluation, and when the technology and comparator are part of a sequence in the care pathway, alternative sequences may be compared. A treatment continuation rule should be analysed as an additional treatment strategy alongside the base-case interventions and comparators, not as a subgroup." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 2.1.2, 2.2.2, 2.2.7, 2.2.14 and 4.9.13, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "intervention" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:intervention .

heiro:6000657 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Intervention"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icd.who.int/dev11/Downloads/Download?fileName=ichi%2FICHI_Reference_Guide.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Intervention Defined in the ICHI Classification" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's International Classification of Health Interventions defines a health intervention as an act performed for, with or on behalf of a person or a population whose purpose is to assess, improve, maintain, promote or modify health, functioning or health conditions. The classification spans medical, surgical, mental health, primary care, allied health, rehabilitation and prevention interventions as well as community and public health ones. Each intervention is described by the target, the action and the means by which the action is carried out." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, International Classification of Health Interventions (ICHI) Reference Guide, maintenance-platform version prepared for the October 2025 annual meeting, ICHI Scope and Structure" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "intervention" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:intervention .

heiro:6000658 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Intervention"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Intervention Described as Used in Dutch Practice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ZIN requires the intervention under consideration to be one that can be applied in Dutch clinical practice within the defined patient population. An accurate description is needed of how the patient or carer will use it, and it must be clear whether the intervention replaces or supplements an existing one. It is compared with the standard or usual intervention in Dutch practice, the most cost-effective alternative is added when the standard is not, and several standard or usual interventions need separate comparisons, which can be made by fully incremental analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland. Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), 16 January 2024, sections 2.3.2 and 2.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "intervention" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:intervention .

heiro:6000659 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Medical Research Council and NIHR on Intervention"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000049,
        heiro:5000050 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8482308/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Medical Research Council and NIHR" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Complex Interventions With Economics in Every Phase" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The framework commissioned by the Medical Research Council and the National Institute for Health Research treats an intervention as complex because of features such as its number of components, the range of behaviours targeted, the skills needed to deliver and receive it, the groups, settings or levels targeted, and the flexibility permitted. Economic evaluation should be a core component of all phases of intervention research, and broad approaches such as cost benefit or cost consequence analysis will often suit complex interventions better than narrower cost-effectiveness or cost-utility analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Skivington K, et al. A new framework for developing and evaluating complex interventions: update of Medical Research Council guidance, BMJ 2021;374:n2061, sections What are complex interventions? and Economic considerations" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "intervention" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:intervention .

heiro:6000660 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Interviewer Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/131230/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Self-Administered Surveys Remove the Potential for Interviewer Bias" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance on patient-focused drug development states that, compared with interviewer-administered surveys, self-administration is typically less costly and removes the potential for interviewer bias. Interviewer administration may still suit some groups, such as patients with visual impairment. For qualitative one-on-one interviews, the guidance recommends selecting and training interviewers, pilot testing the interview guide, and phrasing probing questions so that they do not appear to cast judgement on a participant's beliefs or choices." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Patient-Focused Drug Development: Methods to Identify What Is Important to Patients, Guidance for Industry, Food and Drug Administration Staff, and Other Stakeholders (final, February 2022), section III (One-on-One Interviews; Approaches To Asking the Right Question) and section IV (Choice of Survey Administration Method)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "interviewer-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:interviewer-bias .

heiro:6000661 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Interviewer Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd9/chapter/appendix-4-conduct-of-qualitative-research-studies"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Non-Leading Questions and Researcher Reflexivity in Qualitative Interviews" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's real-world evidence framework asks researchers collecting qualitative data to consider the content, tone, order and phrasing of their questions, and to take care that questions are not leading. It treats reflexivity, meaning how a researcher's own values and preferences may affect the findings, as important throughout data collection, and recommends a clear statement on researcher reflexivity in study reports. Rapport between researcher and participant is also described as important for participants to express their views openly." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE real-world evidence framework (ECD9), appendix 4 (Conduct of qualitative research studies, added January 2025), sections on data collection, data analysis, and reporting and assessment of qualitative studies; framework published 23 June 2022" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "interviewer-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:interviewer-bias .

heiro:6000662 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Investigational Device Exemption"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/premarket-submissions-selecting-and-preparing-correct-submission/investigational-device-exemption-ide"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Approved IDE Needed Before a Device Clinical Study Starts" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The FDA states that an investigational device exemption allows an investigational device to be used in a clinical study to collect safety and effectiveness data, and that such studies are most often conducted to support a PMA. Unless exempt, every clinical evaluation of an investigational device needs an approved IDE before the study starts. Requirements include an investigational plan approved by an institutional review board, informed consent from all patients, investigational-use labelling, monitoring and required records and reports, with FDA approval of the IDE as well when the study involves a significant risk device." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Investigational Device Exemption (IDE) web page, content current as of 22 August 2024" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "investigational-device-exemption" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:investigational-device-exemption .

heiro:6000663 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Investigational Device Exemption"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/medicare/coverage/investigational-device-exemption-ide-studies"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Medicare Coverage in Category A and Category B IDE Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS states that the Medicare Prescription Drug, Improvement, and Modernization Act of 2003 allowed Medicare to pay the routine costs of care for beneficiaries in certain categories of IDE studies, removing a financial barrier to taking part. Since 1 January 2015 CMS has reviewed and approved IDE studies centrally against added coverage criteria. Approval of a Category A (Experimental) study allows coverage of routine care but not of the device, which is statutorily excluded, while approval of a Category B study allows coverage of the device and routine care." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CMS, Medicare Coverage Related to Investigational Device Exemption (IDE) Studies web page, page last modified 10 March 2026, describing 42 CFR Part 405 Subpart B" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "investigational-device-exemption" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:investigational-device-exemption .

heiro:6000664 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Kappa Statistic"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/71147/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "FDA Discourages Stand-Alone Overall Agreement Measures Including Kappa" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's statistical guidance on diagnostic test studies says that measures of overall agreement, including overall percent agreement and Cohen's kappa, can be misleading when a new test is compared with a non-reference standard, because overall agreement can be good even when positive or negative percent agreement is very low. It therefore discourages their stand-alone use to characterise diagnostic performance. It adds that all agreement measures, kappa included, change with prevalence and that agreement does not mean a test is correct." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA Guidance for Industry and FDA Staff, Statistical Guidance on Reporting Results from Studies Evaluating Diagnostic Tests, issued 13 March 2007, sections 4.1 and 7.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "kappa-statistic" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:kappa-statistic .

heiro:6000665 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Karnofsky Performance Status"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng99/chapter/recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Karnofsky Score of 70 or More as a Condition for Glioma Treatment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NICE guideline on primary brain tumours and brain metastases uses the Karnofsky Performance Status as a condition in its glioma recommendations. Radiotherapy with concomitant and adjuvant temozolomide for newly diagnosed grade 4 glioma (glioblastoma) is offered to people aged around 70 or under who have a Karnofsky score of 70 or more, and best supportive care alone is to be considered for people aged around 70 or over with a score under 70. For recurrent high-grade glioma the guideline lists the Karnofsky score among the factors to take into account when choosing treatment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Brain tumours (primary) and brain metastases in over 16s (NG99), Recommendations section 1.2 on glioma, published 11 July 2018, last updated 29 January 2021" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "karnofsky-performance-status" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:karnofsky-performance-status .

heiro:6000666 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on KCCQ"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta773/chapter/3-Committee-discussion"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "KCCQ Quartile Health States Accepted for an Empagliflozin Heart Failure Model" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In the appraisal of empagliflozin for chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, the company model moved people between quartiles of the Kansas City Cardiomyopathy Questionnaire clinical summary score, each with a specific utility and cost. The committee considered whether the KCCQ is an appropriate measure of quality-of-life outcomes and heard clinical experts describe it as well validated for heart failure. It noted that there may be some inconsistencies in how the size of effect translates from trial data into modelled quality-of-life effects, and concluded that the company's model structure was appropriate for decision making." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Empagliflozin for treating chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, NICE technology appraisal guidance TA773, section 3 Committee discussion, 2022" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "kccq" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:kccq .

heiro:6000667 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on KCCQ"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/clinical-outcome-assessment-coa-qualification-program/ddt-coa-000084-kansas-city-cardiomyopathy-questionnaire-kccq"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "KCCQ-23 Qualified as a Clinical Outcome Assessment in Heart Failure" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The FDA Center for Drug Evaluation and Research qualified the KCCQ-23 on 9 April 2020 as DDT COA #000084, for patients with heart failure, with heart failure symptoms and their impact on physical limitations as the concept of interest. The qualification covers the total symptom score, the physical limitations score and the clinical summary score. The statement notes that further evaluation is warranted of the instrument's ability to detect change and of how to interpret clinically meaningful within-patient change, and sponsors using the KCCQ-23 as a key confirmatory endpoint should discuss the plan with the relevant review division." ;
    heiro:2000018 "DDT COA #000084 Kansas City Cardiomyopathy Questionnaire (KCCQ), CDER Clinical Outcome Assessment Qualification Program, qualification statement, qualified 9 April 2020" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "kccq" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:kccq .

heiro:6000668 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Language Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "English and German Restriction Allowed With a Project Check" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's comprehensive information retrieval is usually not limited to specific years, but searches may be restricted to English and German publications. IQWiG justifies this by evaluations showing that excluding non-English publications does not change the conclusion of most systematic reviews. Whether a language restriction can be expected to affect the result of a report is checked project by project, with complementary medicine given as an example. Focused information retrieval may also restrict languages." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation published July 2026; the German original is authoritative), sections 9.1 and 9.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "language-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:language-bias .

heiro:6000669 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Large Simple Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/181871/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Streamlined Randomised Trials in Routine Practice, or Large Simple Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's draft guidance supports randomised trials with streamlined protocols and procedures that integrate research into routine clinical practice, noting that such trials have sometimes been called point of care trials or large simple trials. Trials fit best when the data needed are collected routinely with minimal extra procedures or visits, and outcomes such as strokes, fractures and myocardial infarctions are more readily captured from practice records. For drugs with well-established safety profiles, selective collection of serious adverse events and adverse events of special interest may be appropriate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Integrating Randomized Controlled Trials for Drug and Biological Products Into Routine Clinical Practice, draft guidance for industry, September 2024, docket FDA-2024-D-2052, sections I, III.B, IV.F and IV.G" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "large-simple-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:large-simple-trial .

heiro:6000670 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Last Observation Carried Forward"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-missing-data-confirmatory-clinical-trials_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "LOCF Gives an Unbiased Estimate Only Under Restrictive Assumptions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EMA guideline on missing data in confirmatory clinical trials describes last observation carried forward as a widely used single imputation method that gives an unbiased estimate of the treatment effect only under certain restrictive assumptions. In a condition that deteriorates over time, such as Alzheimer's disease, it is very likely to be overly optimistic in both arms and is not considered appropriate when active-arm withdrawals come earlier. Where a condition improves spontaneously, as in depression, it might be conservative. Any primary analysis is accepted only if an important bias favouring the experimental treatment can reasonably be excluded." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use, Guideline on missing data in confirmatory clinical trials, EMA/CPMP/EWP/1776/99 Rev. 1 (adopted 24 June 2010, in effect 1 January 2011), sections 6.3 and 6.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "last-observation-carried-forward" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:last-observation-carried-forward .

heiro:6000671 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Life Years Gained"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg24/chapter/cost-effectiveness"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Life Years Gained Reported Beside QALYs in Company Base-Case Results" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's user guide for the company evidence submission template asks companies to present base-case results in a table that includes life years gained (LYG) alongside total and incremental costs, QALYs and the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio. The QALY remains the reference-case measure of health effect, so the life years figure sits beside it as part of the reported results. In the reference case of the methods manual, costs and health effects are discounted at the same rate of 3.5% per year." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Single technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies evaluation, user guide for company evidence submission template (PMG24), section 3 Cost effectiveness, base-case results table; NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), chapter 4, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "life-years-gained" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:life-years-gained .

heiro:6000672 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Life Years Gained"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Life Years Tabulated with QALYs and evLYG in the Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's reference case asks for costs, life years, equal value life years gained and QALYs to be tabulated both undiscounted and discounted, with costs and outcomes discounted at 3% per year. A cost per life year is included where a treatment has effects on mortality, beside the cost per QALY and the cost per evLYG. ICER's 2023 Value Assessment Framework describes the evLYG as awarding the quality of life of the general population for the additional years of life gained, irrespective of the health state patients are in during those years." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations, Table 1 Overview of ICER's Reference Case Elements (2025); ICER 2023 Value Assessment Framework (September 2023)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "life-years-gained" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:life-years-gained .

heiro:6000673 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Living HTA"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/guidance-surveillance-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Guidance Updates After Managed Access and Surveillance Review" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE builds planned updating into technology appraisal guidance. A medicine may be recommended with managed access when it has plausible potential to be cost effective but the evidence is too uncertain, and new data could be collected within a maximum of 5 years without undue burden. NICE updates that guidance at the end of the data collection period, or earlier if the joint managed access governance group agrees. Other guidance is updated when changes to the evidence, clinical pathway or economic case are likely to have a material effect on recommendations." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.4.6, 8.2.1, 8.2.16, 8.4.1 and 8.4.2, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "living-hta" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:living-hta .

heiro:6000674 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Loss to Follow-Up"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Results Set Aside When Over 30% of Participants Are Not Analysed" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods generally exclude results from a benefit assessment if more than 30% of the participants who should be analysed are left out of the analysis altogether, or if the proportions left out differ between groups by more than 15 percentage points, which suggests non-random exclusion. These rules are guidance rather than rigid limits. For participants who are analysed but incompletely observed, no fixed limits apply: the risk of bias depends on the number, timing and reasons for losses to follow-up, and on differences between groups." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English version published 1 July 2026), section 10.3.11" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "loss-to-follow-up" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:loss-to-follow-up .

heiro:6000675 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Loss to Follow-Up"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-missing-data-confirmatory-clinical-trials_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Missing Data Cannot Be Ignored in Confirmatory Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA's guideline says trials should aim to capture all data from all patients, including those who stop treatment, and that ignoring missing data is not an acceptable option. It sets no maximum acceptable amount of missing data but strongly encourages collecting outcome data after treatment stops. Complete case analysis is not recommended as the primary analysis of a confirmatory trial, methods must be pre-specified, and sensitivity analyses under different assumptions will almost always be needed. Whether censoring in a survival analysis can be assumed non-informative should be discussed in the protocol or study report." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Guideline on missing data in confirmatory clinical trials (EMA/CPMP/EWP/1776/99 Rev. 1, adopted 2010, legal effective date 1 January 2011), Executive summary and sections 5.1, 6.2 and 6.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "loss-to-follow-up" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:loss-to-follow-up .

heiro:6000676 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Loss to Follow-Up"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/3943ae6a-1bca-4afd-b05c-fd9a54a252d7_en?filename=hta_jca_mp_dossier-template_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Amount, Reasons and Handling of Missing Data Reported in JCA Dossiers" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's guidance on the joint clinical assessment dossier for medicinal products asks developers to describe fully their methods for dealing with missing data, specifying and justifying the assumed mechanism, such as missing completely at random or missing not at random. Results should come from the intention-to-treat analysis and state the number of patients analysed and the extent and handling of missing data. The amount of and reasons for missing data, with all sensitivity analyses, must be provided, and treatment and study discontinuations reported for each population." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment (HTACG), Guidance on filling in the joint clinical assessment (JCA) dossier template: Medicinal products, V1.0 (adopted 28 November 2024), sections 4.3.2, 5.3.1.1 and 5.3.1.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "loss-to-follow-up" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:loss-to-follow-up .

heiro:6000677 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Loss to Follow-Up"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd9/chapter/methods-for-real-world-studies-of-comparative-effects"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Informative Censoring and Missing Data in Real-World Evidence Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's real-world evidence framework lists loss to follow up among the causes of censoring in longitudinal studies and warns that informative censoring causes bias if censoring depends on outcomes and differs across interventions. Developers should compare patterns of missingness across groups and over time. Complete records analysis is sufficient if the amount of missing data is low and likely to be missing completely at random. Otherwise, methods such as imputation, inverse probability weighting or maximum likelihood estimation are available, with sensitivity or bias analysis if data are not missing at random." ;
    heiro:2000018 "National Institute for Health and Care Excellence, NICE real-world evidence framework (ECD9), published 23 June 2022, last updated September 2025, chapter 'Methods for real-world studies of comparative effects', sections on study design, informative censoring and missing data" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "loss-to-follow-up" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:loss-to-follow-up .

heiro:6000678 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Managed Access Agreement"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/what-nice-does/patient-access-schemes-and-pricing-agreements/managed-access"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Ringfenced CDF/IMF Funds Back a \"Recommended With Managed Access\" Route Ending in Re-appraisal" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Beyond ordinary managed-entry deals, NICE can recommend a medicine \"with managed access\" funded from two ringfenced NHS England pots — the Cancer Drugs Fund (since 2016) and the Innovative Medicines Fund (from June 2022), each about £340 million a year. Eligible patients get immediate, time-limited access while a data collection agreement (often real-world data such as SACT) resolves the specific uncertainties NICE identified, up to a maximum of five years. A mandatory re-appraisal at the end routes the medicine to routine commissioning or out of the fund; entry requires high unmet need and uncertainty that the planned evidence can plausibly resolve." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Managed access; NHS England, Cancer Drugs Fund and Innovative Medicines Fund principles" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "managed-access-agreement" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:managed-access-agreement .

heiro:6000679 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Managed Entry Agreement"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg19/chapter/patient-access-schemes-commercial-access-agreements-and-flexible-pricing"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Patient Access Schemes, Commercial Access Agreements & Cancer Drugs Fund Managed Access" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE can consider patient access schemes (typically confidential discounts) and commercial access agreements with NHS England that let a technology be used when the list price would not support a positive recommendation. Where evidence is too uncertain for routine use, a cancer medicine may enter the Cancer Drugs Fund under a Managed Access Agreement with a data-collection arrangement, then be reappraised. The Patient Access Scheme Liaison Unit (PASLU) advises on feasibility." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Guide to the Processes of Technology Appraisal (Patient Access Schemes, Commercial Access Agreements & Flexible Pricing); Cancer Drugs Fund" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "managed-entry-agreement" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:managed-entry-agreement .

heiro:6000680 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Managed Entry Agreement"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Managed Entry / Risk-Sharing Arrangements for Uncertain Evidence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Where evidence is promising but uncertain, the PBAC can recommend listing through a managed entry (risk-sharing) arrangement, in which the medicine is listed at a price justified by the current evidence and its level of uncertainty, often with further data collection or financial arrangements to manage the risk to the health budget." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC (managed entry arrangements)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "managed-entry-agreement" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:managed-entry-agreement .

heiro:6000681 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Mantel-Haenszel Method"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Mantel-Haenszel Method for Binary Data When Only 2 Studies Are Pooled" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods set a standard meta-analysis approach that depends on the number of studies. With only 2 studies, IQWiG applies a fixed-effect model, using the Mantel-Haenszel method for binary data and the inverse variance method for continuous data, unless there are clear arguments against a fixed-effect model. With 3 or 4 studies it moves to a Bayesian random-effects meta-analysis, since heterogeneity cannot be reliably estimated from fewer than 5 studies." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 10.3.7" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "mantel-haenszel-method" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:mantel-haenszel-method .

heiro:6000682 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Mantel-Haenszel Method"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/148910/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel Methods Acceptable for Binary Endpoints" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance on covariate adjustment in randomised trials states that Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel methods are acceptable for analysing binary endpoints when the interest is in a conditional treatment effect. That effect is assumed to be constant across subgroups defined by a covariate with a discrete number of levels. FDA adds that nonlinear regression models, such as logistic regression, extend these methods by allowing adjustment for continuous covariates such as age, and that sponsors should prespecify any covariate-adjusted analysis before unblinding comparative data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Adjusting for Covariates in Randomized Clinical Trials for Drugs and Biological Products, Guidance for Industry, May 2023, sections III.A and III.C" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "mantel-haenszel-method" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:mantel-haenszel-method .

heiro:6000683 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Mapping Study"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg51/chapter/statement-on-using-the-eq-5d-5l-value-set"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Mapped Utilities Must Use the UK EQ-5D-5L Value Set With a Justified Mapping Model" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE allows EQ-5D values to be estimated by mapping other health-related quality-of-life measures, or health benefits seen in trials, when EQ-5D data are not available. For new topics starting after PMG51 was published, the UK EQ-5D-5L value set is used for all adult utility values in reference-case analyses, so EQ-5D-3L or non-EQ-5D data are mapped to utility values based on that value set, through the 3L value set first where needed. The mapping model should be fully described, its choice justified and its fit demonstrated, with sensitivity analyses across mapping models." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE, Interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51), published 27 August 2026, sections 1.10 and 1.11; NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section 4.3.9, updated 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "mapping-study" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:mapping-study .

heiro:6000684 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Scottish Government on Marginal Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000091 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.scot/publications/programme-budgeting-testing-approach-scotland/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Scottish Government" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Testing Programme Budgeting and Marginal Analysis in Scotland" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Scottish Government tested Programme Budgeting and Marginal Analysis as a way to combine expenditure and outcome information when examining how healthcare resources could be allocated more efficiently. Its report also shows that practical use depends on suitable data, clearly defined programmes and the feasibility of linking spending to outcomes." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Programme Budgeting – Testing The Approach in Scotland" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "marginal-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:marginal-analysis .

heiro:6000685 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Market Failure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240034204"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Market Failures Make Common Goods for Health a Case for Public Financing" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's normative guidance defines common goods for health as population-based functions or interventions, such as disease surveillance and health regulation, whose provision is subject to specific market failures because they are public goods, being non-rival and non-exclusionary, or have large social externalities. Because they will not arise through market forces alone and also suffer collective action failures, they are often underfunded, and WHO holds that they require public financing and public action. It treats this as distinct from the market failures of information asymmetry, risk selection and moral hazard that justify pooled financing of individual services." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Financing common goods for health (guidance, ISBN 9789240034204, 14 September 2021), chapters 1 and 2 and Box 1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "market-failure" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:market-failure .

heiro:6000686 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Market Failure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#case-for-change"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Market Failure as a Rationale for Government Intervention" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HM Treasury's Green Book defines a market failure as a situation where the free market, left to its own devices, cannot achieve optimal economic efficiency, and treats it as an important rationale for intervention when an appraisal sets out its case for change. It lists failure to provide public goods, externalities, market power, imperfect information and co-ordination failure, and names equity as a separate rationale. When social costs and benefits are valued, market prices may fail to reflect social value where markets do not function efficiently." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, The Green Book (2026), updated 5 February 2026, chapter 4 (Rationale and objectives), Case for change; chapter 8 (Valuation of social costs and social benefits), Market prices and non-market valuation" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "market-failure" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:market-failure .

heiro:6000687 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Markov Assumption"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-2-computational-methods-and-structure-of-economic_analysis.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cohort Models Under the Memoryless Assumption, With Tunnels and Pathways" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC guidelines reserve cohort-based state transition models for longer time horizons that can be represented with a manageable number of health states under the constraints of the Markovian, or memoryless, assumption. Submissions should consider whether transition probabilities from each state can reasonably be treated as independent of states experienced earlier, using pathway health states to carry the effects of previous events and tunnel states where probabilities vary with time spent in a state. Individual-level models are used only when a cohort model cannot feasibly be implemented, for example because previous events influence later event rates." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee (PBAC Guidelines), version 5.0 (September 2016), subsection 3A.2.3 (Cohort-based state transition (or Markov) models; Individual-level (or microsimulation) models)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "markov-assumption" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:markov-assumption .

heiro:6000688 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Markov Assumption"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cohort Markov Models Do Not Depend on Past History" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's national guidelines list state-transition or Markov models among the available modelling techniques and describe them as useful where the disease or treatment pathway can be represented as a series of mutually exclusive states. They note that cohort Markov models generally do not depend on past history, which can be a disadvantage, and that this can be addressed by using individual-level simulations. The choice of technique should follow from the research question, with model assumptions and inputs documented and justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), section 2.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "markov-assumption" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:markov-assumption .

heiro:6000689 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Markov Chain"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD15_Patient-level_simulation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Markov Label Kept for Memoryless State-Transition Models" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 15 notes that state-transition models are often called Markov models, but that the term should strictly be limited to models with the Markovian property, in which transitions depend only on the current state and not on earlier events or time already spent in that state. A fixed transition matrix applied every cycle gives a time-homogeneous Markov chain, which can be extended to a Markov process with time-varying matrices. Once transitions depend on a patient's earlier history, the Markovian assumption no longer holds and the model cannot be called Markov." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15: Cost-effectiveness modelling using patient-level simulation (Davis, Stevenson, Tappenden and Wailoo, April 2014), sections 2.4 and 4.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "markov-chain" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:markov-chain .

heiro:6000690 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Markov Chain"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-2-computational-methods-and-structure-of-economic_analysis.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cohort Models Must Respect the Memoryless Assumption" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC's guidelines advise using cohort-based state transition, or Markov, models for longer time horizons when the problem can be represented by a manageable number of health states under the constraints of the Markovian, or memoryless, assumption. Sponsors should ask whether transition probabilities from each state are independent of states experienced earlier, and whether a heterogeneous population needs separate model runs whose outputs are then aggregated. The least complicated feasible technique should be chosen, with individual-level models used only when a cohort model cannot represent the structure." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsection 3A.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "markov-chain" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:markov-chain .

heiro:6000691 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force on Markov Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000092 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://doi.org/10.1177/0272989X12455463"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Use a Transparent and Decision-Appropriate State-Transition Structure" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The ISPOR-SMDM task force recommends that state-transition models be structured around the decision problem, with clearly defined states, transitions, cycle length, time horizon, rewards and assumptions. It emphasizes evaluating whether the Markov memory assumption is appropriate, using extensions when relevant history affects future outcomes, and documenting validation and uncertainty rather than treating the model as a purely mechanical calculation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "State-Transition Modeling: A Report of the ISPOR-SMDM Modeling Good Research Practices Task Force-3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "markov-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:markov-model .

heiro:6000692 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Markov Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-2-computational-methods-and-structure-of-economic_analysis.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cohort Markov Models for Longer Horizons Under the Memoryless Assumption" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC's guidelines advise cohort-based state transition, or Markov, models for longer time horizons where the condition can be represented by a manageable number of health states under the Markovian, memoryless, assumption. Sponsors should consider whether transition probabilities depend on states experienced earlier, use tunnel states for time-varying probabilities, describe how the cycle length was chosen and apply a half-cycle correction by default. Individual-level models are to be used only when a defined structure cannot feasibly be implemented as a cohort model, with the reasons described." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsection 3A.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "markov-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:markov-model .

heiro:6000693 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Markov Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Markov Models With Specified States and Justified Cycle Length" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre names decision trees and Markov models as the major categories of model and asks that a model be kept as simple as possible. For each health state in a Markov model the nature of the state, temporary or absorbing, should be specified and the choice of states justified. Transition probabilities should be presented, for example as a matrix, stating whether each is constant or variable, and the cycle length should be justified. The glossary notes that Markov models have a limited ability to remember earlier cycles." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (published 8 May 2025), Guideline 14 (section 5.14.2) and Glossary" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "markov-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:markov-model .

heiro:6000694 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Markov Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD15_Patient-level_simulation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "When Patient-Level Simulation May Replace a Cohort Markov Model" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15 describes state-transition models that assume the Markovian, or memoryless, property, in which transitions depend only on the current state, and notes that Markov models are so often used for cohorts that the two terms are treated as synonyms. Where transitions depend on time since an event or on earlier events, tunnel states can be added, but only up to a point before the cohort approach becomes unwieldy. A patient-level simulation of the same structure may then be preferable, as it may be where patient characteristics have a non-linear effect on costs and QALYs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15: Cost-effectiveness modelling using patient-level simulation (Davis, Stevenson, Tappenden and Wailoo, April 2014), sections 2.4, 3.1 and 3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "markov-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:markov-model .

heiro:6000695 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on MCDA in HTA"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/ICER_2023_2026_VAF_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Formal MCDA Rejected in Favour of Structured Deliberation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's 2023 value assessment framework states that formal multi-criteria decision analysis has been considered but rejected, because ICER does not believe the methods for weighting individual elements are robust enough to add to the reliability of value judgements, and past attempts with its independent committees found the technique too complicated for reliable use. Its reports instead use a variation on MCDA that makes benefits beyond health and special ethical priorities explicit without quantifying them, guiding where within the cost-effectiveness threshold range a judgement of value should fall." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023), section 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "mcda-in-hta" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:mcda-in-hta .

heiro:6000696 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on MCDA in HTA"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "MCDA as a Decision Aid Broader Than Economic Evaluation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS's economic evaluation guidance describes multiple criteria decision analysis as a decision-making aid for complex contexts with several potentially competing objectives, using techniques that document deliberation explicitly against multiple precisely defined criteria, for example in the overall evaluation of a health technology. HAS places it outside its economic evaluation methods because the question it addresses is broader than cost-effectiveness, and states that MCDA conducted for public health or health technology evaluation follows internationally applicable methodological guidelines." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (methodological guidance), validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, section 1.2 (Choice of the evaluation method)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "mcda-in-hta" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:mcda-in-hta .

heiro:6000697 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Mean Difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/1f6b8a70-5ce0-404e-9066-120dc9a8df75_en?filename=hta_practical-guideline_direct-and-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pooling Continuous Outcomes in Joint Clinical Assessments" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group names the inverse variance method as the standard fixed-effect approach for continuous data, although a random-effects model is usually the standard model. For frequentist random-effects meta-analyses with five or more studies it recommends the Knapp-Hartung method with the Paule-Mandel heterogeneity estimator. The choice of method must be justified with regard to the effect scale, including the mean difference, and random-effects results should report prediction intervals alongside confidence intervals." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Coordination Group, Practical Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 4.1.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "mean-difference" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:mean-difference .

heiro:6000698 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS Resolution on Medical Malpractice"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000093 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6a4f5ad843f694ee291df864/nhs-resolution-annual-report-and-accounts-2025-2026-web.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS Resolution" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Clinical Negligence Liability and Yearly Funding of NHS Indemnity" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS Resolution, which manages clinical negligence claims against the NHS in England, states that liability is established when treatment falls below a reasonable standard of competence and causes an injury that would likely have been avoided, or been less severe, with appropriate care. Its indemnity schemes are funded year by year, with members and funders contributing enough each year to meet the liabilities due rather than holding reserves for future settlements. It received 15,236 new clinical negligence claims and reported incidents in 2025/26." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS Resolution, Annual report and accounts 2025/26 (HC 421), published 9 July 2026, Infographic 6 (page 35), Going concern (page 17), The year in review (page 19) and Appendix, Our indemnity schemes (page 191)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "medical-malpractice" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:medical-malpractice .

heiro:6000699 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Meta-Regression"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Meta-regression to Explore Known Clinical Heterogeneity" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE asks that statistical pooling be accompanied by an assessment of heterogeneity, which a random-effects model can address only in part, and that the degree of heterogeneity and its reasons be explored as fully as possible. Known clinical heterogeneity, for example from patient characteristics, may be explored using subgroup analyses and meta-regression. Potential effect modifiers should be identified before data analysis, and where control-group event risks differ substantially, it should be assessed whether the relative effect is constant across baseline risks." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.4.7 and 3.4.10, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "meta-regression" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:meta-regression .

heiro:6000700 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Meta-Regression"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD3-Heterogeneity.final-report.08.05.12.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Meta-regression Is Observational and Open to Ecological Bias" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 3 describes meta-regression as relating the size of a treatment effect to characteristics of the included trials in order to explain between-trial heterogeneity. Even with randomised trials, the study of effect modifiers is observational, so meta-regression shares the confounding of non-randomised studies and cannot show causality from association. Between-trial comparisons can show a highly significant but spurious relation when trials are few and precise, and they are open to ecological bias, which individual patient data avoid while giving far greater power." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 3, Heterogeneity: subgroups, meta-regression, bias and bias-adjustment (Dias, Sutton, Welton, Ades; September 2011, last updated April 2012), sections 1.1 to 1.1.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "meta-regression" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:meta-regression .

heiro:6000701 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Meta-Regression"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Meta-regression Shows Association Only, Firm Conclusions Need IPD" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Where studies are highly heterogeneous, IQWiG investigates potential causes and notes that meta-regression may identify study characteristics that explain differing effect sizes. Even when based on randomised studies, a meta-regression shows only an observed association, not a causal relationship. Analyses relating effects to average patient characteristics are especially difficult to interpret and share the limits of ecological studies; because their high risk of bias cannot be removed by adjustment, definite conclusions are possible only on the basis of individual patient data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 10.3.7" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "meta-regression" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:meta-regression .

heiro:6000702 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Meta-Regression"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/4ec8288e-6d15-49c5-a490-d8ad7748578f_en?filename=hta_methodological-guideline_direct-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Meta-regression for Explained Heterogeneity, Subgroups Often Preferred" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group states that where between-trial heterogeneity is too large for an overall synthesis but can be explained, syntheses should be run in groups of trials, in patient subgroups or by meta-regression, followed by a new assessment of heterogeneity. In joint clinical assessments, subgroup analyses are often more useful than meta-regression because they help target the right intervention to the right subgroup. Meta-regression needs enough studies to estimate every model parameter, and any such analysis must be described and justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EU HTA Coordination Group, Methodological Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons (adopted 8 March 2024), Summary, section 3.1.2 and Key Points 1; and Practical Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons (adopted 8 March 2024), sections 3.3 and 4.1.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "meta-regression" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:meta-regression .

heiro:6000703 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Minimal Clinically Important Difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/77832/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Anchor-Based Evidence Required for Responder Definitions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's guidance on patient-reported outcome measures interprets meaningful individual change through an a priori responder definition, the individual score change over a set period that should be interpreted as a treatment benefit. The definition is determined empirically, may vary by population and trial design, and is evaluated in the context of each trial. Its evidence is derived from anchor-based methods, such as patient ratings of change, while distribution-based methods are supportive only. Cumulative distribution displays of change in each arm may be preferable to a single categorical definition." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Guidance for Industry. Patient-Reported Outcome Measures: Use in Medical Product Development to Support Labeling Claims, final guidance (December 2009), section IV.E" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "minimal-clinically-important-difference" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:minimal-clinically-important-difference .

heiro:6000704 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Minimal Clinically Important Difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Responder Thresholds of at Least 15% of the Scale Range" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods prefer patient-reported anchors for determining a minimally important difference, but find that published values vary widely with the anchor, observation period, disease severity and direction of change, and that no established standard exists for judging their quality. The Institute therefore accepts prespecified responder analyses when the response criterion is at least 15% of the instrument's scale range. Otherwise it uses standardised mean differences (Hedges' g) with an irrelevance threshold of 0.2, or post hoc responder analyses at exactly 15%." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Health Care (IQWiG), General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation published 1 July 2026), section 10.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "minimal-clinically-important-difference" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:minimal-clinically-important-difference .

heiro:6000705 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Minimal Clinically Important Difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/a70a62c7-325c-401e-ba42-66174b656ab8_en?filename=hta_outcomes_jca_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Anchor-Based MIDs for Within-Patient Change in Joint Assessments" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EU HTA Coordination Group's guidance on outcomes for joint clinical assessments defines the minimal important difference, also called the minimal clinically important difference, as the minimal change in score that the patient perceives as an improvement or deterioration, and uses it as a threshold for within-patient change over time. Anchor-based methods linked to a patient global rating of change are considered the most appropriate. Values vary with baseline level, direction of change and population, and continuous results must always be presented alongside any responder analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment (HTACG), Guidance on outcomes for joint clinical assessments, adopted 10 June 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 5.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "minimal-clinically-important-difference" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:minimal-clinically-important-difference .

heiro:6000706 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Minimisation (Trial Allocation)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-adjustment-baseline-covariates-clinical-trials_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Dynamic Allocation Accepted Only With a Random Element" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EMA accepts dynamic allocation, which measures imbalance on each prognostic factor separately and minimises a weighted sum of those imbalances, as a way to balance several factors at once when stratification is impractical, especially in small trials. It does not guarantee balance within combinations of factors, deterministic schemes should be avoided and each allocation should include an element of randomisation. Because conventional methods do not always control the type I error under dynamic allocation, re-randomisation methods should be considered in the analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EMA Guideline on adjustment for baseline covariates in clinical trials (EMA/CHMP/295050/2013, adopted by CHMP 26 February 2015), section 4.2; and ICH Topic E9, Statistical principles for clinical trials, Step 5 (CPMP/ICH/363/96, September 1998), section 2.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "minimisation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:minimisation .

heiro:6000707 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Minimisation (Trial Allocation)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/71336/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Deterministic Dynamic Allocation Avoided, Complex Schemes Discussed First" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA guidance adopting ICH E9 says deterministic dynamic allocation procedures should be avoided and an appropriate element of randomisation should be incorporated for each treatment allocation, with every effort made to keep the trial double blind. The complexity of the logistics and the potential impact on the analysis should be weighed before dynamic allocation is chosen. The FDA's 2023 guidance on covariate adjustment adds that sponsors should discuss proposals for complex covariate-adaptive randomisation with the relevant review division." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Guidance for Industry E9 Statistical Principles for Clinical Trials (September 1998), section II.C.2 (ICH E9 2.3.2); and Adjusting for Covariates in Randomized Clinical Trials for Drugs and Biological Products, Guidance for Industry (May 2023), section III.A" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "minimisation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:minimisation .

heiro:6000708 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Misclassification"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd9/chapter/methods-for-real-world-studies-of-comparative-effects"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Misclassification Treated as Measurement Error in Real-World Data" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's real-world evidence framework describes misclassification as measurement error in categorical variables and lists it among data quality limitations that can affect eligibility criteria, outcomes, exposures and covariates and create information biases. Differential measurement error generally biases comparative effects in a direction that can be hard to predict. NICE notes it is often recommended to define outcomes for high specificity, and suggests sensitivity or bias analyses that can be propagated through to cost-effectiveness analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE real-world evidence framework (ECD9), published 23 June 2022, with minor changes up to September 2025, sections on data quality (Assessing data suitability) and on measurement error and misclassification and assessing robustness, table 3 (Methods for real-world studies of comparative effects)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "misclassification" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:misclassification .

heiro:6000709 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Misclassification"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/152503/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Validate Record and Claims Variables Against Misclassification" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA notes that operational definitions in electronic health records and claims data are usually imperfect, so misclassification of exposures, outcomes or covariates can produce false positives and false negatives and bias associations. Sponsors should consider the degree of misclassification, whether it is differential, whether errors are dependent, and the likely direction of bias. FDA expects outcome validation, and states that differential misclassification may bias associations in either direction, whereas non-differential misclassification tends to bias towards the null." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Real-World Data: Assessing Electronic Health Records and Medical Claims Data To Support Regulatory Decision-Making for Drug and Biological Products, Guidance for Industry, final, July 2024, sections IV.D.2, IV.D.3 and V.D.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "misclassification" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:misclassification .

heiro:6000710 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Misclassification"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Misclassification Bias From an Imperfect Reference Test" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG states that in studies of the accuracy of a diagnostic strategy, results may be biased if the reference test does not correctly distinguish between healthy and sick participants, which it terms misclassification bias. It lists this with partial and differential verification bias and incorporation bias as sources of biased estimates of diagnostic accuracy." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation published July 2026; the German original is authoritative), section 10.3.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "misclassification" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:misclassification .

heiro:6000711 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Model Validation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-7-model-validation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Model Validation and Executable, Respecifiable Model Required" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Validation to demonstrate the model generates what it is intended to represent is expected (Section 3A.7). An electronic copy of the model must be provided in which all variables can be changed independently, the base case can be respecified with new sensitivity analyses run, and results are produced within reasonable running times; more complex, less transparent techniques reduce PBAC confidence in the claim." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC, Sections 3A.2 and 3A.7" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "model-validation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:model-validation .

heiro:6000712 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Model Validation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Transparency and Validation With an Executable Model" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Economic evaluations must be transparently described and provided as a fully executable model, so the committee and review group can interrogate structure, assumptions, and coding and test alternative scenarios; model structures that limit feasibility of probabilistic analysis must be specified and justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Health Technology Evaluations: The Manual (PMG36); company evidence submission requirements" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "model-validation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:model-validation .

heiro:6000713 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Modified Rankin Scale"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng128/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pre-Stroke Modified Rankin Score Below 3 Is a Thrombectomy Selection Criterion" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In its guideline on stroke and transient ischaemic attack in over 16s, NICE selects people with acute ischaemic stroke for thrombectomy using, alongside earlier criteria, a pre-stroke functional status of less than 3 on the modified Rankin scale and a score of more than 5 on the National Institutes of Health Stroke Scale. Clinicians should also take into account the person's overall clinical status and the extent of established infarction on initial brain imaging. NICE states that the thresholds were included so that the recommendation could be implemented in practice and to be consistent with the NHS England clinical commissioning policy on mechanical thrombectomy." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG128, Stroke and transient ischaemic attack in over 16s: diagnosis and initial management, recommendation 1.4.8 [2019] and the committee's rationale in the Recommendations chapter, last updated 13 April 2022" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "modified-rankin-scale" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:modified-rankin-scale .

heiro:6000714 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Monetary Valuation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#valuation-of-social-costs-and-social-benefits"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Valuation Hierarchy and Generic Prices for Life and Health" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Green Book treats valuation methods as a hierarchy from most to least reliable, starting from market prices, and says stated preference should be used only when adequate revealed preference data are not available. Risks to life are valued with the Value of a Prevented Fatality, a generic price for small changes in fatality risk that the guidance says is not the value of a life. The value of a QALY is its example of stated preference valuation, and these values are averages from representative samples of the population." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, The Green Book: UK government guidance on appraisal (2026 edition), chapter 8 (Market prices and non-market valuation; Stated preference; Life and health), updated 5 February 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "monetary-valuation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:monetary-valuation .

heiro:6000715 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Department for Transport on Monetary Valuation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000094 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/tag-data-book"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Department for Transport" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Value of Preventing a Fatal Casualty in the TAG Data Book" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Department for Transport's TAG data book holds the Value of a Prevented Fatality that the Green Book directs appraisers to use. In version 2.03 (May 2026) the average value of preventing a fatal casualty is £2,652,796 in 2023 prices and values, of which £2,483,360 is the willingness-to-pay element, with the remainder made up of net lost output and medical and ambulance costs. The willingness-to-pay figure corresponds to an individual's willingness to pay for a marginal reduction in fatality risk." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Department for Transport, TAG data book, version 2.03, Table A 4.1.1 (average value of prevention per casualty by severity and element of cost), May 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "monetary-valuation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:monetary-valuation .

heiro:6000716 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of US Environmental Protection Agency on Monetary Valuation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000027 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.epa.gov/environmental-economics/mortality-risk-valuation"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "US Environmental Protection Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Central Value of Statistical Life of USD 7.4 Million in 2006 Dollars" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EPA values mortality benefits using estimates of what people are willing to pay for small reductions in their risk of dying, and states that it does not place a dollar value on individual lives. It recommends a central estimate of USD 7.4 million in 2006 dollars, updated to the year of the analysis, for all benefits analyses of mortality risk reduction, regardless of the age, income or other characteristics of the affected population, until revised guidance is available. The agency has proposed replacing the term value of statistical life with one that better describes small changes in mortality risk." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Environmental Protection Agency, Mortality Risk Valuation (frequently asked questions), 'What does it mean to place a value on life?', 'What value of statistical life does EPA use?' and 'Why is EPA proposing to change the terminology', last updated 26 January 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "monetary-valuation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:monetary-valuation .

heiro:6000717 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Monetary Valuation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Measured in QALYs Against an Opportunity Cost Range" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case uses cost-utility analysis with health effects expressed in QALYs, justified by its focus on maximising health gains from a fixed NHS and personal social services budget. Its maximum acceptable ICER is framed as the opportunity cost of programmes displaced by new, more costly technologies, and it uses a range of £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY gained rather than a precise figure. Net health benefits are presented using values of £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY gained." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.14, 4.2.15, 6.3.1, 6.3.2 and 6.3.4 to 6.3.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "monetary-valuation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:monetary-valuation .

heiro:6000718 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of RAND Corporation on Moral Hazard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000095 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.rand.org/pubs/research_briefs/RB9174.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "RAND Corporation" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Evidence from the Health Insurance Experiment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "RAND's Health Insurance Experiment (1971 to 1982) randomly assigned more than 7,700 people to free care or to 25, 50 or 95 per cent coinsurance. Spending was 20 per cent lower under 25 per cent coinsurance and about 30 per cent lower under 95 per cent coinsurance than under free care. Cost sharing reduced highly effective and less effective care in roughly equal proportions, and the poorest and sickest 6 per cent fared better under free care on 4 of 30 conditions. RAND describes cost sharing as a blunt tool that should be minimal or nonexistent for the poor." ;
    heiro:2000018 "RAND Corporation, The Health Insurance Experiment: A Classic RAND Study Speaks to the Current Health Care Reform Debate, research brief RB-9174-HHS, published 6 December 2006, sections Effects on Use of Health Services, Effects on Appropriateness of Care and on Quality of Care, Effects on Health, and Implications for Today's Health Care Reform Discussion" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "moral-hazard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:moral-hazard .

heiro:6000719 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Moral Hazard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241564021"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Direct Payments and Cost Sharing as Barriers to Access" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The World Health Report 2010 identifies reliance on direct payments, including user fees, co-insurance, co-payments and deductibles, as the greatest obstacle to universal coverage. Charges at the point of use discourage use of services, particularly prevention, and encourage overuse by people who can pay and underuse by those who cannot. The report notes that OECD countries raised cost sharing to limit government spending and discourage unnecessary use, but that these payments cause financial hardship and reduce access. Financial catastrophe becomes negligible only when direct payments fall to 15 to 20 per cent of total health expenditure." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, The World Health Report: Health Systems Financing: the Path to Universal Coverage (2010), Message from the Director-General; Executive summary; Chapter 1, Direct payments and Box 1.2 (page 5); Chapter 3, The problems with direct payments (pages 41 to 42) and The retreat from direct payments (pages 45 to 47)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "moral-hazard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:moral-hazard .

heiro:6000720 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development on Moral Hazard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000009 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2010/11/health-care-systems_g1g1199f/9789264094901-en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost Sharing Can Curb Moral Hazard but Raises Equity Concerns" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The OECD's 2010 report on health care systems states that patients who do not bear the full cost of care may consume more than necessary, and that providers may prescribe more for well insured patients. Increasing cost sharing can reduce moral hazard but puts some people at financial risk and raises equity concerns. Higher out-of-pocket payments can cut demand for necessary care as well as care of limited value, and the RAND experiment found harm to people who were both poor and sick. Provider payment design and evidence-based practice are named as other remedies." ;
    heiro:2000018 "OECD (2010), Health Care Systems: Efficiency and Policy Settings, OECD Publishing, Paris, doi 10.1787/9789264094901-en, Chapter 3, Market signals and regulations affecting users (page 97), and Annex 3.A1, Market failures and imperfections in health care systems, Moral hazard (page 115)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "moral-hazard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:moral-hazard .

heiro:6000721 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Moving Target Problem"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg48/chapter/methods-for-guidance-produced-in-the-nice-healthtech-programme"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Switching Values for HealthTech That Iterates Rapidly" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE allows evidence on predecessor versions of a health technology to be considered, particularly when evidence on the current model or version is limited, and expects the assessment report to comment on how far that evidence applies. For technologies likely to have rapid iteration and several new versions after guidance is published, threshold analyses can identify a switching value for the performance parameters that drive cost-effectiveness results. Guidance may then state the performance ranges or values that a recommended technology needs to maintain to remain cost effective." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE HealthTech programme manual (PMG48), chapter 2, sections 2.2.8, 2.3.27 and 2.4.31, last updated 17 December 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "moving-target-problem" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:moving-target-problem .

heiro:6000722 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Multiple Regression"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-adjustment-baseline-covariates-clinical-trials_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pre-Specified Baseline Covariates in the Primary Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EMA guideline on adjustment for baseline covariates states that adjusting for a covariate strongly or moderately associated with the primary outcome generally improves efficiency and avoids conditional bias from chance imbalance. Covariates must be pre-specified in the protocol, only a few should be included, and the baseline value of a continuous outcome should usually be one of them. Variables measured after randomisation, or chosen because of imbalance observed post hoc, should not enter the primary analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EMA, Guideline on adjustment for baseline covariates in clinical trials (EMA/CHMP/295050/2013), adopted 26 February 2015, in effect from 1 September 2015, Executive summary and sections 5.4 to 5.6, 6.1 and 6.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "multiple-regression" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:multiple-regression .

heiro:6000723 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Multiple Regression"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/148910/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Linear Regression Adjustment for the Average Treatment Effect" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA accepts covariate adjustment through a linear model to estimate the average treatment effect in randomised trials. The outcome is regressed on an intercept, a treatment indicator and baseline covariates by ordinary least squares, and the treatment coefficient is the estimate. FDA states that this remains valid even when the model is misspecified, recommends adjusting for the covariates expected to be most strongly associated with the outcome, and asks sponsors to consider robust standard errors such as the Huber-White sandwich when the model has no treatment by covariate interactions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Adjusting for Covariates in Randomized Clinical Trials for Drugs and Biological Products, Guidance for Industry, May 2023, sections III.A and III.B" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "multiple-regression" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:multiple-regression .

heiro:6000724 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Multiple Regression"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reporting and Variable Selection in Multifactorial Regression Models" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's General Methods treat multifactorial methods mainly as a way to compensate for confounding in non-randomised studies, and set out what a report should usually give so that such a model can be judged. This includes an a priori specification of the outcome and all potential explanatory variables, how model assumptions and goodness of fit were checked, and estimates with standard errors and confidence intervals for every explanatory variable. Where variable selection cannot be avoided, IQWiG uses a form of backward elimination, which it prefers to forward selection." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 10.3.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "multiple-regression" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:multiple-regression .

heiro:6000725 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Narrative Review"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Narrative Overview Only When Data Cannot Support Meta-Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE expects evidence on outcomes to come from a systematic review that follows a pre-defined protocol and uses explicit, valid and replicable methods to minimise biased selection of studies. When there is not enough relevant and valid data for pairwise or network meta-analysis, the analysis may have to be restricted to a narrative overview that critically appraises individual studies and presents their results. In those circumstances the committee is particularly cautious when drawing conclusions about relative clinical effectiveness." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.3.4, 3.4.2 and 3.4.21, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "narrative-review" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:narrative-review .

heiro:6000726 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Negative Study"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Non-Significant Result Is Not Evidence of Equivalence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG describes rating the non-significant result of a traditional significance test as evidence that the null hypothesis is true as one of the most common serious errors in interpreting medical data. Equivalence has to be shown with methods that test equivalence hypotheses, against an equivalence range defined in advance, because exact equivalence cannot be demonstrated statistically. A one-sided range of at most irrelevant inferiority gives a non-inferiority hypothesis, and the required sample size should be calculated before the study starts." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of Allgemeine Methoden; German original authoritative), DOI 10.60584/General-Methods_V8.0, section 10.3.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "negative-study" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:negative-study .

heiro:6000727 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of International Council for Harmonisation on Negative Study"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000037 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://database.ich.org/sites/default/files/E9_Guideline.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "International Council for Harmonisation" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Non-Significant Test of No Difference Cannot Show Equivalence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICH E9 states that concluding equivalence or non-inferiority from a non-significant test of the null hypothesis of no difference between an investigational product and an active comparator is inappropriate. A trial meant to show equivalence or non-inferiority must state this intention in its protocol and specify a clinically justified margin. The guideline warns that such trials are not conservative, since many flaws in design or conduct tend to bias results towards a conclusion of equivalence." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICH E9, Statistical principles for clinical trials, Step 4 version, 5 February 1998, section 3.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "negative-study" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:negative-study .

heiro:6000728 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Negative Study"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/points-consider-switching-between-superiority-and-non-inferiority_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Failed Superiority Trial Needs a Prespecified Non-Inferiority Margin" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA guidance states that when a superiority trial fails to detect a significant difference, interest may turn to the lesser objective of non-inferiority, which raises fewer problems if the protocol contains an acceptable, prospectively defined margin. Without one, the margin must be justified after the results are seen, which in many situations will not be possible. The comparator also needs consistent evidence of efficacy, and the confidence interval gives the precision actually achieved, superseding any power calculation made before the trial." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EMA CPMP, Points to consider on switching between superiority and non-inferiority, CPMP/EWP/482/99, 27 July 2000, sections IV.2, IV.2.1 and IV.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "negative-study" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:negative-study .

heiro:6000729 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Net Benefit Approach"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Net Benefit Adopted as an Alternative Metric to the ICER" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS's guidance on economic evaluation adopts net benefit as an alternative to the incremental cost-effectiveness ratio for relating additional cost to health gain, citing the ratio's limitations in analysing uncertainty and net benefit's simpler reading of probabilistic analysis with several comparators. The evaluator chooses whether to express it in monetary or health units. With no predefined French reference value, results should be plotted as net benefit against willingness to pay over an interval wide enough to show every intervention that maximises net benefit, with a table of values at each intersection." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (methodological guidance), validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, introduction (2020 updates) and section 5.1.2 (Guideline 26)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "net-benefit-approach" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:net-benefit-approach .

heiro:6000730 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Net Benefit Approach"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Ranking Technologies by the Probability of Highest Net Benefit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "When several technologies are compared, NICE allows cost-effectiveness rankings to present probabilistic results, showing the probability that each technology is ranked highest, meaning it produces the highest net benefit, and possibly every other rank, for example in rankograms shown alongside each technology's expected net benefit. The manual also suggests reviewing how the variance of probabilistic outputs, such as net benefits, changes as simulations increase, to judge whether the model has been run enough times to minimise Monte Carlo error." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.7.11 and 6.3.3, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "net-benefit-approach" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:net-benefit-approach .

heiro:6000731 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Net Health Benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Net Health Benefit at £25,000 and £35,000, Weighted and Unweighted" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE asks for expected net health benefits alongside ICERs, using values of £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY gained, and sees them as particularly informative with several comparators, small differences in costs or QALYs, subgroup considerations, or technologies giving less benefit at lower cost. When decision-making modifiers apply, net health benefits should be presented with and without the QALY weighting to show the effect on opportunity costs. A positive value means population health rises, and a negative one means health displaced elsewhere exceeds the gain, though such technologies may still be recommended under modifiers." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16, 4.10.8 and 6.3.2, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "net-health-benefit" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:net-health-benefit .

heiro:6000732 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Net Monetary Benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Expected Net Health Benefit Alongside ICERs at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that expected net health benefits should be presented as well as ICERs, using values placed on a QALY gain of £25,000 and £35,000. Net health benefit is described as particularly informative when decision-making modifiers are applied, when there are several technologies or comparators, when differences in costs or QALYs are small, or when a technology provides less health benefit at lower cost. Results are expected in a fully incremental analysis, and net monetary benefits can also be shown alongside ICERs and net health benefits." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.16 and 4.10.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "net-monetary-benefit" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:net-monetary-benefit .

heiro:6000733 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Net Monetary Benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Net Monetary Benefit Among Required Reference-Case Outputs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Zorginstituut Nederland guideline requires the net monetary benefit (NMB) to be reported alongside the ICER, expressed in costs per QALY gained, as part of the reference-case results, with results based on the probabilistic analysis. Calculating the NMB requires a reference value, a cost-effectiveness threshold that can be based on the burden of disease applying to the indication. When more than two interventions are compared, a fully incremental analysis is required, and ranking by NMB can also be used to identify the most cost-effective intervention." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), English version of Richtlijn voor het uitvoeren van economische evaluaties in de gezondheidszorg (versie 2024), published 16 January 2024, chapter 1 (Table 2) and section 5.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "net-monetary-benefit" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:net-monetary-benefit .

heiro:6000734 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Net Monetary Benefit"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-9-uncertainty-analysis-model-inputs-and-assumptions.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Incremental Net Benefit Interval Estimates From Probabilistic Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC Guidelines allow a probabilistic sensitivity analysis to be provided in addition to deterministic sensitivity analysis. Where one is undertaken, its results should be presented using cost-effectiveness planes and acceptability curves, together with tabulated interval estimates for either the ICER or the incremental net benefits of the proposed medicine. The guidelines add that probabilistic analysis can characterise parameter uncertainty but cannot address translational or structural uncertainty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0, September 2016, section 3A.9.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "net-monetary-benefit" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:net-monetary-benefit .

heiro:6000735 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Network Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Network Meta-Analysis Alongside Head-to-Head Trials Using DSU Methods" ;
    heiro:2000017 "When technologies have not been compared within a single randomised controlled trial, NICE asks for the pairwise head-to-head trials to be presented together with a network meta-analysis if appropriate, and it prefers the methods in the Decision Support Unit evidence synthesis series. External information should inform the between-study heterogeneity, and informative priors for that parameter may be preferable in networks with few studies. Trial randomisation must be preserved, so single arms from different trials may not be compared." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.4.11, 3.4.16 and 3.4.20, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "network-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:network-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000736 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Network Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/68553709"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Generalised Linear Modelling Framework for Pairwise and Network Meta-Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Decision Support Unit sets out one generalised linear modelling framework for fixed and random effects synthesis that applies to pairwise meta-analysis, indirect comparisons, multi-arm trials and network meta-analysis without distinction. Different likelihoods and link functions cover binomial, rate, continuous, competing risk and ordered categorical outcomes. Estimation is Bayesian, using Markov chain Monte Carlo simulation with WinBUGS code, and models are compared with the deviance information criterion while goodness of fit is judged by the residual deviance." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 2: A Generalised Linear Modelling Framework for Pairwise and Network Meta-Analysis of Randomised Controlled Trials, August 2011, last updated September 2016, Executive Summary and section 5" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "network-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:network-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000737 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Network Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/4ec8288e-6d15-49c5-a490-d8ad7748578f_en?filename=hta_methodological-guideline_direct-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Anchored Network Meta-Analysis in EU Joint Clinical Assessment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Coordination Group's methodological guideline treats network meta-analysis as a generalisation of meta-analysis that combines direct and indirect evidence and rests on exchangeability, judged through similarity, homogeneity and consistency. Only anchored indirect comparisons that respect randomisation in a connected network are generally appropriate, and a random-effects model is the appropriate choice in most practical situations. Frequentist and Bayesian approaches may both be used, with clear justification of any priors, and comparators beyond those needed to connect the network should generally be avoided." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Methodological Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted by the HTA CG on 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, Summary, Introduction and section 5.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "network-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:network-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000738 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Network Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Indirect Comparisons Give Hints of Added Benefit Rather Than Proof" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG accepts network meta-analysis as an adjusted indirect comparison method, but indirect comparisons require stronger assumptions and generally provide only hints or indications of added benefit or harm, not proofs. Their use needs adequate justification, although they are often necessary in health economic evaluations that compare more than 2 interventions. A random-effects model should be the default, sufficient consistency between direct and indirect evidence is required, and non-adjusted comparisons of single arms from different studies are disapproved." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 10.3.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "network-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:network-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000739 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Network Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Network Meta-Analysis Recommended When Direct Comparison Is Lacking" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For economic evaluations submitted to HAS, a network meta-analysis is recommended when interventions have not been compared directly, subject to validating its feasibility assumptions, including consistency of populations, protocols and risk of bias and verification of transitivity. Bayesian and frequentist approaches are both acceptable, results should appear in tables and forest plots, and the level of evidence of each relative effect should be stated. Matching-adjusted indirect comparisons require justification that a network meta-analysis is impossible, and naive indirect comparisons are not acceptable in the reference case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS Methodological Guidance: Choices in Methods for Economic Evaluation, validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020 (English version), Guideline 13 and section 2.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "network-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:network-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000740 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Network Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-2/2-6-trial-results-additional-analyses.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Network Meta-Analysis as a Supplementary Indirect Comparison" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines list network meta-analysis among indirect comparison methods but state that such complex methods may be presented as supplementary analyses, with pairwise results reported for each link in the network. Nonrandomised studies should be avoided, and where they must be included, results should be shown with and without them. Submissions should weigh the extra demands of complex methods against any reduction in uncertainty they deliver, and provide enough detail, including programming code, for the analysis to be repeated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsection 2.6.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 6 ;
    heiro:2000020 "network-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:network-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000741 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Non-Randomised Controlled Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Non-Randomised Studies at High Risk of Bias from Confounding" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE has a strong preference for high-quality randomised controlled trials for relative treatment effects, but non-randomised studies may complement them when evidence is limited or form the primary source when there is no trial evidence. Non-randomised studies can be interventional without randomisation or observational, and tend to be at high risk of bias because factors influencing treatment assignment may predict outcomes. Each study's risk of bias should be assessed with a validated tool such as ROBINS-I, and extensive sensitivity analyses should cover all key risks of bias." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.3.2, 3.3.9, 3.3.12 and 3.3.13, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "non-randomised-controlled-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:non-randomised-controlled-trial .

heiro:6000742 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Non-Randomised Controlled Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/9f9dbfe4-078b-4959-9a07-df9167258772_en?filename=hta_clinical-studies-validity_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Non-Randomised Controlled Trials Assessed with ROBINS-I" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Coordination Group's validity guidance defines non-randomised controlled trials as trials that allocate participants by methods that are not random, such as investigator's choice, participant's choice or calendar dates. They allow direct estimation of relative effects, but non-random allocation breaks exchangeability and is likely to cause confounding bias, and any adjustment rests on the unverifiable assumption that all relevant confounders and effect modifiers are known and measured. Risk of bias should be assessed with ROBINS-I for each outcome, with domain and overall judgements." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidance on Validity of Clinical Studies, V1.0 of 4 July 2024, adopted by the HTA Coordination Group on 19 September 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 4.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "non-randomised-controlled-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:non-randomised-controlled-trial .

heiro:6000743 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Non-Randomised Controlled Trial"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Certainty of Results from Non-Randomised Controlled Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In IQWiG benefit assessments, a result from a randomised study gives high or moderate qualitative certainty, while a result from a non-randomised comparative study gives low certainty. In assessments of potential, which carry lower evidence requirements, non-randomised controlled studies with active allocation following a preplanned rule count as low certainty when confounders are adequately controlled and as very low certainty otherwise. In classifying non-randomised studies, IQWiG primarily evaluates the control for potential confounders." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, sections 3.1.4, 3.8.1 and 10.1.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "non-randomised-controlled-trial" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:non-randomised-controlled-trial .

heiro:6000744 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on NYHA Functional Classification"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta388/chapter/1-recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Sacubitril Valsartan Recommended Only for NYHA Class 2 to 4 Symptoms" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE recommends sacubitril valsartan as an option for treating symptomatic chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction only in people with New York Heart Association class 2 to 4 symptoms, a left ventricular ejection fraction of 35 per cent or less, and a stable dose of an ACE inhibitor or angiotensin II receptor blocker. Treatment should be started by a heart failure specialist with access to a multidisciplinary heart failure team. The NYHA class therefore sets the population for which the appraisal found the drug an acceptable use of NHS resources." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal guidance TA388, Sacubitril valsartan for treating symptomatic chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, recommendations 1.1 and 1.2, published 27 April 2016" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "nyha-functional-classification" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:nyha-functional-classification .

heiro:6000745 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of American Heart Association, American College of Cardiology and Heart Failure Society of America on NYHA Functional Classification"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000096,
        heiro:5000097,
        heiro:5000098 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001063"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "American Heart Association, American College of Cardiology and Heart Failure Society of America" ;
    heiro:2000016 "NYHA Class Is a Subjective Clinician Assessment That Predicts Mortality" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA heart failure guideline describes the NYHA functional class as a subjective assessment by a clinician that can change over time. It states that reproducibility and validity can be limited and that the class can be inconsistently assessed from one clinician to another, resulting in poor reproducibility. The guideline also states that the NYHA functional classification is an independent predictor of mortality and that it is widely used to determine eligibility for treatment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Heidenreich PA et al., 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guideline for the Management of Heart Failure, Circulation 2022, sections on stages and classification and on functional testing" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "nyha-functional-classification" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:nyha-functional-classification .

heiro:6000746 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Occupancy Rate"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng94/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Monitor Bed Occupancy in Real Time and Plan to Avoid Rates Above 90%" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's emergency and acute care guideline asks healthcare providers to monitor total acute hospital bed occupancy in real time, taking account of changes over 24 hours and the occupancy levels of specific wards and units, and to plan capacity to minimise the risks of occupancy rates above 90%. Its committee distinguished capacity, the number of beds, from occupancy, the proportion of beds filled, and chose 90% as a pragmatic maximum because adverse outcomes rose at that level. Local flexibility is allowed, with higher rates for efficiently managed elective pathways and lower rates where harm is evident." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG94, Emergency and acute medical care in over 16s: service delivery and organisation (published 28 March 2018), recommendation 1.3.1; evidence review 39 (Bed occupancy, March 2018), section 39.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "occupancy-rate" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:occupancy-rate .

heiro:6000747 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on One-Way Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Tornado Diagrams for Key Drivers and Switching Values for Uncertain Inputs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE treats univariate and best- or worst-case sensitivity analysis as an important way of identifying parameters with a substantial effect on cost-effectiveness results and explaining the drivers of a model, and suggests 'tornado' histograms for presenting them. Such analyses become increasingly unhelpful for representing combined uncertainty as the number of parameters grows, and deterministic analysis is less appropriate for decision making when a model is non-linear. Threshold analysis can identify a switching value, for example at £25,000 or £35,000 per QALY gained, and should show how far it lies from the current best estimate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.7.17, 4.7.21 and 4.7.22, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "one-way-sensitivity-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:one-way-sensitivity-analysis .

heiro:6000748 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on One-Way Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/RefCase_Sep2023_For-Publication_100124.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "One-Way Sensitivity Analyses Presented in Tornado Diagrams" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's reference case states that one-way sensitivity analyses should be conducted, with results presented in tornado diagrams that display the findings across a feasible range for each parameter estimate. The model should also provide threshold analyses of the intervention prices needed to reach 50,000,50,000, 50,000,100,000, 150,000and150,000 and 150,000and200,000 per QALY and per evLY gained. These deterministic analyses sit alongside a probabilistic sensitivity analysis that derives expected costs and outcomes for each intervention." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, current as of 23 October 2025, Analyses and Results: Uncertainty and Sensitivity Analyses (page 25)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "one-way-sensitivity-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:one-way-sensitivity-analysis .

heiro:6000749 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on One-Way Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-9-uncertainty-analysis-model-inputs-and-assumptions.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Univariate Analyses for All Uncertain Inputs, Shown in a Tornado Diagram" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC asks submissions to present univariate deterministic sensitivity analyses for all uncertain input parameters, or natural groups of parameters, with ranges defined by interval estimates such as 95% confidence intervals from fitted probability distributions. Where little is known about a parameter, a broad range should be used, and no parameter should be excluded from uncertainty analysis for lack of information. Results are tabulated alongside scenario analyses, and a tornado diagram shows the relative effect of each input on the base-case incremental cost-effectiveness result." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing submissions to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), sections 3A.9.1 and 3A.9.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "one-way-sensitivity-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:one-way-sensitivity-analysis .

heiro:6000750 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on One-Way Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Univariate Ranges Based on the Standard Error, With a Tornado Diagram" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland describes univariate analysis as the simplest deterministic uncertainty analysis, in which one parameter is varied within a range based on its standard error while the others are held constant. Without a standard error, expert elicitation is required for a parameter with a major effect on the outcomes, while a range of plus or minus 20% may be applied to one with only a minor effect. Reports give the lower and upper ICER for each parameter in a table and show the most influential parameters in a tornado diagram." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare, 2024 version, sections 4.7.2.1 and 5.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "one-way-sensitivity-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:one-way-sensitivity-analysis .

heiro:6000751 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on One-Way Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Deterministic Sensitivity Analyses Reported in a Table and Tornado Plot" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG states that health economic evaluations should include both univariate and multivariate deterministic sensitivity analyses, alongside probabilistic sensitivity analysis, to account for parameter uncertainty. The deterministic analyses aim to identify the parameters with particular influence on the result, such as transition probabilities, utilities and costs. Each analysis is documented with the minimum and maximum values used and the underlying assumptions, the results are presented in tabular form, and the influence of the varied parameters can be shown in a tornado plot." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 4.2 (Table 5) and section 4.11" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "one-way-sensitivity-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:one-way-sensitivity-analysis .

heiro:6000752 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on One-Way Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Univariate Deterministic Analyses to Find the Most Influential Parameters" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS states that univariate deterministic sensitivity analyses, or multivariate ones if necessary, should be conducted systematically to identify the parameters with the greatest impact on the results, as a complement to probabilistic sensitivity analysis. The choice of parameters and of the values tested, such as a 95% confidence interval or an arbitrary percentage variation, should be justified. Where a CEESP opinion is required, at least three prices below the base-case price should be tested, and a threshold analysis should be added where appropriate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in methods for economic evaluation, methodological guidance validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, Guideline 25 and section 4.5.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 6 ;
    heiro:2000020 "one-way-sensitivity-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:one-way-sensitivity-analysis .

heiro:6000753 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Opportunity Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Maximum Acceptable ICER Set by the Opportunity Cost of Displaced Care" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that, given the fixed NHS budget, decisions about a new technology must consider the programmes for other patient groups that may be displaced by its adoption, including those not evaluated by NICE. The appropriate maximum acceptable ICER is that of the opportunity cost of programmes displaced by new, more costly technologies, but because NICE lacks complete information on competing programmes it uses a range of £25,000 to £35,000 per QALY gained. When decision-making modifiers apply, net health benefits at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY should show the effect on opportunity costs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.2.25, 6.3.1, 6.3.2 and 6.3.4 to 6.3.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "opportunity-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:opportunity-cost .

heiro:6000754 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Opportunity Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/ICER_2023_2026_VAF_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Price Benchmarks Grounded in an Opportunity Cost Paradigm" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER favours cost-effectiveness thresholds based on an opportunity cost paradigm for conceptual and ethical reasons, and its Health Benefit Price Benchmark uses USD 100,000 to USD 150,000 per QALY and per evLY gained. Prices at or below this range are meant to ensure that patients' health gains are not outweighed by health losses from cost pressures that lead people to delay or abandon care or lose insurance. ICER cites an estimate of about USD 104,000 per QALY within this paradigm and handles other value elements through deliberation, as opportunity cost thresholds reflect trade-offs in health gain alone." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023), sections 3.3 and 3.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "opportunity-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:opportunity-cost .

heiro:6000755 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Opportunity Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240020689"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Every Benefit Package Decision Carries an Opportunity Cost" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's principles for health benefit packages state that every decision within a finite budget carries an opportunity cost, since funding one intervention means that other services, which might have produced more health, protected the most vulnerable from impoverishment or improved access for the least advantaged, will not be provided. Countries should therefore use available resources in the most efficient manner, achieving the greatest possible health benefits within the budget constraint. Where other criteria are given priority, the health loss resulting from the trade-off must be clearly communicated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Principles of health benefit packages (2021), ISBN 978-92-4-002068-9, principle 3, page 8" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "opportunity-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:opportunity-cost .

heiro:6000756 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Opportunity Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Costs Valued at Opportunity Cost, With Prices as Proxies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre states that costs in an economic evaluation are valued at opportunity costs. In practice these are approximated by market prices or reimbursement mechanisms, such as the Belgian per diem hospitalisation price, which are used in the reference case for consistency across analyses even though they do not always reflect real opportunity costs. Alternative cost estimates, for example from micro-costing, can be presented in scenario analyses with arguments for why they are more appropriate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (published 8 May 2025), section 5.9.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "opportunity-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:opportunity-cost .

heiro:6000757 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Outcome Measure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "QALY Measured With the EQ-5D" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Health effects are expressed in QALYs, with health-related quality of life measured using the EQ-5D as the preferred instrument and valued on public preferences via a choice-based method. In the reference case an additional QALY receives equal weight regardless of who receives the benefit." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Health Technology Evaluations: The Manual (PMG36), Section 4 (Economic Evaluation)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "outcome-measure" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:outcome-measure .

heiro:6000758 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Outcome Measure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "QALY-Based Cost-Utility Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The reference case is a cost-utility analysis with outcomes expressed as QALYs, combining length and health-related quality of life into a single measure." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH (now CDA-AMC), Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition (2017)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "outcome-measure" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:outcome-measure .

heiro:6000759 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Outcome Measure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "QALY via Cost-Utility Analysis (Productivity Excluded)" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Where a cost-utility analysis is used, health outcomes are expressed as QALYs; the reference case prioritises health effects and productivity changes must not be included in the primary analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC, Section 3A" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "outcome-measure" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:outcome-measure .

heiro:6000760 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Outcome Measure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/publications/publications/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "QALY via Cost-Utility Analysis (EQ-5D-5L, Dutch Value Set)" ;
    heiro:2000017 "New interventions should be assessed by cost-utility analysis with QALYs as the reference outcome; the EQ-5D-5L with the Dutch value set is the preferred instrument for computing QALYs." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for Economic Evaluations in Healthcare (2024)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "outcome-measure" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:outcome-measure .

heiro:6000761 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Outcome Measure"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/en/about-us/methods/methods-paper/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "QALY Not Adopted as Standard; Efficiency-Frontier Approach" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG does not accept the QALY as its standard outcome for cost-benefit assessment, citing unresolved methodological and distributive-justice concerns. It assesses quality of life separately (e.g. standard gamble, time trade-off, or instruments such as the EQ-5D) and judges cost relative to benefit using an efficiency frontier within each therapeutic area." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 7.0 (2023), Chapter on Health Economic Evaluation" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "outcome-measure" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:outcome-measure .

heiro:6000762 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality on Outcomes Research"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000046 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1320b-12"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Statutory Research on Outcomes, Effectiveness and Appropriateness of Care" ;
    heiro:2000017 "US federal law directs the Secretary of Health and Human Services, acting through the AHRQ Director, to conduct and support research on the outcomes, effectiveness and appropriateness of health care services and procedures. Priorities include conditions where many people could benefit, where services or outcomes vary widely among physicians and where spending is substantial, and the law also calls for research using claims data and patients' clinical and functional status. AHRQ's fact sheet describes outcomes research as seeking to understand the end results of care, including quality of life as well as mortality." ;
    heiro:2000018 "United States Code, Title 42, section 1320b-12 (Social Security Act section 1142, as added by Public Law 101-239, 19 December 1989), subsections (a) to (c); AHRQ, Outcomes Research Fact Sheet, AHRQ Publication No. 00-P011, March 2000" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "outcomes-research" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:outcomes-research .

heiro:6000763 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Pairwise Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pool Comparable Trials With a Forest Plot and an Assessment of Heterogeneity" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE considers combining outcome data through meta-analysis appropriate when there are enough relevant and valid data using comparable outcome measures. Each study's characteristics and limitations should be reported with a forest plot, and pooling should come with an assessment of heterogeneity, which a random rather than fixed effects model accounts for only to some extent, so its reasons should be explored through subgroup analysis, meta-regression or sensitivity analysis. Where a network meta-analysis is presented, pairwise meta-analyses of the direct comparisons should be shown alongside it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.4.8 to 3.4.10 and 3.4.20, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "pairwise-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:pairwise-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000764 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Pairwise Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Model Choice Set by the Number of Studies Pooled" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG chooses a random-effects model when heterogeneity cannot be excluded with sufficient certainty, and sets a standard approach by number of studies. With 2 studies it applies a fixed-effect model, using inverse variance for continuous data or Mantel-Haenszel for binary data. With 3 or 4 studies it uses, for common effect measures, a Bayesian random-effects meta-analysis with informative priors for heterogeneity, checked against a qualitative summary. With 5 or more it uses the Knapp-Hartung method with the Paule-Mandel heterogeneity estimator, keeping the pooled estimate only if it is informative." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 10.3.7" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "pairwise-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:pairwise-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000765 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Pairwise Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/4ec8288e-6d15-49c5-a490-d8ad7748578f_en?filename=hta_methodological-guideline_direct-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Knapp-Hartung as the Standard Random-Effects Method From Five Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EU HTA Coordination Group guideline treats direct comparisons as standard pairwise meta-analyses of trials comparing the treatment of interest with the same comparator, after checking similarity and homogeneity. For fixed-effect pooling the standard methods are inverse variance for continuous data and Mantel-Haenszel for binary data. DerSimonian-Laird is no longer recommended, and Knapp-Hartung with the Paule-Mandel estimator is the standard random-effects method with five or more studies, alongside prediction intervals. With fewer studies or rare events, alternatives include a qualitative summary, Bayesian methods or the beta-binomial model." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Methodological Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted by the HTA CG on 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, sections 4, 4.1 and 4.2 (Key Points 2)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "pairwise-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:pairwise-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000766 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Pairwise Meta-Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pairwise Meta-Analysis Where Several Comparable Trials Exist" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS regards randomised trials comparing the evaluated intervention directly with relevant comparators, together with pairwise meta-analyses, as the best source of evidence on comparative effectiveness in economic evaluations. Where several studies compare two interventions on the same criteria with comparable protocols, their estimates should be summarised by a pairwise meta-analysis conducted according to existing guidelines. A study that cannot be pooled should still be presented and used to discuss uncertainty, and when several sources inform one model parameter a pairwise meta-analysis should be preferred." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (methodological guidance), validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, section 2.2.2 (Guideline 13) and section 4.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "pairwise-meta-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:pairwise-meta-analysis .

heiro:6000767 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Parameter Input"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Parameters Tabulated, Plausible and Fully Represented in PSA" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE expects all model parameter values used in base-case, sensitivity, scenario and subgroup analyses to be clinically plausible and estimated with methods consistent with the data, and every parameter to be tabulated with its data source. The effect of choosing different data sources for key parameters, such as costs, utilities and relative effectiveness, should be explored with alternative data sets. Distributions for probabilistic analysis must represent the available evidence and be justified, formal elicitation can be used when data are lacking, and uncertainty about a parameter is not, in general, a reason to leave it out of the probabilistic analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.6.27, 4.6.28, 4.7.8, 4.7.10, 4.7.12 and 4.10.1, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "parameter-input" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:parameter-input .

heiro:6000768 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Parameter Input"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Ranges From Standard Errors, Elicitation or Plus or Minus 20%" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ZIN requires model input parameters to be estimated from empirical data with recommended statistical methods and, where no data exist, allows expert elicitation, which must use a structured method such as SHELF, Delphi or Cooke. In deterministic analyses each parameter is varied over a range based on its standard error; without one, expert elicitation is required for parameters with a major effect, while plus or minus 20% within logical limits suffices for those with a minor effect. Distributions for probabilistic analysis must be reported and justified, use the same ranges, and preserve correlations, for example through Cholesky decomposition." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), 16 January 2024, sections 3.4.2, 4.1.2, 4.3, 4.7.2.1 and 4.7.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "parameter-input" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:parameter-input .

heiro:6000769 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Parameter Input"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-9-uncertainty-analysis-model-inputs-and-assumptions.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Standard Distributions and Interval Ranges for Every Uncertain Input" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines ask for univariate sensitivity analyses on all uncertain input parameters, with uncertainty represented using common statistical standards: beta distributions for transition probabilities, log-normal for relative risks or hazard ratios, and gamma or log-normal for costs and utilities. Ranges for deterministic analysis should come from interval estimates, such as 95% confidence intervals, of the fitted distributions. Where little is known about a parameter a broad range should be used, and no parameter should be excluded from uncertainty analysis because there is too little information to estimate its uncertainty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0, September 2016, subsection 3A.9.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "parameter-input" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:parameter-input .

heiro:6000770 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Partitioned Survival Model"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/68713912"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "State and Justify the Independence of Survival Endpoints" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 19 found partitioned survival analysis in 22 of 30 NICE cancer appraisals reviewed. It recommends justifying the choice of approach, using consistent terminology without calling the method Markov-like, and stating that every endpoint, including overall survival, is modelled and extrapolated independently. Because the curves lack a structural link, extrapolation may be more uncertain, so all relevant evidence should inform the extrapolation period, and a state transition model may have advantages where intermediate states are prognostic." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 19: Partitioned survival analysis for decision modelling in health care: a critical review (Woods, Sideris, Palmer, Latimer and Soares, 2 June 2017), sections 3.2 and 6.1 to 6.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "partitioned-survival-model" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:partitioned-survival-model .

heiro:6000771 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of MHRA on Patient Information Leaflet"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000026 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7e30c440f0b62302689bec/Best_practice_guidance_on_patient_information_leaflets.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "MHRA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "User Testing Success Criterion of 16 out of 20 Participants" ;
    heiro:2000017 "MHRA best practice guidance explains that user testing of a patient information leaflet shows compliance with the legal duty to consult target patient groups. MHRA does not require a particular test method but looks for evidence that likely users can find and appropriately use the information. Over two rounds of 10 participants on the final leaflet, it expects 16 of 20 to find and understand the information, with each question meeting the criterion individually, and below that level it expects the leaflet to be revised and tested again." ;
    heiro:2000018 "MHRA, Best practice guidance on patient information leaflets, July 2012, section 7 (Compliance with article 59(3), user testing)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "patient-information-leaflet" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:patient-information-leaflet .

heiro:6000772 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of European Commission on Patient Information Leaflet"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000017 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/system/files/2016-11/2009_01_12_readability_guideline_final_en_0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "European Commission" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Readability Guideline on Type Size and User Testing of the Leaflet" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The European Commission readability guideline sets a minimum type size for the package leaflet of 9 points in Times New Roman, not narrowed, with at least 3 mm between lines. For user testing, a satisfactory outcome is that 90% of participants can find the requested information and 90% of those can show they understand it, which means 16 out of 20 participants for each question. The criterion must be met question by question, and results cannot be aggregated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Commission, Guideline on the readability of the labelling and package leaflet of medicinal products for human use, Revision 1, 12 January 2009, chapter 1 section A.1 and annex section 5" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "patient-information-leaflet" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:patient-information-leaflet .

heiro:6000773 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Patient Information Leaflet"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-208"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Medication Guides for Products of Serious Public Health Concern" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under FDA regulations, a Medication Guide is required for prescription products that FDA judges to pose a serious and significant public health concern, mainly those used as outpatients without direct professional supervision. Grounds include patient labelling that could help prevent serious adverse effects, serious risks that could affect a decision to use the product, and products important to health whose effectiveness depends on adherence to directions. The guide must be in nontechnical, understandable English, based on and not in conflict with the approved professional labelling, in type no smaller than 10 points." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Code of Federal Regulations, Title 21, Part 208 (Medication Guides for Prescription Drug Products), sections 208.1 and 208.20, eCFR text up to date as of 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "patient-information-leaflet" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:patient-information-leaflet .

heiro:6000774 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Patient Time Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reference Case Costs Exclude Unreimbursed Patient Costs" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE sets the reference-case perspective on costs as that of the NHS and personal social services (PSS), because its evaluations aim at efficient use of NHS and PSS resources. Costs paid by patients may be included when the NHS or PSS reimburses them, averaged across patients when reimbursement rates vary, and unreimbursed patient costs may be presented separately. Productivity costs are excluded from the reference case. The manual does not mention the value of patients' own time, which therefore falls outside the reference case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.7, 4.2.9 and 4.4.23, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "patient-time-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:patient-time-cost .

heiro:6000775 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Patient Time Cost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/RefCase_Sep2023_For-Publication_100124.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Patient Time Seeking Care Valued at a Post-Tax Wage of $41.08 per Hour" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's default base case takes a health care system perspective, and each analysis should add a modified societal perspective that, for most analyses, includes patient and transportation costs related to treatment. When direct data are lacking, patient time seeking care is estimated from a published US relationship between utility, age, disability and time use. ICER values that time at the post-tax wage plus fringe benefits, $41.08 per hour, on the assumption that it replaces leisure at the margin. Its impact inventory lists patient time costs in the informal health care sector." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, current as of 23 October 2025, section 1, Explanations (Perspective), Table 2 and Appendix B impact inventory" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "patient-time-cost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:patient-time-cost .

heiro:6000776 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on PedsQL 4.0 Generic Core Scales"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ta455/resources/adalimumab-etanercept-and-ustekinumab-for-treating-plaque-psoriasis-in-children-and-young-people-pdf-82604846770117"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "PedsQL Data Mapped to EQ-5D-Y When Psoriasis Trials Lacked EQ-5D-Y" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In its appraisal of adalimumab, etanercept and ustekinumab for plaque psoriasis in children and young people, NICE noted that the trials did not collect the EQ-5D-Y, a generic preference-based measure for young people, and reported only the Children's Dermatology Life Quality Index and the Pediatric Quality of Life Inventory (PedsQL). The assessment group's model derived EQ-5D-Y values from PedsQL scores in the CADMUS trial using a mapping algorithm, and its assessment report tabulated the best-fitting PedsQL to EQ-5D-Y algorithms from Khan and colleagues." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal guidance TA455, Adalimumab, etanercept and ustekinumab for treating plaque psoriasis in children and young people, section 4 (committee discussion), published 2017, and the CRD and CHE University of York assessment report, 2017" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "pedsql" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:pedsql .

heiro:6000777 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Performance Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Performance Bias Defined as Unequal Care Beyond the Intervention" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG defines performance bias as bias caused by different types of care provided, apart from the intervention under investigation, and names blinding as an effective protection against it. Its risk-of-bias assessment of controlled studies, which closely follows the Cochrane tool, includes blinding of patients and treating staff as an item across outcomes. Results of unblinded trials, or of trials where unblinding may have occurred, are interpreted more cautiously than those of blinded studies." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation published July 2026; the German original is authoritative), sections 10.1.4 and 10.3.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "performance-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:performance-bias .

heiro:6000778 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Performance Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/9f9dbfe4-078b-4959-9a07-df9167258772_en?filename=hta_clinical-studies-validity_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "RoB 1 Assessment Covering Performance Bias for Randomised Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For joint clinical assessments, the HTA Coordination Group requires risk of bias in randomised trials to be assessed with the Cochrane RoB 1 instrument, which covers performance bias alongside confounding, detection, attrition and selective reporting bias. Bias is judged separately by outcome where it differs, mainly because of lack of blinding or incomplete outcome data. The guidance drops the labels single-blinded and double-blinded as ambiguous and asks that the groups who were blinded be stated explicitly." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment, Guidance on Validity of Clinical Studies, V1.0, 4 July 2024, adopted 19 September 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, sections 3.1.1 and 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "performance-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:performance-bias .

heiro:6000779 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Performance Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/ich-e-9-statistical-principles-clinical-trials-step-5_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ICH E9 Treats Blinding as a Main Safeguard Against Trial Bias" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The ICH E9 guideline, adopted by EMA as a scientific guideline, states that blinding and randomisation are the most important design techniques for avoiding bias in clinical trials. Blinding aims to limit conscious and unconscious bias arising from knowledge of treatment, including its influence on subjects' subsequent care and their attitudes to the treatments. The double-blind trial is described as the optimal approach, and where only single-blind or open-label designs are feasible the protocol should explain the degree of blinding and other steps taken to minimise bias." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICH Topic E9 Statistical Principles for Clinical Trials, Step 5, CPMP/ICH/363/96, EMA scientific guideline, legal effective date 1 September 1998, sections 2.3 and 2.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "performance-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:performance-bias .

heiro:6000780 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Performance Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Blinding Counted in the Internal Validity of Trial Evidence" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that blinding, when applied, prevents knowledge of treatment allocation from influencing behaviours in randomised controlled trials. Its manual lists blinding among the features used to assess a trial's internal validity, together with the method of randomisation, concealment of allocation and completeness of follow up. NICE notes that blinding is often not possible in trials of specific medical devices or diagnostics, and requires all evidence to be critically appraised and potential biases identified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.3.4 to 3.3.6, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "performance-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:performance-bias .

heiro:6000781 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "NHS and PSS Perspective on Costs, All Relevant Health Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The reference case takes the perspective of the NHS and personal social services (PSS) on costs and counts all relevant health effects on outcomes, whether for patients or, when relevant, other people such as carers. NICE gives the reason that it does not set the NHS budget and aims to guide efficient use of available NHS and PSS resources. Productivity costs are excluded from the reference case, and costs to other government bodies are included only by agreement with the Department of Health and Social Care and reported separately." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.7, 4.2.9, 4.2.10, 4.4.22 and 4.4.23, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:perspective .

heiro:6000782 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Publicly Funded Health Care Payer Perspective" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The reference case adopts the perspective of the publicly funded health care payer; a broader societal perspective may be presented only as a non-reference-case analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH (now CDA-AMC), Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition (2017)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:perspective .

heiro:6000783 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-1-overview-and-rationale-of-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Care System Perspective" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The base-case analysis uses a health care system perspective, which covers health and health-related resource use and health-related outcomes. Costs include those incurred by the patient and by public or private health care providers, while outcomes are those associated with the patient. Costs and outcomes not specifically related to health or the provision of health care are excluded from the base case, and a broader societal perspective may be presented only as a supplementary analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Version 5.0 (September 2016), section 3A.1.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:perspective .

heiro:6000784 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Statutory Health Insurance (SHI) Insured Community Perspective" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In the reference case, the health economic evaluation takes the perspective of the community of people insured under statutory health insurance (SHI), as set out in German social law. This perspective counts direct reimbursable costs together with non-reimbursable time costs of insured persons or their relatives, while co-payments are shown separately and transfer payments such as sickness allowance are left out. Depending on the commission, the pure SHI, social insurance or societal perspective may also be considered, with those results provided separately." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 (19 December 2025), sections 4.3.4, 4.7.1 and 4.7.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:perspective .

heiro:6000785 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Collective Perspective" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The reference case analysis uses a collective perspective, covering all individuals and institutions affected by the production of an intervention within the scope of the overall care provided, including users and their informal caregivers, care providers and providers of medico-social aid. Production costs are valued independently of their sources of funding. A healthcare system perspective may be used instead only when the collective perspective cannot be documented, with justification, and the social perspective is not recommended for the reference case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation, methodological guidance validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, Guideline 3 and section 1.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:perspective .

heiro:6000786 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Societal Perspective" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Economic evaluations must be conducted and reported from a societal perspective, including all relevant costs and benefits irrespective of who bears the costs or to whom the benefits go. Relevant costs include costs in healthcare, costs for patients and families such as informal care, and costs in other sectors such as productivity losses. Analyses from other perspectives, such as the healthcare perspective, may be added as scenario analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), 16 January 2024, sections 2.2 and 3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 6 ;
    heiro:2000020 "perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:perspective .

heiro:6000787 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of TLV on Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000028 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.tlv.se/download/18.467926b615d084471ac3230c/1510316374332/TLVAR_2017_1.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "TLV" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Societal Perspective" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The health economic analysis should be carried out from a societal perspective, taking into account all relevant costs and revenues of treatment and illness regardless of whether they fall on a county council, a municipality, the state, the patient or a relative. Input data should describe Swedish conditions. Costs should be calculated both with and without indirect costs such as lost production, estimated with the human capital method." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Tandvårds- och läkemedelsförmånsverkets allmänna råd om ekonomiska utvärderingar (TLVAR 2003:2), as amended and reprinted by TLVAR 2017:1, in force from 15 February 2017, points 2 and 6" ;
    heiro:2000019 7 ;
    heiro:2000020 "perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:perspective .

heiro:6000788 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Care System Perspective With Modified Societal Co-Base Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The conventional base case takes a health care system perspective, including all direct health care costs and effects, mainly those borne by third-party payers or integrated health systems. Each analysis should also include a modified societal perspective covering impacts beyond direct health care, such as patient and caregiver productivity and caregiver disutility. The two are presented together as a co-base case when direct data on societal effects or costs are available and their impact, or their contribution to the condition's total effects or costs, is judged substantial." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, version 2.2, current as of 23 October 2025, Perspective section" ;
    heiro:2000019 8 ;
    heiro:2000020 "perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:perspective .

heiro:6000789 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on PHQ-9"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2018/06/nhs-talking-therapies-manual-v7.1-updated.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "PHQ-9 Cut-Off of 10 and Reliable Change of 6 Define Recovery in NHS Talking Therapies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NHS England manual for NHS Talking Therapies for anxiety and depression uses the PHQ-9 as the depression measure in its outcome definitions. Table 9 sets a clinical cut-off of 10 and a reliable change index of 6 for the PHQ-9, and a cut-off of 8 and an index of 4 for the GAD-7. Recovery, reliable improvement, reliable recovery and reliable deterioration are calculated on a pair of measures, the PHQ-9 with the anxiety measure suited to the presenting problem, with the GAD-7 used when that measure is missing. A person has reliably recovered when meeting the criteria for both recovery and reliable improvement." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS Talking Therapies for anxiety and depression manual, version 7 (gateway reference 08101), first published June 2018, updated March 2024, Table 9 (clinical cut-offs and reliable change index) and the outcome calculation guidance" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "phq-9" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:phq-9 .

heiro:6000790 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Pigouvian Tax"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/317ba614-5295-4f6f-baba-56f281f228f9/content"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Sugary Drink Excise Taxes as Pigouvian Taxes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The WHO describes a tax that internalises costs to bring consumption of sugar-sweetened beverages to socially optimal levels as a Pigouvian tax. It rests the economic case on negative externalities, including lost productivity from diet-related noncommunicable diseases and treatment costs where health care is publicly funded, and on internalities, where people do not account for the future costs of current consumption. It treats excise taxes as the most important form of consumption tax for health objectives, and notes that taxes levied on sugar content can also encourage producers to reformulate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "WHO manual on sugar-sweetened beverage taxation policies to promote healthy diets, World Health Organization, 2022 (ISBN 9789240056299), sections 1.2.2, 1.2.3 and 4.1.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "pigouvian-tax" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:pigouvian-tax .

heiro:6000791 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Pigouvian Tax"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/increasing-the-rates-of-the-soft-drinks-industry-levy/soft-drinks-industry-levy-uprating"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Soft Drinks Industry Levy Designed to Encourage Reformulation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HM Treasury and HM Revenue and Customs present the Soft Drinks Industry Levy as a measure to reduce childhood obesity by removing added sugar from soft drinks, with producers able to pay less or avoid the charge by reformulating or reducing portion sizes. A lower rate applies at 5 to 7.9 grams of total sugar per 100 millilitres and a higher rate at 8 grams or more. From 1 April 2026 the rates are £2.08 and £2.78 per 10 litres, and they are uprated each year in line with the Consumer Price Index so that the levy keeps encouraging reformulation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Revenue and Customs and HM Treasury, Soft Drinks Industry Levy, policy paper, 5 December 2016, Policy objective; Increasing the rates of the Soft Drinks Industry Levy, policy paper, 30 October 2024, General description of the measure and Policy objective; Finance Act 2017, section 36(1), as amended by Finance Act 2026, section 108" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "pigouvian-tax" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:pigouvian-tax .

heiro:6000792 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Population"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/the-scope-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Scope Defines the Population as Precisely as Possible" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states that the scope defines the population for whom a technology is evaluated as precisely as possible, including where, why and how the technology is used in the care pathway, and may highlight subgroups whose clinical or cost effectiveness might differ. For medicines the relevant population is normally informed by the expected marketing authorisation, and people left out of a scope to keep the evaluation manageable are not thereby judged unsuitable for the technology. Cost-utility analyses should give separate estimates for each relevant subgroup, defined preferably by an expected difference in effect with a clear and plausible rationale." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 2.2.9 to 2.2.11 and 4.9.1 to 4.9.3, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "population" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:population .

heiro:6000793 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Population"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/7be11d76-9a78-426c-8e32-79d30a115a64_en?filename=hta_jca_scoping-process_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Member States Define Populations and Subpopulations in the PICO" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For joint clinical assessments, each Member State identifies the relevant populations from the therapeutic indication applied for, or a device's intended use, and its own healthcare situation, covering the full population applied for and any relevant subpopulations. Population definitions should be as specific as possible and avoid ambiguity. A separate PICO for a subpopulation can be justified where different comparators apply, where the indication explicitly comprises different groups, or where different prognoses imply different effectiveness, and Member States can also request subgroup analyses to explore effect modifiers within a population." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment (HTACG), Guidance on the scoping process, V1.0 of 13 November 2024 (adopted 28 November 2024), section 3.1.4 and glossary" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "population" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:population .

heiro:6000794 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Population"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-3-population-and-setting.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Modelled Population Must Represent the Target Australian Population" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The PBAC guidelines ask that the modelled population in an economic evaluation represent the target Australian population for the proposed medicine, with circumstances of use consistent with the clinical management algorithm and the requested restriction. Submissions describe the population's age, sex, comorbidities and disease characteristics with measures of spread, preferably from Australian data, and justify how heterogeneity is represented in the analysis. Where trial populations differ from the Australian setting, a translation study should test whether model inputs need adjusting, taking particular care when baseline risks differ." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0, September 2016, subsections 1.1.2, 3A.3.1 and 3A.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "population" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:population .

heiro:6000795 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Preference Elicitation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Public Preferences From a Choice-Based Method Value Reference-Case Health Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case asks for the valuation of health-related quality of life measured by patients or their carers to be based on public preferences from a representative sample of the UK population, using a choice-based method. Health effects are expressed in QALYs, with the EQ-5D preferred in adults, so the preference elicitation behind the value set supplies reference-case utility weights. From 27 August 2026 the EQ-5D-5L descriptive system and the UK value set apply to adult reference-case analyses, and the manual's EQ-5D sections are due for revision." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section 4.2 (table 4.1) and section 4.3.4, last updated 13 October 2026; read with the interim methods statement PMG51, 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "preference-elicitation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:preference-elicitation .

heiro:6000796 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Preference Elicitation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/incorporating-voluntary-patient-preference-information-over-total-product-life-cycle"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Patient Preference Information Accepted Across the Device Life Cycle" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The FDA defines patient preference information as qualitative or quantitative assessments of the relative desirability or acceptability to patients of specified alternatives or choices among outcomes or other attributes of health interventions. Its final guidance of March 2026 supersedes the 2016 guidance on voluntary submission in premarket approval, humanitarian device exemption and De Novo reviews, and extends the use of such information to investigational device exemptions, Breakthrough Device requests and 510(k) submissions. Methods may be qualitative, quantitative or mixed, and the FDA separates such information from patient-reported outcomes measured in clinical trials." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Incorporating Voluntary Patient Preference Information over the Total Product Life Cycle, final guidance, March 2026 (Federal Register notice 30 March 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "preference-elicitation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:preference-elicitation .

heiro:6000797 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Premarket Approval"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/premarket-submissions-selecting-and-preparing-correct-submission/premarket-approval-pma"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Class III Devices Need Premarket Approval Before Marketing" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The FDA describes premarket approval as its scientific and regulatory review of the safety and effectiveness of Class III medical devices, which support or sustain human life, are of substantial importance in preventing impairment of human health, or present a potential unreasonable risk of illness or injury. It calls PMA the most stringent type of device marketing application. The applicant must obtain approval before marketing the device, and approval rests on a finding of sufficient valid scientific evidence that the device is safe and effective for its intended use." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Premarket Approval (PMA) web page, Overview, content current as of 16 May 2019" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "premarket-approval" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:premarket-approval .

heiro:6000798 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Primary Research"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Recommendations Only in Research or With Managed Access" ;
    heiro:2000017 "When evidence on clinical effectiveness is absent, weak or uncertain, NICE committees may recommend that a technology is used only while more research is done, after weighing the value of further evidence, the feasibility of the research and how long findings will take. Managed access, for medicines only, applies when new evidence is expected from ongoing or planned trials or from patients in clinical practice and could be collected within at most 5 years without undue burden. A research-only recommendation carries no requirement for NHS funding." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.4.6, 6.4.11 and 6.4.14, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "primary-research" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:primary-research .

heiro:6000799 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Property Rights"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/docs/default-source/documents/global-strategy-plan-action.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Intellectual Property Rights as an Incentive, Bounded by Public Health" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's global strategy on public health, innovation and intellectual property, adopted by the World Health Assembly in 2008 and extended to 2030, treats intellectual property rights as an important incentive for developing new health products, but holds that this incentive alone does not meet the need where the potential paying market is small or uncertain. It states that intellectual property rights do not and should not prevent member states from taking measures to protect public health, and calls for developing countries to make full use of the TRIPS Agreement flexibilities that allow such measures." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Global strategy and plan of action on public health, innovation and intellectual property (resolutions WHA61.21 and WHA62.16; mandate extended to 2030 by WHA75.14), paragraphs 7, 18 and 33" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "property-rights" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:property-rights .

heiro:6000800 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Proportion of Days Covered"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/files/document/2026-star-ratings-technical-notes.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "PDC of 80% or More Defines Adherence in Part D Star Ratings" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS's Part D Star Ratings measure adherence to diabetes medications, RAS antagonists and statins as the share of beneficiaries with a proportion of days covered of 80% or more. PDC is the share of days in the treatment period covered by claims for the same drug or another in its class, from the first fill, with at least two fills and a treatment period of at least 91 days. Overlapping fills of the same drug are shifted, and inpatient and skilled nursing stays are removed. Each adherence measure carries a weight of 3." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Medicare 2026 Part C & D Star Ratings Technical Notes (updated 25 September 2025), measures D08 to D10 and Attachment L; 2027 Star Ratings Measures and Weights, Table 2" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "proportion-of-days-covered" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:proportion-of-days-covered .

heiro:6000801 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Provider Payment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications-detail-redirect/analytical-guide-to-assess-a-mixed-provider-payment-system"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Assessing Mixed Provider Payment Systems in Five Steps" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's analytical guide notes that in nearly all countries several payment methods co-exist as a mixed provider payment system, each creating incentives, chiefly towards over-provision or under-provision, shaped by the payment unit, rate, basis and timing. Misaligned methods and rates send incoherent signals that can undermine universal health coverage objectives. The guide proposes a five-step assessment: mapping purchasers, providers and payment methods; assessing incentives and their effects on provider behaviour; other health system effects; governance arrangements; and policy options to align payment methods within and across purchasers." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Analytical guide to assess a mixed provider payment system (Mathauer and Dkhimi), Health Financing Guidance No. 5, WHO/UHC/HGF/Guidance/19.5 (2019), Part 1 and Box 1.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "provider-payment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:provider-payment .

heiro:6000802 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on Provider Payment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/PRN02348-26-27-nhs-payment-scheme-pay-award-august-update.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "The 2026/27 NHS Payment Scheme and Its Four Payment Mechanisms" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS England's 2026/27 NHS Payment Scheme sets rules for four payment mechanisms for NHS-funded secondary care. Aligned payment and incentive covers almost all NHS provider relationships with expected annual activity above £1.5 million: a fixed element based on planned activity, with elective and community diagnostic activity paid on top at national unit prices. Smaller relationships receive low volume activity block payments, non-NHS providers are paid for activity at scheme unit prices, and remaining activity falls to local payment arrangements." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England, 2026/27 NHS Payment Scheme, August 2026 update (publication reference PRN02348), sections 1, 4, 5, 6 and 7" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "provider-payment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:provider-payment .

heiro:6000803 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services on Provider Payment"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cms.gov/medicare/quality/value-based-programs"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Linking Medicare Payment to Quality Through Value-Based Programmes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS describes its value-based programmes as rewarding providers with incentive payments for the quality of care given to people with Medicare, moving towards paying for quality rather than quantity. Its five original programmes link performance on quality measures to payment. Hospital Value-Based Purchasing withholds 2% of participating hospitals' Medicare payments and uses the total to fund incentive payments applied to diagnosis-related group payments, while the Hospital Readmissions Reduction Program can reduce a hospital's payments by up to 3%." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Value-based programs web pages: overview (last modified 10 March 2026), Hospital Value-Based Purchasing Program and Hospital Readmissions Reduction Program (accessed 3 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "provider-payment" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:provider-payment .

heiro:6000804 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "PSA Preferred in the Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Probabilistic sensitivity analysis is preferred in the reference case, characterising parameter uncertainty through assigned distributions so it is reflected simultaneously in results; NICE has required PSA in cost-effectiveness models since 2004, with results typically shown via cost-effectiveness acceptability curves." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Health Technology Evaluations: The Manual (PMG36), Section 4 (Economic Evaluation)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "psa" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:psa .

heiro:6000805 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/publications/publications/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Probabilistic Analysis Required; EVPI/EVPPI Mandatory" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Probabilistic analysis forms the basis of the reference-case uncertainty assessment; in the 2024 guideline the estimation and reporting of value-of-information measures (EVPI and EVPPI), which build on the probabilistic analysis, became mandatory parts of the reference case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for Economic Evaluations in Healthcare (2024)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "psa" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:psa .

heiro:6000806 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-8-results-of-the-base-case-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Stepped Deterministic Base Case With Sensitivity Analyses" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The base-case economic evaluation is presented using a stepped, deterministic approach, with sensitivity analyses (including probabilistic where appropriate) focused on the steps and assumptions that most influence the ICER, such as extrapolation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC, Section 3A.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "psa" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:psa .

heiro:6000807 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Probabilistic Sensitivity Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Reference-Case-4.3.25.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Deterministic and Probabilistic Sensitivity Analyses Required" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The reference case requires both deterministic and probabilistic sensitivity analyses to characterise uncertainty, with particular attention to inputs whose variation moves the result across the 100,000–100,000–100,000–150,000 per QALY/evLYG threshold range." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, ICER Reference Case (2025)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "psa" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:psa .

heiro:6000808 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Quality-Adjusted Life Year (QALY)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "QALYs in Health Technology Evaluation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case requires health effects to be expressed in QALYs, with changes in health-related quality of life reported by patients and valued using choice-based public preferences from a representative UK sample. The EQ-5D is the preferred adult measure, and for new topics starting after 27 August 2026 utilities come from the EQ-5D-5L and the new UK 5L value set instead of mapping to the 3L value set. All QALYs carry equal weight in the reference case, although committees may weight QALYs by 1.2 or 1.7 for more severe conditions, judged by absolute and proportional QALY shortfall." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.3.1 to 4.3.6, 6.2.10 and 6.2.12 to 6.2.18, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE Interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51), sections 1.6, 1.7 and 1.17, published 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "qaly" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:qaly .

heiro:6000809 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Canada's Drug Agency on Quality-Adjusted Life Year (QALY)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Canada's Drug Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "QALYs in economic evaluation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Canadian guidance uses QALYs to support comparison across interventions while requiring transparent methods, assumptions and uncertainty analysis. Interpretation remains part of a broader decision process rather than a calculation-only rule." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "qaly" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:qaly .

heiro:6000810 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EuroQol Research Foundation on Quality-Adjusted Life Year (QALY)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000099 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://euroqol.org/information-and-support/resources/value-sets/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EuroQol Research Foundation" ;
    heiro:2000016 "EQ-5D Instruments and Value Sets" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EQ-5D is the EuroQol family of health-state instruments, with adult EQ-5D-3L and EQ-5D-5L versions; the EQ-5D-5L describes mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain or discomfort and anxiety or depression at five levels each. EuroQol defines value sets as country-specific lists of utilities for every EQ-5D health state, anchored at 1 for full health and 0 for dead. They usually come from general-population studies, so they reflect societal preferences and differ by country, and EuroQol states that EQ-5D values may be used to calculate QALYs for health technology assessment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EuroQol Research Foundation, Value sets for scoring the EQ-5D and EQ-5D-5L instrument pages, accessed 29 September 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "qaly" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:qaly .

heiro:6000811 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Quality-Adjusted Life Year (QALY)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Cost per QALY and Cost per evLY as Co-Primary Outcomes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's reference case reports incremental cost per QALY gained and cost per equal value life year (evLY) gained as the primary cost-utility outcomes. The evLY applies the same quality-of-life value to every period of life extension, so a year of life added for people with a disability or serious illness counts the same as a year added for healthier people. Citing concerns about how the QALY values life extension, ICER gives the evLY the main role in its health benefit price benchmark while keeping QALY results for comparison with academic and international work." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, current as of 23 October 2025, sections Outcomes and Quantifying Unmet Need (QALY and evLY Shortfalls); ICER, Cost-Effectiveness, the QALY, and the evLY (web page)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "qaly" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:qaly .

heiro:6000812 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of US Congress on Quality-Adjusted Life Year (QALY)"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000100 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1320e-1"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "US Congress" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Statutory Limits on Cost per QALY Thresholds in Medicare" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the Affordable Care Act, the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute may not develop or use a dollars-per-QALY measure, or a similar measure that discounts the value of a life because of disability, as a threshold for deciding what care is cost effective or recommended. The Secretary of Health and Human Services may not use such a threshold to determine Medicare coverage, reimbursement or incentive programmes. The Inflation Reduction Act's drug price negotiation provisions separately bar using comparative effectiveness evidence in a way that values extending the lives of elderly, disabled or terminally ill people less than others." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Social Security Act section 1182(e), added by the Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (Pub. L. 111-148) section 6301(c), 42 U.S.C. 1320e-1(e); Social Security Act section 1194(e)(2), added by the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022 (Pub. L. 117-169) section 11001(a), 42 U.S.C. 1320f-3(e)(2)" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "qaly" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:qaly .

heiro:6000813 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on QALY Shortfall"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Severity Measured as QALY Shortfall Against a Matched General Population" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE defines severity as the future health lost by people living with a condition with standard care in the NHS. It compares remaining lifetime QALYs under current treatment with those of a general population of the same age and sex distribution, as absolute and proportional QALY shortfall. Whichever measure implies greater severity sets a QALY weight of 1, 1.2 or 1.7. NICE's interim methods statement PMG51 (paragraph 1.12) says the EQ-5D-5L value set should also be used for general population utilities in the shortfall, for topics that start evaluation after its publication on 27 August 2026; evaluations already under way keep EQ-5D-3L values." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section 6.2 and table 6.1, last updated 13 October 2026; interim methods statement PMG51, Statement on using the EQ-5D-5L value set, paragraph 1.12, published 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "qaly-shortfall" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:qaly-shortfall .

heiro:6000814 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Quality Score"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg20/resources/appendix-h-appraisal-checklists-evidence-tables-grade-and-economic-profiles-pdf-8779777885"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Economic Studies Rated by Overall Judgement on Applicability and Limitations" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's guidelines manual appraises each economic evaluation with a methodology checklist and then reaches two overall categorical judgements instead of a summed score. Applicability is classed as directly, partially or not applicable, and methodological quality as minor, potentially serious or very serious limitations. Each category depends on how likely it is that failing one or more criteria would change the conclusions about cost effectiveness. Studies judged not applicable would usually be excluded from further consideration." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Developing NICE guidelines: the manual (PMG20), appendix H Appraisal checklists, evidence tables, GRADE and economic profiles, economic evaluation checklist sections 1.8 and 2.12; manual last updated 23 October 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "quality-score" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:quality-score .

heiro:6000815 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Quality Score"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/9f9dbfe4-078b-4959-9a07-df9167258772_en?filename=hta_clinical-studies-validity_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Risk of Bias Judged by Domain With an Explicit Overall Rating" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's guidance for joint clinical assessments requires standardised risk of bias tools, the Cochrane RoB 1 instrument for randomised trials and ROBINS-I for non-randomised comparisons. It notes that many published instruments fail to assess internal validity alone and mix in other concepts such as quality of reporting. Judgements are made by bias domain, and the summary rating of low, unclear or high risk follows explicit logic in which an overall low rating requires low risk in all key domains." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidance on Validity of Clinical Studies, V1.0, adopted by the HTA CG on 19 September 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, sections 4, 4.1 and 4.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "quality-score" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:quality-score .

heiro:6000816 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of United Nations Statistics Division on Random Sampling"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000101 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://unstats.un.org/unsd/demographic/sources/surveys/Handbook23June05.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "United Nations Statistics Division" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Probability Sampling Strongly Recommended for Household Surveys" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The UN Statistics Division's household survey handbook sets three requirements for probability sampling, namely that each element has a known chance of selection, that the chance is greater than zero and that it can be calculated, although chances need not be equal. It strongly recommends always using probability sampling, even when it costs more, because only then can estimates be inferred to the population and sampling errors be estimated. Quota, convenience, purposive and random walk samples give biased estimates whose size and often direction are unknown." ;
    heiro:2000018 "United Nations Department of Economic and Social Affairs, Statistics Division, Designing Household Survey Samples: Practical Guidelines, Studies in Methods Series F No. 98, 2005, chapter 3, sections 3.2.1 and 3.2.2 (paragraphs 7 to 9 and 18)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "random-sampling" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:random-sampling .

heiro:6000817 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality on Random Sampling"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000046 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://meps.ahrq.gov/data_files/publications/mr38/mr38.shtml"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality" ;
    heiro:2000016 "MEPS Analyses Must Reflect Its Complex Probability Sample Design" ;
    heiro:2000017 "AHRQ states that because the Medical Expenditure Panel Survey is a complex probability sample with stratification, clustering and unequal selection probabilities, methods that assume a simple random sample are generally not appropriate and can give biased estimates and inaccurate significance levels. Valid estimates and standard errors must use the survey weights and the design's strata and clusters. Standard software that assumes simple random sampling will usually underestimate the variance, so AHRQ supplies strata and cluster variables for Taylor series linearisation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "AHRQ, Methodology Report #38: Estimation Procedures for the 2023 Medical Expenditure Panel Survey Household Component, AHRQ Publication No. 26-0018, August 2026, sections 1 and 6 (Variance Estimation)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "random-sampling" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:random-sampling .

heiro:6000818 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Real-World Evidence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd9"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Real-World Evidence Framework (2022) to Improve Quality, Not Set Minimum Standards" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE’s Real-World Evidence Framework (published June 2022, applied in appraisals since 2023) encourages registry, claims, and other real-world data to supplement RCTs across many use cases (including external controls for single-arm trials), and gives guidance on study design, data quality, and transparent reporting. It is explicitly aimed at improving quality rather than setting minimum acceptability standards." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Real-World Evidence Framework (Corporate Document ECD9, 2022)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "real-world-evidence" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:real-world-evidence .

heiro:6000819 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Real-World Evidence"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Guidance for Reporting Real-World Evidence (2023)" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CADTH has published guidance to standardise the planning, conduct, and transparent reporting of real-world evidence studies used to inform reimbursement decisions in Canada, emphasising methodological rigour and reporting quality." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH (now CDA-AMC), Guidance for Reporting Real-World Evidence (MG0020, 2023)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "real-world-evidence" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:real-world-evidence .

heiro:6000820 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Reference Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Table 4.1 Reference Case With EQ-5D-5L for New Topics From 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Economic evaluations considered by NICE should include an analysis using reference-case methods, and any additional analyses must be justified and clearly distinguished from it. The reference case covers all health effects for patients and, when relevant, carers; NHS and personal social services costs; cost-utility analysis with QALYs; a time horizon long enough to reflect all important differences; and a 3.5% annual discount rate for costs and health effects. From 27 August 2026 the EQ-5D-5L and its UK value set are preferred for adults in new topics, under an interim methods statement (PMG51) that takes precedence over the manual." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.1 to 4.2.3 (table 4.1), last updated 31 March 2026; NICE interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51), sections 1.7 and 1.17, published 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-case .

heiro:6000821 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Canada’s Drug Agency on Reference Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Canada’s Drug Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Canadian economic-evaluation reference case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Canadian guidance provides a reference case while requiring transparent justification of methods, assumptions and uncertainty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-case .

heiro:6000822 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Reference Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Care System Reference Case Aligned With the Second Panel" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER defines a reference case setting out the components, methods and reporting elements for its cost-effectiveness and potential budget impact analyses, generally aligned with the Second Panel's health care perspective reference case. The default is the health care system perspective over a lifetime horizon, with costs and outcomes discounted at 3% a year and results reported per QALY and per evLY gained. A modified societal perspective is presented as a co-base case when relevant data are available and societal effects or costs are judged substantial, and any deviation must be specified and justified in the model analysis plan." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale, version 2.2, current as of 23 October 2025, Overview and Table 1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-case .

heiro:6000823 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Reference Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-1-overview-and-rationale-of-economic-evaluation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Base Case Using a Health Care System Perspective and 5% Discount Rate" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC expresses its reference-case requirements through the base case of a cost-effectiveness submission. The base case takes the PBAC's preferred health care system perspective, with broader societal effects presented only as supplementary analyses, and a cost-utility analysis is preferred over a cost-effectiveness analysis, particularly where life-years are gained. Costs and outcomes beyond one year are discounted at a uniform 5% a year, with sensitivity analyses at 3.5% and 0%." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing submissions to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee (PBAC), version 5.0 (September 2016), sections 3A.1.2, 3A.1.4 and 3A.1.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-case .

heiro:6000824 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Reference Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Societal Perspective Reference Case With Mandatory Value of Information" ;
    heiro:2000017 "All economic evaluations carried out for decision-making in the Netherlands must comply with the reference case, and other choices may appear only as justified scenario analyses that cannot replace it. The reference case takes a societal perspective, a lifetime horizon for model-based evaluations, cost-utility analysis with QALYs from the EQ-5D-5L with Dutch valuations, and discounting at 3% for costs and 1.5% for effects. Value of information analysis, as EVPI and EVPPI, is obligatory for model-based evaluations." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland (National Health Care Institute), Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare, 2024 version (published 16 January 2024), chapter 1 (table 2), sections 2.2, 4.2 and 4.7.2.4" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-case .

heiro:6000825 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Second Panel on Reference Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000102 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2016.12195"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Second Panel" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Two Reference Cases: Health Care Sector and Societal Perspectives" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Second Panel on Cost-Effectiveness in Health and Medicine recommends a reference case, a set of standard methodological practices that all cost-effectiveness analyses should follow to improve quality and comparability. Every analysis should report 2 reference case analyses, one from a health care sector perspective and one from a societal perspective. An impact inventory, a structured table of consequences inside and outside the formal health care sector, should clarify the scope of both. The recommendations are consensus methodological guidance, not a reimbursement rule." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Sanders GD, Neumann PJ, Basu A, et al. Recommendations for Conduct, Methodological Practices, and Reporting of Cost-effectiveness Analyses: Second Panel on Cost-Effectiveness in Health and Medicine. JAMA 2016;316(10):1093-1103, Findings and Conclusions" ;
    heiro:2000019 6 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-case .

heiro:6000826 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of International Decision Support Initiative (iDSI) on Reference Case"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000103 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.idsihealth.org/resource-items/idsi-reference-case-for-economic-evaluation/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "International Decision Support Initiative (iDSI)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "iDSI Reference Case: 11 Principles for Low- and Middle-Income Countries" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The iDSI reference case, commissioned by the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation, guides economic evaluations that inform health spending decisions in low- and middle-income countries. It comprises 11 principles, covering transparency, comparators, perspective, outcome and cost measurement, time horizon, costs and effects outside health, heterogeneity, uncertainty, budget impacts and equity, each with methodological specifications and reporting standards. It does not fix particular metrics or parameter values, and its authors describe it as an aid to thought that should not be followed slavishly without regard to context." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Wilkinson T, Sculpher MJ, Claxton K, et al. The International Decision Support Initiative Reference Case for Economic Evaluation: An Aid to Thought. Value in Health 2016;19(8):921-928; iDSI Reference Case for Economic Evaluation web page (The principles)" ;
    heiro:2000019 7 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-case" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-case .

heiro:6000827 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Reference Case Requirement"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reference Case Requirements Set Out Element by Element in Table 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE states its reference case as a table in which each row pairs one element of the analysis with the expected method. Health effects for patients and, when relevant, carers are counted, costs take the NHS and personal social services perspective, the time horizon reflects all important differences in costs or outcomes, health effects are expressed in QALYs with the EQ-5D preferred in adults, and costs and health effects share an annual discount rate of 3.5%, with 1.5% considered only as a non-reference case under three conditions in section 4.5.3. Departures are allowed in additional analyses when justified and kept apart from the reference case. The EQ-5D row is due for revision after PMG51." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section 4.2 and table 4.1, last updated 13 October 2026; read with the interim methods statement PMG51, 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-case-requirement" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-case-requirement .

heiro:6000828 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Reference Case Requirement"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Health Care System Perspective and 3% Discounting in the Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER takes the health care system perspective by default and adds a modified societal perspective, which can become a co-base case when societal effects are substantial and direct evidence exists. Costs and outcomes are discounted at 3% a year. The reference case reports costs with life-years, QALYs and equal value life-years gained, so a submission prepared under another body's requirements, such as NICE's 3.5% rate, needs its discounting and perspective changed before it meets the ICER reference case." ;
    heiro:2000018 "ICER, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations, current as of 23 October 2025, Perspective and Discounting" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-case-requirement" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-case-requirement .

heiro:6000829 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CDA-AMC on Reference Case Requirement"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cadth.ca/sites/default/files/pdf/guidelines_for_the_economic_evaluation_of_health_technologies_canada_4th_ed.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CDA-AMC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Public Payer Perspective and 1.5% Discount Rate in the Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Canadian guidelines set a reference case that takes the publicly funded health care payer perspective, asks for a cost-utility analysis with outcomes in QALYs and uses a time horizon long enough to capture all relevant differences in future costs and outcomes. Costs and outcomes beyond one year are discounted at a real rate of 1.5% a year, linked to the long-term cost of borrowing for Canadian provinces. Non-reference-case analyses are expected at 0% and at 3%, and the earlier third edition used 5%." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH, Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th edition, March 2017, reference case (perspective, time horizon, discounting)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-case-requirement" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-case-requirement .

heiro:6000830 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Reference Standard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Accuracy Studies Judge Index Tests Against a Reference Standard" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's technology appraisal manual describes diagnostic test accuracy studies as evaluating one or more index tests against a reference standard that classifies people as having or not having the target condition. Comparative accuracy studies assess two or more index tests against a single reference standard, usually in the same people. The manual lists differences in reference standard among the likely sources of heterogeneity in meta-analysis of test accuracy, and asks for the choice of synthesis method to be justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.3.16 and 3.4.23, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-standard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-standard .

heiro:6000831 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Reference Standard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reference Test Errors and Verification Patterns as Sources of Bias" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG, which uses the term reference test, states that accuracy estimates for an index test may be biased if the reference test does not correctly distinguish sick from healthy participants, if only a non-random sample receives it, or if the reference test applied depends on the index test result. Including the index test within the reference test may overstate accuracy. For linked evidence, the same reference test should ideally verify results in the accuracy study and serve as the inclusion criterion in the treatment study." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of Allgemeine Methoden 8.0), sections 3.5.1.2 and 10.3.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-standard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-standard .

heiro:6000832 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Reference Standard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-clinical-evaluation-diagnostic-agents-revision-1_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Standard of Truth Required for Confirmatory Diagnostic Agent Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EMA's guideline on diagnostic agents uses the term standard of truth for the reference against which diagnostic performance is judged, and treats one as required in general. Its choice must be justified, and in confirmatory trials it should be established independently of the investigational agent and comparator results, without including information from the agent under test. Where no standard of truth is available, a surrogate standard, such as a combination of tests, clinical data and follow-up, may be used if fully described and justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "EMA CHMP Guideline on clinical evaluation of diagnostic agents (CPMP/EWP/1119/98/Rev. 1), adopted 23 July 2009, in effect from 1 February 2010 and currently under revision, sections 5.4.1 to 5.4.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-standard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-standard .

heiro:6000833 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Reference Standard"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/71147/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "FDA Adopts STARD Definition and Asks Sponsors to Consult on Choice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA's diagnostic test statistics guidance adopts the STARD definition of a reference standard as the best available method for establishing the presence or absence of the target condition. It may be a single test or a combination of methods, including clinical follow-up, in which case the algorithm combining the results is part of the standard. Where no consensus standard exists, or it is known to be in error for a non-negligible share of the intended use population, sponsors should consult FDA before the study begins." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA Guidance for Industry and FDA Staff, Statistical Guidance on Reporting Results from Studies Evaluating Diagnostic Tests, issued 13 March 2007, section 3 (Benchmarks; Diagnostic accuracy and the reference standard)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reference-standard" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reference-standard .

heiro:6000834 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NHS England on ReQoL-10"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000019 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.england.nhs.uk/long-read/commissioner-guidance-adult-community-mental-health-rehabilitation-services/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NHS England" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ReQoL-10 in the Recommended Outcome Suite for Community Mental Health Services" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NHS England's commissioner guidance for adult community mental health rehabilitation services states that DIALOG, the 10-item Recovering Quality of Life scale (ReQoL-10) and goal-based outcomes are the recommended suite of measures for community mental health services. Within that suite the ReQoL-10 is the patient-reported measure of recovery-focused quality of life, alongside DIALOG and a goal-based measure. The recommendation supports routine collection of ReQoL-10 scores in community mental health services in England, where the scores can serve service evaluation as well as clinical review." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NHS England, Commissioner guidance for adult community mental health rehabilitation services, published 9 June 2025, updated 18 June 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reqol-10" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reqol-10 .

heiro:6000835 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Welsh Information Standards Board on ReQoL-20"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000104 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://dhcw.nhs.wales/data/information-standards/data-standards/data-standards-files/20250717-dscn-2025-15-recovering-quality-of-life-reqol-20-pdf/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Welsh Information Standards Board" ;
    heiro:2000016 "ReQoL-20 Adopted as a Patient-Reported Outcome Data Standard in Wales" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Welsh Information Standards Board approved a data standards change notice in July 2025 that sets out the content of the Recovering Quality of Life questionnaire (ReQoL-20) as a patient-reported outcome measure standard. The notice sits within the Value in Health programme, which the Welsh Government commissioned to improve health outcomes in a financially sustainable way, and it notes that questionnaires such as the ReQoL-20 can be issued at several points along a treatment pathway. The standard gives NHS Wales a common structure for recording ReQoL-20 responses in routine data." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Welsh Information Standards Board, Data Standards Change Notice DSCN 2025/15, Recovering Quality of Life (ReQoL-20), approved 17 July 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "reqol-20" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:reqol-20 .

heiro:6000836 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Resource"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Resources Under NHS and PSS Control Valued at NHS Prices" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For the reference case, NICE requires costs to relate to resources under the control of the NHS and personal social services, valued at prices relevant to the NHS and PSS, with evidence that resource use and cost data were identified systematically. Resource use estimates should include changes in infrastructure, use and maintenance and, where appropriate, staff training. For technologies with several uses, average costs are identified first and a marginal cost analysis may be added when an already recommended technology has spare capacity, and carers' time may be considered when the NHS or PSS might otherwise have provided that care." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.4.1, 4.4.2, 4.4.20 and 4.4.24, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "resource" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:resource .

heiro:6000837 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Resource"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/924156198X"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Three Principal Health System Inputs Kept in Balance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO identifies three principal health system inputs, namely human resources, physical capital and consumables, bought with financial resources of both a capital and a recurrent character. It treats human resources as the most important input and notes that labour accounts for two-thirds or more of recurrent health expenditure in many countries. It notes that investment decisions are generally irreversible, and that new investment choices must be made carefully and the existing mix of inputs monitored regularly to reduce imbalances that impair performance." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, The World Health Report 2000. Health Systems: Improving Performance, Geneva, 2000, chapter 4 (What resources are needed?)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "resource" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:resource .

heiro:6000838 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Risk Pooling"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/pooling-financial-resources-for-universal-health-coverage-options-for-reform"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pooling reform can strengthen redistribution and financial protection" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The World Health Organization has published guidance describing pooling as the accumulation and management of prepaid financial resources and as a core health-financing function alongside revenue raising and purchasing. WHO explains that broader participation, larger and more diverse pools, transfers between pools and harmonised arrangements can strengthen redistribution and financial protection, while fragmented pools can limit equitable access to healthcare." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pooling Financial Resources for Universal Health Coverage: Options for Reform" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "risk-pooling" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:risk-pooling .

heiro:6000839 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Scarcity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adoption Weighed Against Care Displaced From a Fixed NHS Budget" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE considers the overall resources available to the NHS when judging value for money, so decisions about a new technology must weigh the healthcare programmes for other patient groups that its adoption may displace, including those NICE has not evaluated. The manual treats the NHS budget as fixed, so new, more costly technologies displace other programmes. When decision-making modifiers are applied, net health benefit is presented to show this opportunity cost, and a negative value means health gains too small to prevent an overall health loss from care not funded elsewhere." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.2.25, 6.3.1 and 6.3.2, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "scarcity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:scarcity .

heiro:6000840 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Scarcity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241507158"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Country Can Cover Every Beneficial Service, So Priorities Must Be Set" ;
    heiro:2000017 "A WHO report setting out the collective views of its Consultative Group on Equity and Universal Health Coverage states that available resources in every country fall short of what is required to meet all needs. Universal health coverage is therefore taken to require a comprehensive range of key services, not every beneficial one, and countries must choose which services to expand first. Ranking services by cost-effectiveness usually gives the largest sum of health benefits for a given budget, and the same criteria can guide displacing less important services within a fixed budget." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Making fair choices on the path to universal health coverage: final report of the WHO Consultative Group on Equity and Universal Health Coverage (2014, ISBN 978 92 4 150715 8), chapter 1 (pages 1 and 5) and chapter 3 (pages 11 to 13)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "scarcity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:scarcity .

heiro:6000841 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Secondary Research"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/evidence-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Outcome Evidence Must Come From a Systematic Review" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE requires evidence on outcomes to come from a systematic review, defined as systematically locating, including, appraising and synthesising the evidence for a clearly formulated question, and carried out to a pre-defined protocol. Synthesis of the included studies is allowed but not required. Attempts should be made to find evidence outside the public domain, such as unpublished or abstract-only trial data. Where an economic evaluation rests on new analyses, a review of published, relevant economic evaluations should also be done." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 3.3.4, 3.3.25, 3.3.26 and 3.4.2, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "secondary-research" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:secondary-research .

heiro:6000842 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Secondary Research"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Benefit Assessments Based on Systematic Reviews" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG mostly uses systematic reviews to identify relevant primary studies, but an assessment can rest partly or solely on them if they show only minimal risk of bias and present the evidence completely, transparently and reproducibly. As a rule at least 2 independent high-quality reviews should be available, appraised with AMSTAR, AMSTAR 2 or ROBIS. If the reviews suggest that an assessment based on primary studies could reach different conclusions, IQWiG carries out that assessment instead." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of Allgemeine Methoden; German original authoritative), DOI 10.60584/General-Methods_V8.0, section 10.2.1 and 10.2.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "secondary-research" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:secondary-research .

heiro:6000843 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on SGRQ"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/files/drugs/published/Chronic-Obstructive-Pulmonary-Disease--Use-of-the-St.-George%E2%80%99s-Respiratory-Questionnaire-as-a-PRO-Assessment-Tool-Guidance-for-Industry.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "SGRQ Total Score With a Minimum Important Change of 4 Units in COPD Drug Trials" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The FDA guidance for industry on the St George's Respiratory Questionnaire supports its use as a patient-reported outcome assessment tool in COPD drug development. It states that the minimum clinically important difference for the total score, both between patients and within a patient, is at least 4 units and that no evidence supports other values. Sponsors should use the total score only, not the Symptoms, Activity or Impacts component scores, and responder analysis is the preferred primary method for reporting results. The guidance lists 1-year, 3-month and 4-week recall versions of the 50-item SGRQ, of which only the 3-month and 4-week versions were available as of March 2016." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Food and Drug Administration, Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease, Use of the St. George's Respiratory Questionnaire as a PRO Assessment Tool, Guidance for Industry, Center for Drug Evaluation and Research, March 2018" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "sgrq" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:sgrq .

heiro:6000844 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Six-Minute Walk Test"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/172042/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "A Statistically Significant 6MWT Gain Was Judged Small" ;
    heiro:2000017 "At the Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs Advisory Committee meeting of 13 September 2023 on a supplemental application for patisiran in transthyretin amyloid cardiomyopathy, the FDA clinical reviewer presented the APOLLO-B trial, whose primary endpoint was the change in six-minute walk test distance from baseline at month 12. The reviewer reported a median difference of 14.7 metres (95% confidence interval 0.7 to 28.7) against a median baseline of 364 metres and described the effect as statistically significant but small. The committee was asked whether patisiran has clinically meaningful benefits, so statistical significance on walk distance was not treated as settling clinical benefit." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA clinical reviewer presentation, sNDA 210922/015, patisiran (Onpattro), Cardiovascular and Renal Drugs Advisory Committee meeting, 13 September 2023" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "six-minute-walk-test" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:six-minute-walk-test .

heiro:6000845 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Social Health Insurance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/69018"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "WHO perspective on Social Health Insurance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO presents social health insurance as a possible route towards universal health coverage when compulsory contributions are pooled broadly and public funding covers people who cannot contribute. Progress depends on extending population coverage, protecting households from financial hardship, defining appropriate benefits and developing the administrative capacity to collect and manage contributions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Reaching Universal Coverage via Social Health Insurance: Key Design Features in the Transition Period" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "social-health-insurance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:social-health-insurance .

heiro:6000846 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Bank on Social Health Insurance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000007 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/c8d23e1e-1066-5846-b3d3-71dbab332dbe"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Bank" ;
    heiro:2000016 "World Bank perspective on Social Health Insurance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The World Bank cautions that social health insurance does not automatically produce universal or equitable coverage. Systems that rely heavily on payroll contributions may exclude informal workers, making tax-funded subsidies, broader risk pooling and measures to reduce fragmentation important parts of system design." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Social Health Insurance for Developing Nations" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "social-health-insurance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:social-health-insurance .

heiro:6000847 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development on Social Health Insurance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000009 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/primary-health-care-for-resilient-health-systems-in-latin-america_743e6228-en/full-report/component-7.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development" ;
    heiro:2000016 "OECD perspective on Social Health Insurance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The OECD describes social health insurance as an organisational model in which compulsory insurance entities purchase care and funding commonly combines social contributions with public subsidies for people unable to contribute. It emphasises that performance depends on governance, pooling, coordination and benefit design rather than on the financing label alone." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Primary Health Care for Resilient Health Systems in Latin America" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "social-health-insurance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:social-health-insurance .

heiro:6000848 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Societal Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Societal Perspective Required in the Dutch Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland requires economic evaluations to be conducted and reported from a societal perspective, including all relevant societal costs and benefits irrespective of who bears the costs or receives the benefits. Relevant costs cover healthcare, including costs in life years gained, costs for patients and families such as travel and informal care, and costs in other sectors such as productivity losses, education or the judicial system. Productivity losses must be valued with the friction cost method, and a healthcare perspective scenario must always be reported." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), sections 2.2, 3.2, 3.2.1 and 4.7.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "societal-perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:societal-perspective .

heiro:6000849 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Societal Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "NHS and PSS Cost Perspective, With Wider Costs Reported Separately" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case takes the NHS and personal social services perspective on costs, while counting all relevant health effects for patients and, when relevant, carers. Costs to other government bodies may be included only exceptionally, when agreed with the Department of Health and Social Care, and must be reported separately from the reference case. Productivity costs are excluded from the reference case but can be presented separately when they may be a critical component of a technology's value, and the time of family carers can be costed and shown separately." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.7, 4.2.9 and 4.4.22 to 4.4.24, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "societal-perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:societal-perspective .

heiro:6000850 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Societal Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/ICER_Reference-Case_For-Publication_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Modified Societal Perspective Alongside the Health System Base Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER's reference case takes a health care system perspective as its conventional base case, and every analysis must also include a modified societal perspective covering impacts beyond direct health care, documented in an impact inventory adapted from the Second Panel on Cost-Effectiveness in Health and Medicine. This usually includes patient and transport costs, patient and caregiver productivity, and caregiver disutility. The two perspectives are presented together as a co-base case when societal effects or costs are judged substantial, most often when the cost-effectiveness ratio changes by more than 20% or crosses 100,000or100,000 or 100,000or150,000 per evLY or QALY gained." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, ICER's Reference Case for Economic Evaluations: Elements and Rationale (current as of 23 October 2025), Perspective" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "societal-perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:societal-perspective .

heiro:6000851 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Societal Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Societal Perspective as a Secondary Analysis in Ireland" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's national guidelines set the reference-case cost perspective as the publicly funded health and social care system in Ireland, with all health benefits to individuals counted. A societal perspective, capturing all relevant costs and consequences regardless of who bears them, including productivity costs and effects on other public agencies, patients and carers, is described as the most comprehensive approach. Where it is expected to change results significantly it may be presented as a secondary analysis, with non-reference-case costs shown separately and subjected to sensitivity analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), section 2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "societal-perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:societal-perspective .

heiro:6000852 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Societal Perspective"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Collective Perspective Preferred, Social Perspective Not Recommended" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS bases its reference case on a collective perspective, covering all people or institutions affected in health or cost by producing an intervention within the scope of overall care, with production costs measured independently of who funds them. Choosing a narrower healthcare system perspective must be justified. HAS does not recommend the social perspective for the reference case because it involves resources not directly included in the care production process, such as the value of production losses resulting from illness." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (methodological guidance), validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, Guideline 3 and section 1.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 5 ;
    heiro:2000020 "societal-perspective" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:societal-perspective .

heiro:6000853 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Standardised Mean Difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/corporate/ecd9/chapter/methods-for-real-world-studies-of-comparative-effects"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Absolute Standardised Differences Below 0.1 Indicate Good Balance" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's real-world evidence framework states that some propensity score methods allow the similarity of intervention groups on observed covariates to be examined, and that absolute standardised differences of less than 0.1 are generally considered to indicate good balance. Small absolute differences may still matter if the variable has a strong effect on the outcome. The framework also lists absolute standardised mean differences as one way to compare an analytical sample with the target population when assessing external validity." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE real-world evidence framework (ECD9), published 23 June 2022, chapter Methods for real-world studies of comparative effects, sections Selecting methods for addressing confounding and Assessing external validity" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "standardised-mean-difference" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:standardised-mean-difference .

heiro:6000854 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Standardised Mean Difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Hedges' g Thresholds of 0.2 to 0.5 for Relevance and Added Benefit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG prefers responder analyses for continuous outcomes, but where none meet its criterion of at least 15% of the scale range it may use the SMD, expressed as Hedges' g, with an irrelevance threshold of 0.2 that the whole 95% confidence interval must exceed. In drug benefit assessments the lower confidence limit must exceed 0.2 for a minor extent of added benefit, 0.3 for serious or 0.4 for non-serious symptoms for a considerable extent, and 0.5 for a major extent, which applies to serious outcomes only." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of Allgemeine Methoden 8.0), sections 3.3.3 (part D and Table 4) and 10.3.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "standardised-mean-difference" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:standardised-mean-difference .

heiro:6000855 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Standardised Mean Difference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/4ec8288e-6d15-49c5-a490-d8ad7748578f_en?filename=hta_methodological-guideline_direct-indirect-comparisons_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "SMD Accepted as a Summary Statistic in Pairwise and Bucher Comparisons" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's methodological guideline lists the standardised mean difference among the summary statistics typically used in pairwise meta-analysis, alongside odds ratios, risk ratios, risk differences, hazard ratios, rate ratios and differences of means. The same summary statistic must be available for every study pooled, either extracted directly from the publications or computed from extracted data. The guideline also states that Bucher's anchored indirect comparison method is compatible with standardised mean differences." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA CG Methodological Guideline for Quantitative Evidence Synthesis: Direct and Indirect Comparisons, adopted 8 March 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, sections 4 and 5.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "standardised-mean-difference" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:standardised-mean-difference .

heiro:6000856 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Strict Dominance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Dominated Technologies Removed From a Fully Incremental Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case calls for cost-utility analysis with fully incremental analysis, following standard decision rules that reflect dominance and extended dominance. Results should be presented with dominated technologies, defined as more costly and less effective than another technology in the analysis, and extendedly dominated technologies removed. Acceptability curves should be accompanied by the cost-effectiveness acceptability frontier, and expected net health benefits at £25,000 and £35,000 per QALY should also be presented when appropriate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), table 4.1 and sections 4.2.16, 4.7.15 and 4.10.8, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "strict-dominance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:strict-dominance .

heiro:6000857 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Structural Uncertainty"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-9-uncertainty-analysis-model-inputs-and-assumptions.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Parameterise Structural Assumptions or Use Scenario Analyses" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PSA characterises parameter uncertainty but cannot address structural (or translational) uncertainty. Where multiple plausible model structures exist, the submission must assess their impact and, if substantial, characterise it formally — parameterising structural assumptions where clinical evidence or expert opinion allows, otherwise using scenario analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC, Section 3A.9" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "structural-uncertainty" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:structural-uncertainty .

heiro:6000858 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Structural Uncertainty"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Structural Assumptions Justified and Explored via Scenario Analysis" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Structural assumptions and the choice of model structure should be justified and made transparent; their impact on results should be explored through scenario analyses (preferably probabilistic), and model structures that limit feasibility of probabilistic analysis must be specified and justified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Health Technology Evaluations: The Manual (PMG36), Section 4 (Economic Evaluation)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "structural-uncertainty" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:structural-uncertainty .

heiro:6000859 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Subgroup Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pre-Specified, Biologically Plausible Subgroups; No Post-Hoc Dredging" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The manual expresses a preference for pre-specified subgroups supported by biological plausibility and warns against post-hoc \"dredging\" for subgroup effects. This applies to subgroups defined by effectiveness (effect modifiers) as well as by costs, baseline risk, or adverse events." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Health Technology Evaluations: The Manual (PMG36), Section 4.9" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "subgroup-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:subgroup-analysis .

heiro:6000860 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Subgroup Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-1/1-1-clinical-issue-addressed_by-the-submission.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pre-Specified Subgroups With Justification" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Subgroup analyses should be pre-specified and justified; where listing is sought for a subpopulation, the submission must justify the claimed difference in response, show that the price reflects the relevant comparison, and characterise the size of the subpopulation." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, Guidelines for Preparing a Submission to the PBAC, Section 1.1 / Section 3" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "subgroup-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:subgroup-analysis .

heiro:6000861 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Subgroup Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/Reference-Case-4.3.25.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "A Priori Clinical Subgroups Encouraged; Caution on Race/SES-Only Subgroups" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Examining relative cost-effectiveness in subpopulations defined a priori by clinical characteristics is often an important goal; however, analyses focused on subpopulations defined solely by race/ethnicity or socioeconomic status are treated with caution, consistent with ICER’s health-equity approach." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, ICER Reference Case (2025) and Health Equity white paper" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "subgroup-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:subgroup-analysis .

heiro:6000862 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Summary ROC Curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg48/chapter/methods-for-guidance-produced-in-the-nice-healthtech-programme"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Justified Methods for Meta-Analysis of Test Accuracy" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE notes that meta-analysis of test accuracy data is complicated by the correlation between sensitivity and specificity, by heterogeneity across settings, populations, reference standards and operators, and by cut-off points that differ between studies. Several methods exist that vary in complexity and assumptions. The choice of method depends on the data available and should be justified, and NICE points to its Decision Support Unit's technical support document 25 for guidance on methods." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE HealthTech programme manual (PMG48), section 2.2.15, published 14 July 2025, last updated 17 December 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "summary-roc-curve" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:summary-roc-curve .

heiro:6000863 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Summary ROC Curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/media/83856/download?attachment"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Bivariate Model With HSROC Curve When Thresholds Are Implicit" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Decision Support Unit recommends bivariate random-effects meta-analysis of test accuracy and adding a hierarchical summary ROC curve when implicit threshold effects are anticipated. It discourages the Moses-Littenberg approach now that exact methods are available and advises against extrapolating the curve beyond the range of the observed data. Because the model does not use threshold values, it cannot show which point on the curve a given threshold relates to. The DSU states its documents are not formal NICE guidance." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 25: Evidence Synthesis of Diagnostic Test Accuracy for Decision Making (Derezea et al., November 2024), sections 4 and 4.4, and its one-page summary" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "summary-roc-curve" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:summary-roc-curve .

heiro:6000864 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Summary ROC Curve"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Bivariate Random Effects Methods for Summary ROC Curves" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG usually advises random-effects models for meta-analyses of diagnostic accuracy studies because heterogeneity is expected. Separate models for sensitivity and specificity may be used if evidence on bivariate models is insufficient, but when a summary ROC curve or a joint estimate of sensitivity and specificity is of interest, bivariate random-effects meta-analyses show advantages. IQWiG regards accuracy-only studies, and so their meta-analyses, as of limited relevance in evaluating diagnostic tests." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), section 10.3.7 (D) and section 3.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "summary-roc-curve" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:summary-roc-curve .

heiro:6000865 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Time Horizon"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Capture All Material Differences" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE requires the analytical horizon to be long enough to capture relevant differences in costs and outcomes between technologies; a lifetime horizon is appropriate when consequences continue over the remaining lifetime." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE Health Technology Evaluations: The Manual (PMG36)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "time-horizon" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:time-horizon .

heiro:6000866 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CDA-AMC on Time Horizon"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CDA-AMC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Decision-Appropriate Analytical Horizon" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Canadian reference-case guidance requires a horizon that captures all relevant differences in costs and outcomes, with extrapolation and uncertainty made explicit when evidence is shorter than the analysis." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "time-horizon" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:time-horizon .

heiro:6000867 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Time Horizon"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-2-computational-methods-and-structure-of-economic_analysis.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Justified Horizon and Stability Checks" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC guidance requires the time horizon to capture important differences without extending unnecessarily, and asks analysts to examine horizon scenarios and the stability of model results." ;
    heiro:2000018 "PBAC Guidelines — Sections 3A.2 and 3A.9" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "time-horizon" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:time-horizon .

heiro:6000868 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Zorginstituut Nederland on Time Horizon"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Zorginstituut Nederland" ;
    heiro:2000016 "National Economic-Evaluation Horizon" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Dutch guidance treats time horizon as a reference-case design choice that should capture relevant long-term consequences and support comparable economic evaluations." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guideline for Economic Evaluations in Healthcare (2024)" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "time-horizon" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:time-horizon .

heiro:6000869 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Time-Lag Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Later Publication of Negative Studies Counted as Publication Bias" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG defines publication bias as studies with a statistically significant negative difference, or no significant difference, being either not published at all or published later than studies with positive, statistically significant results. Pooling only published results can therefore bias the common effect estimate. Because funnel plots and meta-regression can neither confirm nor exclude this bias, IQWiG stresses searching for unpublished data and contacts authors or sponsors when studies have not yet been published at the time of its assessment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation published July 2026; the German original is authoritative), sections 10.3.10 and 10.3.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "time-lag-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:time-lag-bias .

heiro:6000870 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Time Preference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/698f30a4492ea446ea7f43d3/Green_Book_supplementary_guidance_-_discounting.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Pure Time Preference of 0.5% Within the 3.5% Discount Rate" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HM Treasury's Green Book discounts costs and benefits at a social time preference rate of 3.5% for years 1 to 30, made up of time preference and a wealth effect. Time preference of 1.5% combines pure time preference, described as society's impatience and set at 0.5%, with 1.0% for catastrophic risk, while the wealth effect adds 2.0%. Health and life effects are discounted at 1.5% because the wealth effect is excluded. For appraisals beyond 50 years, a sensitivity analysis without pure time preference shows the wealth transfer it implies." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, Green Book supplementary guidance: discounting (February 2026, ISBN 978-1-918417-18-0), paragraphs 2.1 to 2.17 and 3.1 to 3.5, tables 2.A, 3.A and 3.B" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "time-preference" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:time-preference .

heiro:6000871 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on Time Preference"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "A 4.0% Discount Rate Reflecting Society's Time Preference" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's national guidelines require costs and health outcomes occurring after the first year to be discounted to present values to reflect society's rate of time preference, a preference for benefits sooner and costs later. The reference case applies a common rate of 4.0% a year to both, taken from the Public Spending Code, and the rate is varied from 0% to 10% in sensitivity analysis. Hyperbolic discounting using Public Spending Code factors is permitted as a secondary analysis for time horizons beyond 30 years." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), section 2.14 and Appendix 6" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "time-preference" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:time-preference .

heiro:6000872 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Timed Up and Go Test"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng249/chapter/Rationale-and-impact"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Gait and Balance Tests Such as the TUG Do Not Predict Falls Risk" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In its falls guideline NICE records that community studies of gait and balance assessment, such as the Timed Up and Go test, were identified, and that although the committee agreed these tests help in observing gait and balance problems, they do not predict a person's risk of falling. The guideline recommends against using falls risk prediction tools to predict a person's risk of falling. Case-finding people who have had a previous injury from a fall or multiple falls, are living with frailty, or have gait or balance problems was judged useful for identifying those who may need a more detailed assessment." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Falls: assessment and prevention in older people and in people 50 and over at higher risk, NICE guideline NG249, Rationale and impact, 2025" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "timed-up-and-go-test" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:timed-up-and-go-test .

heiro:6000873 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Transition Matrix"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-4-model-transition-probabilities-or-variables-transformation-extrapolation.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Transition Probabilities Matched to the Model Cycle and Structure" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC asks submissions to present every transition probability in the base-case model with point and interval estimates and justified sources, and to adjust values from all sources to the transitions the model structure requires, for example translating reported rates or cumulative probabilities to probabilities for the model cycle. For cohort state transition models, sponsors should consider whether transitions depend on earlier states, use tunnel states for time-varying probabilities, explain the choice of cycle length and apply a half-cycle correction by default. Correlation between transition probabilities should be assessed." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsections 3A.2.3 and 3A.4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "transition-matrix" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:transition-matrix .

heiro:6000874 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE) on Transition Matrix"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000022 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://kce.fgov.be/sites/default/files/2025-05/KCE400_Method_guidelines_economic_evaluations.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Markov Transition Probabilities Presented in Matrix Form" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For Markov models, the Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre requires the nature of each health state, temporary or absorbing, to be specified and the choice of health states to be justified. Transition probabilities should be presented, for example in matrix form, with an indication of whether each probability is constant or variable, and the choice of cycle length should be justified. Half-cycle corrections are recommended, except for events that occur specifically at the beginning of a cycle, such as surgery or a first drug administration." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Belgian Health Care Knowledge Centre (KCE), Belgian guidelines for economic evaluations and budget impact analyses: third edition, KCE Reports 400C (published 8 May 2025), Guideline 14 (sections 5.14.2 and 5.14.5)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "transition-matrix" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:transition-matrix .

heiro:6000875 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Transition Probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Transition Probabilities Derived from the Benefit Assessment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG's reference case assumes that a benefit assessment under §35a SGB V already exists, and its comparison of the drug with the appropriate comparator therapy forms the basis of the decision-analytic model, for example to derive transition probabilities. The health economic evaluation does not call that result into question, so where no added benefit was shown, none is suggested by the model. Further data to determine transition probabilities for certain events, and background mortality, may come from exploratory searches of bibliographic databases and other sources." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation), sections 4.5 and 4.14" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "transition-probability" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:transition-probability .

heiro:6000876 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Transition Probability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Model Must Include Memory When Transition Probabilities Depend on Past Events" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HAS sample grid for judging the quality of a cost-effectiveness model, adapted from Weinstein et al., asks whether the model includes memory when transition probabilities depend on prior events. It also asks whether the cycle duration has been justified by the pace of disease progression, symptoms, treatment decisions or costs. The HAS glossary defines a transitional event as one occurring during a cycle that moves a patient from one model status to another, unlike an intercurrent event, which generally affects only costs and utility." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Haute Autorité de Santé, Choices in methods for economic evaluation, methodological guidance validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, Annex 2 (sample grid for the evaluation of the quality of a cost-effectiveness model) and Glossary" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "transition-probability" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:transition-probability .

heiro:6000877 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Treatment Switching"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Avoid Censoring or Excluding Switchers When Adjusting Survival" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE accepts that statistical methods adjusting for treatment switching can be presented when control patients move onto the technology and an intention-to-treat analysis is considered inappropriate. Simple methods such as censoring or excluding data from patients who cross over should be avoided because they are very susceptible to selection bias. The chosen method should be justified against the trial's switching mechanism, the available baseline and time-dependent data and the expected treatment effect, and the resulting uncertainty should be explored and quantified." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), section 4.6.26, last updated 13 October 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "treatment-switching" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:treatment-switching .

heiro:6000878 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Treatment Switching"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/68716099"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adjust Only for Switches Outside Standard Clinical Practice" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 24, which supplements TSD 16, says adjustment is likely to be appropriate for switches that do not follow treatment pathways expected in clinical practice in the decision jurisdiction, and not usually for switches that do. For survival outcomes in NICE appraisals it recommends presenting results under both the treatment policy (intention-to-treat) and hypothetical (switching-adjusted) strategies. It also recommends several adjustment analyses to test sensitivity to model assumptions, with matching adjustments to costs in economic analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 24, Adjusting survival time estimates in the presence of treatment switching: an update to TSD 16 (Bell Gorrod, Latimer and Abrams, April 2024), executive summary and sections 2, 3.2 and 6; read with TSD 16 (Latimer and Abrams, July 2014)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "treatment-switching" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:treatment-switching .

heiro:6000879 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Treatment Switching"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/publications/guidance-validity-clinical-studies-joint-clinical-assessments_en"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Treatment Switching Distinguished From Cross-Over in Joint Clinical Assessment" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HTA Coordination Group's guidance for joint clinical assessments warns that the term cross-over is sometimes used incorrectly for treatment switching, which in a broad sense covers any move from the randomised intervention to another. In a stricter sense it refers to control patients who receive the experimental intervention, for example after disease progression. Such switching is common in oncology trials and can influence estimated effects on survival, and statistical adjustment can reduce this bias although the methods have limitations." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Member State Coordination Group on Health Technology Assessment, Guidance on Validity of Clinical Studies, version 1.0 (4 July 2024), adopted 19 September 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 3.1.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "treatment-switching" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:treatment-switching .

heiro:6000880 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Treatment Switching"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Treatment Policy Estimand and Complete Data After Switching" ;
    heiro:2000017 "For benefit assessments, IQWiG's General Methods require analyses based on the treatment policy estimand, the effect of the whole treatment strategy regardless of discontinuation or switching, together with the intention-to-treat principle. Data on all relevant outcomes, including patient-reported outcomes and adverse events, should therefore be collected completely even after treatment is discontinued or switched. Censoring at a switch can make censoring informative and create a high risk of bias, and recording adverse events for only 30 days afterwards is insufficient." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, version 8.0 of 19 December 2025, section 10.3.12" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "treatment-switching" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:treatment-switching .

heiro:6000881 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of PBAC on Tunnel State"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000004 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pbac.pbs.gov.au/section-3a/3a-2-computational-methods-and-structure-of-economic_analysis.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "PBAC" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Tunnel States Required for Time-Varying Transition Probabilities" ;
    heiro:2000017 "PBAC's guidelines ask sponsors building a cohort state transition model whether transition probabilities vary with how long individuals have remained in each health state, and state that tunnel states are required to represent such time-varying probabilities. Dependence on events experienced before entering a state can be represented by health states that describe pathways through the model. Individual-level models are reserved for structures that cannot feasibly be run as cohort models, for example where time-varying event rates or the influence of previous events make a cohort model impractical." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Guidelines for preparing a submission to the Pharmaceutical Benefits Advisory Committee, version 5.0 (September 2016), subsection 3A.2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "tunnel-state" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:tunnel-state .

heiro:6000882 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE Decision Support Unit on Tunnel State"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000002 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.sheffield.ac.uk/sites/default/files/2022-02/TSD15_Patient-level_simulation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE Decision Support Unit" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Tunnel States Record Time Since the Last Transition" ;
    heiro:2000017 "TSD 15 notes that state-transition models usually assume future events are independent of past events, which makes it hard to represent risks that depend on time since a previous transition. Replicate states that move the patient on each cycle, sometimes called tunnel states, record the passage of time even when the actual health state does not change. The document warns that only so many replicate states can be added before a cohort Markov model becomes unwieldy, and offers patient-level simulation with history-dependent transition matrices as one solution." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE DSU Technical Support Document 15: Cost-effectiveness modelling using patient-level simulation (Davis, Stevenson, Tappenden and Wailoo, April 2014), section 3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "tunnel-state" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:tunnel-state .

heiro:6000883 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EU HTA Coordination Group on Unit of Allocation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000016 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://health.ec.europa.eu/document/download/9f9dbfe4-078b-4959-9a07-df9167258772_en?filename=hta_clinical-studies-validity_guidance_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EU HTA Coordination Group" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Unit of Analysis Issues Must Be Addressed in Cluster Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The guidance for joint clinical assessments defines a cluster-based study as one in which each unit of analysis comprises more than one patient, such as randomised hospital wards. In cluster-randomised trials, selection bias from recruiting participants after randomisation needs to be assessed, and stepped-wedge designs need secular trends examined. Unit of analysis issues should be addressed because data dependencies require adequate statistical analysis, and conventional analyses can overestimate the precision of effect estimates." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HTA Coordination Group, Guidance on Validity of Clinical Studies, V1.0 of 4 July 2024, adopted 19 September 2024 under Regulation (EU) 2021/2282, section 3.1.1 (Table 1) and section 4.8" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "unit-of-allocation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:unit-of-allocation .

heiro:6000884 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Unit of Allocation"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Clustered Data Need Methods for Dependent Observations" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG notes that simple standard procedures assume independent experimental units within a treatment arm, which does not hold in cluster-randomised studies or studies of eyes or teeth. Applying them anyway underestimates variance and gives p values that are too small. Results from such studies can still be considered only if the related error can be properly classified, and suitable methods such as generalised estimating equations or mixed models should be applied. For prevention programmes, IQWiG accepts studies allocating clusters to arms where contamination between groups is a concern." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG, General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation of Allgemeine Methoden; German original authoritative), DOI 10.60584/General-Methods_V8.0, sections 3.7 and 10.3.14" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "unit-of-allocation" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:unit-of-allocation .

heiro:6000885 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of World Health Organization on Universal Health Coverage"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/universal-health-coverage-(uhc)"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "World Health Organization" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Quality Services Without Financial Hardship, Tracked by Two SDG Indicators" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO defines universal health coverage as all people having access to the full range of quality health services they need, when and where they need them, without financial hardship, from health promotion to palliative care. Under definitions revised in 2025, progress is tracked by a service coverage index scored from 0 to 100 and by the share of people whose out-of-pocket health spending exceeds 40% of the household discretionary budget. The 2025 monitoring report with the World Bank put the index at 71 in 2023 and financial hardship at 26% in 2022." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, Universal health coverage (UHC), fact sheet (5 December 2025); World Health Organization and World Bank, Tracking universal health coverage: 2025 global monitoring report (5 December 2025), executive summary; SDG indicator metadata for indicators 3.8.1 (last updated 12 December 2025) and 3.8.2 (last updated 27 March 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "universal-health-coverage" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:universal-health-coverage .

heiro:6000886 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Unmet Need"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Unmet Health Need Reflected Within the Severity Modifier" ;
    heiro:2000017 "In NICE technology appraisals the extent of unmet health need is reflected within the severity of the condition, defined as the future health lost by people living with it under standard care in the NHS. Severity is measured by absolute and proportional QALY shortfall, and the committee may weight QALYs by 1.2 for a proportional shortfall of 0.85 to 0.95 or an absolute shortfall of 12 to 18, and by 1.7 at 0.95 or 18 and above. The modifier is not initially applied to appraisals of HealthTech." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 6.2.12 to 6.2.18 and table 6.1, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "unmet-need" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:unmet-need .

heiro:6000887 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of EMA on Unmet Need"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000014 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/documents/scientific-guideline/guideline-scientific-application-and-practical-arrangements-necessary-implement-commission-regulation-ec-no-5072006-conditional-marketing-authorisation-medicinal-products-human-use-falling_en.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "EMA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Unmet Medical Need in Conditional Authorisation and PRIME" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EU law defines an unmet medical need as a condition with no satisfactory authorised method of diagnosis, prevention or treatment, or one where the new medicine would be of major therapeutic advantage. EMA's CHMP grants a conditional marketing authorisation only if, among other criteria, the medicine fulfils such a need, and applicants must review available methods, quantify the need with medical or epidemiological data and justify how far the product addresses it. PRIME gives enhanced support to medicines with the potential to address an unmet medical need to a significant extent." ;
    heiro:2000018 "European Medicines Agency, Guideline on the scientific application and the practical arrangements necessary to implement Commission Regulation (EC) No 507/2006 on the conditional marketing authorisation for medicinal products for human use (EMA/CHMP/509951/2006, Rev.1, 25 February 2016), section 4.1(c); Commission Regulation (EC) No 507/2006, Article 4; EMA, PRIME: priority medicines (web page, read 3 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "unmet-need" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:unmet-need .

heiro:6000888 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Unmet Need"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/86377/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Unmet Medical Need as the Gateway to Expedited Programmes" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA defines an unmet medical need as a condition whose treatment or diagnosis is not addressed adequately by available therapy, covering an immediate need for a defined population and a longer-term need for society, such as antibacterial resistance. Where no therapy exists the need is clear; where therapy exists, a new drug generally qualifies if it affects a serious outcome that current therapy does not, improves on it, helps patients who cannot tolerate or do not respond to it, or avoids serious toxicity. The concept underlies fast track, breakthrough therapy, accelerated approval and priority review." ;
    heiro:2000018 "U.S. Food and Drug Administration, Guidance for Industry: Expedited Programs for Serious Conditions, Drugs and Biologics (final guidance, May 2014), sections I and III.C" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "unmet-need" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:unmet-need .

heiro:6000889 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Unmet Need"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/ICER_2023_2026_VAF_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Committee Votes on Substantial Unmet Need Informed by Health Shortfalls" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER treats substantial unmet need despite currently available treatments as a special ethical priority, a framing it adopted in place of earlier approaches to the severity of a condition. Its reports calculate absolute and proportional evLY shortfalls and include perspectives from patients and patient groups, and its independent appraisal committees vote on unmet need. These considerations are weighed qualitatively rather than by formal weighting, guiding where within the cost-effectiveness threshold range a judgement of long-term value for money falls." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework (updated 25 September 2023), section 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "unmet-need" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:unmet-need .

heiro:6000890 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) on Urgent Care"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000018 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-B/part-422/subpart-C/section-422.113"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Urgent Care Defined, and Urgently Needed Services in Medicare Advantage" ;
    heiro:2000017 "CMS's HealthCare.gov glossary defines urgent care as care for an illness, injury or condition \"serious enough that a reasonable person would seek care right away, but not so severe it requires emergency room care\". For Medicare Advantage, federal regulation defines urgently needed services as non-emergency covered services immediately required for an unforeseen illness, injury or condition while the member is outside the plan's service area or its network is unavailable, and using the plan is not reasonable. Plans must pay for them inside or outside the network, regardless of prior authorisation, with cost sharing capped at the professional services limit." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, HealthCare.gov Glossary: Urgent care (accessed 3 October 2026); Code of Federal Regulations title 42, section 422.113(b) (eCFR current text, 1 October 2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "urgent-care" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:urgent-care .

heiro:6000891 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Utility"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Utility Values From Public Preferences, Now on the UK EQ-5D-5L Value Set" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case expresses health effects in QALYs, giving each health state a utility that reflects its health-related quality of life and multiplying it by the time spent in that state. Patients should report changes in quality of life, valued using public preferences from a representative UK sample and a choice-based method, since different methods produce utility values that cannot always be compared. Under NICE's August 2026 interim methods statement, the UK EQ-5D-5L value set should be used to derive all adult utility values in reference-case analyses." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.3.1 to 4.3.5, last updated 31 March 2026; NICE Interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51), section 1.7, published 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "utility" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:utility .

heiro:6000892 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Utility"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Utility Scores From a Generic Multi-Attribute Approach Using EQ-5D-5L" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS measures health-related quality of life in cost-utility analysis through utility scores reflecting preferences for health states, used to weight time in each state when calculating QALYs. Scores should come from a multi-attribute approach, with health states described by patients on a generic questionnaire and valued by general population preferences; the EQ-5D-5L with the prevailing French value set is recommended, falling back on the 3L version or a validated mapping. Direct standard gamble or time trade-off values belong only in sensitivity analysis, and vignettes or visual analogue scales are not acceptable." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (methodological guidance), validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, sections 2.1.2 and 2.3.1 (Guidelines 10 and 15)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "utility" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:utility .

heiro:6000893 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Utility"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Utilities From the EQ-5D-5L Valued With the Dutch Tariff" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland expresses effects in a cost-utility analysis as QALYs by multiplying health-related quality of life, referred to as utilities, by life years. Patients describe their own health status with a validated generic questionnaire, and the resulting state is valued with a fixed tariff based on preferences in the general public. Quality of life should be measured with the EQ-5D-5L and valued with the Dutch tariff, using the EQ-5D-Y-3L for children aged 8 to 12 and proxy versions where needed; disutilities from side effects must also use the EQ-5D-5L." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), paragraphs 3.3, 3.3.1 and 3.3.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "utility" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:utility .

heiro:6000894 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Vaccination Externalities"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://iris.who.int/handle/10665/329389"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Counting Indirect Effects and Choosing Dynamic Models for Vaccination" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's guide to economic evaluation of immunisation programmes holds that vaccine impact in a population includes indirect effects in unvaccinated or unsuccessfully vaccinated people, whose exposure falls as circulation of the pathogen declines, and that these effects should be estimated from empirical data integrated in a mathematical model. Ignoring herd immunity is described as not best practice. A static model suits cases where vaccination is unlikely to change the force of infection in susceptibles, while a dynamic model is called for where it is likely to and a static model would not give a conservative estimate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, WHO guide for standardization of economic evaluations of immunization programmes, second edition (WHO/IVB/19.10, October 2019), sections 5.2, 5.4, 6.2 and 6.6" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "vaccination-externalities" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:vaccination-externalities .

heiro:6000895 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Joint Committee on Vaccination and Immunisation on Vaccination Externalities"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000105 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a7b9b3440f0b62826a04a80/JCVI_Code_of_Practice_revision_2013_-_final.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Joint Committee on Vaccination and Immunisation" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Indirect Benefits to the Unvaccinated Count Towards Cost Effectiveness" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Under the Health Protection (Vaccination) Regulations 2009, the Secretary of State must, so far as reasonably practicable, implement JCVI recommendations on national programmes that rest on an assessment demonstrating cost effectiveness. JCVI applies NICE's methodology: a programme can be considered cost effective if its health benefits, both direct benefits to those vaccinated and indirect benefits to the unvaccinated population, exceed the opportunity costs of programmes that may be displaced. Reviewers of modelling are asked whether the approach to estimating indirect protection is appropriate." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Joint Committee on Vaccination and Immunisation, Code of Practice (June 2013), paragraphs 13 to 15 and footnote 7; Annex 5, Report of the Working Group on Uncertainty in Vaccine Evaluation and Procurement, paragraph 18" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "vaccination-externalities" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:vaccination-externalities .

heiro:6000896 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Vaccination Externalities"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Population Analysed Extends to People Protected Without Vaccination" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HAS requires the population analysed in an economic evaluation to include all individuals whose health is affected by the intervention, directly or indirectly, and any inability to include affected individuals must be duly justified. For vaccination, the vaccinated population is directly affected and the general population indirectly affected, and the protection of non-vaccinated persons is given as an example of health consequences for other people that can extend the population analysed." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in methods for economic evaluation (methodological guidance), validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, Guideline 4 and section 1.4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "vaccination-externalities" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:vaccination-externalities .

heiro:6000897 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of TLV on Value-Based Pricing"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000028 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.tlv.se/in-english/medicines/pricing-and-reimbursement-of-medicines.html"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "TLV" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Accept/Reject at the Company’s Requested Price; Value-Based Pricing Without Negotiation" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Sweden applies value-based pricing without price negotiation: the company states its requested price in the reimbursement application, and TLV grants inclusion at that price if the health-economic analysis shows it is justified by the value delivered (cost-effective, with marginal benefit over the most cost-effective existing therapies). In practice there is no price bargaining — prices can be set freely under this value-based ceiling; a product rejected as too expensive can reapply at a lower price or with new evidence. This contrasts with the negotiation-based models used in most other jurisdictions (a three-party managed-entry route with the regions now supplements it)." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Tandvårds- och läkemedelsförmånsverket (TLV) under the Pharmaceutical Benefits Act; value-based pricing" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "value-based-pricing" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:value-based-pricing .

heiro:6000898 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Value of Information"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "EVPI and EVPPI Mandatory in the Reference Case" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The 2024 Dutch guideline makes value of information analysis, in the form of the expected value of perfect information (EVPI) and expected value of partial perfect information (EVPPI), an obligatory element of model-based economic evaluations. Both are derived from the probabilistic analysis, scaled to the population that benefits over a time horizon of at least 5 years, and reported at least at population level in an EVP(P)I curve. EVSI and ENBS are optional further analyses, used when population EVP(P)I is high relative to the cost of more research." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), sections 4.7.2.4 and 5.3.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "value-of-information" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:value-of-information .

heiro:6000899 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Value of Information"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "No Formal VOI Requirement, but Research Recommendations Weigh Evidence Value" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NICE manual does not name value of information analysis or require EVPI, but asks models to quantify decision uncertainty: the probability that a different decision would be reached if true cost effectiveness were known. Before recommending a technology for use while more research is done, the committee weighs the potential value to the NHS of additional evidence and what could be gained by reconsidering the decision, balancing net benefits to current patients against those to current and future patients. Managed access for medicines needs resolvable uncertainty that data could address within 5 years." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.7.2, 6.2.27, 6.4.6 and 6.4.14, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "value-of-information" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:value-of-information .

heiro:6000900 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Value Set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg51"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "UK EQ-5D-5L Value Set for New Topics" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's interim methods statement PMG51, published 27 August 2026, requires the UK EQ-5D-5L value set for all new topics, so utilities are calculated directly from EQ-5D-5L data. When only EQ-5D-3L data are available, they are mapped to 5L utility values using the mapping model developed by the Decision Support Unit and the Economic Evaluation Policy Research Unit. Topics started before publication continue to map 5L data to the 3L value set. PMG51 replaces NICE's 2017 position statement on the 5L value set." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE interim methods statement: implementing the EQ-5D-5L value set (PMG51), published 27 August 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "value-set" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:value-set .

heiro:6000901 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Value Set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/publications/publications/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "EQ-5D-5L With the Dutch Value Set" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Utilities for QALY calculation should be derived using the EQ-5D-5L with the Dutch (country-specific) value set as the preferred instrument." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for Economic Evaluations in Healthcare (2024)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "value-set" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:value-set .

heiro:6000902 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CADTH (CDA-AMC) on Value Set"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000023 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.cda-amc.ca/guidelines-economic-evaluation-health-technologies-canada-4th-edition"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CADTH (CDA-AMC)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Canadian-Specific Value Sets Recommended" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Validated generic health-state classification systems with Canadian-specific value sets are recommended for estimating QALYs; no single instrument is mandated, though the EQ-5D-5L (with the Canadian value set) is commonly used." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CADTH (now CDA-AMC), Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies: Canada, 4th Edition (2017)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "value-set" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:value-set .

heiro:6000903 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of FDA on Verification Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000011 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.fda.gov/media/71147/download"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "FDA" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Adjust Sensitivity and Specificity When Verification Is Partial" ;
    heiro:2000017 "FDA recommends comparing a new qualitative diagnostic test with a reference standard where one is available. If applying the reference standard to all subjects is impractical, it can be applied to a subset, such as a random sample, and sensitivity and specificity should then be adjusted to correct for verification bias. FDA states that the simple formulas are not correct for these partial verification designs and give biased estimates, an example of verification or work-up bias." ;
    heiro:2000018 "FDA, Statistical Guidance on Reporting Results from Studies Evaluating Diagnostic Tests, Guidance for Industry and FDA Staff, final, issued 13 March 2007 (CDRH), section 4.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "verification-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:verification-bias .

heiro:6000904 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of IQWiG on Verification Bias"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000003 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.iqwig.de/methoden/general-methods_version-8-0.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "IQWiG" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Partial and Differential Verification Bias in Diagnostic Studies" ;
    heiro:2000017 "IQWiG states that estimates of diagnostic accuracy may be biased if the reference test is conducted only in a non-random sample of participants who received the index test, which it calls partial verification bias. Bias may also arise when the reference test applied depends on the result of the index test, termed differential verification bias. IQWiG adds that all study participants, including those with unclear test results, need to be considered to avoid systematic bias." ;
    heiro:2000018 "IQWiG General Methods, Version 8.0 of 19 December 2025 (English translation published July 2026; the German original is authoritative), section 10.3.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "verification-bias" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:verification-bias .

heiro:6000905 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Vertical Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/committee-recommendations-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Equal QALY Weight, Except Severity Weights of 1.2 or 1.7" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE's reference case gives an additional QALY the same weight regardless of the other characteristics of the people receiving the health benefit, except in specific circumstances. In technology appraisals the committee may apply a greater weight for severity, judged by absolute and proportional QALY shortfall with current care, whichever implies greater severity: 1.2 for proportional shortfall of 0.85 to 0.95 or absolute shortfall of 12 to 18, and 1.7 for at least 0.95 or at least 18. The modifier is not initially applied to appraisals of HealthTech, and highly specialised technologies receive no severity weight." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), table 4.1 (section 4.3.4) and sections 6.2.10, 6.2.12 to 6.2.18 (table 6.1) and 6.2.20, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "vertical-equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:vertical-equity .

heiro:6000906 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Vertical Equity"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#distributional-weighting"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Distributional Weights Value Gains to Low-Income Households More" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Green Book allows analysts to weight costs and benefits by the groups in society that bear them, because additional income is worth more to a low-income household than to a high-income one. A group's welfare weight is the median equivalised income of the national population divided by that of the group, raised to the power 1.3, the estimated elasticity of marginal utility of income. Household incomes should be equivalised first, and weighted estimates must be presented alongside unweighted estimates." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, The Green Book (2026), updated 5 February 2026, chapter 7 (Distributional analysis and place-based analysis), Equivalisation and Distributional weighting" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "vertical-equity" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:vertical-equity .

heiro:6000907 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on VOI Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "EVPI and EVPPI Obligatory in Model-Based Evaluations" ;
    heiro:2000017 "Zorginstituut Nederland makes value of information analysis, as the expected value of perfect information and of partial perfect information, an obligatory part of the reference case for model-based economic evaluations. All parameters in the probabilistic analysis enter the EVPI, and both measures are scaled to the population affected over a time horizon of at least 5 years. If population EVPI is below the cost of further research, decisions can rest on current information; otherwise expected value of sample information and expected net benefit of sampling can show which research is worthwhile. Results appear as EVPI curves across reference values." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), paragraphs 4.7.2.4 and 5.3.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "voi-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:voi-analysis .

heiro:6000908 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA) on VOI Analysis"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000021 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.hiqa.ie/sites/default/files/2025-03/Economic-Evaluation-Guidelines.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "Health Information and Quality Authority (HIQA)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Expected Value of Perfect Information as a Guide to Further Research" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HIQA's national guidelines call for probabilistic sensitivity analysis to assess parameter uncertainty and state that the expected value of perfect information may also be evaluated. EVPI, determined directly from the probabilistic results, estimates the value of eliminating all uncertainty affecting the decision, and if it exceeds the expected cost of further research, additional research on the technology is potentially cost effective. EVPI may be computed for a range of ICER thresholds and shown graphically, and partial EVPI can identify the parameters contributing most to decision uncertainty." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Health Information and Quality Authority, National Guidelines for the Economic Evaluation of Health Technologies in Ireland (published 26 March 2025), sections 2.16 and 2.16.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "voi-analysis" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:voi-analysis .

heiro:6000909 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HAS on Weak Dominance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000013 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.has-sante.fr/upload/docs/application/pdf/2020-11/methodological_guidance_2020_-choices_in_methods_for_economic_evaluation.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HAS" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Strict Dominance Defined to Include Tied Costs or Effects" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The HAS glossary defines strict dominance in a way that covers the tied cases usually grouped under weak dominance, a term HAS does not use: an intervention dominates its comparator when it is less costly for an identical or higher effectiveness, or more effective for an identical or lower cost. HAS asks for the costs and health outcomes of each intervention to be tabulated so that all positions of strict or extended dominance are visible, and its cost-effectiveness frontier consists of all non-dominated interventions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HAS, Choices in methods for economic evaluation: methodological guidance, validated by the CEESP on 6 April 2020, sections 5.1.1 and 5.1.2 and glossary (strict dominance, extended dominance, cost-effectiveness frontier)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "weak-dominance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:weak-dominance .

heiro:6000910 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Weak Dominance"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://english.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/01/16/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare/guideline-for-economic-evaluations-in-healthcare.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Fully Incremental Analysis Flags Weakly Dominated Options" ;
    heiro:2000017 "When more than two interventions are compared, Zorginstituut Nederland's guideline requires a fully incremental analysis in which the interventions are ranked from low to high costs or QALYs and those that are strongly and weakly dominated are identified. ICERs are then calculated only for the interventions that are not dominated, and the results can be presented on an efficiency frontier. The guideline does not define weak dominance in its own text and cites Paulden (2020) for the procedure." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Guideline for economic evaluations in healthcare (2024 version), 16 January 2024, section 5.3.1" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "weak-dominance" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:weak-dominance .

heiro:6000911 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Welfare Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#distributional-weighting"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Social Value Grounded in Welfare Economics, With Income Welfare Weights" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Green Book states that appraisal estimates the social value of options and that this concept of social value is based on the principles of welfare economics, covering all effects on the wellbeing of UK residents rather than financial returns alone. Because the marginal utility of income diminishes, analysts may weight costs and benefits by who bears them: a group's welfare weight is national median equivalised household income divided by the group's median, raised to the power 1.3, an estimate of the elasticity of marginal utility of income. Weighted estimates must be presented alongside unweighted ones." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, The Green Book (2026), updated 5 February 2026, chapter 2 (Overview of appraisal): Principles of appraisal; chapter 7 (Distributional analysis and place-based analysis): Distributional analysis, Equivalisation and Distributional weighting" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "welfare-economics" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:welfare-economics .

heiro:6000912 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of US Environmental Protection Agency on Welfare Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000027 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2024-12/appendix-a-guidelines-for-preparing-economic-analyses_final_508-compliant.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "US Environmental Protection Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Fundamental Welfare Theorems and the Kaldor-Hicks Compensation Test" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The EPA guidelines set out the welfare theory underlying the agency's economic analyses, starting from the two fundamental theorems of welfare economics: every competitive equilibrium is Pareto optimal, and every Pareto optimal allocation can be reached as a competitive equilibrium after suitable redistribution. Judging the fairness of net gains would need a social welfare function, on which there is no general agreement, so the guidelines apply the Kaldor-Hicks test: winners could compensate losers and remain better off, and losers could not pay winners to forgo the change. Whether compensation is paid is a separate value judgement for government." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Environmental Protection Agency, Guidelines for Preparing Economic Analyses, 3rd edition (2024), EPA-240-R-24-001, Appendix A Economic Theory, sections A.1 (Market Economy) and A.3 (Benefit-Cost Analysis)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "welfare-economics" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:welfare-economics .

heiro:6000913 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Welfare Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg36/chapter/economic-evaluation-2"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Maximising Health Gains From a Fixed NHS and PSS Budget" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE justifies cost-effectiveness analysis by its focus on maximising health gains from a fixed NHS and personal social services budget, and states that its evaluations aim to represent an efficient use of available NHS and PSS resources. Health effects are measured in QALYs valued from the preferences of a representative sample of the UK population, and productivity costs are excluded. In the reference case an additional QALY has the same weight whoever receives it, except in specific circumstances, and distributional cost-effectiveness analysis may be used only as supporting evidence on health inequalities." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE technology appraisal and highly specialised technologies guidance: the manual (PMG36), sections 4.2.9, 4.2.15, 4.3.4 and 4.12.3 and table 4.1, last updated 31 March 2026" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "welfare-economics" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:welfare-economics .

heiro:6000914 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on Wellbeing or Capability Instrument"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/process/pmg20/chapter/incorporating-economic-evaluation"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Capability or Social Care Measures Such as ICECAP or ASCOT for Social Care Focused Interventions" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The NICE guidelines manual sets a reference case that varies with the decision problem and notes that for interventions with a social care focus the intended outcomes are broader than health. In table 7.1 the column for interventions with a social care focus names capability or social care-related quality of life measures, such as ICECAP or ASCOT, when an intervention results in both health and capability or social care outcomes. For public-sector interventions with health and non-health outcomes the measure is decided case by case. The manual says section 7.4, which holds table 7.1, will be updated." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Developing NICE guidelines: the manual (PMG20), chapter 7 Incorporating economic evaluation, section 7.4 and table 7.1, last updated 23 October 2025 (sections 7.4, 7.7 and 7.9 under update)" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "wellbeing-or-capability-instrument" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:wellbeing-or-capability-instrument .

heiro:6000915 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Wellbeing or Capability Instrument"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/60fa9169d3bf7f0448719daf/Wellbeing_guidance_for_appraisal_-_supplementary_Green_Book_guidance.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Life Satisfaction Valued in WELLBYs for Central Government Appraisal" ;
    heiro:2000017 "HM Treasury's supplementary Green Book guidance on wellbeing explains where, when and how wellbeing evidence may contribute to the appraisal of social or public value, and it is used within the Green Book's five-case model. It defines a wellbeing year, or WELLBY, as a one-point change in life satisfaction on a 0 to 10 scale per person per year, and says future changes in wellbeing are discounted at the Green Book health discount rate. The Green Book refers analysts to this guidance for the latest monetary value of a WELLBY." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, Green Book supplementary guidance: wellbeing (Wellbeing guidance for appraisal), published 26 July 2021, last updated 18 November 2022; The Green Book (2026)" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "wellbeing-or-capability-instrument" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:wellbeing-or-capability-instrument .

heiro:6000916 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NICE on WEMWBS"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000001 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng212/chapter/Recommendations"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NICE" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Named as an Example of a Validated Mental Wellbeing Measure for Employers" ;
    heiro:2000017 "NICE guideline NG212 on mental wellbeing at work, published in March 2022, names the Warwick-Edinburgh Mental Wellbeing Scales in recommendation 1.1.7. The recommendation says that a validated measure should be used when measuring mental wellbeing. It gives Warwick Medical School's Warwick-Edinburgh Mental Wellbeing Scales (WEMWBS) as one example, alongside the government's voluntary reporting framework for large employers and the workplace wellbeing questionnaire from What Works Wellbeing. The recommendation is addressed to employers measuring the mental wellbeing of their workforce." ;
    heiro:2000018 "NICE guideline NG212, Mental wellbeing at work, recommendation 1.1.7, published 2 March 2022" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "wemwbs" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:wemwbs .

heiro:6000917 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Willingness to Pay"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#stated-preference"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Stated Willingness to Pay Ranks Below Market and Revealed Preference Values" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Green Book asks appraisers to value social costs and benefits from market prices first, turning to non-market valuation where prices are missing or distorted, and ranks valuation methods in a hierarchy of reliability. Stated preference valuation, which asks people what they would be willing to pay or willing to accept, sits below market prices, generic prices and revealed preference. HM Treasury warns that survey responses are often biased or inconsistent and says stated preference should be used only if robust revealed preference data are unavailable, citing the DHSC value of a QALY as a UK government example." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, The Green Book: UK government guidance on appraisal (2026), Chapter 8, paragraphs 8.1 to 8.6, 8.13 and 8.16 to 8.18, and Figure 8" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "willingness-to-pay" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:willingness-to-pay .

heiro:6000918 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of US Environmental Protection Agency on Willingness to Pay"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000027 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.epa.gov/system/files/documents/2024-12/chapter-7-guidelines-for-preparing-economic-analyses_final_508-compliant.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "US Environmental Protection Agency" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Willingness to Pay as the Preferred Benefit Measure, Including the VSL" ;
    heiro:2000017 "EPA guidelines name willingness to pay as the preferred measure of benefits, because it accounts for individual trade-offs between wealth and benefits in monetary terms and so allows net benefits to be calculated; the term is used to cover willingness to accept compensation as well. Mortality risk reductions are valued from individuals' willingness to pay for small reductions in the risk of dying, aggregated as the value of statistical life (VSL), which EPA stresses is not the value of life itself. EPA policy applies a single VSL estimate across all its programmes, and willingness to pay is also the preferred measure for morbidity effects." ;
    heiro:2000018 "US Environmental Protection Agency, Guidelines for Preparing Economic Analyses, 3rd edition (EPA-240-R-24-001, 2024), Chapter 7 introduction and footnote 1, sections 7.2.1.1 and 7.2.1.2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "willingness-to-pay" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:willingness-to-pay .

heiro:6000919 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of NOAA Blue Ribbon Panel on Willingness to Pay"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000106 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://repository.library.noaa.gov/view/noaa/60900"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "NOAA Blue Ribbon Panel" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Conservative Referendum Design for Contingent Valuation of Willingness to Pay" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The panel appointed by NOAA under the Oil Pollution Act of 1990 concluded that contingent valuation studies can produce estimates reliable enough to be the starting point of a judicial process of damage assessment, including lost passive-use values, provided they follow its guidelines. It recommended the willingness to pay format in place of compensation required, because it is the conservative choice, with the valuation question posed as a vote on a referendum. Where design choices are ambiguous, the option that tends to underestimate willingness to pay is preferred, since hypothetical markets tend to overstate it." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Arrow K, Solow R, Portney PR, Leamer EE, Radner R, Schuman H. Report of the NOAA Panel on Contingent Valuation, 11 January 1993, Federal Register 58:4601 to 4614, Section IV (survey guidelines) and Section VI (conclusions and recommendations)" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "willingness-to-pay" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:willingness-to-pay .

heiro:6000920 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of CPB and PBL (Netherlands) on Willingness to Pay"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000044,
        heiro:5000045 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.pbl.nl/uploads/default/downloads/pbl-cpb-2015-general-guidance-for-cost-benefit-analysis_01512.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "CPB and PBL (Netherlands)" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Non-Priced Effects in Dutch Cost-Benefit Analysis Valued by Willingness to Pay" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Dutch general guidance for social cost-benefit analysis states that welfare changes caused by policy measures are expressed through people's willingness to pay for their positive effects. Market prices are used as far as possible, and the valuation of non-priced effects must be based on willingness to pay, so valuations based on expert judgement are not acceptable. Revealed preference methods rest on observed behaviour but may value an effect only partially, while stated preference methods suit existence values but can be biased. The same willingness to pay approach is required for health and social care markets." ;
    heiro:2000018 "CPB Netherlands Bureau for Economic Policy Analysis and PBL Netherlands Environmental Assessment Agency, General Guidance for Cost-Benefit Analysis (2015), sections 3.1 and 7.1, footnotes 12 and 59, and Chapter 8 box on willingness to pay and health and social care markets" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "willingness-to-pay" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:willingness-to-pay .

heiro:6000921 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ZIN on Willingness-to-Pay Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000012 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.zorginstituutnederland.nl/site/binaries/site-content/collections/documents/2024/11/26/beoordelingskader-kosteneffectiviteit-van-zorg/Beoordelingskader+kosteneffectiviteit+van+zorg.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ZIN" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Reference Values as Society's Maximum Spend per QALY, Set by Burden of Disease" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Zorginstituut defines a reference value as, in principle, the maximum amount society wishes to spend per QALY gained, reflecting the societal value of an extra QALY. Values of €20,000, €50,000 and €80,000 per QALY apply as the proportional shortfall rises, because Dutch research on solidarity found that society accepts higher costs per QALY for more severe disease. The framework cites Dutch willingness to pay estimates of €52,000 per QALY for care for others and €83,000 for oneself or relatives, alongside opportunity cost evidence, and notes the values have not been indexed since 2015." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Zorginstituut Nederland, Beoordelingskader kosteneffectiviteit van zorg (assessment framework for the cost-effectiveness of care), definitive version of 26 November 2024, chapter 3 introduction, section 3.2.2, Boxes 4 and 5 and Table 1" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "willingness-to-pay-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:willingness-to-pay-threshold .

heiro:6000922 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of HM Treasury on Willingness-to-Pay Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000020 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-green-book-appraisal-and-evaluation-in-central-government/the-green-book-2026#stated-preference"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "HM Treasury" ;
    heiro:2000016 "QALY Valued by Stated Preference in Central Government Appraisal" ;
    heiro:2000017 "The Green Book treats the value of a quality-adjusted life year as an example of stated preference valuation, which asks people what they would be willing to pay, with the Department of Health and Social Care using surveys of the value people place on health outcomes. QALY, prevented fatality and statistical life-year values rest on average values from representative population samples and are not intended for emergency or rescue contexts. The wellbeing supplementary guidance cites a QALY value of £60,000 in 2014 prices, or £70,158 in 2019 prices, derived from willingness to pay values for a statistical life year." ;
    heiro:2000018 "HM Treasury, The Green Book (2026), paragraphs 8.16, 8.17, 8.57 and 8.58, updated 5 February 2026; HM Treasury, Wellbeing Guidance for Appraisal: Supplementary Green Book Guidance, July 2021, Annex 2" ;
    heiro:2000019 2 ;
    heiro:2000020 "willingness-to-pay-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:willingness-to-pay-threshold .

heiro:6000923 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of ICER on Willingness-to-Pay Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000015 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://icer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/ICER_2023_2026_VAF_070126.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "ICER" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Opportunity Cost Preferred as Both Paradigms Point Near $100,000 per QALY" ;
    heiro:2000017 "ICER sets the range for its Health Benefit Price Benchmark at 100,000to100,000 to 100,000to150,000 per QALY and per evLY gained, judging that recent research under both the opportunity cost and willingness to pay paradigms puts an operational threshold at about $100,000 per QALY. It sees a confluence of results between the two approaches in the United States but, for conceptual and ethical reasons, favours thresholds based on opportunity cost. It accepts a case for willingness to pay thresholds that differ by payer type, yet frames one range intended to apply broadly across American health insurance systems." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Institute for Clinical and Economic Review, 2023 Value Assessment Framework, updated 25 September 2023, sections 3.9 and 3.13" ;
    heiro:2000019 3 ;
    heiro:2000020 "willingness-to-pay-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:willingness-to-pay-threshold .

heiro:6000924 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Willingness-to-Pay Threshold"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5153921/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "GDP per Capita Benchmarks Not to Be Used Alone as a Funding Rule" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO staff writing in the WHO Bulletin trace GDP-based thresholds to the Commission on Macroeconomics and Health's estimate of the economic value of a year of healthy life, and compare them with value-of-life measures of willingness to pay for an extra healthy year. WHO-CHOICE authors called an intervention very cost-effective if it averted a DALY for less than average per capita income, and cost-effective below three times that level. None of these thresholds should be used alone as a funding rule or as a measure of affordability, and WHO-CHOICE has never recommended such use." ;
    heiro:2000018 "Bertram MY, Lauer JA, De Joncheere K, Edejer T, Hutubessy R, Kieny MP, Hill SR. Cost-effectiveness thresholds: pros and cons. Bulletin of the World Health Organization 2016;94(12):925-930, doi:10.2471/BLT.15.164418, sections 'Thresholds based on gross domestic product' and 'Misuse of thresholds'" ;
    heiro:2000019 4 ;
    heiro:2000020 "willingness-to-pay-threshold" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:willingness-to-pay-threshold .

heiro:6000925 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Years Lived with Disability"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/gho-documents/global-health-estimates/ghe2023_daly_methods.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Prevalence-Based YLDs Without Discounting or Age Weights" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's Global Health Estimates calculate years lived with disability as the prevalence of each sequela multiplied by its disability weight, replacing the incidence-based method of the original Global Burden of Disease study and earlier WHO updates. WHO gives the reasons: an incidence count misses current burden when incidence has fallen, needs estimates of duration as well as incidence, and assigns all health loss to the age of onset. YLDs carry no discounting or age weighting and are adjusted for independent comorbidity, with disability weights combined multiplicatively for people with several conditions." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2023, Global Health Estimates Technical Paper WHO/DSA/DDA/GHE/2026.3 (September 2026), sections 1.4, 2, 2.1 and 2.3 to 2.5" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "years-lived-with-disability" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:years-lived-with-disability .

heiro:6000926 a owl:NamedIndividual,
        heiro:0000016 ;
    rdfs:label "institutional perspective of WHO on Years of Life Lost"@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heiro/heiro-wiki.owl> ;
    heiro:1000012 heiro:5000005 ;
    heiro:2000005 "https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/gho-documents/global-health-estimates/ghe2023_daly_methods.pdf"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    heiro:2000015 "WHO" ;
    heiro:2000016 "Standard Loss Function From Projected Frontier Life Expectancy" ;
    heiro:2000017 "WHO's Global Health Estimates calculate years of life lost as deaths at each age multiplied by a standard loss function giving the years lost for a death at that age. Because even the lowest observed death rates include avoidable deaths, WHO bases the standard on the frontier national life expectancy implied by the lowest projected age-specific mortality rates for 2050 in the United Nations World Population Prospects 2024, giving life expectancy at birth of 92.7 years. Since consultations in 2012, WHO has used a 0% discount rate and no age weighting." ;
    heiro:2000018 "World Health Organization, WHO methods and data sources for global burden of disease estimates 2000-2023, Global Health Estimates Technical Paper WHO/DSA/DDA/GHE/2026.3 (September 2026), sections 2, 2.2 (Table 2.1) and 2.3" ;
    heiro:2000019 1 ;
    heiro:2000020 "years-of-life-lost" ;
    heiro:9000001 hewc:years-of-life-lost .

